A person close to liquidation can remove all feeders, giving themselves a free option on whether the extra time it takes for the admins to resolve the issue, is enough time for their position to go back into the green. Alternatively, a competitor can analyze what price most liquidations will occur at (based on on-chain data about every user's account health), and can time the removal of feeders for maximum effect. Note that even if the admins re-add the feeders, the malicious user can just remove them again.

## Tools Used
Code inspection

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Add the `onlyRole(DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE)` modifier to `removeFeeder()`

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/main/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/ParaSpaceOracle.sol#L128
https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/main/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/ParaSpaceOracle.sol#L130-L132


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Referring to the [docs](https://docs.chain.link/data-feeds/price-feeds/api-reference), Chainlink oracles are returning the price as an `int256`, which means that the answer can be a negative price.
Later, this price is [casted as an `uint256`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/main/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/ParaSpaceOracle.sol#L128) in the case of an oracle that is set, which overflows when `price < 0`.
Any price returned that would be negative, would return a over-inflated price and a massive lent asset of any user which has deposited this specific token in the protocol, allowing to borrow any asset almost for free.

## Proof of Concept
See this `solidity-shell` instance showing that the answer, when negative, and casted to a `uint256`, will overflow.

```sh
🚀 Entering interactive Solidity ^0.8.17 shell (🧁:Ganache built-in). '.help' and '.exit' are your friends.
 »  int256 bad_answer = -1000;
 »  uint256 casted = uint256(bad_answer);
 »  casted
115792089237316195423570985008687907853269984665640564039457584007913129638936
```

## Tools Used
Manual inspection

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

A solution could be to instead use:

```solidity
if (price <= 0) {
  if (address(_fallbackOracle) != address(0)) {
    return _fallbackOracle.getAssetPrice(asset);
  } else {
      revert(Errors.ORACLE_PRICE_NOT_READY);
  }
} else {
  return price;
}
```

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/c6820a279c64a299a783955749fdc977de8f0449/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/NFTFloorOracle.sol#L351


# Vulnerability details

## Proof of Concept

During initialization, the `MAX_DEVIATION_RATE` is set to `150`. This means that price is rejected if it increases or decreases 1.5 times more than the original value

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/c6820a279c64a299a783955749fdc977de8f0449/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/NFTFloorOracle.sol#L14

```solidity
File: NFTFloorOracle.sol
08: //we need to deploy 3 oracles at least
09: uint8 constant MIN_ORACLES_NUM = 3;
10: //expirationPeriod at least the interval of client to feed data(currently 6h=21600s/12=1800 in mainnet)
11: //we do not accept price lags behind to much
12: uint128 constant EXPIRATION_PERIOD = 1800;
13: //reject when price increase/decrease 1.5 times more than original value
14: uint128 constant MAX_DEVIATION_RATE = 150;
```

Within the `NFTFloorOracle._checkValidity`, it will verify that the new price is within the acceptable deviation in Line 365-372 below.

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/c6820a279c64a299a783955749fdc977de8f0449/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/NFTFloorOracle.sol#L351

```solidity
File: NFTFloorOracle.sol
351:     function _checkValidity(address _asset, uint256 _twap)
352:         internal
353:         view
354:         returns (bool)
355:     {
356:         require(_twap > 0, "NFTOracle: price should be more than 0");
357:         PriceInformation memory assetPriceMapEntry = assetPriceMap[_asset];
358:         uint256 _priorTwap = assetPriceMapEntry.twap;
359:         uint256 _updatedAt = assetPriceMapEntry.updatedAt;
360:         uint256 priceDeviation;
361:         //first price is always valid
362:         if (_priorTwap == 0 || _updatedAt == 0) {
363:             return true;
364:         }
365:         priceDeviation = _twap > _priorTwap
366:             ? (_twap * 100) / _priorTwap
367:             : (_priorTwap * 100) / _twap;
368: 
369:         // config maxPriceDeviation as multiple directly(not percent) for simplicity
370:         if (priceDeviation >= config.maxPriceDeviation) {
371:             return false;
372:         }
373:         return true;
374:     }
```

However, in the event of a sudden fall in price, the latest price submitted by all feeders might get rejected if all feeders provide the actual market price too quickly. At this point, the price within the NFTFloorOracle will be stale and cannot be updated by the feeders.

Assume there are three feeders within NFTFloorOracle, and LUNANFT is a supported asset/NFT within ParaSpace. The current price for LUNANFT is `$1` within NFTFloorOracle.

Due to a black swan event, LUNANFT crashes to `$0.01` within a short period. Thus, the three feeders submit `$0.05`, `$0.1`, `$0.3`, respectively. 

However, the NFTFloorOracle will reject all the new prices submitted by feeders because it deviates from the original price (`$1`) by too much (deviation > 1.5 times). As such, the NFTFloorOracle will ignore the new prices and continue to believe that the original price of `$1` is the correct one. This causes all the LUNANFT to be overvalued within ParaSpace, and malicious users can purchase a large number of LUNANFT cheaply from the open market (`$0.01`), and use them as collateral within ParaSpace to borrow all the assets within ParaSpace.

## Impact

Stale prices within NFTFloorOracle might cause certain assets to be overvalued within ParaSpace. This leads to inflated collateral value and allows malicious users to overborrow.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

If there is a large deviation in the price reported by a feeder, do not discard the price immediately. Instead, compare it with other feeders. If both feeders report a similar price, it can be safely concluded that the large price deviation reported by a feeder was likely a legitimate market price movement.

This has been implemented within Liquidy's price oracle. The code for checking if both oracles have the same price can be found [here ](https://github.com/liquity/dev/blob/81f319d415e67b0f1bd43aa3cef67727580ab4aa/packages/contracts/contracts/PriceFeed.sol#L425)for those interested.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/c6820a279c64a299a783955749fdc977de8f0449/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/NFTFloorOracle.sol#L331-L334


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The NFTFloorOracle admin may not be able to remove a malicious or a hacked feeder due to the flawed feeder indexes updating. The malicious feeder may harm the protocol before the admin adds another feeder, which will unlock removal of the malicious one.
## Proof of Concept
The `NFTFloorOracle` contract maintains a list of allowed feeders who provide prices to the contract. Feeders are stored in two data structures: the `feeders` array and the `feederPositionMap` mapping ([NFTFloorOracle.sol#L76-L81](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/c6820a279c64a299a783955749fdc977de8f0449/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/NFTFloorOracle.sol#L76-L81)). The values in the mapping have the `index` key to link feeders from the mapping to their position in the array ([NFTFloorOracle.sol#L311-L313](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/c6820a279c64a299a783955749fdc977de8f0449/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/NFTFloorOracle.sol#L311-L313)):
```solidity
feeders.push(_feeder);
feederPositionMap[_feeder].index = uint8(feeders.length - 1);
feederPositionMap[_feeder].registered = true;
```

When a feeder is removed, it's being replaced by the last feeder in the array. However, that feeder's index is not updated in the mapping ([NFTFloorOracle.sol#L330-L335](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/c6820a279c64a299a783955749fdc977de8f0449/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/NFTFloorOracle.sol#L330-L335)):
```solidity
uint8 feederIndex = feederPositionMap[_feeder].index;
if (feederIndex >= 0 && feeders[feederIndex] == _feeder) {
    feeders[feederIndex] = feeders[feeders.length - 1];
    feeders.pop();
}
delete feederPositionMap[_feeder];
```

This causes denial of service when trying to remove the feeder that was previously placed at a removed feeder's position. To unlock the removal of the feeder, the admin would need to add another feeder:
```js
// paraspace-core/test/_oracle_nft_floor_price.spec.ts
it("doesn't update feeder index when a feeder is removed [AUDIT]", async () => {
  const { nftFloorOracle } = testEnv;

  expect(await nftFloorOracle.getFeederSize()).to.eq(7);

  const feeder1 = await nftFloorOracle.feeders(1);
  const feeder6 = await nftFloorOracle.feeders(6); // last feeder

  await nftFloorOracle.removeFeeder(feeder1);

  // feeder6 was placed at the feeder1's position
  expect(await nftFloorOracle.feeders(1)).to.eq(feeder6);

  // Cannot remove feeder6 because its index is still 6 and feeders.length == 6
  await expect(
    nftFloorOracle.removeFeeder(feeder6)
  ).to.be.revertedWith("Array accessed at an out-of-bounds or negative index");

  // Had to add a new feeder to be able to remove feeder6
  await nftFloorOracle.addFeeders([feeder1]);
  expect(await nftFloorOracle.feeders(6)).to.eq(feeder1);

  await nftFloorOracle.removeFeeder(feeder6);

  // However, feeder6 is still in the feeders array
  expect(await nftFloorOracle.feeders(1)).to.eq(feeder6);
});
```

### Exploit Scenario
1. The admin of `NFTFloorOracle` detects a malicious or a hacked feeder.
1. The malicious feeder was previously placed at the index of a previously removed feeder in the `feeders` array.
1. The admin calls the `removeFeeder` function to remove the malicious feeder and gets their transaction reverted.
1. Before the admin figures out what causes the revert and manages to add another feeder to remove the malicious one, the malicious feeder make more damage to the protocol.
## Tools Used
Manual review
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider this change:
```diff
--- a/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/NFTFloorOracle.sol
+++ b/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/NFTFloorOracle.sol
@@ -330,6 +330,7 @@ contract NFTFloorOracle is Initializable, AccessControl, INFTFloorOracle {
         uint8 feederIndex = feederPositionMap[_feeder].index;
         if (feederIndex >= 0 && feeders[feederIndex] == _feeder) {
             feeders[feederIndex] = feeders[feeders.length - 1];
+            feederPositionMap[feeders[feederIndex]].index = feederIndex;
             feeders.pop();
         }
         delete feederPositionMap[_feeder];
```

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/c6820a279c64a299a783955749fdc977de8f0449/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/NFTFloorOracle.sol#L331-L334


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
NFTFloorOracle's removeFeeder() calls _removeFeeder(), which moves the last feeder in the feeders array to position feederIndex without updating feederPositionMap for that feeder. This means the index stored in feederPositionMap for the moved feeder is set incorrectly. It can result in:
- the stored feeder index may be greater than the length of feeders - 1, resulting in DOS when attempting to remove the feeder
- if removeFeeder() is successfully called on the moved feeder, the feeder will not actually be removed from the feeders array and the existing prices for this feeder will continue to be used until they age out
- if, after removeFeeder() is called, the feeder is then re-added, it can be present in the feeders array multiple times. This can allow this one single feeder to get multiple entries in the list of prices and potentially determine on its own what the median price reported by the oracle will be (reporting an arbitrary value).

## Proof of Concept
The POC demonstrates the impact described above, in particular the DOS issue and ability of a single feeder to set an arbitrary price to be reported by the NFTFloorOracle.

Test
```
npx hardhat test ./test/_oracle_nft_floor_price.spec.ts --grep "TC-oracle-nft-floor-price-XXX:Feeder list corruption, single feeder dictates price"
```

