# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Many important functionalities of this protocol rely on the `getAssetPrice` function in the `ParaSpaceOracle` contract, which would be called when calling functions like `GenericLogic._getAssetPrice`, `LiquidationLogic._calculateERC20LiquidationParameters`, `ValidationLogic.validateBorrow`, etc. As shown below, calling the `ParaSpaceOracle.getAssetPrice` function will execute `price = uint256(source.latestAnswer())`, where `source` is a Chainlink aggregator.

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-11-paraspace/blob/main/paraspace-core/contracts/misc/ParaSpaceOracle.sol#L115-L136
```solidity
    function getAssetPrice(address asset)
        public
        view
        override
        returns (uint256)
    {
        if (asset == BASE_CURRENCY) {
            return BASE_CURRENCY_UNIT;
        }

        uint256 price = 0;
        IEACAggregatorProxy source = IEACAggregatorProxy(assetsSources[asset]);
        if (address(source) != address(0)) {
            price = uint256(source.latestAnswer());
        }
        if (price == 0 && address(_fallbackOracle) != address(0)) {
            price = _fallbackOracle.getAssetPrice(asset);
        }

        require(price != 0, Errors.ORACLE_PRICE_NOT_READY);
        return price;
    }
```

As mentioned by https://blog.openzeppelin.com/secure-smart-contract-guidelines-the-dangers-of-price-oracles/, there is a possibility that Chainlink’s "multisigs can immediately block access to price feeds at will". When this happens, calling the `ParaSpaceOracle.getAssetPrice` function will revert because executing `source.latestAnswer()` reverts. As a result, these important functionalities that rely on the `ParaSpaceOracle.getAssetPrice` function are DOS'ed, which greatly limits the protocol's usability.

## Proof of Concept
The following steps can occur for a scenario involving a DOS'ed `ParaSpaceOracle.getAssetPrice` function call.
1. Alice calls the `supply` function in the `PoolCore` contract to supply some of an ERC20 token.
2. Chainlink's multisigs suddenly blocks access to the price feed for the corresponding reserve address.
3. Alice calls the `withdraw` function in the `PoolCore` contract, which further calls the `SupplyLogic.executeWithdraw`, `ValidationLogic.validateHFAndLtvERC20`, `ValidationLogic.validateHealthFactor`, `GenericLogic.calculateUserAccountData`, and `GenericLogic._getAssetPrice` functions, for withdrawing her supplied amount of the ERC20 token.
4. Calling the `GenericLogic._getAssetPrice` function further calls the `getAssetPrice` function in the `ParaSpaceOracle` contract, which executes `price = uint256(source.latestAnswer())`.
5. Due to step 2, executing `source.latestAnswer()` reverts so Alice's `PoolCore.withdraw` function call reverts; as a result, she fails to withdraw her supplied amount of the ERC20 token.

## Tools Used
VSCode

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The `getAssetPrice` function in the `ParaSpaceOracle` contract can be updated to refactor `source.latestAnswer()` into `try source.latestAnswer() returns (int256 answer) { ... } catch Error(string memory) { ... }`. The logics for setting `price` using `answer` returned by the Chainlink aggregator should be placed in the `try` block while the logics for setting `price` using the fallback oracle should be placed in the `catch` block.

