# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Despite the price of GGP being called off-chain, it is still possible for the LP pairs aggregated by 1inch to be manipulated by an attacker, knowing that an update will have to happen at some point. If an update has been given at a price thats been temporarily manipulated to be higher than its supposed to be, this could allow stakers to unstake more of their GGP than they are expected to, because of an increased collateralisation ratio.
If tokens have been bought to manipulate the liquidity pairs, an attacker will then sell these back to the pool where they could be leaving unhealthy collateral in the Vault as a result.

## Proof of Concept
- Attacker doesn't have enough of the minimum GGP Stake to collateralise their minipool or they have already staked and want to reduce their stake through the attack -  stakeGGP() / restakeGGP()
Notice - Attacker has enough collateral for a new minipool, if the price is increased by a particular amount
- Attacker pushes the price up, by buying through the GGP pairs that are aggregated on 1inch (won't be many at launch)
- Wait for the price update to be executed by GoGoPool
- Collateralisation ratio is now above 150% if they wanted to stake for a new minipool or if they wanted to remove GGP that was already staked
- Attacker now sells the bought GGP, causing the minipools to be undercollateralised, yet still receiving rewards


## Recommended Mitigation Steps

- Revert if the price difference between the current and last price is extreme e.g greater than 100%

- Create a Window size variable to be used during price calcs.
If the difference in time between the last update and the current update is within the window size then perform some math to average out the difference between the last price and the price to update

// Variable to add
