# Vulnerability details

## Impact
This could render the price oracle useless and throw off the protocol if the gogo protocol price in AVAX is 0 OR if the price is enormously large. This is a very easy thing to forget when working with price oracles because the price returned can come back as insanely large. Also, with the reservation that the price variable does rely on a different party's (AVAX) price to compute, there is another attack vector to be weary of.

## Proof of Concept
For the following lines of code, it only outputs the InvalidGGPPrice() error if the price equals 0 but not if the price is enormous, which is possible with price oracles.

function getGGPPriceInAVAX() external view returns (uint256 price, uint256 timestamp) {
		price = getUint(keccak256("Oracle.GGPPriceInAVAX"));
		if (price == 0) {
			revert InvalidGGPPrice();
		}
		timestamp = getUint(keccak256("Oracle.GGPTimestamp"));
	}

## Tools Used
Found this bug manually.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
(1) For Oracle.sol, line 48, the if-statement can be changed to 
if (price == 0 || price > num)

where num equals a designated number that is too large to be gogo protocol price in AVAX.

(2) Use a try/catch function to capture the large number error with the same logic as suggestion 1.
(3) Instead of...

 if (price == 0) ....
			revert InvalidGGPPrice();
Changing to...

 if(price > 0) {
            return price;
        } else {
        revert InvalidGGPPrice();

