# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Function `_removeCollateral()` removes collateral from `msg.senders` vault and  code allows for onReceive hook to sell and repay debt before the debt check. during the hook function user can call other function of the PaprController contract, it's possible for attacker to perform reentrancy attack and remove his collaterals one by one by calling `removeCollaterl()` and during the hook call except the last collateral and in the last hook attacker can call `startLiquidationAuction()` and `purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT()` to liquidate his last collateral (his debt is > max debt) and because this is last collateral so code would set the debt of the attacker to 0 and then the hook functions returns and debt checks after the transfer would pass because attacker debt is 0 and in the end attacker was able to withdraw all of his collaterals and keep the debts and only pay for liquidation of one of the NFTS.

the second scenario is that during hook call user debt is bigger than max debt and it's possible for attacker to call and liquidate user tokens if the execution reaches the attacker controlled address even if user pays his debt during the hook(for example user wants to sell the removed collateral in another on-chain exchange and user's contract sell token  to attacker order and NFT token get transferred to attacker and in the hook function of that transfer attacker address would get execution control and call liquidate for user). this is not critical impact but shows that it's possible to do more with this reentrancy.

## Proof of Concept
This is `_removeCollateral()` code:
```
    function _removeCollateral(
        address sendTo,
        IPaprController.Collateral calldata collateral,
        uint256 oraclePrice,
        uint256 cachedTarget
    ) internal {
        if (collateralOwner[collateral.addr][collateral.id] != msg.sender) {
            revert IPaprController.OnlyCollateralOwner();
        }

        delete collateralOwner[collateral.addr][collateral.id];

        uint16 newCount;
        unchecked {
            newCount = _vaultInfo[msg.sender][collateral.addr].count - 1;
            _vaultInfo[msg.sender][collateral.addr].count = newCount;
        }

        // allows for onReceive hook to sell and repay debt before the
        // debt check below
        collateral.addr.safeTransferFrom(address(this), sendTo, collateral.id);

        uint256 debt = _vaultInfo[msg.sender][collateral.addr].debt;
        uint256 max = _maxDebt(oraclePrice * newCount, cachedTarget);

        if (debt > max) {
            revert IPaprController.ExceedsMaxDebt(debt, max);
        }

        emit RemoveCollateral(msg.sender, collateral.addr, collateral.id);
    }
```
As you can see code make external call to attacker specified address(`sendTo`) by `collateral.addr.safeTransferFrom(address(this), sendTo, collateral.id)` which would call `sendTo` address `onERC721Received()` function and then code checks to make sure that debt is lower than max debt and code retrieves the updated debt after the call to give user a chance to pay some of the debt in the hook function.
This  is `purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT()` code:
```
    function purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT(
        Auction calldata auction,
        uint256 maxPrice,
        address sendTo,
        ReservoirOracleUnderwriter.OracleInfo calldata oracleInfo
    ) external override {
        uint256 collateralValueCached = underwritePriceForCollateral(
            auction.auctionAssetContract, ReservoirOracleUnderwriter.PriceKind.TWAP, oracleInfo
        ) * _vaultInfo[auction.nftOwner][auction.auctionAssetContract].count;
        bool isLastCollateral = collateralValueCached == 0;
.......
.......
.......
        if (isLastCollateral && remaining != 0) {
            /// there will be debt left with no NFTs, set it to 0
            _reduceDebtWithoutBurn(auction.nftOwner, auction.auctionAssetContract, remaining);
        }
    }
```
function `purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT()` purchases a liquidation auction with the controller's papr token and as you can see in the last part of it if the auctioned token is the last token of borrower collaterals contract would set the debt of that user as 0.
attacker can combine this two functionality and withdraw his collaterals and don't pay his debts and only pay for liquidation of the one of his tokens. these are the steps to perform this attack: (attacker would write contract to perform the steps)
1. attacker's contract add 10 token of NFT1 as collateral into the protocol. each token price is 100 Papr (for simplicity let's set prices in papr) and LTV is 0.5.
2. attacker's contract then get `10 * 100 * 0.5 = 500` paper token as loan and the `debt < max debt` would be true. (target is 1 for simplicity).
3. attacker's contract would call `removeCollateral(NFT1 , 1, attackerContract as receiver)` to remove collateral id 1 and in the hook function token transfer attacker would call `removeCollateral(NFT1 , 2, attackerContract as receiver)` and again in the hook function of the token transfer attacker contract would call `removeCollateral(NFT1 , 3, attackerContract as receiver)` and would perform this loop until attacker's contract withdraw 9 tokens. because debt check is after hook function so none of the debt checks are executed yet. (9 call to `removeCollateral()` in a recessive way, `removeCollateral()` -> `onERC721Received()` -> `removeCollateral()` -> .....)
4. for the last collateral attacker would call `startLiquidationAuction()` to start an auction for NFT id 10 and because debt is 500 and there is one collateral so `debt < max debt` and code would start the auction and the price would be `3*100 = 300`
5. then attacker would call `purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT()` to buy the NFT1 token 10 from the auction and pay the 300 Papr.
6. function `purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT()` would transfer the NFT1 token 10 to attacker's contract and transfers 300 Papr from attacker's contract and pay 300 out of 500 debt of attacker's debt and 200 debt would remain which because this was the last collateral code would set attacker's debt to 0 (`isLastCollateral && remaining != 0`). now attacker owns all the 10 NFT1 tokens and get 500 loan and paid 300 so it has 200 Papr as balance too and the debt is 0 in the protocol.
7. now attacker's contract would return the hook functions and the debt checks after the transfer in the `_removeCollaterl()` would get executed but because updated debt is 0 and code uses the updated debt so all the checks would pass and the transaction would finish.
8. so in the end attacker minted 200 Papr as loan and didn't pay it and withdraws all of his collaterals. by performing this attack attacker can mint unlimited number of papr tokens.
So these steps shows that attacker can create loans and don't pay them and mint unlimited amount of papr token for himself.

There are other scenarios to exploit this reentrancy too, for example one less critical one is when user remove collateral and wants to sell it to attacker order in other platforms, and pay the debt, which gives attacker opportunity to call liquidation for user tokens and start auction for them even when user pays his debt. these are the steps:
1. user add two token NFT1 as collateral which price of each is 100 papr and LTV is 0.5 and target is 1.
2. user get 90 loan.
3. user call `removeCollateral()` to remove one of the collaterals and in the hook function of the token transfer user would try to sell that token in another on-chain exchange and user's contract would sell NFT1 token to attacker order for 90 Papr.
4. token would get transferred to attacker and the hook function of the transfer get executed and execution would reach attacker controlled address, now user has 1 collateral worth 100 papr and 90 papr as loan so attacker can call `startLiquidationAuction()` for user another collateral and start an auction for that collateral.
5. user would transfer 90 paper received from token selling to to protocol and pay his debt.
6. in the end user paid his debt in the transaction but still he got liquidated by attacker and his token get auctioned.
the steps are this scenario is difficult and also the impact is not too high but it shows the reentrancy can be used to perform another actions too.

## Tools Used
VIM

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
add reentrancy guard for critical function. and don't set debt as 0 when user has no collateral.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/NFTEDA/NFTEDA.sol#L91
https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L264-L294


# Vulnerability details


## Impact
Malicious users can steal PAPR tokens through reentrancy attacks.

## Proof of Concept
Function [PaprController.purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT()](https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L264) may trigger a reentrancy becase it calls `ERC721.safeTransferFrom()` in [NFTEDA._purchaseNFT()](https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/NFTEDA/NFTEDA.sol#L91):

```solidity
auction.auctionAssetContract.safeTransferFrom(address(this), sendTo, auction.auctionAssetID);
```

Combined with `purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT()`'s ability of burning a debt, an attacker can profit by reentering to burn a debt multiple times.

Here is a sample attack:
Assume that `x` is an attack contract deployed by the attacker, `nft` be an allowed collateral, `price` is the price of the `nft`, V is the vault `_vaultInfo[x][nft]`.
1. `x` add two collaterals(nft-1, nft-2) to V, and make a loan (`L0 = maxdebt(price * 2)`).
2. The `price` fell, and V is liquidatable now.
3. Someone calls [`startLiquidationAuction()`](https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L297) to start an auction (`auction1`) for nft-1 in V.
4. If the `price` keeps falling, nobody will purchage `auction1`.
5. [`liquidationAuctionMinSpacing = 2days`](https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L41) later, someone (or x self) start an auction (`auction2`) for nft-2 in V.
6. `x` start attack:
  - purchase auction1 begin
    - `x.onERC721Received(nft-1)`: purchase auction2, add collaterals to V, `increaseDebt()` with `maxDebt()`
  - purchase auction1 end
  - remove all collaterals
7. Finally, `x` will earn more than `L0` PAPR tokens.

> The `Attack.sol` in the following test code is a reference implementation of `x`.

Test code for PoC:
```solidity
diff --git a/test/paprController/Attacker.sol b/test/paprController/Attacker.sol
