# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The formula used for`_multiplier()` will not work as expected. The target price update will be inaccurate and all the borrow/repay/liquidation functions will use the inaccurate target price. The impacts might be:
- Target price and mark price track will not work work as expected.
- More than max LTV could be borrowed and the contract could lose fund due to wrong price reference.


## Proof of Concept

#### "Static" Situation

![static](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/141345/report-assets/main/2022-12-backed/image/2022-12-backed_static.png)

Imagine a "static" situation, everything does not change, including the "interest rate". The mark price, target price should increase steadily at some constant growth rate (interest rate). The target price, is expected to be the forwarded mark price after `fundingPeriod` time.

Assuming, `fundingPeriod` is 30 days, expected $\frac{target}{mark}$ for `fundingPeriod` is 110% (interest rate for `fundingPeriod` 30 days is 10%). Initial mark price $mark_0$ is 1000. Then the expected situation will be the following:
- day 0, mark price $mark_0$ is 1000, target price $target_0$ is 1100.
- day 30, if everything go as expected, mark price $mark_{30}$ should increase to $target_0$ 1100 (1000 * 110%), $target_{30}$ will continue increase at the same rate to 1210 (1100 * 110%).
- day 60, the next `fundingPeriod`, the same process will go on, $mark_{60}$ be 1210, $target_{60}$ be 1331.

Just like in the pic below, in each `fundingPeriod`, the mark price will steadily increase to the target, and at the same time, target price will increase at the same rate. So $mark_{0}$ -> $mark_{30}$ -> $mark_{60}$ and $target_{0}$ -> $target_{30}$ -> $target_{60}$, 1000 -> 1100 -> 1210 -> 1331, they will move in the same line, with target price one `fundingPeriod` (30 days in this example) ahead.

#### Current Formula

However, in current implementation, it won't work as expected:
- day 0, initial condition, same as above
- day 30. If the contract is not "touched" until end of `fundingPeriod`, day 30, someone calls `updateTarget()`. In function `_multiplier()`:
    - `periodRatio` will be 1:
    Because in `updateTarget()`, `_lastUpdated` is still day 0, it is updated in line 55, after `_newTarget()` is called. So `period` will be 30 days (`block.timestamp - _lastUpdated`), `periodRatio` = 30/30 = 1.
    - `targetMarkRatio` will be 1: 
    At the end the the `fundingPeriod`, $mark_{30}$ will grow to 1100 as expected (comes from uniswap, external trading (`_latestTwapTickAndTickCumulative()` -> `_mark(latestTwapTick)`). At this time, the `_target` stored remains 1100 since it is not updated yet, `targetMarkRatio` = 1100/1100 = 1.

As a result, `_multiplier()` will return $1^1 = 1$ in line 172, the target price will not be increased to 1210 as expected, but stays 1100 (1100 * $1^1$ = 1100). 

```solidity
File: papr/src/UniswapOracleFundingRateController.sol

45:     function updateTarget() public override returns (uint256 nTarget) {
46:         if (_lastUpdated == block.timestamp) {
47:             return _target;
48:         }
49: 
50:         (int56 latestCumulativeTick, int24 latestTwapTick) = _latestTwapTickAndTickCumulative();
51:         nTarget = _newTarget(latestTwapTick, _target);
52: 
53:         _target = SafeCastLib.safeCastTo128(nTarget);
54:         // will not overflow for 8000 years
55:         _lastUpdated = uint48(block.timestamp);
56:         _lastCumulativeTick = latestCumulativeTick;
57:         _lastTwapTick = latestTwapTick;
58: 
59:         emit UpdateTarget(nTarget);
60:     }


132:     function _newTarget(int24 latestTwapTick, uint256 cachedTarget) internal view returns (uint256) {
133:         return FixedPointMathLib.mulWadDown(cachedTarget, _multiplier(_mark(latestTwapTick), cachedTarget));
134:     }


156:     function _multiplier(uint256 _mark_, uint256 cachedTarget) internal view returns (uint256) {
157:         uint256 period = block.timestamp - _lastUpdated;
158:         uint256 periodRatio = FixedPointMathLib.divWadDown(period, fundingPeriod);
159:         uint256 targetMarkRatio;
160:         if (_mark_ == 0) {
161:             targetMarkRatio = targetMarkRatioMax;
162:         } else {
163:             targetMarkRatio = FixedPointMathLib.divWadDown(cachedTarget, _mark_);
164:             if (targetMarkRatio > targetMarkRatioMax) {
165:                 targetMarkRatio = targetMarkRatioMax;
166:             } else if (targetMarkRatio < targetMarkRatioMin) {
167:                 targetMarkRatio = targetMarkRatioMin;
168:             }
169:         }
170: 
171:         // safe to cast because targetMarkRatio > 0
172:         return uint256(FixedPointMathLib.powWad(int256(targetMarkRatio), int256(periodRatio)));
173:     }

```

#### Reason: Mark Price and Period Mismatch

![0-10-30](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/141345/report-assets/main/2022-12-backed/image/2022-12-backed_0%2010%2030.png)

From the picture:
- point A:
    On day 0, the spot $mark_0$ on point A, is expected to move along line AC, to the forward $target_0$ on point C. 
- point B:
    On day 10, the intention seems to use line BC, and extrapolate to point D. 
    But the wrong `period` is used. The purpose is to use $\Delta T_{C,B}$, the current formula actually is using $\Delta T_{B,A} + \Delta T_{C,B}$. 





## Tools Used
Manual analysis.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Here are 2 possible suggestions:

### 1. Use Previous Mark Price

Which means on day 10, set the target along line AC, not BC. If using BC, the following picture illustrates some edge scenario not favorable: negative interest rate. 

![negative](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/141345/report-assets/main/2022-12-backed/image/2022-12-backed_negative.png)

But if going along AC to point E, it will be more robust to negative interest rate, since there is some "lag" as buffer period for the mark price to take effect. Negative interest rate is still possible, but much less and numerically more stable.

In this approach, previous mark price needs to be stored. And current mark price will be used next time.

### 2. Use the Spot Mark Price

Let's revisit the day 0-10-30 pic. The idea to use spot mark price is to quickly adaptive to twap mark price change. For point B below B', the target will be adjust from point E to D to reflect the desired interest increase. 
![0-10-30](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/141345/report-assets/main/2022-12-backed/image/2022-12-backed_0%2010%2030.png)

But from the pic in the 1st approach, it introduces higher possibility of negative interest rate. To mitigate this issue, maybe enforce positive interest rate, then the result can be more robust.

Of course some interest rate in between can also be used, the target will lie among point D and E.

Also noted the $\Delta T_{C,B}$ need to be validated in case of too small interval and overflow.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L159


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The collateral is assigned to the operator's vault because of a parameter mismatch. This impacts the ability of third parties to integrate the PaprController contract. You're not able to create an intermediary contract that adds collateral to a user's vault. It has to be the user themselves sending the NFT directly else the collateral is assigned to the calling contract (operator). If the collateral is assigned to the NFT owner's vault you can have two types of integrations:
- the intermediary contract has its own vault and handles all the user logic itself (user approved NFT to intermediary contract -> contract transfers NFT to itself -> contract sends NFT to PaprController to add the collateral to their vault)
- intermediary contract works with the user's own vault (user approves NFT to intermediary contract -> contract transfers NFT to PaprController to add to user's vault)

Only the first integration type is possible in the current form because the collateral is always assigned to the operator (intermediary contract).

Generally, I believe that the collateral being assigned to the operator is unexpected behavior that might cause headaches if the caller doesn't read the contracts properly. To undo that, the operator has to pay back the debt, remove the collateral and send it back to the user. Depending on the contract used to transfer the collateral in the first place, that might not be possible. In that rare scenario that might cause a loss of funds.

## Proof of Concept
The `onERC721Received()` function has the following interface 
```sol
function onERC721Received(address _operator, address _from, uint256 _tokenId, bytes _data) external returns(bytes4);
```
The PaprController contract expects the first parameter to be the NFT owner, although that's the operator's address (see NATSPEC comment):
```sol
    /// @notice Handler for safeTransferFrom of an NFT
    /// @dev Should be preferred to `addCollateral` if only one NFT is being added
    /// to avoid approval call and save gas
    /// @param from the current owner of the nft
    /// @param _id the id of the NFT
    /// @param data encoded IPaprController.OnERC721ReceivedArgs
    /// @return selector indicating succesful receiving of the NFT
    function onERC721Received(address from, address, uint256 _id, bytes calldata data)
        external
        override
        returns (bytes4)
    {
        IPaprController.OnERC721ReceivedArgs memory request = abi.decode(data, (IPaprController.OnERC721ReceivedArgs));

        IPaprController.Collateral memory collateral = IPaprController.Collateral(ERC721(msg.sender), _id);

        _addCollateralToVault(from, collateral);

        if (request.swapParams.minOut > 0) {
            _increaseDebtAndSell(from, request.proceedsTo, ERC721(msg.sender), request.swapParams, request.oracleInfo);
        } else if (request.debt > 0) {
            _increaseDebt(from, collateral.addr, request.proceedsTo, request.debt, request.oracleInfo);
        }

        return ERC721TokenReceiver.onERC721Received.selector;
    }
```

## Tools Used
none

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Use the owner's address instead of the operator's.

