# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Petty users can liquidate other people's NFT immediately when the liquidation threshold is reached.

## Proof of Concept

The owner controls the function setLiquidationsLocked and calls the function when a collateral needs to be liquidated

    function setLiquidationsLocked(bool locked) external override onlyOwner {
        liquidationsLocked = locked;
    }

The liquidation process starts by checking whether liquidation is locked or not. If it is unlocked / false, then the liquidating process starts

    function startLiquidationAuction(
        address account,
        IPaprController.Collateral calldata collateral,
        ReservoirOracleUnderwriter.OracleInfo calldata oracleInfo
    ) external override returns (INFTEDA.Auction memory auction) {
        if (liquidationsLocked) {
            revert LiquidationsLocked();
        }

Since the function startLiquidationAuction() is external, anyone can call the function and pass in the appropriate account that needs to be liquidated once the owner unlocks liquidation. The choice of liquidation should not be done by the public, but instead a trusted DAO or even a trusted owner, disregarding centralization issues. For example:

Alice has maximised her debt and is almost reaching the threshold of liquidation. If her NFT price drops by just a little, she would need to liquidate. Assume that owner has already unlocked liquidations. Petty whale Bob sees that Alice is on the verge of liquidation. He starts to create false rumor of the NFT that Alice owns as collateral (Bob also owns the same collection, he just wants to buy more at a discounted price). Since Bob is a whale, people believe his words and the price of the NFT starts to drop drastically. Before the NFT creators can clarify the rumors and raise the price back to its original value, Bob already took the opportunity to liquidate Alice's NFT and buy it from the auction.

https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L360-L362
https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L297-L304

## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

There should be a time delay between reaching the threshold of liquidation and actually liquidating the collateral. For example, once the debt is greater than maxDebt, a timer of one day starts. If the NFT is still liquid after a day, then the NFT will be forced to liquidate. Also, instead of setting the whole protocol to be liquid, focus on the particular address to liquidate. The owner / trusted owners should be in charge of auctioning and liquidation as well.

