# Vulnerability details

## Impact
address `oracleSigner` has been used to verify signed messages by comparing the return value of `ecrecover()` by `oracleSigner`. but the return value of `ecrecover()` would be 0x0 and code doesn't check that return value is not 0x0. if owner set `oracleSigner` as 0x0 by mistake then all the unsigned messages would be considered a valid messages. this would cause total funds loss in the protocol.

## Proof of Concept
This is `underwritePriceForCollateral()` code:
```
    function underwritePriceForCollateral(ERC721 asset, PriceKind priceKind, OracleInfo memory oracleInfo)
        public
        returns (uint256)
    {
        address signerAddress = ecrecover(
            keccak256(
                abi.encodePacked(
                    "\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\n32",
                    // EIP-712 structured-data hash
                    keccak256(
                        abi.encode(
                            keccak256("Message(bytes32 id,bytes payload,uint256 timestamp)"),
                            oracleInfo.message.id,
                            keccak256(oracleInfo.message.payload),
                            oracleInfo.message.timestamp
                        )
                    )
                )
            ),
            oracleInfo.sig.v,
            oracleInfo.sig.r,
            oracleInfo.sig.s
        );

        if (signerAddress != oracleSigner) {
            revert IncorrectOracleSigner();
        }
.....
.....
     }
```
As you can see code checks the return value of `ecrecover()` with `oracleSigner` so if the message had incorrect sign the return value of `ecrecover()` would be 0x0 and if the `oracleSigner` was 0x0 too then messages with incorrect sign would be considered valid message. as these are prices of NFTs then attacker can set any price for provided NFTs.
This is `constructor()` in PaprController and ReservoirOracleUnderwriter contracts:
```
    constructor(address _oracleSigner, address _quoteCurrency) {
        oracleSigner = _oracleSigner;
        quoteCurrency = _quoteCurrency;
    }

    constructor(
        string memory name,
        string memory symbol,
        uint256 _maxLTV,
        uint256 indexMarkRatioMax,
        uint256 indexMarkRatioMin,
        ERC20 underlying,
        address oracleSigner
    )
        NFTEDAStarterIncentive(1e17)
        UniswapOracleFundingRateController(underlying, new PaprToken(name, symbol), indexMarkRatioMax, indexMarkRatioMin)
        ReservoirOracleUnderwriter(oracleSigner, address(underlying))
    {
        maxLTV = _maxLTV;
        token0IsUnderlying = address(underlying) < address(papr);
        uint256 underlyingONE = 10 ** underlying.decimals();
        uint160 initSqrtRatio;

        // initialize the pool at 1:1
        if (token0IsUnderlying) {
            initSqrtRatio = UniswapHelpers.oneToOneSqrtRatio(underlyingONE, 10 ** 18);
        } else {
            initSqrtRatio = UniswapHelpers.oneToOneSqrtRatio(10 ** 18, underlyingONE);
        }

        address _pool = UniswapHelpers.deployAndInitPool(address(underlying), address(papr), 10000, initSqrtRatio);

        _init(underlyingONE, _pool);
    }
```
As you can see there is no validation for `oracleSigner` value. because the impact of setting wrong value is high code should perform this validity checks for this value or function `underwritePriceForCollateral()` should be updated and checks that return value of `ecrecover()` is not zero.
The scenario that this issue happens is that:
1. owner deploy contract with 0x0 value for oracleSigner by mistake.
2. attacker can provide wrong NFT prices and mint unlimited Papr tokens.

also right now the value of `oracleSigner` is set in constructor but if in the future code changes and owner could change the value of the `oracleSigner` then the issue become more critical because the real issue is that message sign checking is wrong and don't check that return value of the `ecrecover()` is not 0x0 so any time owner changes `oracleSigner` to 0x0 by mistake all funds would be lost.

