# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Function `_increaseDebt()` and `increaseDebt()` increase debt balance of the vault uniquely identified by `msg.sender` and the collateral address but there is no check that collateral address is whitelisted address. according to the comment on the `IPaprController` function `setAllowedCollateral()` sets whether a collateral is allowed to be used to mint debt and owner can set a asset as allowed to be as collateral or not allowed any time. if an asset set as allowed and users add this tokens as collateral to contract and then for any reason(the collateral has been compromised or prices are not correct) owner set this asset as not allowed for collateral then users would still be able to create debt for this collateral because `_increaseDeb()` has no check that the specified asset is allowed to use as collateral.

## Proof of Concept
This is `_increaseDebt()` and `increaseDebt()` code:
```
    function increaseDebt(
        address mintTo,
        ERC721 asset,
        uint256 amount,
        ReservoirOracleUnderwriter.OracleInfo calldata oracleInfo
    ) external override {
        _increaseDebt({account: msg.sender, asset: asset, mintTo: mintTo, amount: amount, oracleInfo: oracleInfo});
    }

    function _increaseDebt(
        address account,
        ERC721 asset,
        address mintTo,
        uint256 amount,
        ReservoirOracleUnderwriter.OracleInfo memory oracleInfo
    ) internal {
        uint256 cachedTarget = updateTarget();

        uint256 newDebt = _vaultInfo[account][asset].debt + amount;
        uint256 oraclePrice =
            underwritePriceForCollateral(asset, ReservoirOracleUnderwriter.PriceKind.LOWER, oracleInfo);

        uint256 max = _maxDebt(_vaultInfo[account][asset].count * oraclePrice, cachedTarget);

        if (newDebt > max) revert IPaprController.ExceedsMaxDebt(newDebt, max);

        if (newDebt >= 1 << 200) revert IPaprController.DebtAmountExceedsUint200();

        _vaultInfo[account][asset].debt = uint200(newDebt);
        PaprToken(address(papr)).mint(mintTo, amount);

        emit IncreaseDebt(account, asset, amount);
    }
```
As you can see there is no check that `asset` is currently is allowed to be used as collateral when creating a debt and code would create debt for unallowed assets if user added some collaterals for that asset in the past. these are the steps that the issues happens:
1. owner set NFT1 as allowed to be used as collateral by calling `setAllowedCollateral([(NFT1, true)])`.
2. attacker add 10 NFT1 as collateral.
3. after some times owner decides to not allow NFT1 as collateral for any reason (NFT1 collection has been compromised or price of the NFT1 is not updating or other issues...), owner would call `setAllowedCollateral([(NFT1, false)])`
4. attacker still can create debt for 10 NFT1 tokens by calling `increaseDebt()` because `increaseDebt()` has no check that specified collateral asset is currently is allowed.
5. NFT1 would be worthless and attacker debt would be unbacked.

by this issue attacker can mint debt for un allowed assets and probably there would be some unbacked debt because something bad was happening to those not allowed collaterals. code only don't let new collaterals get added to the protocol and it don't prevent debt creation for old collaterals that are added in the past.

## Tools Used
VIM

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
check collateral asset to see that if it is allowed before increasing debt.

