# Lines of code

https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/UniswapOracleFundingRateController.sol#L24-L27
https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/UniswapOracleFundingRateController.sol#L122-L129


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Currently the target mark ratio bounds are hardcoded as immutable, and are tied to specific time frame. Take the `fundingPeriod` of 4 weeks, with `targetMarkRatioMax` of 120% and `targetMarkRatioMin` of 80% respectively, which can be interpreted as, the upper and lower bounds for 4 weeks interest are +20%/-20%. But the same interest rate will have different value if using other time frame. For instance, 20% for 4 weeks, is equivalent to 240% for 48 weeks. As a result, when `fundingPeriod` changed, the original bounds will probably not be suitable for the new time frame. Then the $\frac{target}{mark}$ guard cannot serve the purpose of interest rate validation.



## Proof of Concept


Although target mark ratio bounds of `targetMarkRatioMax/targetMarkRatioMin` are hardcoded, `fundingPeriod` can be changed.
```solidity
File: src/UniswapOracleFundingRateController.sol
24:     /// @dev the max value of target / mark, used as a guard in _multiplier
25:     uint256 public immutable targetMarkRatioMax;
26:     /// @dev the min value of target / mark, used as a guard in _multiplier
27:     uint256 public immutable targetMarkRatioMin;


122:     function _setFundingPeriod(uint256 _fundingPeriod) internal {
123:         if (_fundingPeriod < 7 days) revert FundingPeriodTooShort();
124:         if (_fundingPeriod > 90 days) revert FundingPeriodTooLong();
125: 
126:         fundingPeriod = _fundingPeriod;
127: 
128:         emit SetFundingPeriod(_fundingPeriod);
129:     }
```

The issue is, after the change, the `targetMarkRatio` will not fit in the original time frame for the bounds.
```solidity
156:     function _multiplier(uint256 _mark_, uint256 cachedTarget) internal view returns (uint256) {

163:             targetMarkRatio = FixedPointMathLib.divWadDown(cachedTarget, _mark_);
164:             if (targetMarkRatio > targetMarkRatioMax) {
165:                 targetMarkRatio = targetMarkRatioMax;
166:             } else if (targetMarkRatio < targetMarkRatioMin) {
167:                 targetMarkRatio = targetMarkRatioMin;
168:             }
```

Imagine the upper and lower bounds for 4 weeks interest rate are +20%/-20%, `targetMarkRatioMax/targetMarkRatioMin` being 120%/80%. When the `fundingPeriod` is set to 1 week, if the $\frac{target}{mark}$ reach 120%/80%, the original 4 weeks interest rate become +80%/-80%, but the function `_multiplier()` bound check can still pass, although it is way off the original threshold. On the other hand, when the `fundingPeriod` is set to 12 weeks, if the $\frac{target}{mark}$ reach 120%/80%, the original 4 weeks interest rate become +6.67%/-6.67%, the function `_multiplier()` check will easily revert since the bounds are too strict.

## Tools Used
Manual analysis.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Instead of specifying the bound directly for $\frac{target}{mark}$, normalized bound value can be used, such as annualized rate. For example, assuming 360 days in 1 year, $\frac{target}{mark}$ of 101% with `fundingPeriod` of 1 month (30 days) will be the same for $\frac{target}{mark}$ of 100.33% with `fundingPeriod` of 10 days, both will normalized to 12% per year.
$$
\left( \frac{Target}{Mark} \right)^\frac{365}{fundingPeriod} = \left( 101\% \right)^{\frac{360}{30}} = \left( 100.33\% \right)^{\frac{360}{10}} = 112\% 
$$

In this way, the `fundingPeriod` change in time scale won't influence the bound for target mark ratio. It will also be easy to interpret.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/UniswapOracleFundingRateController.sol#L34-L42
https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/UniswapOracleFundingRateController.sol#L156-L173


# Vulnerability details


## Impact

In [whitepaper](https://backed.mirror.xyz/8SslPvU8of0h-fxoo6AybCpm51f30nd0qxPST8ep08c), the Risks section discusses some situation of negative interest rate. However, I still think allowing negative interest rate could do great harm to the protocol in extreme conditions, with on obvious benefits. The scenario is different from the case in the whitepaper. If market crashes, just like similar situation of LUNA/UST happened to Papr, it could be detrimental.


## Proof of Concept

The whitepaper's argument is, it will be hard to keep buying Papr to keep the price going up everlasting. This applies to the case Papr price keep increasing. And even $\frac{target}{mark} < 1$, the feedback loop has the tendency to bring mark price down. 

However, the issue is when market crash and lose confidence. When Papr price keep decreasing, the following could happen:
- on the sell side, the borrowers have incentives to keep borrowing and dump the borrowed Papr, the total supply will keep increasing, further depreciating Papr.
- on the buy side, there is much less incentive to buy Papr to repay the loan, since the interest rate is negative, it would be more profitable to wait for the price drop even more.
- once the interest rate goes negative, $\frac{target}{mark} < 1$, the next target will still decrease. So the feedback will enlarge itself, being a positive feedback loop, other than some negative feedback system to stabilize itself. 



Now there is no restriction on the `targetMarkRatioMax/targetMarkRatioMin`. And negative interest rate is allowed.
```solidity
File: src/UniswapOracleFundingRateController.sol
34:     constructor(ERC20 _underlying, ERC20 _papr, uint256 _targetMarkRatioMax, uint256 _targetMarkRatioMin) {
35:         underlying = _underlying;
36:         papr = _papr;
37: 
38:         targetMarkRatioMax = _targetMarkRatioMax;
39:         targetMarkRatioMin = _targetMarkRatioMin;
40: 
41:         _setFundingPeriod(4 weeks);
42:     }

156:     function _multiplier(uint256 _mark_, uint256 cachedTarget) internal view returns (uint256) {
157:         uint256 period = block.timestamp - _lastUpdated;
158:         uint256 periodRatio = FixedPointMathLib.divWadDown(period, fundingPeriod);
159:         uint256 targetMarkRatio;
160:         if (_mark_ == 0) {
161:             targetMarkRatio = targetMarkRatioMax;
162:         } else {
163:             targetMarkRatio = FixedPointMathLib.divWadDown(cachedTarget, _mark_);
164:             if (targetMarkRatio > targetMarkRatioMax) {
165:                 targetMarkRatio = targetMarkRatioMax;
166:             } else if (targetMarkRatio < targetMarkRatioMin) {
167:                 targetMarkRatio = targetMarkRatioMin;
168:             }
169:         }
170: 
171:         // safe to cast because targetMarkRatio > 0
172:         return uint256(FixedPointMathLib.powWad(int256(targetMarkRatio), int256(periodRatio)));
173:     }

```


When the target goes below markThe, interest rate goes negative, it will continue decrease. Because `newTarget` = `_multiplier()` * `oldTarget`, and `_multiplier()` is the negative interest rate forwarded, which will be less than 1.

```solidity
132:     function _newTarget(int24 latestTwapTick, uint256 cachedTarget) internal view returns (uint256) {
133:         return FixedPointMathLib.mulWadDown(cachedTarget, _multiplier(_mark(latestTwapTick), cachedTarget));
134:     }
```



## Tools Used
Manual analysis.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Negative interest rate is against common practice, and not necessary. From the bad side, it could lead to potential severe impact (Papr system crashes, just like LUNA/UST). On the good side, it does not seem have any obvious benefits.

The suggestion is to consider not allowing negative interest rate:
- it could be strict:
    only allow monotonic increase of `_target`.
- or could be moderate:
    by setting `targetMarkRatioMin` to some value slightly above 1. `_target` can still decrease, but the bottom line is it must be larger than mark price to keep the interest rate positive.


By disallowing negative interest rate, the above bad feedback loop won't exist. Because in this case, the target can serve as the defender for positive feedback:
- on the sell side, the amount borrowers can borrow is capped, so new supply into the market is limited. Even if market crashes, Papr will not fall to 0, but has some bounded total supply (temporary). And after the market resume normal, the system can recover gradually.
- on the buy side, due the positive interest rate, there is always some cost for the borrowers, so always some incentives to buy Papr to repay.
- the interest rate is always positive, $\frac{target}{mark} > 1$, the next target will always increase, the case discussed in the whitepaper applies, everlasting buyout is hard. So the feedback will not enlarge itself, it is a negative feedback system can self stabilize.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L264-L294
https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L532-L552


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
When liquidation happens and excess value is present, then protocol burns liquidation fees instead of holding them and later use.

## Proof of Concept
When user has a bad debt, then anyone can start auction for his nft. To purchase token, liquidator can call `purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT` function.
https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L264-L294
```solidity
    function purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT(
        Auction calldata auction,
        uint256 maxPrice,
        address sendTo,
        ReservoirOracleUnderwriter.OracleInfo calldata oracleInfo
    ) external override {
        uint256 collateralValueCached = underwritePriceForCollateral(
            auction.auctionAssetContract, ReservoirOracleUnderwriter.PriceKind.TWAP, oracleInfo
        ) * _vaultInfo[auction.nftOwner][auction.auctionAssetContract].count;
        bool isLastCollateral = collateralValueCached == 0;


        uint256 debtCached = _vaultInfo[auction.nftOwner][auction.auctionAssetContract].debt;
        uint256 maxDebtCached = isLastCollateral ? debtCached : _maxDebt(collateralValueCached, updateTarget());
        /// anything above what is needed to bring this vault under maxDebt is considered excess
        uint256 neededToSaveVault = maxDebtCached > debtCached ? 0 : debtCached - maxDebtCached;
        uint256 price = _purchaseNFTAndUpdateVaultIfNeeded(auction, maxPrice, sendTo);
        uint256 excess = price > neededToSaveVault ? price - neededToSaveVault : 0;
        uint256 remaining;


        if (excess > 0) {
            remaining = _handleExcess(excess, neededToSaveVault, debtCached, auction);
        } else {
            _reduceDebt(auction.nftOwner, auction.auctionAssetContract, address(this), price);
            remaining = debtCached - price;
        }


        if (isLastCollateral && remaining != 0) {
            /// there will be debt left with no NFTs, set it to 0
            _reduceDebtWithoutBurn(auction.nftOwner, auction.auctionAssetContract, remaining);
        }
    }
```

This function checks current debt of debtor. Then it purchases nft with current price and after that it checks if the price paid is bigger then the difference between debt and maxDebt. In case if it is bigger that means that protocol owes some funds(excess) to debtor and it should send them to him. It uses ` _handleExcess` function to send excess.

https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L532-L552
```solidity
    function _handleExcess(uint256 excess, uint256 neededToSaveVault, uint256 debtCached, Auction calldata auction)
        internal
        returns (uint256 remaining)
    {
        uint256 fee = excess * liquidationPenaltyBips / BIPS_ONE;
        uint256 credit = excess - fee;
        uint256 totalOwed = credit + neededToSaveVault;


        PaprToken(address(papr)).burn(address(this), fee);


        if (totalOwed > debtCached) {
            // we owe them more papr than they have in debt
            // so we pay down debt and send them the rest
            _reduceDebt(auction.nftOwner, auction.auctionAssetContract, address(this), debtCached);
            papr.transfer(auction.nftOwner, totalOwed - debtCached);
        } else {
            // reduce vault debt
            _reduceDebt(auction.nftOwner, auction.auctionAssetContract, address(this), totalOwed);
            remaining = debtCached - totalOwed;
        }
    }
```

As you can see protocol takes fee on excess value.
The problem is that protocol is burning that value using `PaprToken(address(papr)).burn(address(this), fee);`.
I believe that they should on contrary, collect fees to later use it.

## Tools Used
VsCode
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Do not burn protocol fees.

