# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Implementation in PaprController.sol does not match the documentation: excess fee during liquidation is burned instead of giving to the protocol

## Proof of Concept

From the whitepaper documentation of the project:

https://backed.mirror.xyz/8SslPvU8of0h-fxoo6AybCpm51f30nd0qxPST8ep08c

> The protocol is designed to be robust to liquidation shortfalls and values timely liquidations above covering all debt. The protocol does not wait for a minimum bid for the collateral, but instead sells collateral in exponential decay dutch auction.

> The collateral is sold for papr. Any papr amount greater than what is needed to bring a borrower under the max allowed debt threshold is considered excess. If there is excess papr, a liquidation fee is subtracted and given to the protocol (added to an insurance fund) and the remainder is used to further reduce the borrowers debt. If the excess debt exceeds a borrower’s total debt, what is left over is sent to the borrower.

note the doc:

> f there is excess papr, a liquidation fee is subtracted and given to the protocol (added to an insurance fund) 

However, in the code, when the function purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT in PaprController is called:

```solidity
/// @inheritdoc IPaprController
function purchaseLiquidationAuctionNFT(
	Auction calldata auction,
	uint256 maxPrice,
	address sendTo,
	ReservoirOracleUnderwriter.OracleInfo calldata oracleInfo
) external override {
```

which calls:

```solidity
uint256 excess = price > neededToSaveVault ? price - neededToSaveVault : 0;
uint256 remaining;

if (excess > 0) {
	remaining = _handleExcess(excess, neededToSaveVault, debtCached, auction);
} else {
	_reduceDebt(auction.nftOwner, auction.auctionAssetContract, address(this), price);
	remaining = debtCached - price;
}
```

which calls the function _handleExcess

```solidity
function _handleExcess(uint256 excess, uint256 neededToSaveVault, uint256 debtCached, Auction calldata auction)
	internal
	returns (uint256 remaining)
{
	uint256 fee = excess * liquidationPenaltyBips / BIPS_ONE;
	uint256 credit = excess - fee;
	uint256 totalOwed = credit + neededToSaveVault;

	PaprToken(address(papr)).burn(address(this), fee);

	if (totalOwed > debtCached) {
		// we owe them more papr than they have in debt
		// so we pay down debt and send them the rest
		_reduceDebt(auction.nftOwner, auction.auctionAssetContract, address(this), debtCached);
		papr.transfer(auction.nftOwner, totalOwed - debtCached);
	} else {
		// reduce vault debt
		_reduceDebt(auction.nftOwner, auction.auctionAssetContract, address(this), totalOwed);
		remaining = debtCached - totalOwed;
	}
}
```

note the section:

```solidity
uint256 fee = excess * liquidationPenaltyBips / BIPS_ONE;
uint256 credit = excess - fee;
uint256 totalOwed = credit + neededToSaveVault;

PaprToken(address(papr)).burn(address(this), fee);
```

According to the documentation, the fee should go to the protocol's insurance pool, but in the implementation, the fee is burned. 

The impact is that the protocol can have less fund in the insurance protocol to handle the unforeseen market change that require funds. 

## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

We recommend the project either transfer the fee to the protocol address in _handleExcess or update the documentation to make the implementation and the documentation match.

