# Vulnerability details

Buyout's start() now determine the price for buyout with the truncation to 1% of supply. When buyout initiator brings in the big enough amount of fractional tokens current formula can yield substantial mispricing of initiator's fractional tokens value, which will be cheaper as the ETH amount will correspond to the higher remaining share corresponding to what was actually supplied. This will allow an attacker to buy out the initiator shares at a discounted price, leading to losses for the initiator.

Setting the severity to be high as this is principal funds stealing impact with the only precondition of the fractional percentage supplied by buyout initiator which can happen frequently and constitutes a valid use case for the platform. 

## Proof of Concept

buyoutPrice is determined with the precision of 1 / 100 = 1%:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-07-fractional/blob/8f2697ae727c60c93ea47276f8fa128369abfe51/src/modules/Buyout.sol#L84-L88

```solidity
        uint256 buyoutPrice = (msg.value * 100) /
            (100 - ((depositAmount * 100) / totalSupply));
```

```solidity
        // Calculates price of buyout and fractions
        // @dev Reverts with division error if called with total supply of tokens
        uint256 buyoutPrice = (msg.value * 100) /
            (100 - ((depositAmount * 100) / totalSupply));
        uint256 fractionPrice = buyoutPrice / totalSupply;
```

This can lead to substantial losses for Bob the buyout proposer:

1. Bob owns 98.9% of Vault's totalSupply, which is 10000, and wants to buyout the 1 NFT in custody, which now has value of 10000 ETH (say it's a rare BAYC fast forward 10 years)

2. Bob wants to minimize ETH funds utilization and uses all his fractional tokens in start()

3. The amount Bob needs to provide is the current market value of the remaining part of fractional tokens supply, i.e. 1.1% * 10000 = 110 ETH

4. Bob's buyoutPrice will be (110e18 * 100) / (100 - ((9890 * 100) / 10000)) = 550 ETH

5. Bob's fractionPrice will be 550e18 / 10000 = 0.55 ETH

6. As start() completes, Alice can now run buyFractions() to buy Bob's fractional tokens, paying 0.55 * 9899 = 5444.45 for the whole Bob's 98.99% share

7. Now Alice can wait for the auction to end, the buyout will not be successful as Alice took out the tokens from Vault balance with buyFractions()

8. Then Alice can initiate another buyout, running start with 98% of tokens to avoid the precision pitfall Bob experienced. She needs to supply ETH value of the remaining 2%

9. Alice's buyoutPrice will be (200e18 * 100) / (100 - ((9800 * 100) / 10000)) = 10000 ETH

10. Alice's fractionPrice will be 10000e18 / 10000 = 1 ETH

This way Alice buyout will either succeed or someone else would buy her fractional tokens at even higher valuation. Either way her payoff will be at least 1 ETH per fractional token, yielding 0.45 ETH per token profit. Observing such a possibility Alice can now make a bot that immediately back runs start() transaction similar to Bob's, so initiator will not have the chance to correct the mispricing when observing the buyoutPrice realized.


Notice that the similar approach is used in Migration's commit() as proposal.targetPrice utilize the same formula with `_scalar = 100`:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-07-fractional/blob/8f2697ae727c60c93ea47276f8fa128369abfe51/src/modules/Migration.sol#L197-L213

```solidity
        // Calculates current price of the proposal based on total supply
        uint256 currentPrice = _calculateTotal(
            100,
            IVaultRegistry(registry).totalSupply(_vault),
            proposal.totalEth,
            proposal.totalFractions
        );

        // Checks if the current price is greater than target price of the proposal
        if (currentPrice > proposal.targetPrice) {
            // Sets token approval to the buyout contract
            IFERC1155(token).setApprovalFor(address(buyout), id, true);
            // Starts the buyout process
            IBuyout(buyout).start{value: proposal.totalEth}(_vault);
            proposal.isCommited = true;
            started = true;
        }
```

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-07-fractional/blob/8f2697ae727c60c93ea47276f8fa128369abfe51/src/modules/Migration.sol#L539-L548

```solidity
    function _calculateTotal(
        uint256 _scalar,
        uint256 _lastTotalSupply,
        uint256 _totalEth,
        uint256 _totalFractions
    ) private pure returns (uint256) {
        return
            (_totalEth * _scalar) /
            (_scalar - ((_totalFractions * _scalar) / _lastTotalSupply));
    }
```

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Consider adding more precision to the buyoutPrice formula, for example:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-07-fractional/blob/8f2697ae727c60c93ea47276f8fa128369abfe51/src/modules/Buyout.sol#L86-L87

```solidity
+       // @notice Fraction multiplier
+       uint256 public constant FRACTION_MULTI = 1e18;
        ...
+       uint256 buyoutPrice = (msg.value * FRACTION_MULTI) /
+           (FRACTION_MULTI - ((depositAmount * FRACTION_MULTI) / totalSupply));

-       uint256 buyoutPrice = (msg.value * 100) /
-           (100 - ((depositAmount * 100) / totalSupply));
```

Notice that the approach is already used in migrateFractions:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-07-fractional/blob/8f2697ae727c60c93ea47276f8fa128369abfe51/src/modules/Migration.sol#L450-L456

```solidity
        // Calculates the total ether amount of a successful proposal
        uint256 totalInEth = _calculateTotal(
            1 ether,
            lastTotalSupply,
            migrationInfo[_vault][_proposalId].totalEth,
            migrationInfo[_vault][_proposalId].totalFractions
        );
```

This way introducing the multiplier to Buyout's start() and Migration's commit() also unify the calculations across the system.

