# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The `getUnderlyingPrice ` function in the contract Oracle.sol fetches the `answer` directly from a Chainlink aggregator using the `latestRoundData` function. There is no check if the return value is > 0 or indicates stale data. This could lead to incorrect or stale prices from a function that is called frequently from other internal functions.

## Proof of Concept
https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-02-hubble/blob/ed1d885d5dbc2eae24e43c3ecbf291a0f5a52765/contracts/Oracle.sol#L24-L35

```
    function getUnderlyingPrice(address underlying)
        virtual
        external
        view
        returns(int256 answer)
    {
        if (stablePrice[underlying] != 0) {
            return stablePrice[underlying];
        }
        (,answer,,,) = AggregatorV3Interface(chainLinkAggregatorMap[underlying]).latestRoundData();
        answer /= 100;
    }
```

## Tools Used
Visual Studio Code, Remix

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
I recommend calling the internal function `getLatestRoundData` instead to handle incorrect or stale data.

```
    function getUnderlyingPrice(address underlying)
        virtual
        external
        view
        returns(int256 answer)
    {
        if (stablePrice[underlying] != 0) {
            return stablePrice[underlying];
        }

        AggregatorV3Interface aggregator = AggregatorV3Interface(chainLinkAggregatorMap[underlying]);
        requireNonEmptyAddress(address(aggregator));

        (,uint256 answer256,) = getLatestRoundData(aggregator);
        answer = (answer256 / 100).toInt256();
    }

```

