# Vulnerability details

## Impact
This will prevent all future calls of `LPDA.buy()` or `LPDA.refund()` meaning that no funds can be retrieved by any actor from the sale.

The `getPrice()` function returns the current price per NFT in a LPDA sale, if sale variables become too big it is possible for this function call to revert. This is possible because there are no maximum value checks in place on the value sale.dropPerSecond or for the maximum time elapsed over the sale (i.e. sale.endTime - sale.startTime).

 If this happens line 124 of `getPrice()` will revert due to integer overflow. Either of these values being too large is unlikely to be noticed until it is too late as initial early calls to getPrice() may succeed, this increases the likelihood that funds will get stuck after buyers have already interacted with the sale.

## Proof of Concept
Forge test: (sorry I cannot get this to format correctly in markdown, if copy pasted into a solidity IDE it should work)
function test_Overflow() public {
        LPDA.sale badLpdaSale = LPDA.Sale({
            currentId: uint48(0),
            finalId: uint48(10),
            edition: address(edition),
            startPrice: uint80(uint256(1 ether)),
            finalPrice: uint80(uint256(0.1 ether)),
            //dropPerSecond is accidentally set too large
            dropPerSecond: uint80(uint256(0.1 ether)),
            startTime: uint96(block.timestamp),
            saleReceiver: payable(address(69)),
            endTime: uint96(block.timestamp + 1 days)
        });

        // make the lpda sales contract
        badSale = LPDA(lpdaSales.createLPDASale(badLpdaSale));
        // authorize the lpda sale to mint tokens
        edition.grantRole(edition.MINTER_ROLE(), address(badSale));
        //lets buy an NFT
        badSale.buy{value: 1 ether}(1);
        vm.warp(block.timestamp + 20);
        //after time has passed further buy attempts will fail
        vm.expectRevert();
        badSale.buy{value: 1 ether}(1);
        //requests for a refund will also fail
        vm.expectRevert();
        badSale.refund();
    }

## Tools Used
Manual analysis

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Add initial value check for `sale.dropPerSecond` and the time delta between `sale.endTime` and `sale.startTime` like so in `LPDAFactory.createLPDASale()`

`require(sale.dropPerSecond * (sale.endTime - sale.startTime) > sale.startPrice, "INVALID PARAMS");`
This can be injected on line 37 before the cloning of the implementation has occured. It will ensure the largest reduction in price cannot exceed the `sale.startPrice` preventing the overflow revert.

