# Vulnerability details

## Impact
```LDPA``` sale is automatically finalized in ```buy``` when the ```finalId``` is reached:
```solidity
  if (newId == temp.finalId) {
      sale.finalPrice = uint80(price);
      uint256 totalSale = price * amountSold;
      uint256 fee = totalSale / 20;
      ISaleFactory(factory).feeReceiver().transfer(fee);
      temp.saleReceiver.transfer(totalSale - fee);
      _end();
  }
```
However, it does not prevent from finalizing it again if ```buy``` is invoked again with the ```_amount``` of 0. This will skip all the minting, etc, and will send the fees and sale funds again. The remaining funds were meant for the early buyers to refund the final price difference but can be drained by an owner.

## Proof of Concept

PoC:
1. ```LDPA``` sale is created to sell 5 NFTs. Starting price is 1 ETH.
1. User A buys 1 NFT for 1 ETH.
2. User B buys the remaining 4 NFTs for 0.4 ETH ea.
3. The sale is automatically finalized and the final price is set to 0.4 ETH. ```totalSale``` 0.4 x 5 = 2 ETH. The fee recipient receives 5% (0.1 ETH) and the owner receives 1.9 ETH. The remaining 0.6 ETH is left for refunds (user A).
4. The owner force sends 1.4 ETH to the contract and calls the finalization again. The fee recipient receives 5% (0.1 ETH) and the owner receives 1.9 ETH. No ETH is left for refunds.
5. The owner profits. User A can't refund anymore.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
A simple solution would be to require an amount > 0 when buying.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/main/src/minters/OpenEdition.sol#L122
https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/main/src/minters/FixedPrice.sol#L110


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Once the sale ends, ```OpenEdition``` and ```FixedPrice``` contracts are destructed:
```solidity
  function _end(Sale memory _sale) internal {
      emit End(_sale);
      selfdestruct(_sale.saleReceiver);
  }
```

The problem is that even after the destruction, the contract will remain and accept ETH but not perform its functions. So any pending transactions that come after will get their funds stuck forever.

Citing from: https://ethereum.stackexchange.com/a/83580/17387
"As you have discovered on your own, calling the non-existent function in the non-existent contract returns true because there is nothing to do."

In a blockchain environment, the users can't predict 100% when their txs will be executed, thus it is very likely that some users will come when it is already too late. E.g. in a ```FixedPrice``` a sale is automatically finalized once the ```finalId``` is reached. If more than one user is competing for that final spot, those who got later would lose their ETH because the contract would be destructed. Also, there could be many pending txs with low gas prices that eventually were mined but again it would be too late.

## Proof of Concept
PoC:

```solidity
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.17;

contract OpenEdition {

  event End();

  event Buy(address indexed _buyer, uint256 _amount, uint256 _value);

    function buy(uint256 _amount) external payable {
        require(msg.value == _amount);

        uint24 amount = uint24(_amount);

        emit Buy(msg.sender, amount, msg.value);
    }

    function _end() public {
        emit End();
        selfdestruct(payable(msg.sender));
    }
}

contract Test {

    function test(address _openEdition) external payable {
        OpenEdition(_openEdition).buy{value: msg.value / 2}(msg.value / 2);

        OpenEdition(_openEdition)._end();

        OpenEdition(_openEdition).buy{value: msg.value / 2}(msg.value / 2);
    }
}
```
You can execute it and see that the ```test``` function succeeds even after the sale contract is destroyed.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
A simple solution would be not to destroy the contract, just don't accept any new txs after the end time.

