# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Given the current setup of `getPrice()`, depending on the configuration of `startPrice` and `dropPerScond` calling the function can unexpectedly underflow and cause all other functions to revert such as `buy()` and `refund()`.

When making a purchase, the auction will revert with math underflow rather than a specified requirement. Critically, the time at which the auction underflows is not only-relative to a single value and is dependent on the entire auction configuration which means the place of error is not consistent across configurations.

The Onus of confirming an auction can run may move between parties, however this nuance results in auctions bricking unexpectedly.

## Proof of Concept

In the current test, the configuration is:

```
        lpdaSale = LPDA.Sale({
            currentId: uint48(0),
            finalId: uint48(10),
            edition: address(edition),
            startPrice: uint80(uint256(1 ether)),
            finalPrice: uint80(uint256(0.1 ether)),
            dropPerSecond: uint80(uint256(0.1 ether) / 1 days),
            startTime: uint96(block.timestamp),
            saleReceiver: payable(address(69)),
            endTime: uint96(block.timestamp + 1 days)
        });
```

The implementation results in a `getPrice()` that has the calculation of:

$(1 eth) - (.1 eth / 1 days) * seconds passed$

 $1000000000000000000 - (1157407407407 * n)$

Given this setup, the time to underflow is quite long.

However, if we just adjust things slightly, rate moves by a large degree and errors come far sooner.

$(50 eth) - (25 eth / 1 days) * seconds passed$

 $500000000000000000000 - (289351851851851 * n)$

Seconds passed will forever grow. Therefore, regardless of the exact specification of the LPDA, underflow is not a matter of if, but when. Due to this underflow, given sufficient time, all auctions will eventually become locked and result in individuals that are both incapable of buying and claiming their refund.

The owner of the contract also does not have the ability to save it from this state as `cancel()` cannot be called even if `endTime` has come. Additionally, even if it could be called after `endTime` the finalPrice of the `sale` in memory is not updated; thus keeping all functions bricked.

## Tools Used

- Hardhat
- Wolfram Alpha

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Maintaining the existing model, one can accomplish this by simply using `lowestPrice()` when `block.timestamp > endTime`. While incorrect configuration could steal lead to a state of bricking, it would be less expected while gaining the ability to shift the onus of confirming proper configuration.

Additionally and/or alternatively, checks preventing a time of running longer than the revert may be prevented. However, this would require a bigger model change.

Below an example of a method of mitigation is included.

At the time of configuration, a `require` to confirm sufficient configuration is possible in $O(1)$. This check, effectively removes the configuration of auctions that *may* underflow.

To accomplish this, the `createLPDASale()` in `LPDAFactory.sol` goes from:

```
    /// @notice create a fixed sale proxy contract
    /// @param sale the sale info
    function createLPDASale(LPDA.Sale calldata sale) external returns (address clone) {
        require(IEscher721(sale.edition).hasRole(bytes32(0x00), msg.sender), "NOT AUTHORIZED");
        require(sale.saleReceiver != address(0), "INVALID SALE RECEIVER");
        require(sale.startTime >= block.timestamp, "INVALID START TIME");
        require(sale.endTime > sale.startTime, "INVALID END TIME");
        require(sale.finalId > sale.currentId, "INVALID FINAL ID");
        require(sale.startPrice > 0, "INVALID START PRICE");
        require(sale.dropPerSecond > 0, "INVALID DROP PER SECOND");

        clone = implementation.clone();
        LPDA(clone).initialize(sale);

        emit NewLPDAContract(msg.sender, sale.edition, clone, sale);
    }
```

to become:

```
    /// @notice create a fixed sale proxy contract
    /// @param sale the sale info
    function createLPDASale(LPDA.Sale calldata sale) external returns (address clone) {
        require(IEscher721(sale.edition).hasRole(bytes32(0x00), msg.sender), "NOT AUTHORIZED");
        require(sale.saleReceiver != address(0), "INVALID SALE RECEIVER");
        require(sale.startTime >= block.timestamp, "INVALID START TIME");
        require(sale.endTime > sale.startTime, "INVALID END TIME");
        require(sale.finalId > sale.currentId, "INVALID FINAL ID");
        require(sale.startPrice > 0, "INVALID START PRICE");
        require(sale.dropPerSecond > 0, "INVALID DROP PER SECOND");
        require(sale.dropPerSecond * (sale.endTime - sale.startTime) <= sale.startPrice, "INVALID AUCTION");

        clone = implementation.clone();
        LPDA(clone).initialize(sale);

        emit NewLPDAContract(msg.sender, sale.edition, clone, sale);
    }
```

