## Impact

All user refunds can be stolen

## Proof of Concept

    function buy(uint256 _amount) external payable {
        uint48 amount = uint48(_amount);
        Sale memory temp = sale;
        IEscher721 nft = IEscher721(temp.edition);
        require(block.timestamp >= temp.startTime, "TOO SOON");
        uint256 price = getPrice();

        @audit amount = 0 so msg.value = 0 and this will pass
        require(msg.value >= amount * price, "WRONG PRICE");

        amountSold += amount;
        uint48 newId = amount + temp.currentId;

        @audit _amount = 0 so newId = temp.currentId so this will pass
        require(newId <= temp.finalId, "TOO MANY");

        receipts[msg.sender].amount += amount;
        receipts[msg.sender].balance += uint80(msg.value);

        @audit nothing will be minted here because newId == temp.currentId
        for (uint256 x = temp.currentId + 1; x <= newId; x++) {
            nft.mint(msg.sender, x);
        }

        sale.currentId = newId;

        emit Buy(msg.sender, amount, msg.value, temp);

        @audit this code will be run again and distribute twice
        if (newId == temp.finalId) {
            sale.finalPrice = uint80(price);
            uint256 totalSale = price * amountSold;
            uint256 fee = totalSale / 20;
            ISaleFactory(factory).feeReceiver().transfer(fee);
            temp.saleReceiver.transfer(totalSale - fee);
            _end();
        }
    }

When an auction is sold out, the funds are automatically distributed to `feeReceiver` and `factory`. Since the contract is dutch auction there is almost guaranteed to be additional funds in the contract. After the auction is sold out `buy` can still be called with `_amount = 0`. This call will pass all require statements and cause the contract to attempt to pay the `feeReceiver` and `factory` again. If there are enough funds in the contract then it will pay them again, stealing refunds from bidders.

