# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/LPDA.sol#L71


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In function `LPDA.buy()`, it casted `msg.value` that users sent to `uint80` before updating receipt.

```solidity
receipts[msg.sender].amount += amount;
receipts[msg.sender].balance += uint80(msg.value); 
// @audit amount * decimals = uint48 * uint64 = uint100 already
```

In case `msg.value` is larger than uint80, it can cause overflow and receipts of sender will be updated with less amount than expected, result in loss of funds.

Note that, with Ethereum, it can happen with small chance. However, if there is another EVM network where native token has larger decimals, the chance of overflow will be much higher.

## Proof of Concept

```solidity
struct Sale {
    // slot 1
    uint48 currentId;
    uint48 finalId;
    address edition;
    // slot 2
    uint80 startPrice;
    uint80 finalPrice;
    uint80 dropPerSecond;
    // slot 3
    uint96 endTime;
    address payable saleReceiver;
    // slot 4
    uint96 startTime;
}

struct Receipt {
    uint48 amount;
    uint80 balance;
}
```

In LPDA, `amount`, `currentId` and `finalId` is `uint48`. And `decimals` of ETH is 10^18 which is fit to `uint64`. 

So even when did not accounted for `price`. With `amount` is `uint48` and `decimals` is `uint64`, the result could be `uint100` already, higher than `uint80`. 


