# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/FixedPrice.sol#L62


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In function `FixedPrice.buy()`, it used unsafe casting to cast the amount from `uint256` to `uint48`. However, unlike Open Edition where this unsafe cast is done first in `buy()` function:
```solidity
function buy(uint256 _amount) external payable {
    uint24 amount = uint24(_amount);
    Sale memory temp = sale;
    IEscher721 nft = IEscher721(temp.edition);
    ...
}
```

In `FixedPrice.buy()`, it does the unsafe casting after checking `msg.value` that user sent. If users are not aware of this and send the amount of ETH enough to buy `_amount` token, they will lose ETH and only get the amount of token after overflow casting.
```solidity
function buy(uint256 _amount) external payable {
    Sale memory sale_ = sale;
    IEscher721 nft = IEscher721(sale_.edition);
    require(block.timestamp >= sale_.startTime, "TOO SOON");
    require(_amount * sale_.price == msg.value, "WRONG PRICE");
    // @audit unsafe cast _amount, user might send more ETH
    uint48 newId = uint48(_amount) + sale_.currentId; 
    require(newId <= sale_.finalId, "TOO MANY");

    for (uint48 x = sale_.currentId + 1; x <= newId; x++) {
        nft.mint(msg.sender, x);
    }

    sale.currentId = newId;

    emit Buy(msg.sender, _amount, msg.value, sale);

    if (newId == sale_.finalId) _end(sale);
}
```


## Proof of Concept
Consider the scenario with FixedPrice sale
1. Alice call `FixedPrice.buy(type(uint48).max + 1)` and sent along the amount of ETH enough to buy `type(uint48).max + 1` NFT
2. Expected behaviour in this case should be reverting the transaction. However the check for price `require(_amount * sale_.price == msg.value, "WRONG PRICE");` is still passed. Then `_amount` is cast to uint48, which is overflow and become `1`. 
3. Transaction is not reverted, Alice is minted 1 NFT and losing all ETH enough to buy `2^48` NFT


