# Vulnerability details

## Impact
If there is not sufficient public interest during an `LPDA` sale, the creator needs to mint the remaining tokens himself to get access to the funds collected by previous mints for which he will also need to pay 5% fee. In the worst case scenario, the creator does not have enough capital to finish the auction and all funds paid for mints will be locked in the contract.

## Proof of Concept
In the `LPDA` contract the only way to retrieve native tokens (e.g. ether) after the sale has started is to reach the final mint in `LPDA.buy`:

```js
if (newId == temp.finalId) {
    sale.finalPrice = uint80(price);
    uint256 totalSale = price * amountSold;
    uint256 fee = totalSale / 20;
    ISaleFactory(factory).feeReceiver().transfer(fee);
    temp.saleReceiver.transfer(totalSale - fee);
    _end();
}
```

Assuming that the mint has not gained nearly as much traction as the creator has accounted for, he needs to call the `LPDA.buy` himself until the finalId has been minted. This can be quite costly depending on the remaining amount and the value of `LPDA.lowestPrice`, considering he also has to pay the 5% fee. 


## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Enable the creator to cancel the sale and retrieve the value of mints after `sale.endTime` (maybe add a grace period in which users can mint for the lowest price).

