# Vulnerability details


## Impact
The FixedPrice / LPDA sale may be hard or never be able to `_end()`.
All ETHs sent by buyers in the minter contract would be locked.

## Proof of Concept
The minter [FixedPrice.sol](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/main/src/minters/FixedPrice.sol) or [LPDA.sol](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/main/src/minters/LPDA.sol#L81-L88) can be ended if:
1. owner calls `cancel()` before `startTime`
2. all the NFTs have been sold out:
  - [FixedPrice.sol#L73](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/FixedPrice.sol#L73)
  - [LPDA.sol#L81-L88](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/LPDA.sol#L81-L88)

All ETHs in the contract will be released to the seller and the fee receiver only when it is ended.

If the contract is initialized with a **VERY BIG finalId** (for example, accidentally fill in the wrong value), it would make the amount of NFTs being sold too BIG to sold out.
But, after the `startTime`, the sale can only be `_end()`ed when all NFTs are sold out.

Finally, this improperly configured FixedPrice sale will never end and ETHs in it is locked.

## Tools Used
VS Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Two options to consider:
1. Validate Sale parameters, `require(finalId - currentId <= MAX_AMOUNT, ..)` where `MAX_AMOUNT` is a fixed limit number, like 100000.
2. Allows owner to adjust `finalId`:
```solidity
