# Vulnerability details

## Impact
If an artist carelessly sets a sale with `sale.price = 0`, anybody can get any number of the NFTs without paying anthing. 

## Proof of Concept

In the below test case, a user can buy `1000000` NFTs with `0 ether`:

```solidity
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.17;

import {EscherTest} from "./utils/EscherTest.sol";
import {OpenEditionFactory, OpenEdition} from "src/minters/OpenEditionFactory.sol";

contract OpenEditionZeroPriceTest is EscherTest {
    OpenEditionFactory public openSales;
    OpenEdition.Sale public openSale;
    OpenEdition public sale;

    function test_ZeroPrice_Buy() public {
        super.setUp();
        openSales = new OpenEditionFactory();
        openSale = OpenEdition.Sale({
            price: uint72(uint256(0 ether)),
            currentId: uint24(1),
            edition: address(edition),
            startTime: uint96(block.timestamp),
            saleReceiver: payable(address(69)),
            endTime: uint96(block.timestamp + 10 days)
        });

        sale = OpenEdition(openSales.createOpenEdition(openSale));
        // authorize the fixed price sale to mint tokens
        edition.grantRole(edition.MINTER_ROLE(), address(sale));
        // buy any number of NFTs with 0 ether
        sale.buy{value: 0 ether}(1000000);
        assertEq(address(sale).balance, 0 ether);
        assertEq(edition.balanceOf(address(this)), 1000000);
    }
}

```


## Tools Used
manual audit

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Validating that the sale price `sale.price` is larger than 0.

