# Vulnerability details

## Impact
In all types of sales, funds can be claimed only after sale end. However, there can be several cases in which a sale does not end and therefore seller never receives his funds.

## Proof of Concept
### Case 1:
`FixedPrice` and `LPDA` sale is ended when `finalId` is reached. There might be a case where seller specifies too large `finalId` or there are not enough interested buyers for it to be reached.

[FixedPrice.sol](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/FixedPrice.sol#L73)
```
if (newId == sale_.finalId) _end(sale);
```
[LPDA.sol](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/LPDA.sol#L81-L88)
```
if (newId == temp.finalId) {
            sale.finalPrice = uint80(price);
            uint256 totalSale = price * amountSold;
            uint256 fee = totalSale / 20;
            ISaleFactory(factory).feeReceiver().transfer(fee);
            temp.saleReceiver.transfer(totalSale - fee);
            _end();
}
```

### Case 2:
`OpenEdition` sale ends when `endTime` is reached. There might be a case where seller sets the `endTime` too far in the future.

[OpenEdition.sol](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/OpenEdition.sol#L91-L93)
```
require(block.timestamp >= temp.endTime, "TOO SOON");
ISaleFactory(factory).feeReceiver().transfer(address(this).balance / 20);
_end(temp);
```

### Case 3:
There is a possibility for `currentId` to clash with already minted token ID, which is a separate issue I included in "`currentId` clash with already minted token ID breaks `buy` function and can result in loss of funds". But it also effects sale not being able to end and funds being stuck, so I want to point it out here.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
In `FixedPrice` and `OpenEdition` sales you could transfer the funds to seller immediately at the time of buying or add a `payout` function that can be called any time to transfer the funds that were collected so far.
```
function payout() external onlyOwner {
        uint246 bal = address(this).balance;
        uint256 fee = bal / 20;
	(bool paidFee, ) = ISaleFactory(factory).feeReceiver().call{value: fee}("");
	require(paidFee "Failed to pay fee");
	(bool paid, ) = sale.saleReceiver.call{value: bal - fee }(""); 
	require(paid, "Failed to pay");
}
```

In `LPDA` you can add `payout` function that can be called to withdraw what was sold so far. In such case you would have to also track how much was paid out.

```
uint public totalPaidOut;
function payout() external onlyOwner {
	uint total = (sale.finalPrice * amountSold);
	uint toPay = total - totalPaidOut;
	require(toPay != 0, "Nothing to pay");
	totalPaidOut = total;
	uint256 fee = toPay / 20;
      (bool paidFee, ) = ISaleFactory(factory).feeReceiver().call{value: fee}("");
	require(paidFee, "Failed to pay fee");
	(bool paidout, ) = sale.saleReceiver.call{value: toPay - fee}(""); 
	require(paidout, "Failed to pay");
}
```
Notice: If you are implementing `payout` function, remove the current way of paying out from `LPDA`.

The above recommendations only fixes the issue of not being able to withdraw funds, but sales can still be stuck (not being able to end). To also try to minimize that, you can limit how long a sale can last. You can do that by introducing constants like `MAX_SALE_DURATION` and adding a check like this `require(sale.startTime - sale.endTime <= MAX_SALE_DURATION, "Too long");` when creating a sale.

