# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Note that this attack is currently not possible due to the use of now depreciated `transfer()` that incidentally protects the protocol from this reentrant attack. I still report it as a medium as protocol should not be using `transfer()` to transfer funds anymore as gas cost changes frequently in the EVM and can break this function. This is also an extremely common vulnerability and is also part of the publicly known issue, and it is very likely that after this audit, the protocol fix this by using `call()` instead to `transfer()` which opens it up to this vulnerability.

Though, when it happens, impact is severe. A malicious `feeReceiver()` will be able to steal all funds (almost) from the `saleReceiver()` by repeatedly taking 5% of the remaining amount each reentrant call.

## Proof of Concept
We first take a look at `_end()` to understand how this attack works.
This should only be called **once** after every NFTs are sold out and transfer the funds to its respective addresses. We notice that 5% of the funds are first transferred to `feeReceiver()` , before the remaining is transferred to `saleReceiver()` with `selfdestruct()`.

[FixedPrice.sol#L107-112](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/FixedPrice.sol#L107-L112)
```solidity
    function _end(Sale memory _sale) internal {
        emit End(_sale);
        ISaleFactory(factory).feeReceiver().transfer(address(this).balance / 20);
        selfdestruct(_sale.saleReceiver);
    }
```
We now take a look at `buy()` which triggers `_end()`. We observe that passing in `_amount = 0` will successfully run through the function and triggers `end()` if NFTs are all sold out.

[FixedPrice.sol#L57-L74)](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-escher/blob/5d8be6aa0e8634fdb2f328b99076b0d05fefab73/src/minters/FixedPrice.sol#L57-L74)
```solidity
    function buy(uint256 _amount) external payable {
        Sale memory sale_ = sale;
        IEscher721 nft = IEscher721(sale_.edition);
        require(block.timestamp >= sale_.startTime, "TOO SOON");
        require(_amount * sale_.price == msg.value, "WRONG PRICE");
        uint48 newId = uint48(_amount) + sale_.currentId;
        require(newId <= sale_.finalId, "TOO MANY");

        for (uint48 x = sale_.currentId + 1; x <= newId; x++) {
            nft.mint(msg.sender, x);
        }

        sale.currentId = newId;

        emit Buy(msg.sender, _amount, msg.value, sale);

        if (newId == sale_.finalId) _end(sale);
    }
```
So what a malicious `feeReceiver()` can do is, once the fixed sale is reaching the end, they can buy up the remaining amount of NFTs to trigger `end()`. Control is passed over once fees are transferred to `feeReceiver()` and it can reenter with `FixedPrice.buy(_amount = 0)` and take 5% of the remaining balance each time, and drains almost all of the funds in the contract.

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
I recommend to add the check `require(_amount != 0)`. This will prevent the infinite `buy()` calls that is possible and it will only run once, once all NFTs are sold out.

