# Vulnerability details

## Impact
When a new sale contract (FixedPrice, LDPA, OpenEdition) is created the factory contracts don't check whether the caller is actually a registered Escher creator. The factory contract's events will likely be used to collect existing sales to display on the marketplace's frontend or on other third-party aggregation tools. That allows an attacker to inject NFT sales into the marketplace that don't belong to the Escher ecosystem which is supposed to be:
>  a decentralized curated marketplace for editioned artwork

## Proof of Concept
Factory contracts only check whether the caller has the `DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE` for the NFT contract:
```sol
    function createOpenEdition(OpenEdition.Sale calldata sale) external returns (address clone) {
        require(IEscher721(sale.edition).hasRole(bytes32(0x00), msg.sender), "NOT AUTHORIZED");
        // ...
    }
    function createLPDASale(LPDA.Sale calldata sale) external returns (address clone) {
        require(IEscher721(sale.edition).hasRole(bytes32(0x00), msg.sender), "NOT AUTHORIZED");
        // ...
    }
    function createFixedSale(FixedPrice.Sale calldata _sale) external returns (address clone) {
        require(IEscher721(_sale.edition).hasRole(bytes32(0x00), msg.sender), "NOT AUTHORIZED");
        // ...
    }
```
## Tools Used
none

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
There's a central register for Escher creators in the `Escher` contract. You can use that to verify that the caller is actually a legitimate creator.

