# Vulnerability details

### Impact

With the change made the `OfferInfo` struct in RubiconMarket.sol, it is now possible to create `Offers` which will revert when attempts to buy or cancel the offers are made. This is problematic if the offer becomes the result of the  `RubiconMarket::getBestOffer()`, as now all functions attempting to purchase the malformed best offer will revert. This is made worse by the fact that the malicious order cannot be removed from the order book. The affected best-offer-purchasing functions are `sellAllAmount()` and `buyAllAmount()`. These functions are utilized by the `Position.sol` contract.  

### Cause

The RubiconMarket contract has the following function signature which allows the user to specify the address of the owner and recipient: `function offer(uint256 pay_amt, ERC20 pay_gem, uint256 buy_amt, ERC20 buy_gem, uint256 pos, bool matching, address owner, address recipient) public can_offer returns (uint256)`. This endpoint is problematic as it allows for both the owner and recipient addresses to be the zero address. 

It is common for ERC20s to revert on attempts to transfer funds to the zero address. For example, the OpenZeppelin’s boilerplate ERC20 contract does so: [https://github.com/OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts/blob/f2346b6749546707af883e2c0acbc8a487e16ae4/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol#L223-L224](https://github.com/OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts/blob/f2346b6749546707af883e2c0acbc8a487e16ae4/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol#L223-L224) 

In order to be able to buy or cancel an order, tokens must be able to be transferred back to the owner or recipient:

```solidity
// RubiconMarket Contract
function cancel(
        uint256 id
    ) public virtual can_cancel(id) synchronized returns (bool success) {
	<... snip>
        _offer.owner == address(0) && msg.sender == _offer.recipient
            ? require(_offer.pay_gem.transfer(_offer.recipient, _offer.pay_amt))
            : require(_offer.pay_gem.transfer(_offer.owner, _offer.pay_amt));
	<... snip>
}

function buy(
        uint256 id,
        uint256 quantity
    ) public virtual can_buy(id) synchronized returns (bool) {
     <... snip>
			require(
            _offer.buy_gem.transferFrom(msg.sender, _offer.recipient, spend),
            "_offer.buy_gem.transferFrom(msg.sender, _offer.recipient, spend) failed - check that you can pay the fee"
        );
    <... snip>
}
```

Both of these function will fail to run as they will revert when the transfers are attempted to be made. Additionally, there is no way to remove these malformed orders from the sorted list of offers as `RubiconMarket` does not have that functionality. 

### Remediations to Consider:

- Do not allow an offer’s owner or recipient addresses to be zero.

