# Vulnerability details

## Description
When swapping using strategy functions i.e. `ZetaTokenConsumerPancakeV3` and others, `exactInputSingle` is called to execute the swap.
The issue here stems from `minAmountOut` which is assigned to `ExactInputSingleParams.amountOutMinimum`.
```solidity
struct ExactInputSingleParams {
        address tokenIn;
        address tokenOut;
        uint24 fee;
        address recipient;
        uint256 amountIn;
        uint256 amountOutMinimum;
        uint160 sqrtPriceLimitX96;
    }
```

## Impact
This parameter here is determined by user to specify a minimum amount for the output token for the swap to be executed. Not pre-calculating the value creates an attack vector as being vulnerable to getting an **unusually** bad price due to a *front running sandwich* or another type of price manipulation for user.

**Example cases would be:**
* User specifies the `minAmountOut` with not exact decimals will create a potential risk for price manipulation.

* User specifies the `minAmountOut` at any price not tolerating little slippage will cause the swap to simply revert. 


## Tools Used
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
To resolve the issue I suggest adjusting `amountOutMinimum` utilizing an on-chain price oracle and ensuring swap won't fail if the output amount is slightly lower than expected due to slippage.

*It is highly recommended to **not use** UniswapV3 oracle since it has [statistical vulnerabilites](https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/statistical-vulnerabilities-in-uniswap-v3-price-oracle/viewer).*


