# Vulnerability details

## Impact
This vulnerability leads to hijacking of a virtual account's all ERC20 tokens and all ether inside of it.

If an attacker creates a root bridge agent with a malicious router and a branch bridge agent, and a user with a virtual account tries to deposit / settle funds or execute a dApp omnichain call through the attacker's created branch bridge agent, the attacker will be able to hijack the all of the virtual account's ERC20 tokens and ether stored inside of the contract

## The vulnerability
This vulnerability exists because a root bridge agent trusts it's core router and allows it to access a virtual account's funds by executing the function `toggleVirtualAccountApproved()` on the RootPort. Check the code starting from line https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-maia/blob/f5ba4de628836b2a29f9b5fff59499690008c463/src/RootBridgeAgent.sol#L554C37-L554C65.

That means if an attacker creates a root bridge agent with a malicious root router and creates a branch bridge agent for the user to interact with as an entry-point, if the user sends a "signed" request whether to execute a dApp omnichain call or deposit funds or settle funds (move funds from root to branch) through the attacker's branch bridge agent or branch router. The attacker-created root bridge agent will trigger the function `toggleVirtualAccountApproved()` on the RootPort and allow the attacker-created malicious root router to control and steal all of the virtual account funds (ERC20/721/1155[tokens] or native ether).

## Proof of Concept Code Explanation

I created a PoC code which you will find down below, here I will be explaining it:

--------- Initial Attack Setup ---------
1. First the attacker creates the malicious multicall root router contract (you'll find it down below, It's similar to the code of `MulticallRootRouter` but with modified code that allows theft of user funds).
2. Then the attacker will create a new root bridge agent through the factory and couple it with the newly-created malicious root multicall router
3. The attacker will then deploy a normal branch bridge agent and a normal core branch router and sync them with the attacker-created root bridge agent

--------- User Simulation ---------
1. A user virtual account is created and is given 10,000 ether, so now there will be 10,000 ether in the user's virtual account.
2. User tries to send a normal omnichain dApp call through the branch bridge agent using the function `callOutSigned` in `BranchBridgeAgent`.
3. User gets his 10,000 ether stolen from his wallet and sent to the attacker's address

## PoC Code
1. First, we will add the malicious root multicall router file. In the "src" folder, add the file "MaliciousMulticallRouter.sol" with the following code:
