# Vulnerability details

## Impact
MulticallRootRouter withdraws/require output hToken more than needed in execute()/executeSigned* multicallSingleOutput and multicallMultipleOutput handlers (0x02/0x03). And the excess tokens can be locked in the contract and lost later.

## Proof of Concept
In executeSigned* MulticallRootRouter multicallSingleOutput and multicallMultipleOutput handlers (0x02/0x03), we withdraw the output htokens from users' virtual accounts:

```solidity
            IVirtualAccount(userAccount).withdrawERC20(outputParams.outputToken, outputParams.amountOut);

            // Bridge Out assets
            _approveAndCallOut(
                IVirtualAccount(userAccount).userAddress(),
                outputParams.recipient,
                outputParams.outputToken,
                outputParams.amountOut,
                outputParams.depositOut,
                dstChainId,
                gasParams
            );

// similar for multicallMultipleOutput
```

As we can see, we withdrew outputParams.amountOut htokens, didn't account for the outputParams.depositOut. However, in _approveAndCallOut --> IBridgeAgent(_bridgeAgentAddress).callOutAndBridge --> _updateStateOnBridgeOut, we only transfer ( _amount - _deposit) htokens from the router:

```solidity
        // Move output hTokens from Root to Branch
        if (_amount - _deposit > 0) {
            unchecked {
                _globalAddress.safeTransferFrom(_depositor, localPortAddress, _amount - _deposit);
            }
        }

        // Clear Underlying Tokens from the destination Branch
        if (_deposit > 0) {
            // Verify there is enough balance to clear native tokens if needed
            if (IERC20hTokenRoot(_globalAddress).getTokenBalance(_dstChainId) < _deposit) {
                revert InsufficientBalanceForSettlement();
            }
            IPort(localPortAddress).burn(_depositor, _globalAddress, _deposit, _dstChainId);
        }
```

The issue is similar for execute() 0x02/0x03 handlers, except it requires the tokens are transferred in `_multicall(callData)`.

So after the _approveAndCallOut/_approveMultipleAndCallOut call, there will be htokens left in the contract. The left tokens can be left forever or be used by execute() multicallSingleOutput/_approveMultipleAndCallOut handlers (need to transfer from the _multicall call, but now has htokens left in the contract).


## Tools Used
Manual Review.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
1. In _approveAndCallOut/_approveMultipleAndCallOut, change safeApprove(_bridgeAgentAddress, amountOut)/safeApprove(_bridgeAgentAddress, amountsOut[i]) to amountOut - depositOut.
2. In executeSigned* functions, change withdrawERC20(outputParams.outputToken, outputParams.amountOut)/withdrawERC20(outputParams.outputTokens[i], outputParams.amountsOut[i]) to outputParams.amountOut-outputParams.depositOut. 


