# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-maia/blob/90a2bda6f00b588e607344d88eae2810dbd6c443/src/RootBridgeAgent.sol#L938-L948
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-maia/blob/746905cdd3aa165ba1f9274c360dfd9f0c52e781/src/BranchBridgeAgent.sol#L422-L431


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The `RootBridgeAgent#_performFallbackCall` refund address may be incorrect, potentially causing fund loss in `RootBridgeAgent`.

## Proof of Concept
In `RootBridgeAgent`, the function `lzReceive` processes messages from layer0. The procedure called is listed below, with `_performFallbackCall` as an example:

1. `lzReceive`
2. `lzReceiveNonBlocking`
3. In `lzReceiveNonBlocking`, if `_payload == 0x08` -> `_performFallbackCall`. In this step, notice the input parameter `_payload[PARAMS_START:PARAMS_START_SIGNED]`. This is wrapped in `BranchBridgeAgent#retrieveDeposit`, and it's `msg.sender` who calls `retrieveDeposit`.
4. `layer0.send()`, and the refund address is `msg.sender` who calls `retrieveDeposit` in `BranchBridgeAgent`.

The issue is in `RootBridgeAgent#_performFallbackCall`, where the layer0 send refund address is in another chain call. Due to the lack of limitations in `BranchBridgeAgent#retrieveDeposit`, a malicious user could call `retrieveDeposit` an infinite number of times to drain the native token left in `RootBridgeAgent`.





Poc: 
I only mock the situation of calling retrieve deposit multiple times.Add this script in  `RootTest.t.sol`

