# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-maia/blob/main/src/CoreBranchRouter.sol#L43
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-maia/blob/main/src/CoreRootRouter.sol#L103-L110


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Some actions within the Branch/Root routers involves multiple cross chain messages in a single call. However, given the refund mechanism exisiting in the LayerZero `send()` function, it is likely that no matter how much initial gas is supplied, ther will be insufficient gas forwarded to execute the subsequent LayerZero cross chain messages.

The logic of refund for a cross-chain message can be seen here within the `UltraLightNodeV2.sol`. The fee calculation logic within `send()` is identical to when calling `estimateFees()`.

[UltraLightNodeV2.sol#L142-L156](https://github.com/LayerZero-Labs/LayerZero/blob/48c21c3921931798184367fc02d3a8132b041942/contracts/UltraLightNodeV2.sol#L142-L156)
```solidity
uint relayerFee = _handleRelayer(dstChainId, uaConfig, ua, payload.length, _adapterParams);
uint oracleFee = _handleOracle(dstChainId, uaConfig, ua);
uint nativeProtocolFee = _handleProtocolFee(relayerFee, oracleFee, ua, _zroPaymentAddress);

// total native fee, does not include ZRO protocol fee
uint totalNativeFee = relayerFee.add(oracleFee).add(nativeProtocolFee);

// assert the user has attached enough native token for this address
require(totalNativeFee <= msg.value, "LayerZero: not enough native for fees");
// refund if they send too much
uint amount = msg.value.sub(totalNativeFee);
if (amount > 0) {
    (bool success, ) = _refundAddress.call{value: amount}("");
    require(success, "LayerZero: failed to refund");
}
```

Actions that are impacted:

- `CoreBranchRouter.addGlobalToken()`: [Link](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-maia/blob/main/src/CoreBranchRouter.sol#L43), Global Token cannot be added to a branch chain
- `CoreRootRouter.addBranchToBridgeAgent()`: [Link](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-maia/blob/main/src/CoreRootRouter.sol#L103-L110): Bridge Agent Manager cannot add new chain to a root bridge agent

## Proof of Concept
Take for example, `CoreBranchRouter.addGlobalToken()`, the initial input already requires 3 `_gParams`, indicating 3 cross-chain messages involved in the function call to add a global token as following:

1. First cross-chain message involves local branch bridge agent calling out the root bridge agent [here](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-maia/blob/main/src/CoreBranchRouter.sol#L54), this call will be successful, note that any excess gas will be refunded via `send()`.

<br/>

2. `RootBridgeAgent` will call `RootBridgeAgentExecutor.executeNoDeposit()` which inturn calls  `CoreRootRouter.execute()`. This in turn calls the internal `CoreRootRouter._addGlobalToken()` function, which intiates the second cross chain message from root chain root bridge agent to destination chain branch bridge agent [here](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-maia/blob/main/src/CoreRootRouter.sol#L437-L439). This is where it will revert, since the `msg.value` pass on to RootBridgeAgent (`address(this).balance == 0`) is zero and will be insufficient for the layerzero cross-chain message.

<br/>

3. This results in the third cross-chain LZ message initiated from `CoreBranchRouter.executeNoSettlement()` [here](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-maia/blob/main/src/CoreBranchRouter.sol#L184) via the internal `_receiveAddGlobalToken()` function to never go through since the third and final cross-chain LZ message is dependent on the second cross-chain message succeeding.

## Recommendation
Perform estimation of fees similar to a example contract by LZ [here](https://github.com/LayerZero-Labs/LayerZero/blob/48c21c3921931798184367fc02d3a8132b041942/contracts/mocks/PingPong.sol) to avoid reverts when sending cross-chain messages, especially for core routers which involves multiple cross-chain messages. Some suggestions are:

- Call `esimateFees()` before `.send()` is called for an action requiring multiple LZ cross-chain messages. 

- Compare the previous LZ message fee required to user supplied gas, and forward the excess gas to the respective bridge agents (Root/Branch). This ensures that NONE of the cross-chain messages will fail due to the refund mechanism existing in `send()` function.


