# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The impact of this vulnerability is that users attempting to execute cross-chain deposit requests will always encounter a transaction failure. This leads to a denial of service (DoS) situation, preventing users from accessing the intended service and causing frustration and wasted gas costs.
## Proof of Concept
The `executeDepositSingle` function in the `MulticallRootRouter` contract has the following code snippet:
```solidity
function executeDepositSingle(bytes calldata, DepositParams calldata, uint16) external payable override {
    revert();
}
```
This function is designed to handle cross-chain deposit requests but unconditionally reverts all incoming transactions. It lacks proper logic and condition checks to determine whether a transaction should proceed or revert based on the specific requirements of the contract.
## Tools Used
Manual
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
This modification ensures that transactions are only reverted when they do not comply with the contract's intended behavior, allowing legitimate users to access the service as intended while maintaining security.
```solidity
function executeDepositSingle(bytes calldata params, DepositParams calldata dParams, uint16 srcChainId) external payable override {
    // Add appropriate logic and conditions here to determine whether the transaction should proceed or revert.
    // Example condition: Check if the sender is authorized to execute this function.
    require(isAuthorized(msg.sender), "Unauthorized sender");

    // Additional checks and processing for cross-chain deposit requests.

    // If all conditions are met, proceed with the transaction logic.
    // Otherwise, revert the transaction if any condition fails.
}
```


