# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Detailed description of the impact of this finding.
Both functions might set the addresses improperly due to failure to delete existing mappings. As a result, the mapping might not be 1-1 - there might be two global tokens point to one local token.  Such confusion will lead to wrong mappings in the future. 

## Proof of Concept
Provide direct links to all referenced code in GitHub. Add screenshots, logs, or any other relevant proof that illustrates the concept.

setAddresses() and setLocalAddress() allow the establishment of mappings between local tokens and global tokens. However, when such mapping already exists and these two functions are used to modify the mapping, then the addresses might not be set properly. For example, suppose we had L1 <-> G1 and we need to change it to L2 <-> G1, then we need to delete the mapping L1 -> G1. For another example, suppose we had L1 <-> G1, and we need to change it to L1 <-> G2, we also need to delete the mapping  G1-> L1. Otherwise, the mapping will not be 1-1 anymore. Similar delete is needed for underlying tokens as well.

The functions also fail to delete old global tokens. 

The following POC confirms my finding; see function ``testTokenArbitrum2()``: both l1 and l2 point to the same global token after the update, not a 1-1 mapping anymore.

