## Recommended Mitigation Steps

- To mitigate the issue, the contract should either use `msg.sender` as the from address in the `transferFrom` function calls, or use the `safeTransferFrom` function from the `OpenZeppelin` ERC20 library, which checks that the from address is equal to `msg.sender` before transferring the tokens. This will ensure that only the caller of the mint callback function can transfer the tokens to the pool, and prevent any unauthorized transfers from other addresses.

- Here is how the code could be modified to implement these recommendations:

```solidity
// This function is called by the Uniswap V3 pool after a mint operation
// It transfers the required amounts of token0 and token1 from the caller to the pool
// It also validates the caller and the pool features using the CallbackLib
function uniswapV3MintCallback(
    uint256 amount0Owed,
    uint256 amount1Owed,
    bytes calldata data
) external {
    // Decode the mint callback data
    CallbackLib.CallbackData memory decoded = abi.decode(data, (CallbackLib.CallbackData));
    // Validate caller to ensure we got called from the AMM pool
    CallbackLib.validateCallback(msg.sender, address(FACTORY), decoded.poolFeatures);
    // Sends the amount0Owed and amount1Owed quantities provided
    if (amount0Owed > 0) {
        // Use msg.sender as the from address to prevent unauthorized transfers
        SafeTransferLib.safeTransferFrom(
            decoded.poolFeatures.token0,
            msg.sender,
            address(this),
            amount0Owed
        );
    }
    if (amount1Owed > 0) {
        // Use msg.sender as the from address to prevent unauthorized transfers
        SafeTransferLib.safeTransferFrom(
            decoded.poolFeatures.token1,
            msg.sender,
            address(this),
            amount1Owed
        );
    }
}

```
