# Vulnerability details

## Impact

If the data in the loop silently overflow, this will result in unexpected behavior, i.e the loop will continue to iterate even though there are no more elements in the data array.

## Proof of Concept

For `multicall` applications such as batch operations or creating advanced positions, `SFPM` inherits the `multicall` function from the `Multicall.sol` contract. This is done for `multicall` applications and collects data for each call that occurs on an array. Below is the `multicall` function :

```solidity
File : contracts/multicall/Multicall.sol

12     function multicall(bytes[] calldata data) public payable returns (bytes[] memory results) {
13              results = new bytes[](data.length);
14              for (uint256 i = 0; i < data.length; ) {
15              (bool success, bytes memory result) = address(this).delegatecall(data[i]);
16
17              if (!success) {
18                  // Bubble up the revert reason
19                  // The bytes type is ABI encoded as a length-prefixed byte array
20                  // So we simply need to add 32 to the pointer to get the start of the data
21                  // And then revert with the size loaded from the first 32 bytes
22                  // Other solutions will do work to differentiate the revert reasons and provide paranthetical information
23                  // However, we have chosen to simply replicate the the normal behavior of the call
24                  // NOTE: memory-safe because it reads from memory already allocated by solidity (the bytes memory result)
25                  assembly ("memory-safe") {
26                      revert(add(result, 32), mload(result))
27                  }
28               }
29
30               results[i] = result;
31
32               unchecked {
33                    ++i;
34               }
35           }
36       }
```

In this function there is a loop that is used to collect data from each call executed based on `data.length` and store it in an array called `results`. The problem here is if the `data.length` of the `multicall` being performed exceeds the maximum value of an unsigned integer (2^256 - 1), then incrementing it again will cause it to wrap around to zero. This could lead to an out-of-bounds access to the 'data' array, as the loop will continue to iterate even though there are no more elements in the array. This can happen because in this function, when performing an increment there is no check, so a silent overflow may occur ([line 32 - 33](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-11-panoptic/blob/aa86461c9d6e60ef75ed5a1fe36a748b952c8666/contracts/multicall/Multicall.sol#L32-L33)). 

```solidity
32               unchecked {
33                    ++i;
```

## Tools Used

Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Consider adding check to the loop as below : 

```solidity
if (i < data.length) {
++i;
}
```

This check will ensure that the loop counter 'i' never exceeds the length of the 'data' array, preventing out-of-bounds accesses.





