# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/periphery/oracles/CTokenOracle.sol#L34-L35


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In the `CTokenOracle` contract, the underlying token is set in the constructor with the internal function `_setUnderlying` and cannot be changed after, there is an error in the constructor as the `_cToken` address is set as underlying token instead of the true underlying token address `_underlying`. 

If this error is not noticed at the deployment of the `CTokenOracle` contract, it will cause the oracle to not work correctly and return wrong (incorrect) prices which will negatively impact the protocol functions that rely on accurate oracle prices. 

## Proof of Concept

The issue is occurring in the constructor of the `CTokenOracle` contract below :

```solidity
constructor(
    address _cToken,
    IOracleRelay _anchoredViewUnderlying
) OracleRelay(OracleType.Chainlink) {
    cToken = ICToken(_cToken);
    anchoredViewUnderlying = _anchoredViewUnderlying;

    // If underlying is ETH, decimals are 18, if not, get the decimals from the underlying
    uint256 _underlyingDecimals = cETH_ADDRESS == _cToken
        ? 18
        : IERC20Metadata(cToken.underlying()).decimals();

    // Save the divisor to convert the underlying to the cToken
    div = 10 ** (18 + _underlyingDecimals - cToken.decimals());

    // If the underlying is different, revert
    address _underlying = cETH_ADDRESS == _cToken
        ? wETH_ADDRESS
        : cToken.underlying();
    if (_underlying != anchoredViewUnderlying.underlying())
        revert OracleRelay_DifferentUnderlyings();

    // Set the underlying address
    // @audit should be _underlying not _cToken 
    _setUnderlying(_cToken);
}
```

As you can see from the code above, the address `_cToken` is given to the `_setUnderlying` function instead of the true underlying token address represented by the address `_underlying`.

Because the `_setUnderlying` function is an internal function and the `CTokenOracle` contract does not contain another public/external function to change the underlying token address after the deployment, this means that the incorrect underlying token address can never be changed in the future and this error will cause the oracle to return wrong prices which will impact the protocol functions that rely on accurate oracle prices in their logic.

## Tools Used

Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

To fix this issue, the correct underlying token address should be set in the constructor as follows : 

```solidity
constructor(
    address _cToken,
    IOracleRelay _anchoredViewUnderlying
) OracleRelay(OracleType.Chainlink) {
    cToken = ICToken(_cToken);
    anchoredViewUnderlying = _anchoredViewUnderlying;

    // If underlying is ETH, decimals are 18, if not, get the decimals from the underlying
    uint256 _underlyingDecimals = cETH_ADDRESS == _cToken
        ? 18
        : IERC20Metadata(cToken.underlying()).decimals();

    // Save the divisor to convert the underlying to the cToken
    div = 10 ** (18 + _underlyingDecimals - cToken.decimals());

    // If the underlying is different, revert
    address _underlying = cETH_ADDRESS == _cToken
        ? wETH_ADDRESS
        : cToken.underlying();
    if (_underlying != anchoredViewUnderlying.underlying())
        revert OracleRelay_DifferentUnderlyings();

    // Set the underlying address
    // @audit set correct address _underlying
    _setUnderlying(_underlying);
}
```


