# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/daae020331404647c661ab534d20093c875483e1/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L443-L456


# Vulnerability details


## Impact

The `peekCheckVault()` function is critical within the `VaultController.sol` contract because it checks whether a vault is over-collateralized or under-collateralized. This solvency status is used in the internal `_peekLiquidationMath()` function. However, there is a potential issue due to the lack of a `payInterest` modifier on these functions.

Without this modifier, the functions may rely on stale data. For instance, a vault's insolvency status might change due to to accumulated interest, but the current implementation would fail to recognize this change in status if the interest is not appropriately updated. This inconsistency easily results in incorrect evaluations of the solvency status, which could have significant implications for users interacting with these vaults and easily cost them to lose trust in the protocol

## Proof of Concept

Take a look at [VaultController.sol#L443-L456](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/daae020331404647c661ab534d20093c875483e1/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L443-L456)

```solidity

  /// @notice Check a vault for over-collateralization
  /// @dev This function calls peekVaultBorrowingPower so no state change is done
  /// @param _id The id of vault we want to target
  /// @return _overCollateralized Returns true if vault over-collateralized; false if vault under-collaterlized
function peekCheckVault(uint96 _id) public view override returns (bool _overCollateralized) {
  // grab the vault by id if part of our system. revert if not
  IVault _vault = _getVault(_id);
  // calculate the total value of the vault's liquidity
  uint256 _totalLiquidityValue = _peekVaultBorrowingPower(_vault);
  // calculate the total liability of the vault
  uint256 _usdaLiability = _truncate((_vault.baseLiability() * interest.factor));
  // if the ltv >= liability, the vault is solvent
  _overCollateralized = (_totalLiquidityValue >= _usdaLiability);
}
```

Can be seen that this is called from the internal [`__peekLiquidationMath()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/daae020331404647c661ab534d20093c875483e1/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L715-L735) function, with an explicit comment that the call to `peekCheckVault()` is done to check that the vault is not solvent at line [727](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/daae020331404647c661ab534d20093c875483e1/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L727)

```solidity
  /// @notice Internal function with business logic for liquidation math without any state changes
  /// @param _id The vault to get info for
  /// @param _assetAddress The token to calculate how many tokens to liquidate
  /// @param _tokensToLiquidate The max amount of tokens one wishes to liquidate
  /// @return _actualTokensToLiquidate The amount of tokens underwater this vault is
  /// @return _badFillPrice The bad fill price for the token
  function _peekLiquidationMath(
    uint96 _id,
    address _assetAddress,
    uint256 _tokensToLiquidate
  ) internal view returns (uint256 _actualTokensToLiquidate, uint256 _badFillPrice) {
      //@audit
    //require that the vault is not solvent
    if (peekCheckVault(_id)) revert VaultController_VaultSolvent();

    CollateralInfo memory _collateral = tokenAddressCollateralInfo[_assetAddress];
    uint256 _price = _collateral.oracle.peekValue();
    uint256 _usdaToSolvency = _peekAmountToSolvency(_id);

    (_actualTokensToLiquidate, _badFillPrice) =
      _calculateTokensToLiquidate(_collateral, _id, _tokensToLiquidate, _assetAddress, _price, _usdaToSolvency);
  }

```

In the current implementation, the interest is not updated before checking the vault's solvency.

If a vault insolvency status changes due to interest accumulation, the `peekCheckVault()` function might incorrectly return that the wrong value

