# Vulnerability details


## Impact

Asides other changes the `updateRegisteredErc20()` function could do, this report is solely focused on the loan-to-value ratio aspect, _i.e `ltv` for short._ The `ltv` could be adjusted in a manner that is unfavorable to the borrower (_intentionally or unintentiolly by owner_).

Say the `ltv` was originally set to a high value like 0.9, and then it is changed to a significantly lower value, say 0.5, this immediately decreases the borrower's ability to borrow more money based on their collateral by a large amount (~44.44%). Most importantly the decrease in `ltv` increases the chances of a borrower getting liquidated and could even in some cases make borrowers immediately liquidatable, since a reduction to the `ltv` could lead to their loan to be under-collateralized, or even if not, just a small price drop could cause them to be liquidatable.

NB: This report can't be classified as "owner error/risk", that arguement I believe can only be made if an owner specifically targets a user and intentionally reduce the `ltv` of his collateral, but otherwise the ``updateRegisteredErc20()` is good functionality that just need to be better implemented

## Proof of Concept

Take a look at the [updateRegisteredErc20()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/daae020331404647c661ab534d20093c875483e1/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L376-L412) function of VaultController.sol, t

```solidity
  /// @notice Updates an existing collateral with new collateral parameters
  /// @param _tokenAddress The address of the token to modify
  /// @param _ltv The new loan-to-value of the token, 1e18=100%
  /// @param _oracleAddress The address of oracle to modify for the price of the token
  /// @param _liquidationIncentive The new liquidation penalty for the token, 1e18=100%
  /// @param _cap The maximum amount to be deposited
  /// @param _poolId The convex pool id of a crv lp token
  function updateRegisteredErc20(
    address _tokenAddress,
    uint256 _ltv,
    address _oracleAddress,
    uint256 _liquidationIncentive,
    uint256 _cap,
    uint256 _poolId
  ) external override onlyOwner {
    CollateralInfo storage _collateral = tokenAddressCollateralInfo[_tokenAddress];
    if (_collateral.tokenId == 0) revert VaultController_TokenNotRegistered();
    // _ltv must be compatible with liquidation incentive
    if (_ltv >= (EXP_SCALE - _liquidationIncentive)) revert VaultController_LTVIncompatible();
    if (_poolId != 0) {
      (address _lpToken,,, address _crvRewards,,) = BOOSTER.poolInfo(_poolId);
      if (_lpToken != _tokenAddress) revert VaultController_TokenAddressDoesNotMatchLpAddress();
      _collateral.collateralType = CollateralType.CurveLPStakedOnConvex;
      _collateral.crvRewardsContract = IBaseRewardPool(_crvRewards);
      _collateral.poolId = _poolId;
    }
    // set the oracle of the token
    _collateral.oracle = IOracleRelay(_oracleAddress);
    // set the ltv of the token
    _collateral.ltv = _ltv;
    // set the liquidation incentive of the token
    _collateral.liquidationIncentive = _liquidationIncentive;
    // set the cap
    _collateral.cap = _cap;

    emit UpdateRegisteredErc20(_tokenAddress, _ltv, _oracleAddress, _liquidationIncentive, _cap, _poolId);
  }
```

This function can be used to update the _loan-to-value (LTV) ratio_, oracle address, liquidation incentive, maximum deposit limit (cap), and the pool ID of the token. This function can only be called by the contract owner, and as such, proves this issue.
Note that this report focuses on two primary issues:

1. The lack of a timelock on the `updateRegisteredErc20()` function. This absence is problematic because users should be given ample notification time to adjust to changes in the LTV ratio and to act accordingly, for example, if a user would be liquidatable after the change , they can just add collateral while the changes are timelocked to maintain their healthy position

2. Additionally, the ability for the LTV to be set to any value is not really ideal as this can be highly costly for borrowers.

## Tool used

Manual Audit

## Recommendation

I'd recommend that a timelock be implemented for calls to the `updateRegisteredErc20()` function. Even a short timeframe would be beneficial since an abrupt change in the LTV ratio could lead to immediate liquidations of certain borrowers. Furthermore, additional checks should be put in place to limit the range of possible LTV values to protect the interests of borrowers.



