# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/daae020331404647c661ab534d20093c875483e1/core/solidity/contracts/periphery/oracles/StableCurveLpOracle.sol#L45-L53


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
`StableCurveLpOracle::_get` calculates the latest exchange rate by multiplying the minimum price of anchored underlying tokens by the updated virtual price for the pool. If any of the underlying tokens for a liquidity pool has a price of zero, this might suggest some form of system failure or malicious activity, as the price of a token should not ordinarily be zero in a functioning market, and might be indicative of an issue with the oracle providing the price feed for that token.

```solidity
/// @notice Calculates the lastest exchange rate
  function _get() internal view returns (uint256 _value) {
    // As the price should never be negative, the unchecked conversion is acceptable
    uint256 _minStable = anchoredUnderlyingTokens[0].peekValue();
    for (uint256 _i = 1; _i < anchoredUnderlyingTokens.length;) {
      _minStable = Math.min(_minStable, anchoredUnderlyingTokens[_i].peekValue());
      unchecked {
        ++_i;
      }
    }

    uint256 _lpPrice = _getVirtualPrice() * _minStable; // @audit - if the price of one of the underlying tokens is 0, this will return 0, resulting in a 0 price. Perhaps should handle or revert.

    _value = _lpPrice / 1e18;
  }
```

If this is the case, with any of the oracles for the underlying tokens returning a price of zero for the respective token, then the entire function will return zero. This may not be desired unless all underlying tokens return a price of zero.

## Proof of Concept
N/A.

## Tools Used
Manual review.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Handle the case where one of the anchored underlying tokens returns a zero price, perhaps continuing the loop without considering such a value for minimum calculations but reverting if all prices are zero.


