# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/daae020331404647c661ab534d20093c875483e1/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L383-L412


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The unintended collateral parameter changes could lead to inconsistencies in the system, different liquidation mechanisms, or unexpected interest calculations. 

## Proof of Concept
The [`updateRegisteredErc20`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/daae020331404647c661ab534d20093c875483e1/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L383-L412) function is designed to enable the contract owner to modify the collateral parameters for an already registered ERC-20 token. However, the absence of proper access control checks allows the contract owner to make these modifications without restrictions. This introduces a potential security vulnerability that could lead to unexpected changes in the system and possible harm to users.

```solidity
function updateRegisteredErc20(
    address _tokenAddress,
    uint256 _ltv,
    address _oracleAddress,
    uint256 _liquidationIncentive,
    uint256 _cap,
    uint256 _poolId
) external override onlyOwner {
    CollateralInfo storage _collateral = tokenAddressCollateralInfo[_tokenAddress];
    if (_collateral.tokenId == 0) revert VaultController_TokenNotRegistered();

    // ...

    if (_poolId != 0) {
        (address _lpToken,,, address _crvRewards,,) = BOOSTER.poolInfo(_poolId);
        if (_lpToken != _tokenAddress) revert VaultController_TokenAddressDoesNotMatchLpAddress();
        _collateral.collateralType = CollateralType.CurveLPStakedOnConvex;
        _collateral.crvRewardsContract = IBaseRewardPool(_crvRewards);
        _collateral.poolId = _poolId;
    }

    // ...
}
```
In this function, the code conditionally updates the `collateralType`, `crvRewardsContract`, and `poolId` based on the `_poolId` parameter, even if the token is already registered. This behavior allows changing these parameters for an already registered token, which could potentially lead to inconsistencies or unexpected behavior in the contract.

The issue is that the contract owner can modify the collateral-related parameters, even if a token has already been registered with specific collateral settings. This may not be the intended behavior, as it allows the contract owner to change the token's behavior after users have already interacted with it. Users who previously interacted with the token may continue their operations, unaware of the changed collateral parameters.

## Tools Used
Manual Audit.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider adding additional validation checks to ensure that the collateral settings for already registered tokens remain consistent unless explicitly intended otherwise.


