# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Since all tokens are connected in a way with [checkVault](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L462-L471), if one of them fails to deliver the price or reverts somewhere along the line, the whole transaction would fail.
## Proof of Concept
During the withdrawal process from the user's vault, the [checkVault](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L462-L471) function is called. This, in turn, calls [_getVaultBorrowingPower](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L866-L888), which is an important function that is also used in [liquidateVault](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L691). However  [_getVaultBorrowingPower](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L866-L888) has an issue in the way it cycles the allowed tokens. It gets the list of allowed tokens and calls on each one `_collateral.oracle.currentValue()`, where of course after the call is made, the value is checked for return of 0, but if it were to return 0, it would have failed way earlier.
```jsx
      uint192 _rawPrice = _safeu192(_collateral.oracle.currentValue());
      if (_rawPrice == 0) continue;
```
For example, if a BlackSwan event occurs and Chainlink stops one of its price feeds (like the collapse on Luna), the oracle call will revert at the moment the price is extracted from Chainlink. The [oracle.currentValue](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L879) call would be [currentValue](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/periphery/oracles/OracleRelay.sol#L24) => [peekValue](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/periphery/oracles/ChainlinkOracleRelay.sol#L51-L53) => [_getLastSecond](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/periphery/oracles/ChainlinkOracleRelay.sol#L73-L76) => [getCurrentPrice](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/periphery/oracles/ChainlinkStalePriceLib.sol#L11-L15) where at the last one `getCurrentPrice` the actual revert would be. The Chainlink price itself is correctly checked (even tho more checks could be made), but the vulnerability lies in the way all [tokens are cycled](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/main/core/solidity/contracts/core/VaultController.sol#L868C1-L887), and if one of them fails to return the price the whole call revert. 

Consequences of it reverting is:
- Users not being able to withdraw their balances from the Vaults
- Liquidators not being able to liquidate, tho the time needs them to do so, because of the falling prices

Example is [Venus protocol](https://therecord.media/collapse-of-luna-cryptocurrency-leads-to-11-million-exploit-on-venus-protocol) which lost around 11m when Luna collapsed. These accidents happen really, but if, or should I say, when they happen they can collapse protocols and cost millions in lost tokens or bad debt due to failed liquidation. 

## Tools Used
Manual review
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The vulnerability is not with the check on ChainLink's price, but on how the tokens are cycled. When cycling them put the call to the oracle in a `try` and `catch` block, and if the `catch` triggers you can assume the price is 0.
```jsx
   uint192 _rawPrice;
   try{
      _rawPrice = _safeu192(_collateral.oracle.currentValue());
   }catch{
     _rawPrice = 0;
   }
```


