# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-amphora/blob/41cf810b02a150bb168698ff1eeb1f768d6bdcb0/core/solidity/contracts/periphery/oracles/ChainlinkStalePriceLib.sol#L12-L14


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
- On ___ChainlinkStalePriceLib.soll___  function "getCurrentPrice(AggregatorV2V3Interface)" , which uses latestRoundData, but there is no check if the returned data is stale, neither if the round has been completed.

 ```
/// @notice Returns the current price from the aggregator
  function getCurrentPrice(AggregatorV2V3Interface _aggregator) internal view returns (uint256 _price) {
    (, int256 _answer,,,) = _aggregator.latestRoundData();
    if (_answer <= 0) revert Chainlink_NegativePrice();
    _price = uint256(_answer);
  }
  ```
- The library is used inside __ChainlinkOracleRelay.sol___. Judging by the name of the function there - "_getLastSecond" and the comment "@notice Returns last second value of the oracle", we assume that the logic should return the latest data

Regarding Chainlink's documentation https://docs.chain.link/data-feeds/historical-data this could lead to stale prices and then confusion throughout the following code logic.
Also if the 'timeStamp' value is 0, means that the round is not complete and the answer should not be used.

## Proof of Concept
https://github.com/code-423n4/2021-05-fairside-findings/issues/70
## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The returned data should be checked as follows:
```
  function getCurrentPrice(AggregatorV2V3Interface _aggregator) internal view returns (uint256 _price) {
    (uint80 roundID, int256 answer, uint256 timestamp, uint256 updatedAt, ) = _aggregator.latestRoundData();
	require(updatedAt >= roundID, "Stale price");
    require(timestamp != 0,"Round not complete");
    if (_answer <= 0) revert Chainlink_NegativePrice();
    _price = uint256(_answer);
  }
```


