https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-ethereumcreditguild/blob/2376d9af792584e3d15ec9c32578daa33bb56b43/src/governance/ProfitManager.sol#L305
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-ethereumcreditguild/blob/2376d9af792584e3d15ec9c32578daa33bb56b43/src/tokens/GuildToken.sol#L123-L129
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-ethereumcreditguild/blob/2376d9af792584e3d15ec9c32578daa33bb56b43/src/tokens/GuildToken.sol#L133-L161


# Vulnerability details

When a gauge experiences a loss, usually either by calling loans of offboarded lending terms or overdue partial payment,  `ProfitManager.notifyPnL()` is called, which internally notify gauge loss to GUILD token:

```javascript
    function notifyPnL(
        address gauge,
        int256 amount
    ) external onlyCoreRole(CoreRoles.GAUGE_PNL_NOTIFIER) {
        uint256 _surplusBuffer = surplusBuffer;
        uint256 _termSurplusBuffer = termSurplusBuffer[gauge];
        address _credit = credit;

        // handling loss
        if (amount < 0) {
            uint256 loss = uint256(-amount);

            // save gauge loss
            GuildToken(guild).notifyGaugeLoss(gauge);
			// [...]
```

Then, in order to perform any action on the GUILD tokens, user has to apply the loss to tgheir account. However, no matter how big the loss is, ALL his stake is burned from them in the following code: 
```javascript
    function applyGaugeLoss(address gauge, address who) external
        // [...]

        // read user weight allocated to the lossy gauge
        uint256 _userGaugeWeight = getUserGaugeWeight[who][gauge];

        // remove gauge weight allocation
        lastGaugeLossApplied[gauge][who] = block.timestamp;
        _decrementGaugeWeight(who, gauge, _userGaugeWeight); // @issue burning all user assets no matter the loss
        if (!_deprecatedGauges.contains(gauge)) {
            totalTypeWeight[gaugeType[gauge]] -= _userGaugeWeight;
            totalWeight -= _userGaugeWeight;
        }

        // apply loss
        _burn(who, uint256(_userGaugeWeight)); // @audit user is burned whole stake, no matter how big is the loss
```

The worst part is that the loss can be forced by creating a small loan and not performing partial repay and then calling a loan. Then, an auction is started, and when it's close to being profitable, a malicious actor can block stuff until protocol loss is incurred. It actually may even happen naturally, without anyone's action due to delays in processing, L2 sequencer being down etc. Actually, the biggest risk of this happening is on L2s which are less stable than Ethereum and block stuffing attack is extremely cheap.

## Impact
Users loose all GUILD tokens staked on a gauge by forced loss. Given that it's hard to get GUILD tokens and they are untransferrable by default, can even impair voting completely, in extreme case not allowing to veto malicious vote.

## Proof of Concept

1. Create a small loan with partial repay.
2. Wait for the partial repay delay to pass.
3. Call your loan via `LendingTerm.call()`.
4. Wait for the Dutch auction to reach almost profitable state.
5. Stuff blocks, disallowing anyone to vote until the protocol will have to record the slightest loss (>= 1 wei)
6. Now everyone's who staked any GUILD token for this vault has to apply the loss of ALL their staked GUILD tokens.

## Tools Used

Manual analysis

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
I think that the biggest issue is the unmached punishment for gauge loss compared to the amount of loss. I would focus on redesigning the system in a way, that users loose percentage of their position and are forced to exit their stake.


