# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Even though this protocol isn't dependent on external oracles for fetching prices, etc. there are still certain variables which can and will change and may impact the redemption / stake / mint / borrow ratio, which may cause fluctuations in the ratio between assets. Since all key functions are lacking any kind of deadline and/or slippage checks without a deadline a transaction can be left in the mempool and be executed at a later stage which can cause an unfavorable outcome. 

## Proof of Concept

There are certain variables that affect the ratio between assets when redeeming / staking / borrowing. We have the ProfitManager.creditMultiplier() which is the multiplier for credit value in they system, which can go down in an event where CREDIT loses value over time when bad debt is created / accumulated in the system. 

Another such variable of similar kind would be the SurplusGuildMinter.mintRatio() which is the ratio of GUILD tokens minted per CREDIT tokens staked - this ratio can be changed by the Governor.

I will provide a possible scenario that may cause negative and unintended outcomes for the user which may affect their experience with the protocol negatively, due to the lack of deadline checks: 

**Scenario A:**

When a user wants to redeem their CREDIT (.e.g. gUSDC) for their underlying tokens (e.g. USDC / WETH, etc.), we will get an amount out calculated based on the getRedeemAmount() function, which uses the creditMultiplier() as the ratio for the exchange rates between assets. In an event where the transaction has been sitting for a longer time in the mempool due to very low gas fee provided by the user or some other reason, when the transaction is executed the ratio might have changed due to the creditMultiplier() going down and the user would receive more unfavorable conditions for their redemption.

```
    /// @notice redeem `amountIn` CREDIT for `amountOut` underlying tokens and send to address `to`
    /// @dev see getRedeemAmountOut() to pre-calculate amount out
    function redeem(
        address to,
        uint256 amountIn
    ) external returns (uint256 amountOut) {
        require(!redemptionsPaused, "SimplePSM: redemptions paused");
        amountOut = getRedeemAmountOut(amountIn);
        CreditToken(credit).burnFrom(msg.sender, amountIn);
        pegTokenBalance -= amountOut;
        ERC20(pegToken).safeTransfer(to, amountOut);
        emit Redeem(block.timestamp, to, amountIn, amountOut);
    }
````
The getRedeemAmountOut function upon which the price is based: 

```
    /// @notice calculate the amount of underlying out for a given `amountIn` of CREDIT
    function getRedeemAmountOut(
        uint256 amountIn
    ) public view returns (uint256) {
        uint256 creditMultiplier = ProfitManager(profitManager)
            .creditMultiplier();
        return (amountIn * creditMultiplier) / 1e18 / decimalCorrection;
    }
```
Similar situation as described above can occur due to a change in the mintRatio. If a user has submitted a transaction which hasn't been executed for some time, but in the meantime the mintRatio has decreased due to GUILD voters reducing the external first loss capital in the system, this may result in unfavorable conditions for the user(s).

## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

A deadline (timestamp) parameter can be provided to some of these functions to revert the transaction if the deadline has passed. 





