# Vulnerability details

when a token is minted the randomizer contract is called to generate a random hash, once the hash is generated is sent to the Core contract that stores it to be used to generate the generative art token.
```
function _mintProcessing(uint256 _mintIndex, address _recipient, string memory _tokenData, uint256 _collectionID, uint256 _saltfun_o) internal {
        tokenData[_mintIndex] = _tokenData;
        collectionAdditionalData[_collectionID].randomizer.calculateTokenHash(_collectionID, _mintIndex, _saltfun_o);
        tokenIdsToCollectionIds[_mintIndex] = _collectionID;
        _safeMint(_recipient, _mintIndex);
    }
```
NextGen currently considers 3 different Randomizer contracts that can be used for generating the tokenHash : 

1. A Randomizer contract that uses the Chainlink VRF to calculate the tokenHash.
2. A Randomizer contract that uses ARRNG for generating the tokenHash.
3. A custom-made implementation Randomizer contract that uses the token id, the blockchash of the previous block and a random pool of words and numbers to generate the tokenHash.

In case of a collection using the first or the second Randomizers ( that uses Chainlink VRF or ARRNG) for generating the token Hash, the process is divided in two separate transactions : 

1. on the first transaction the `calculateTokenHash()` function is called it triggers a call to the `requestRandomWords()` function that requests a random number from the off-chain oracle . and the token is minted .
2. on the second transaction the oracle fulfills the callback function, returns back the random number and calls the `setTokenHash()` function on the Core contract to store the hash.

The problem here is if the second transaction fails for any particular reason, the token will still be minted but will not have a tokenHash associated to it, and it will never have because the only time a token hash is set is when the token its minted .

*Note : you technically can re-request with Chainlink VRF but [it should be avoided](https://docs.chain.link/vrf/v2/security#do-not-re-request-randomness), and with ARRNG its not available .*
