# Vulnerability details

## Impact
A user could transfer more or lesser amount than the actual expected value to the configurator contract due to a precision error in the calculation of ```payAmount```, which is a vital local variable to determine the path of the function.

## Proof of Concept
``` uint256 payAmount = (((realAmount * getAssetPrice()) / 1e18) * getDutchAuctionDiscountPrice()) / 10000;```
Solidity will round up division to a whole number i.e 2.5 will be taken as 2, because of how solidity handles floating numbers.
```
 if (payAmount > income) {
            bool success = EUSD.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(configurator), income);
            require(success, "TF");

            try configurator.distributeRewards() {} catch {}

            uint256 sharesAmount = EUSD.getSharesByMintedEUSD(payAmount - income);
            if (sharesAmount == 0) {
                //EUSD totalSupply is 0: assume that shares correspond to EUSD 1-to-1
                sharesAmount = (payAmount - income);
            }
            //Income is distributed to LBR staker.
            EUSD.burnShares(msg.sender, sharesAmount);
            feeStored = 0;
            emit FeeDistribution(address(configurator), income, block.timestamp);
        } else {
            bool success = EUSD.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(configurator), payAmount);
            require(success, "TF");
            try configurator.distributeRewards() {} catch {}
            feeStored = income - payAmount;
            emit FeeDistribution(address(configurator), payAmount, block.timestamp);
        }
```
From the code snippet, we observe that the path the function will follow depends on ```if (payAmount > income)```
If the ```payAmount``` is less than ```income``` because of the precision loss in payAmount, ``` bool success = EUSD.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(configurator), payAmount);``` will run instead of  ```bool success = EUSD.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(configurator), income);```

