## Recommended Mitigation Steps
All the withdraw functionalities should use the same math used by _repay() to compute the fees, in order to avoid sending the full amount of underlying asset to the withdrawer leaving enough in the vault to cover the fees.








## Assessed type

Other

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraEUSDVaultBase.sol#L142
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L113


# Vulnerability details

## Description

Both base vaults either [PeUSD](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L113) and [EUSD](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraEUSDVaultBase.sol#L142) allow borrower to repay their debt by calling burn function. This function invoke the internal function `_repay` passing the `provider` as the msg.sender.

The current implementation of `_repay` function burn the minted EUSD from the provider and reduce the amount borrowed from the `_onBehalfOf` address.

This design works fine when the user who initiated the borrow request mint EUSD for himself when calling [mint function passing the `onBehalfOf` to his own address](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L96)

The issue with the implementation of `burn` function in both vaults is that it doesn't allow the user who borrowed the asset to repay the debt if he minted the EUSD for another address.

In cases where [user borrow assets](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L179) and [mint EUSD for a contract address](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L181) that use the minted EUSD for different tasks to profit the user, user will not be able to repay his debt, since the caller in this case, according to the design of `_repay` must be the contract itself otherwise the function will revert due to insufficient funding since it try to [transfer tokens from the msg.sender](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L199-L203) who doesn't hold token.

## Impact

In liquidate and borrow DeFi, the main repay function should be designed to allow user to repay his debt either he minted for himself or another address, and if the protocol wants to allow users to repay for another user, this is done through a different function. But the repay flow here is not applicable with what the user except. 

User will not be able to repay his debt and this expose his collateral to be liquidated.

## Proof of Concept

Following steps applied to PeUSD vault, but it affects the EUSD vault as well.

1. Alice call [mint](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L96) and specify the `onBehalfOf` to contract address.
2. Function [_mintPeUSD](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L181) will mint new PeUSD tokens to the contract address.
3. And it [increase the totalAmount borrowed](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L179) for the provider which in this case is Alice.
4. If Alice wants to repay her dept, she will call [burn](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L110) function passing the contract address as `onBehalfOf`
5. The function `_repay` will try to [transfer PeUSD token](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/lybra/pools/base/LybraPeUSDVaultBase.sol#L199) passing the `provider` address which is Alice. But since Alice not the one who minted the PeUSD and she doesn't hold any tokens to be transfer nor burned the function continue without reverting but no state change, and borrowed amount of Alice remain as it.

## Foundry test

1. Before running the test we need to do some changes

Change the constructor of `LybraEUSDVaultBase` as follows to make it work with the EUSDMock:

```
constructor(address _collateralAsset, address _etherOracle, address _configurator, address eusdAddress) {
        collateralAsset = IERC20(_collateralAsset);
        configurator = Iconfigurator(_configurator);
        EUSD = IEUSD(eusdAddress);
        etherOracle = IPriceFeed(_etherOracle);
    }
```

Change the constructor of `LybraStETHVault` as folows:

```
constructor(address _config, address _stETH, address _oracle, address eusd) LybraEUSDVaultBase(_stETH, _oracle, _config, eusd) {
    }
```

Modify the [_mintShares](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-lybra/blob/main/contracts/mocks/stETHMock.sol#L461) of the `stETHMock` to be public so we can mint tokens to use as collateral asset.

