# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-autonolas/blob/2a095eb1f8359be349d23af67089795fb0be4ed1/tokenomics/contracts/GenericBondCalculator.sol#L70-L92


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Upon creation of a new bond product, an attacker with enough funds could temporarily unbalance the Uniswap pool from which the price will be fetched, so that the newly created bond has a pumped `priceLP`. After that, the attacker could deposit a large amount of the product token into the newly created product in exchange of *"discounted"* OLAS, which would lead to a possible governance takeover scenario where the attacker had the majority of OLAS available (as the attacker would only need a $3\%$ of the total voting power, check [this](https://github.com/valory-xyz/autonolas-aip/blob/aip-3/content/aips/core-enhancing-autonolas-protocol-security.md#motivation)).

## Proof of Concept

It's a well known issue that using `getReserves` on an Uniswap pair with *"low"* liquidity to calculate the price of one of the tokens is dangerous as it can be manipulable via whales, flashloans and MEV bots. From the provided tests:

[**tokenomics/test/Depository.t.sol, line 120 and 121**](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-autonolas/blob/2a095eb1f8359be349d23af67089795fb0be4ed1/tokenomics/test/Depository.t.sol#L119C1-L121C70)

```solidity
    function testCreateProduct() public {

        ...

        uint256 priceLP = depository.getCurrentPriceLP(pair);
        depository.create(pair, priceLP, supplyProductOLAS, vesting);

        ...
    }
```

it can be seen that the usual workflow will be the following:

1. Fetch price by calling `Depository::GetCurrentPriceLP -> Uniswap::getReserves` and doing some maths.
2. Create the product with the derived `priceLP` from the previous step.

Unfortunately,

[**GenericBondCalculator, function getCurrentPriceLP**](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-autonolas/blob/2a095eb1f8359be349d23af67089795fb0be4ed1/tokenomics/contracts/GenericBondCalculator.sol#L70C1-L92C6)

```solidity
    function getCurrentPriceLP(address token) external view returns (uint256 priceLP)
    {
        IUniswapV2Pair pair = IUniswapV2Pair(token);
        uint256 totalSupply = pair.totalSupply();

        if (totalSupply > 0) {
            address token0 = pair.token0();
            address token1 = pair.token1();
            uint256 reserve0;
            uint256 reserve1;

            (reserve0, reserve1, ) = pair.getReserves(); // @audit red flag -> whales, flashloans -> manipulable

            if (token0 == olas || token1 == olas) {
                if (token0 == olas) {
                    reserve1 = reserve0;
                }

                priceLP = (reserve1 * 1e18) / totalSupply; // @audit no TWAP, no oracle, just raw price from reserves
            }
        }
    }
```

does calculate the exchange price by fetching the reserves of the Uniswap pair, which do reflect the exchange ratio at a **SINGLE** point of time, that is, even if the price were constant, if the fetch took place right after a short squeeze, it will use that value regardless of whether it reflects the real market or not. Because of that, the next situation is possible:

1. The Autonolas owner executes the transactions mentioned above, with the tokens to be used being USDT and OLAS (as an example, see [here](https://www.geckoterminal.com/es/eth/pools/0x41480b7bfb45ab2add9f461af50c5953a3fbfa34))
2. Bob, a malicious user watching the mempool, sees this and the fact that the given pool has very low liquidity (~102K at the time of writing this) and sends the following transactions
   1. First, pumps the price of the LP token by unbalancing the Uniswap pool **BEFORE** the Autonolas owner fetches the pair price by calling `Depository, function getCurrentPriceLP`
   2. Let both transactions execute and the newly bond product be created
   3. Get a loan of USDT (or if he is a whale, use his own funds), front-run any depositor/actor and call `Depository, function deposit` with `productId` being the product ID of the newly bond product and `tokenAmount` enough to satisfy the mint cap and the product supply
   4. Mint all the OLAS available *"by free"* (depending of the dump price in step 1.i) due to the `payout` calculation in:

[**Depository, function deposit**](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-autonolas/blob/2a095eb1f8359be349d23af67089795fb0be4ed1/tokenomics/contracts/Depository.sol#L320)

```solidity
    function deposit(uint256 productId, uint256 tokenAmount) external
        returns (uint256 payout, uint256 maturity, uint256 bondId)
    {

        ...

        payout = IGenericBondCalculator(bondCalculator).calculatePayoutOLAS(tokenAmount, product.priceLP);

        ...

        // Deposit that token amount to mint OLAS tokens in exchange
        ITreasury(treasury).depositTokenForOLAS(msg.sender, tokenAmount, token, payout);

        ...
    }
```

[**GenericBondCalculator, function calculatePayoutOLAS**](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-12-autonolas/blob/2a095eb1f8359be349d23af67089795fb0be4ed1/tokenomics/contracts/GenericBondCalculator.sol#L46C1-L65C6)

```solidity
    function calculatePayoutOLAS(uint256 tokenAmount, uint256 priceLP) external view
        returns (uint256 amountOLAS)
    {

        // @audit some comments ...

        uint256 totalTokenValue = mulDiv(priceLP, tokenAmount, 1); // @audit manipulable, 5 * 100 = 50 * 10 = 500 * 1 = ...

        // Check for the cumulative LP tokens value limit
        if (totalTokenValue > type(uint192).max) {
            revert Overflow(totalTokenValue, type(uint192).max);
        }

        // Amount with the discount factor is IDF * priceLP * tokenAmount / 1e36
        // At this point of time IDF is bound by the max of uint64, and totalTokenValue is no bigger than the max of uint192
        amountOLAS = ITokenomics(tokenomics).getLastIDF() * totalTokenValue / 1e36; // @audit dump and pump
    }
```

Depending on the amount of OLAS earned, and if the process were to be repeated again and again for every product, then Bob could have under his control a majority of the OLAS supply and take over the governance of the project (as veOLAS is the governance token being used, which is a virtual representation of the underlying OLAS, and the governance is just a fork of the OZ one).

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Fetch the price directly from an off-chain oracle or implement a TWAP approach to calculate the price correctly, avoiding markets manipulations like the described one.






