# Vulnerability details

## Impact

To drain user balances without permission, the key balances and approval check happens in `_doInteraction()`: [ocean.sol#_doInteraction](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-11-shellprotocol/blob/485de7383cdf88284ee6bcf2926fb7c19e9fb257/src/ocean/Ocean.sol#L380-L430)

```solidity
    function _doInteraction(
        Interaction calldata interaction,
        address userAddress
    )
        internal
        returns (uint256 inputToken, uint256 inputAmount, uint256 outputToken, uint256 outputAmount)
    {
        // Ether payments are push only.  We always wrap ERC-X tokens using pull
        // payments, so we cannot wrap Ether using the same pattern.
        // We unwrap ERC-X tokens using push payments, so we can unwrap Ether
        // the same way.
        if (msg.value != 0) {
            inputToken = 0;
            inputAmount = 0;
            outputToken = WRAPPED_ETHER_ID;
            outputAmount = msg.value;
            emit EtherWrap(msg.value, userAddress);
        } else {
            // Begin by unpacking the interaction type and the external contract
            (InteractionType interactionType, address externalContract) = _unpackInteractionTypeAndAddress(interaction);


            // Determine the specified token based on the interaction type and the
            // interaction's external contract address, inputToken, outputToken,
            // and metadata fields. The specified token is the token
            // whose amount the user specifies.
            uint256 specifiedToken = _getSpecifiedToken(interactionType, externalContract, interaction);


            // Here we call _executeInteraction(), which is just a big
            // if... else if... block branching on interaction type.
            // Each branch sets the inputToken and outputToken and their
            // respective amounts. This abstraction is what lets us treat
            // interactions uniformly.
            (inputToken, inputAmount, outputToken, outputAmount) = _executeInteraction(
                interaction, interactionType, externalContract, specifiedToken, interaction.specifiedAmount, userAddress
            );
        }


        // if _executeInteraction returned a positive value for inputAmount,
        // this amount must be deducted from the user's Ocean balance
        if (inputAmount > 0) {
            // since uint, same as (inputAmount != 0)
            _burn(userAddress, inputToken, inputAmount);
        }


        // if _executeInteraction returned a positive value for outputAmount,
        // this amount must be credited to the user's Ocean balance
        if (outputAmount > 0) {
            // since uint, same as (outputAmount != 0)
            _mint(userAddress, outputToken, outputAmount);
        }
    }
```
When this function is called, `userAddress` is the address whose balances will be impacted. This means an attacker cannot directly drain another user's balances.

However, there is a risk with the `forwardedDoInteraction()` function: [ocean.sol#forwardedDoInteraction](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-11-shellprotocol/blob/485de7383cdf88284ee6bcf2926fb7c19e9fb257/src/ocean/Ocean.sol#L256-L268)

```solidity
    function forwardedDoInteraction(
        Interaction calldata interaction,
        address userAddress
    )
        external
        payable
        override
        onlyApprovedForwarder(userAddress)
        returns (uint256 burnId, uint256 burnAmount, uint256 mintId, uint256 mintAmount)
    {
        emit ForwardedOceanTransaction(msg.sender, userAddress, 1);
        return _doInteraction(interaction, userAddress);
    }
```
Here the `userAddress` is specified by the caller. The only protection is the `onlyApprovedForwarder` modifier which checks: [ocean.sol#modifier onlyApprovedForwarder](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-11-shellprotocol/blob/485de7383cdf88284ee6bcf2926fb7c19e9fb257/src/ocean/Ocean.sol#L185-L188)

```solidity
    modifier onlyApprovedForwarder(address userAddress) {
        if (!isApprovedForAll(userAddress, msg.sender)) revert FORWARDER_NOT_APPROVED();
        _;
    }
```
This means if a malicious forwarder contract is granted approval by a user, it can drain balances by specifying any user address it wants.

The risk comes from users approving without realizing the forwarder gets access to drain balances. An unsuspecting user may approve a forwarder to "save gas" without considering the drain risk. This could severely impact users who approve forwarders without understanding the privilege escalation risk.

## Proof of Concept

This risk enables an attacker to drain arbitrary user balances by exploiting the forwarding mechanism. Specifically, if a malicious contract is granted `isApprovedForAll` permission by a user, it can specify any `userAddress` in `forwardedDoInteraction()` and drain their tokens.

For example:

```solidity
MaliciousForwarder forwarder = MaliciousForwarder(0x123...);

// Victim approves forwarder contract  
victim.setApprovalForAll(address(forwarder), true); 

// Attacker uses forwarder to drain victim's tokens
forwarder.forwardedDoInteraction(drainInteraction, victim.address);
```

This could allow the loss of all assets for any user that unwittingly grants the forwarding permission. Because `forwardedDoInteraction()` relies on `isApprovedForAll` for authorization. This approval is not limited to specific token IDs. So if granted, the forwarder has permission to drain any and all tokens belonging to that user. There is no way to restrict the approval to prevent draining assets that did not originate from the forwarder.

Can be triggered whenever the following sequence occurs.

1. Victim approves forwarder contract
2. Attacker uses forwarder to execute `forwardedDoInteraction` targeting victim account

This drain can be performed repeatedly as long as approval remains in place.

Now we can see the root cause of the risk of draining user balances stems from the overprivileged access granted to forwarder contracts via the `isApprovedForAll` functionality. 

The expected behavior is that forwarders, once approved, should only be able to interact with the specific tokens or token types that a user has allowed them access to. However, the current `onlyApprovedForwarder` modifier check does not restrict or scope the approval in any way: [Ocean.sol#onlyApprovedForwarder](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-11-shellprotocol/blob/485de7383cdf88284ee6bcf2926fb7c19e9fb257/src/ocean/Ocean.sol#L185-L188)

```solidity
modifier onlyApprovedForwarder(address userAddress) {
    if (!isApprovedForAll(userAddress, msg.sender)) revert FORWARDER_NOT_APPROVED();
    _;
}
```

Means that once approved, a malicious forwarder contract can drain ANY tokens by specifying an arbitrary `userAddress`:

```solidity
function drainTokens(address userAddress) external {
  Interaction memory maliciousInteraction;

  ocean.forwardedDoInteraction(
     maliciousInteraction,
     userAddress
  );
}
```

The deviation from expected behavior is that forwarder access is not restricted to specific token IDs or token types. This effectively grants a carte blanche approval to the forwarder over all user assets.

The security implication is that a user may unwittingly approve a malicious contract as a forwarder, which then programmatically drained all tokens via `forwardedDoInteraction()`.

## Tools Used

Vs

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Scope `isApprovedForAll` to certain token IDs, revoke approval after interactions or emit separate events for forwarded transactions to increase visibility into the elevated privilege abuse.

The goal is to restrict the implicit trust granted to forwarders in the system. 



