# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Position that have bad and malicious parameters value can be denied/vetoed by qualified pool share holders by calling the "deny" method on the position. If a position is denied/vetoed, it cannot ever be used to mint Frankencoins, but it can still be challenged. In case a new position is both challenged and vetoed, the challenge cannot be averted any more and the collateral is simply auctioned off to the highest bidder.

This state of position where both challenged and vetoed can be abused by the attacker to mint arbitrary amount of Frankencoin

## Proof of Concept

PoC Scenario  :

1) user propose open position using collateral token A (0 decimals) with the key parameters below :

min collateral = 10
initial collateral = 10
challenge period = 1 seconds
zchf price per unit of the collateral = 100000000000 * (10 ** 36) // this can be any high amount

the other parameters can be set to usual as not relevant to the attack.

2) The qualified share holders see this malicious open position and vetoed it.

3) Attacker challenge his own position, with providing minimum collateral token A (10)

4) No bidder would attracted to this challenge since the collateral that will be bought is minimal, even though there are some bidders put bid, the attack still can work.

5) The challenge can't averted since it vetoed and challenged, so it has wait until challenge end time.

6) When `end()` is called at end time, `notifyChallengeSucceeded()` will be called to calculate effective bid, volume and repayment :

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-frankencoin/blob/main/contracts/MintingHub.sol#L252-L276

```solidity
    function end(uint256 _challengeNumber, bool postponeCollateralReturn) public {
      ....
        (address owner, uint256 effectiveBid, uint256 volume, uint256 repayment, uint32 reservePPM) = challenge.position.notifyChallengeSucceeded(recipient, challenge.bid, challenge.size);
      ....
    }
```

inside `notifyChallengeSucceeded`, it will calculate the volume that will be used to calculate reward for challenger, by calculating the configured price multiplied with the position collateral or size, depend on which one is smaller :

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-frankencoin/blob/main/contracts/Position.sol#L329-L354

```solidity
    function notifyChallengeSucceeded(address _bidder, uint256 _bid, uint256 _size) external onlyHub returns (address, uint256, uint256, uint256, uint32) {
        challengedAmount -= _size;
        uint256 colBal = collateralBalance();
        if (_size > colBal){
            // Challenge is larger than the position. This can for example happen if there are multiple concurrent
            // challenges that exceed the collateral balance in size. In this case, we need to redimension the bid and
            // tell the caller that a part of the bid needs to be returned to the bidder.
            _bid = _divD18(_mulD18(_bid, colBal), _size);
            _size = colBal;
        }

        // Note that thanks to the collateral invariant, we know that
        //    colBal * price >= minted * ONE_DEC18
        // and that therefore
        //    price >= minted / colbal * E18
        // such that
        //    volumeZCHF = price * size / E18 >= minted * size / colbal
        // So the owner cannot maliciously decrease the price to make volume fall below the proportionate repayment.
        uint256 volumeZCHF = _mulD18(price, _size); // How much could have minted with the challenged amount of the collateral
        // The owner does not have to repay (and burn) more than the owner actually minted.  
        uint256 repayment = minted < volumeZCHF ? minted : volumeZCHF; // how much must be burned to make things even

        notifyRepaidInternal(repayment); // we assume the caller takes care of the actual repayment
        internalWithdrawCollateral(_bidder, _size); // transfer collateral to the bidder and emit update
        return (owner, _bid, volumeZCHF, repayment, reserveContribution);
    }
```

going back to the `end()` function, it will calculate the price for challenger using volume, and it will call ` zchf.notifyLoss()` since the fund needed is higher than the effective bid :

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-frankencoin/blob/main/contracts/MintingHub.sol#L265-L271

```solidity
        uint256 reward = (volume * CHALLENGER_REWARD) / 1000_000;
        uint256 fundsNeeded = reward + repayment;
        if (effectiveBid > fundsNeeded){
            zchf.transfer(owner, effectiveBid - fundsNeeded);
        } else if (effectiveBid < fundsNeeded){
            zchf.notifyLoss(fundsNeeded - effectiveBid); // this will be triggered
        }
```

inside `zchf.notifyLoss()` , it will first try to use the reserve funds to cover the needed fund, if not enough it will mint the new zchf token to pay the rewards.

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-frankencoin/blob/main/contracts/Frankencoin.sol#L280-L288

```solidity
   function notifyLoss(uint256 _amount) override external minterOnly {
      uint256 reserveLeft = balanceOf(address(reserve));
      if (reserveLeft >= _amount){
         _transfer(address(reserve), msg.sender, _amount);
      } else {
         _transfer(address(reserve), msg.sender, reserveLeft);
         _mint(msg.sender, _amount - reserveLeft);
      }
   }
```

and `end()` will transfer the zchf token reward to the attacker (based on 2% of arbitrary very high price multiplied by the min collateral), in the case of example parameters attacker will get 20_000_000_000 Frankencoin :

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-frankencoin/blob/main/contracts/MintingHub.sol#L272

```solidity
 zchf.transfer(challenge.challenger, reward); // pay out the challenger reward
```

