https://github.com/dopex-io/rdpx-eth-oracle/blob/5762c2339b1c45b87ff4db172e43cef4a0ff603a/src/RdpxEthOracle.sol#L244-L248
https://github.com/dopex-io/rdpx-eth-oracle/blob/5762c2339b1c45b87ff4db172e43cef4a0ff603a/src/RdpxEthOracle.sol#L130-L160


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The `RdpxV2Core._purchaseOptions` function is used to purchase rdpx put options. For the `put option` purchasing this function calls the `perpetualAtlanticVault.purchase` function. The `purchase` function calls the `getUnderlyingPrice` function to get the rDPX token price with respect to the collateral token.

The `perpetualAtlanticVault.getUnderlyingPrice` function calls the `RdpxEthOracle.getRdpxPriceInEth` function to retrieve the price.

The `RdpxEthOracle.getRdpxPriceInEth` function performs the price staleness check as shown below:

        require( //@audit-info - timePeriod -> 30 minutes , nonUpdateTolerance = 5 minutes
            blockTimestampLast + timePeriod + nonUpdateTolerance >
                block.timestamp,
            "RdpxEthOracle: UPDATE_TOLERANCE_EXCEEDED"
        );

So if the `block.timestamp >= blockTimestampLast + timePeriod + nonUpdateTolerance` then the transaction will revert since the price is stale. In the above `require` statement both `timePeriod` and `nonUpdateTolerance` are fixed values. `blockTimestampLast` value is updated based on the last UniswapV2 price oracle update for the given `rDPX-CollateralToken` liquidity pool.

If the price is stale then we can call the `RdpxEthOracle.update` function to update the `rDPX` price.

There is an issue with the `perpetualAtlanticVault.getUnderlyingPrice` function implementation as shown in the below scenario:

1. `RdpxV2Core._purchaseOptions` function is called to purchase a `rDPX put option`.
2.  `perpetualAtlanticVault.purchase` function is called which in turn calls the `perpetualAtlanticVault.getUnderlyingPrice` function to get the price of the `rDPX` price with respect to collateral token.
3. `RdpxEthOracle.getRdpxPriceInEth` function is called to retrieve the price of `rDPX` but the `block.timestamp >= blockTimestampLast + timePeriod + nonUpdateTolerance` is true, hence the entire transaction will revert.
4. Since the `RdpxV2Core._purchaseOptions` transaction is computationally extensive, the transaction revert will waste considerable amount of gas units thus incurring loss of funds to the transaction originator.

But the above transaction reverted because the `blockTimestampLast` was not updated correctly by calling the `RdpxEthOracle.update` external function. Hence this resulted in unnecessary gas wastage (loss of funds to the transaction originator) as a result of stale price feed data.

## Proof of Concept

