# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Ethena uses delta-neutral strategies to hedge fluctuations of collateral values, partially ensuring a 1:1 collateralization ratio. However, such 1:1 ratio can still be violated unexpectedly during minting and redeeming order on-chain settlement process from EthenaMinting.sol.

In EthenaMinting.sol - `mint()`/ `redeem()` to `verifyOrder()` flow, there is only check on transaction expiry timestamp, but no check on collateral amount is still within an acceptable slippage value. Lack of on-chain slippage check, will permit unexpected lower than acceptable collateral value to transfer during `mint()`, or unexpected higher than acceptable collateral value to send to the user during `redeem()`. 

Although the protocol's doc claimed to have accounted for slippage in initial pricing- [`Slippage & execution fees are included in the price when minting & redeeming`](https://ethena-labs.gitbook.io/ethena-labs/10CaMBZwnrLWSUWzLS2a/solution-overview/usde-overview), this doesn't prevent on-chain settlement of the collateral transfer at an unfair price due to the off-chain mechanism involved.

## Proof of Concept
Based on [doc](https://ethena-labs.gitbook.io/ethena-labs/10CaMBZwnrLWSUWzLS2a/video-guides/how-to-mint-usde), the usde minting starts by user interaction with off-chain mechanism, signing the amount1 of usde and the amount2 of collateral token exchanged as suggested by off-chain calculation. The exact amount1 and amount2 will be used in order submission for `mint()`/`redeem()` from EthenaMinting.sol on-chain.

```solidity
// contracts/EthenaMinting.sol
  function mint(Order calldata order, Route calldata route, Signature calldata signature)
    external
    override
    nonReentrant
    onlyRole(MINTER_ROLE)
    belowMaxMintPerBlock(order.usde_amount)
  {
    if (order.order_type != OrderType.MINT) revert InvalidOrder();
|>  verifyOrder(order, signature);
...
