(https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-10-ethena/blob/ee67d9b542642c9757a6b826c82d0cae60256509/contracts/EthenaMinting.sol#L346)

Suppose the case of user minting 10 ether stETH through dApp UI, and the calculation at the time of query is 9950 ether USDe  (suppose 1 stETH = 10000 USD, 10000 ether based on price query - 25 ether fee - 25 ether slippage= 9950 ether). 

(1) The user signed their signature on 10 ether -> 9950 ether at timestamp A.

(2) `MINTER_ROLE` picked up the user-signed signature and submit the order on the user's behalf through `mint()` on EthenaMinting.sol at timestamp B. At timestamp B, price of stETH dropped to 1 stETH = 9900 USD, the user sent 1 stETH is now worth 9900 USD, and 9900 ether - 25 ether fee = 9875 ether.

(3) `MINTER_ROLE`'s transaction didn't settle on-chain until timestamp C. At timestamp C, price of stETH dropped further to 1 stETH = 9850 USD, the user sent 1 stETH is now worth 9850 USD, and 9850 ether - 25 ether fee = 9825 ether. At the same transaction, 9950 ether of USDe is minted to the user. Now user receives 9950 ether -9825 ether = 125 ether more in USD value compared to the actual USD value of collateral deposited. 

In the above example, the USDe minted to the user should be considered undercollateralized. This under-collateralization happened before the 'delta-neutral' trading and cannot be offset by hedging. Even though in `mint()`, each `Order` has its expiry timestamp, but this only prevents stale transaction from being executed but doesn't prevent slippage as described in the example above. 

The above POC can also take place in a similar manner in `redeem()` user flow as well. This means, there are multiple time windows (between timestamps A and B, B and C ) for both user flows that potentially allow under-collateralization at minting or redeeming. I consider this a vulnerability that might result in material loss critical to stablecoin stability. 

On-chain slippage checks cannot be substituted by any preemptive slippage accounting.


