As the above code shows, the price for rsETH token is calculated using totalETHInPool and rsEthSupply.
But in the depositAsset function the asset is first transfered to the system and this makes the rsETHPrice bigger than it should be. And this reduces the rsETHToken amount compared to expected amount.
For example, suppose that the rsETH price is 1 eth and total asset eth amount is 10 eth. If the user tries to deposit 10 eth worth asset, the user should receive 10 rsETH token. But since the asset of user is transfered to the pool before minting, the total amount of asset is changed to 20 eth and the rsETH price is 20 / 10 = 2 (eth) and the user will receive only 5 rsETH token. After minting the rsETH price is (10 + 10) / 15 = 1.33 (eth).
The price will keep rising as the deposit goes on.

