https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-11-kelp/blob/c5fdc2e62c5e1d78769f44d6e34a6fb9e40c00f0/src/LRTDepositPool.sol#L79-L88


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
When users deposit assets, due to an incorrect execution order, it results in the incorrect calculation of the RsETH price (overstated). This will lead to users not obtaining the expected equivalent value of assets, and such losses will increase as the value of the deposited assets grows. Since each deposit causes an increase in the RsETH price, it also triggers the issue of gaining profits through front-running.

## Proof of Concept
In depositAsset function, the contract will first transfer assets from the user's account and then mint RsETH for the user.
```solidity
function depositAsset(
        address asset,
        uint256 depositAmount
    )
        external
        whenNotPaused
        nonReentrant
        onlySupportedAsset(asset)
    {
        ......
        if (!IERC20(asset).transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), depositAmount)) {
            revert TokenTransferFailed();
        }
        // interactions
        uint256 rsethAmountMinted = _mintRsETH(asset, depositAmount);

        emit AssetDeposit(asset, depositAmount, rsethAmountMinted);
    }

```
 When calling the _mintRsETH function, the contract will first invoke getRsETHAmountToMint to calculate the quantity to be minted.
```
    function _mintRsETH(address _asset, uint256 _amount) private returns (uint256 rsethAmountToMint) {
        (rsethAmountToMint) = getRsETHAmountToMint(_asset, _amount);
        address rsethToken = lrtConfig.rsETH();
        // mint rseth for user
        IRSETH(rsethToken).mint(msg.sender, rsethAmountToMint);
    }
```
It can be observed that in the getRsETHAmountToMint function, the lrtOracle contract is called to query the prices of RsETH and the asset. The value of depositing the asset is then calculated by dividing the asset's price by the price of RsETH, thus determining the quantity of RsETH to mint for the user. From this, it can be understood that the developer intends to mint RsETH for the user with an equivalent value.
```
   function getRsETHAmountToMint(
        address asset,
        uint256 amount
    )
        public
        view
        override
        returns (uint256 rsethAmountToMint)
    {
      ......
        rsethAmountToMint = (amount * lrtOracle.getAssetPrice(asset)) / lrtOracle.getRSETHPrice();
    }
```
 When calculating the price of RsETH, the oracle will invoke the getTotalAssetDeposits function in the LRTDepositPool contract, which returns the total quantity of assets deposited.
```
    function getRSETHPrice() external view returns (uint256 rsETHPrice) {
        .......
        for (uint16 asset_idx; asset_idx < supportedAssetCount;) {
            address asset = supportedAssets[asset_idx];
            uint256 assetER = getAssetPrice(asset);
            uint256 totalAssetAmt = ILRTDepositPool(lrtDepositPoolAddr).getTotalAssetDeposits(asset);
            totalETHInPool += totalAssetAmt * assetER;
         ......
        }
        return totalETHInPool / rsEthSupply;
    }
```
The getTotalAssetDeposits function calls the getAssetDistributionData function to examine the asset's distribution data. getAssetDistributionData directly returns the quantity of assets deposited based on the current balance of the asset in the LRTDepositPool at that moment.
```
function getAssetDistributionData(address asset)
        public
        view
        override
        onlySupportedAsset(asset)
        returns (uint256 assetLyingInDepositPool, uint256 assetLyingInNDCs, uint256 assetStakedInEigenLayer)
    {   
        ......
        assetLyingInDepositPool = IERC20(asset).balanceOf(address(this));
        ......
    }
```
 Since assets have already been transferred from the user to the contract through transferFrom, the asset balance in LRTDepositPool has increased. As mintRsETH has not been executed yet to provide the user with RsETH, the total supply of RsETH has not changed, resulting in an artificially high price for RsETH. Furthermore, the calculated quantity of RsETH to be minted for the user in the getRsETHAmountToMint function will be reduced. This implies that the more valuable assets a user deposits, the higher the RsETH price, leading to greater losses for the user. The user will never receive an equivalent amount of RsETH. Even after the minting process, as the minted quantity of RsETH is less than expected, the final RsETH price remains artificially high. Each deposit causes an increase in the RsETH price, thereby increasing the risk of front-running for profit.

  For example, if the current pool contract has 1 ETH, and the total supply of RsETH is also 1 ETH, the current price of RsETH is 1 ETH. Now, Alice decides to deposit 9 ETH into the pool contract, resulting in a total of 10 ETH in the contract. The total supply of RsETH remains at 1 ETH, causing the price of RsETH to surge to 10 ETH. Based on this price, Alice can only receive 9/10 = 0.9 RsETH, incurring a loss of nine-tenths. Even though the total supply of RsETH increases after the deposit, the price at that point is approximately 10/1.9, which is roughly equal to 5 ETH. The impact on the price remains significant.
