# Vulnerability details

## Explanation
For depositing assets into the `LRTDepositPool.sol` contract users will use the `depositAsset()` function. This function will have higher gas costs when the project starts growing, which will make all the calls to the function to revert, not letting any new deposits. Here is a brief explanation of why this function will stop working.

When using `depositAsset()`, `getAssetCurrentLimit()` will be called.
````
        if (depositAmount > getAssetCurrentLimit(asset)) {
            revert MaximumDepositLimitReached();
        }
````
`getAssetCurrentLimit()` will need to get the deposit limit for the asset being deposited and also all the total deposited assets of that type. For doing the latter, `getAssetDistributionData()` function will be used, which will need to iterate through `nodeDelegatorQueue()` array. This array has a limit of 10 for the moment, but it's intended to make it bigger.

````
        uint256 ndcsCount = nodeDelegatorQueue.length;
        for (uint256 i; i < ndcsCount;) {
            assetLyingInNDCs += IERC20(asset).balanceOf(nodeDelegatorQueue[i]);
            assetStakedInEigenLayer += INodeDelegator(nodeDelegatorQueue[i]).getAssetBalance(asset);
            unchecked {
                ++i;
            }
        }
````
````
        maxNodeDelegatorCount = 10;
````

After this, rsETH will be minted for the user using `_mintRsETH()`. 
````
        uint256 rsethAmountMinted = _mintRsETH(asset, depositAmount);
````
This function will calculate the `rsethAmountToMint` by calling `getRsETHAmountToMint()`.
````
        (rsethAmountToMint) = getRsETHAmountToMint(_asset, _amount);
````
This function will get the price of rsETH by using `getRSETHPrice()`.
````
        rsethAmountToMint = (amount * lrtOracle.getAssetPrice(asset)) / lrtOracle.getRSETHPrice();
````
This calculation will consume a lot of gas. For getting the price of rsETH, the code will go through all the assets the project currently holds in all of their NodeDelegators and all the asserts the NodeDelegators have invested into the EigenLayer's strategies.
````
        for (uint16 asset_idx; asset_idx < supportedAssetCount;) {
            address asset = supportedAssets[asset_idx];
            uint256 assetER = getAssetPrice(asset);


            uint256 totalAssetAmt = ILRTDepositPool(lrtDepositPoolAddr).getTotalAssetDeposits(asset);
            totalETHInPool += totalAssetAmt * assetER;


            unchecked {
                ++asset_idx;
            }
        }


        return totalETHInPool / rsEthSupply;
    }
````

With the current implementation of Kelp, there are 3 supported assets: stETH, cbETH and rETH. Also, there is a maximum of 10 NodeDelegators. This means that for depositing funds into the pool at least all the NodeDelegators balance and invested amount in strategies of the deposited assets will be iterated twice, and the rest of the of the assets will be iterated once. 

Also, we have to take into account that there is no possible way of removing Node Delegators if this happens, since there is no functionality for it. Once this issue arises, there will be no way of fixing it.
## Impact
Users will run out of gas when depositing assets into the pool when calling `depositAsset()`, which will make it impossible to deposit.

## Proof of Concept
Currently Kelp supports 3 assets and 10 Node Delegators, but its intention is to grow this numbers. Supported assets will grow since the intention is to support all the LSTs that EigenLayer gives support to. Imagine the case where after some time Kelp supports 5 assets and has 20 Node Delegators.

1. Alice tries depositing by calling `depositAsset()` function.
2. The function calculates the `getAssetCurrentLimit()`. For this, `nodeDelegatorQueue` will be iterated, which has a length of 20.
3. Now the price of rsETH is calculated for getting the amount to mint for the users after his deposit by calling `getRSETHPrice()`.
4. `getRSETHPrice()` will need to iterate through all the supported assets and all the node delegators for each asset. This will have a specially high cost. Take into account that the `nodeDelegatorQueue` array, which has a length of 20, will be iterated 5 times.
5. With all the gas costs of fetching prices with the Chainlink oracle, balances from the contracts, deposited funds in each strategy of EigenLayer and iterating thorough all the Node Delegators, the transaction runs out of gas, reverting the transaction and making it impossible for users to deposit. 
## Tools Used
Manual review.
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
I suggest adding a function for removing Node Delegators if this happens. The main problem is that with the current implementation there is no way of fixing this issue, so once it happens, it will always happen. I think the best approach is to have a solution for removing gas costs, such as removing Node Delegators.



## Assessed type

DoS

