# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-10-ens/blob/ed25379c06e42c8218eb1e80e141412496950685/contracts/ERC20MultiDelegate.sol#L57-L63
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-10-ens/blob/ed25379c06e42c8218eb1e80e141412496950685/contracts/ERC20MultiDelegate.sol#L16-L19


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

There is no way to revoke delegations or withdraw tokens once delegated. Users may want an escape hatch if a delegate turns malicious.

## Proof of Concept

The contract is missing functionality for users to revoke delegations if needed. 

The issue is that once tokens are delegated via the **[delegateMulti() function](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-10-ens/blob/ed25379c06e42c8218eb1e80e141412496950685/contracts/ERC20MultiDelegate.sol#L57-L63)**, there is no way for the user to withdraw or revoke that delegation.

The concerning code is in the `ERC20ProxyDelegator` contract deployed by `delegateMulti()`: **[ERC20MultiDelegate.sol#ERC20ProxyDelegator](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-10-ens/blob/ed25379c06e42c8218eb1e80e141412496950685/contracts/ERC20MultiDelegate.sol#L16-L19)**

```solidity
// ERC20ProxyDelegator.sol

constructor(ERC20Votes _token, address _delegate) {
  _token.approve(_delegate, uint(-1));
  _token.delegate(_delegate); 
}
```

On lines 17-18, the delegation is approved with a max uint value and delegated to the target. This proxy contract has no functions to revoke approval or withdraw delegation.

Since the problem is, that there are **no functions to revoke this delegation or withdraw it back to the user.** 

This means if a malicious delegate emerges later, users have no way to get their voting power back!

Some examples of risks this introduces:

- Delegates could act against users' interests if they turn malicious.
- Users are locked into delegations, even if delegates change their behavior.
- No escape hatch if a delegate is hacked or sells out.

Ideally, there should be a `revokeDelegation` function like.

```solidity
function revokeDelegation() external {
   token.revokeApproval(delegate);
   token.delegate(msg.sender); 
}
```

This would allow users to take back control of their voting power if needed.

Without any revocation functionality, users are fully dependent on delegates acting honestly and benevolently in perpetuity. This complete lack of recourse is highly dangerous in the case delegates turn malicious for any reason.

He's an example of proof here that will help substantiate this vulnerability. Here is a demonstration:

```solidity

contract Vulnerable {
  function delegate(address delegate) external {
    // approve and delegate voting power
    ERC20(token).approve(delegate, uint(-1));
    ERC20(token).delegate(delegate);
  }
}

// No revokeDelegation function!

contract Attacker {
  function exploit(address user) external {
    Vulnerable(vuln).delegate(attackerAddress); 

    // user now locked into delegating to attackerAddress  
    // No way to revoke due to missing function

  }
}
```

This shows:

1. Vulnerable contract lacks a `revokeDelegation` function

2. Malicious contracts can force delegation from the user 

3. User has no way to withdraw delegation from a malicious address

4. Lack of escape hatch locks users into unwanted delegations

Adding a `revokeDelegation` function would enable users to withdraw voting power and prevent this lock-in vulnerability.

This example demonstrates the inability to escape malicious delegations due to the lack of a revocation mechanism.

## Tools Used

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

The fix would be to add a `revokeDelegation()` function to `ERC20ProxyDelegator`:

```solidity
function revokeDelegation() external {
  _token.revokeApproval(delegate);
  _token.delegate(msg.sender);
}
```

And allow the delegator to call this through the main `ERC20MultiDelegate` contract if needed.

This would provide users an escape hatch to revoke malicious or unwanted delegations. The current lack of any revocation presents a major limitation.


