As seen above, a user doesn't need to send any native token to pass as `msg.value` during the transaction, and there is no check to ensure there is `msg.value` sent to cover `gasValue`. It should be noted that InterchainTokenService contract can have native token balances, partially because the current implementation of `multicall()` feature requires there is sufficient balance in the contract for it to work, and also the BaseProxy contract of InterchainTokenService can receive any native token balance transfer from users, operators, and contract owner. 

I think this is a high severity because the attack is very easy to carry out at little extra cost for malicious users, and there is no limit as to how much funds malicious users can steal from the contract.
In addition, if there is a native token balance transfer to the contract by other users, a malicious user can also time their attack to spend the transferred funds. 

## Tools Used
Manual review
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
(1) To mitigate the vulnerability, add a check to ensure `msg.value` is no less than `gasValue` in `deployRemoteCanonicalToken()` or `deployCustomTokenManager()` or `deployAndRegisterRemoteStandardizedToken()`.
(2) Adding this check might require modifying the behavior of `multicall()` function as well because these functions might revert when they are called inside `multicall()`. 
But I think it's important to mitigate the risk detailed above because these two functions don't necessarily get called as part of the `multicall()` and need to be considered as independent. 


