# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/2f9b234bb8222d5fbe934beafede56bfb4522641/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L325
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/2f9b234bb8222d5fbe934beafede56bfb4522641/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L678


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Canonical bridges will use a lock-unlock token manager to transfer assets from one chain to the other.
A user locks tokens on one chain (by transferring them to the manager) and than is allowed to unlock them on the receiving chain (by receiving a transfer from the manager).
The canonical bridge is implemented by deploying a standardized token on the receiving chain that will be used by the manager to bridge the assets.
Due to how the parameters are being passed by the deployRemoteCanonicalToken function, the standard token deployed on the receiving chain won't have any token upon deployment, and also no one will be able to mint, due to lack of permissions, meaning that no tokens can be unlocked on the receiving chain.
Also since the only way to get tokens in and out of the token manager's balance, is through the bridging functionalities, this lack of liquidity means that token will stay locked on the sending bridge indefinitely.

## Proof of Concept
1) Lock-Unlock Token Manager (https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/2f9b234bb8222d5fbe934beafede56bfb4522641/contracts/its/token-manager/implementations/TokenManagerLockUnlock.sol#L16):
The canonical bridge uses a lock-unlock token manager which, essentially, enables users to transfer tokens to it's balance (to lock them), and than to receive a transfer from the manager to unlock them, with the following two functions:
```
    function _takeToken(address from, uint256 amount) internal override returns (uint256) {
        IERC20 token = IERC20(tokenAddress());
        uint256 balance = token.balanceOf(address(this));

        SafeTokenTransferFrom.safeTransferFrom(token, from, address(this), amount);

        // Note: This allows support for fee-on-transfer tokens
        return IERC20(token).balanceOf(address(this)) - balance;
    }
```
and
```
    function _giveToken(address to, uint256 amount) internal override returns (uint256) {
        IERC20 token = IERC20(tokenAddress());
        uint256 balance = IERC20(token).balanceOf(to);

        SafeTokenTransfer.safeTransfer(token, to, amount);

        return IERC20(token).balanceOf(to) - balance;
    }
```

2) The _giveToken listed above, on the receiving chain, uses the deployed standardized tokens as it's underlying asset.
This token, when using the `deployRemoteCanonicalToken` function (https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/2f9b234bb8222d5fbe934beafede56bfb4522641/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L325C14-L325C40), will mint 0 tokens upon deployment.
In fact calling `deployRemoteCanonicalToken` on the source chain will result in `_processDeployStandardizedTokenAndManagerPayload` (https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/2f9b234bb8222d5fbe934beafede56bfb4522641/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L678) being called on the destination chain. Which includes the deployment call:
```
 _deployStandardizedToken(tokenId, distributor, name, symbol, decimals, 0, distributor);
```
As can be noted the 6th argument of the `_deployStandardizedToken` function (correspoinding to `mintAmount`) (https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/2f9b234bb8222d5fbe934beafede56bfb4522641/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L841) is set to 0, meaning that upon deployment 0 tokens will be minted.
Also the `mint(address account, uint256 amount)` function of the `` contract (https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/2f9b234bb8222d5fbe934beafede56bfb4522641/contracts/its/token-implementations/StandardizedToken.sol#L76C14-L76C51) has a `onlyDistributor` modifier, which obviously, only allows the distributor address to mint.

3) The distributor role, which is usually an argument upon deployment of the various tokens, is set to an empty string, if the token is deployed by calling `deployRemoteCanonicalToken` (https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/2f9b234bb8222d5fbe934beafede56bfb4522641/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L325C14-L325C40), which internally calls:
```
_deployRemoteStandardizedToken(tokenId, tokenName, tokenSymbol, tokenDecimals, '', '', destinationChain, gasValue)
```
(the 5th and 6th arguments being, distributor and operator addresses)

This means that upon deployment the `_processDeployStandardizedTokenAndManagerPayload` function (https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/2f9b234bb8222d5fbe934beafede56bfb4522641/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L678) on the destination chain will use the address of the manager as distributor:
```
        address distributor = distributorBytes.length > 0 ? distributorBytes.toAddress() : tokenManagerAddress;
        _deployStandardizedToken(tokenId, distributor, name, symbol, decimals, 0, distributor);
```
This means that the only contract allowed to mint new tokens is the token manager, also the ` setDistributor(address distr)` function of the `Distributable` contract (https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/2f9b234bb8222d5fbe934beafede56bfb4522641/contracts/its/utils/Distributable.sol#L51), can only be called by the distributor it self.

The lock-unlock token manager has no minting functionalities, and no functionalities to give up the distributor role. And also due to the fact that upon deployment 0 tokens are being minted. On the receiving chain there will always be 0 tokens available, meaning that the _giveToken function won't be able to operate, and tokens cannot be withdrawn (or sent to the manager).

Since the only two ways to get token in and out of the manager are the bridging functionalities, this means that not only the user won't receive anything on the destination chain, but won't be able to get the locked tokens, on the source chain, out as well.


Here is a slightly customized version of the test provided by the project for the ITS. Place it in the `/test/its/` folder to preserve dependencies.
It verifies that the values used by the deploy functionalities match the ones described above.
The relevant checks are highlighted in the comments:
```
'use strict';

const chai = require('chai');
const { expect } = chai;
require('dotenv').config();
const { ethers } = require('hardhat');
const { AddressZero, MaxUint256 } = ethers.constants;
const { defaultAbiCoder, solidityPack, keccak256 } = ethers.utils;
const { Contract, Wallet } = ethers;

