# Lines of code

https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-periph-audit/blob/023751a4e987cf7c203ab25d3abba58f7344f213/contracts/Multicall/Multicall3.sol#L41-L61
https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-periph-audit/blob/023751a4e987cf7c203ab25d3abba58f7344f213/contracts/Multicall/Multicall3.sol#L63-L91


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
In the ```Multicall3.sol``` contract, when ```multicall(...)``` and ```multicallValue(...) are called respectively, default (or possibly empty) values are returned, this can lead to mishaps during contract deployment with the TapiocaDeployer contract .

## Proof of Concept
### In the ```multicall(...)``` case
The ```multicall(...)``` contract as shown below returns an array of struct each holding a boolean and a byte element data. However due to its implementation and array ```Result``` struct holding default values is return.
```
    function multicall(
        Call[] calldata calls
    ) public payable returns (Result[] memory returnData) {
        ...
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < length; ) {
            // @audit at this point, each returnData[i] is default value
            Result memory result = returnData[i];
            calli = calls[i];

            // @audit however result is only used to cache each return values for this particular call and as such it is not an array element and will not be recorded or automatically pushed into the returnData array
            (result.success, result.returnData) = calli.target.call(
                calli.callData
            );
            if (!result.success) {
                _getRevertMsg(result.returnData);
            }

            // @note add the line returnData[i] = result here
            unchecked {
                ++i;
            }
        }
    }
```
as shown below, each returnData[i] is default value 
```
Result memory result = returnData[i];
```
and result is used to cache this value which as shown below is used to hold the return value when a call is made to the ```calli``` target address as shown below:
```
(result.success, result.returnData) = calli.target.call( calli.callData );
```
Then the result call is checked for ```success``` but unfortunately, ```returnData[i]``` is not updated with the value of the ```result``` of this particular call and the loops index is increased to make the next call to the hence ```returnData[i]``` still holds default values.

### In the ```multicallValue(...)``` case
Similar explanation holds true for calling ```multicallValue(...)```. To reduce verbosity, i'll summarise as shown below, each returnData[i] is default value 
```
Result memory result = returnData[i];
```
and result is used to cache this value which as shown below is used to hold the return value when a call is made to the ```calli``` target address as shown below:
```
(result.success, result.returnData) = calli.target.call{value: val}(calli.callData);
```
Then the result call is checked for ```success``` but unfortunately, ```returnData[i]``` is not updated with the value of the ```result``` of this particular call and the loops index is increased to make the next call to the hence ```returnData[i]``` still holds default values(bool success = false and bytes returnData = 0x0).

## Tools Used
Manual review with a pair of eyes.


## Recommended Mitigation Steps
### the ```multicall()``` recommendation
add the line ```returnData[i] = result``` before incrementing the index as shown below.

```
    function multicall(
        Call[] calldata calls
    ) public payable returns (Result[] memory returnData) {

        ...
            if (!result.success) {
                _getRevertMsg(result.returnData);
            }

 +           returnData[i] = result;
            unchecked {
                ++i;
            }
        }
    }
```

### the ```multicallValue(...)``` recommendation
add the line ```returnData[i] = result``` before incrementing the index as shown below.
```
    function multicallValue(
        CallValue[] calldata calls
    ) public payable returns (Result[] memory returnData) {

        ...
            if (!result.success) {
                _getRevertMsg(result.returnData);
            }

 +           returnData[i] = result;
            unchecked {
                ++i;
            }
        }
    }
```


