# Lines of code

https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-bar-audit/blob/2286f80f928f41c8bc189d0657d74ba83286c668/contracts/markets/Market.sol#L34
https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-bar-audit/blob/2286f80f928f41c8bc189d0657d74ba83286c668/contracts/markets/Market.sol#L470


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Borrow must call updateExchange rate first

## Proof of Concept

In BigBang.sol, we have a [function updateExchangRate](https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-bar-audit/blob/2286f80f928f41c8bc189d0657d74ba83286c668/contracts/markets/Market.sol#L340)

```solidity
    function updateExchangeRate() public returns (bool updated, uint256 rate) {
        (updated, rate) = oracle.get("");

        if (updated) {
            require(rate > 0, "Market: invalid rate");
            exchangeRate = rate;
            emit LogExchangeRate(rate);
        } else {
            // Return the old rate if fetching wasn't successful
            rate = exchangeRate;
        }
    }
```

When we are calling function repay, we are calling updateExchangeRate() and accure() to fetch the latest exchange rate from the oracle

```solidity
updateExchangeRate();

accrue();

amount = _repay(from, to, part);
```

however, when we are borrowing, we are not calling updateExchangeRate first

```solidity
function borrow(
	address from,
	address to,
	uint256 amount
) public notPaused solvent(from) returns (uint256 part, uint256 share) {
	uint256 allowanceShare = _computeAllowanceAmountInAsset(
		from,
		exchangeRate,
		amount,
		asset.safeDecimals()
	);
	_allowedBorrow(from, allowanceShare);
	(part, share) = _borrow(from, to, amount);
}
```

but when borrowing, we are using the exchangeRate to calculate and [derive the
allowanceShare](https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-bar-audit/blob/2286f80f928f41c8bc189d0657d74ba83286c668/contracts/markets/Market.sol#L470)

```solidity
   function _computeAllowanceAmountInAsset(
        address user,
        uint256 _exchangeRate,
        uint256 borrowAmount,
        uint256 assetDecimals
    ) internal view returns (uint256) {
        uint256 maxBorrowabe = _computeMaxBorrowableAmount(user, _exchangeRate);

        uint256 shareRatio = _getRatio(
            borrowAmount,
            maxBorrowabe,
            assetDecimals
        );
        return (shareRatio * userCollateralShare[user]) / (10 ** assetDecimals);
    }

```

then determine how much we can borrow

in a low activity market, the updateExchangeRate in repay may not be called very often and user can borrow asset with outdated / stale exchange rate oracle price

In the case of when the fresh exchangeRate > stale exchange rate, user can borrow less asset than they should borrow

In the case of when the fresh exchangeRate < stale exchange rate, user can borrow more asset than they should (this is more dangerous) and can leads to the insolvency of the protocol quickly

## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

We reocmmnd the protocol always call updateExchangeRate in each action including borrow to make sure the exchange rate fetched from oracle is the updated oracle price


