# Lines of code

https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-periph-audit/blob/023751a4e987cf7c203ab25d3abba58f7344f213/contracts/Magnetar/MagnetarV2.sol#L1045


# Vulnerability details


# Vulnerability details
Magnetar is a multi-purpose helper contract designed to provide a simplified and consistent access point for users to interact with the broader Tapioca protocol.  `MagnetarV2#_permit()` is intended to allow users to call `permit()` on any contract that supports it. 

However, as written the `_permit()` function allows for an attacker to call any function on any contract with Magnetar as `msg.sender`, with the lone restriction that the first argument passed in the target call must be the same as the attacker's address.

`MagnetarV2#_permit()` is a private function that is only accessible through a call to Magnetar's multicall funciton, `burst()`. The attacker needs to provide a call with an "id" equal to 1 or 2 (`PERMIT_ALL` or `PERMIT`), a target contract to call, and calldata for the call he'd like to make. Note that it doesn't matter if we use PERMIT_ALl or PERMIT - the attack is the same.

```solidity
function burst(
        Call[] calldata calls
    ) external payable returns (Result[] memory returnData) {
        ...
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < length; i++) {
            Call calldata _action = calls[i];
            ...
            if (_action.id == PERMIT_ALL) { // Either of these will enter _permit
                _permit(
                    _action.target, // All parameters here attacker-controlled
                    _action.call,
                    true,
                    _action.allowFailure
                );
            } else if (_action.id == PERMIT) {
                _permit(
                    _action.target,
                    _action.call,
                    false,
                    _action.allowFailure
                );
            }
}
```
In `_permit()`, the function checks if we're using PERMIT or PERMIT_ALL, and decodes the data based on the appropriate struct. Note that Solidity is flexible when using abi.decode() as nothing is packed in calldata. We just need to pass in words of calldata that can fit in each type of the struct. For example, any uint160 would be fine as "address spender".

The first member of the decoded struct `permitData.owner` (first 32 bytes after the function selector in the attacker-supplied actionCalldata) is then validated to be equal to `msg.sender` in `_checkSender(permitData.owner)`.

Subsequently, `target` is called with the `actionCalldata` that the attacker supplied. Note that there is no validation of the function selector, the target, or any of the other parameters. The only restraint is that the first argument is `msg.sender`. 

```solidity
struct PermitData {
    address owner; // This must equal msg.sender in the decoding (attacker's address)
    address spender;
    uint256 value;
    uint256 deadline;
    uint8 v;
    bytes32 r;
    bytes32 s;
}

function _permit(
    address target,
    bytes calldata actionCalldata,
    bool permitAll,
    bool allowFailure
) private {
    if (permitAll) { // the decoded struct is different for permit/permitAll but attack is the same
        PermitAllData memory permitData = abi.decode(
            actionCalldata[4:],
            (PermitAllData)
        );
        _checkSender(permitData.owner); // require(permitData.owner == msg.sender)
    } else {

        PermitData memory permitData = abi.decode(
            actionCalldata[4:],
            (PermitData)
        );
        _checkSender(permitData.owner); // require(permitData.owner == msg.sender)
    }
    // At this point 
    (bool success, bytes memory returnData) = target.call(actionCalldata);
    if (!success && !allowFailure) {
        _getRevertMsg(returnData);
    }
}

```
One function that the attacker could then use magnetar to call is `ERC721#approve(address to, uint256 tokenId)` ([link](https://github.com/OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts/blob/master/contracts/token/ERC721/ERC721.sol#L115)):

```
function approve(address to, uint256 tokenId) public virtual {
    address owner = ownerOf(tokenId);
    if (to == owner) {
        revert ERC721InvalidOperator(owner);
    }
    // Does not revert if the caller isApprovedForAll
    if (_msgSender() != owner && !isApprovedForAll(owner, _msgSender())) {
        revert ERC721InvalidApprover(_msgSender());
    }

    _approve(to, tokenId);
}
```

If the victim has set Magnetar to be approvedForAll for any ERC721s, the attacker can call `MagnetarV2#_permit()` with `ERC721#approve(attacker, tokenId)` as the actionCalldata to grant himself approval to steal the ERC721. It is typical in normal operation for Magnetar to be approved for ERC721s; for example, in order to use `TAP_EXERCISE_OPTION`, Magnetar needs to be approved for the oTAP token. 

Note that this vulnerability is not restricted to just stealing ERC721s - this is just one possible attack when we have arbitrary execution with Magnetar as `msg.sender`. 

## Impact
High - theft of funds

