# Lines of code

https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tap-token-audit/blob/59749be5bc2286f0bdbf59d7ddc258ddafd49a9f/contracts/options/TapiocaOptionBroker.sol#L426-L427
https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tap-token-audit/blob/59749be5bc2286f0bdbf59d7ddc258ddafd49a9f/contracts/tokens/TapOFT.sol#L201
https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tap-token-audit/blob/59749be5bc2286f0bdbf59d7ddc258ddafd49a9f/contracts/options/TapiocaOptionBroker.sol#L567-L574


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
When activating a new epoch on the `TapiocaOptionBroker` by calling `newEpoch()` it retrieves this week's emission by calling `emitForWeek()` on `TapOFT`. This function is supposed to calculated and set the weekly emission in it's own contract and return the calculated value.
When a new epoch is activated in `TapiocaOptionBroker` it uses the returned value to set the emission gauges for the different pools
However when the emission has already been set in the `TapOFT` for the current week the return value is hardcoded to 0. Normally this isn't a problem as the minimum epoch time in `TapaiocaOptionBroker` is a week, so the `emitForWeek()` is only called once a week.
However `emitForWeek()` has no access control and can be called  by anyone.
Setting the week's TAP emission to 0 means no option can be executed in this epoch as executing options uses the weekly TAP emission to draw from.
An attacker can thus make sure no one can execute  any options for this epoch at the simple cost of calling `emitForWeek()`. This effectively blocks the TAP emission for as long as the attacker can keep front running the `newEpoch()` call. 
Note that this is easier as it sounds as the `newEpoch()` has a require statement where a minimum of 1 week needs to have passed since the last call, which the `emitForWeek()` does not.

## Proof of Concept
The weekly emission of TAP tokens is distributed over the different pools when  the `TapiocaOptionbroker`'s `newEpoch()` is called. The underlying `emitForWeek()`call to the `tapOFT` contract determines the total amount of TAP released in this week. 
The returned amount `epochTAP` is then used to set the gauges .
```solidity
    function newEpoch() external {
        ...
        // Extract TAP
        uint256 epochTAP = tapOFT.emitForWeek();
        _emitToGauges(epochTAP);
		...
    }
```

The `emitForWeek()` however returns the value 0 when the emissions for this week have already been set.
```solidity
