# Lines of code

https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-bar-audit/blob/master/contracts/markets/singularity/SGLLiquidation.sol#L97


# Vulnerability details

### Impact
Users who are liquidated through the `_orderBookLiquidation()` function call in the `liquidate()` function in `SGLLiquidation.sol` may still have insolvent accounts. Hence, they need to be liquidated again through the `liquidate()` function. This results in potential fund loss for users who buy the collateral. 

### Proof of Concept
An account is not solvent when the debt to collateral ratio exceeds 75% (as per spec).

During the `_orderBookLiquidation()` function call, the insolvent account's debt is liquidated to 25% of the collateral, the difference being calculated from the `_computeAssetAmountToSolvency()` function call on line 110. This amount is removed from the accounts debt on line 123. Then, the equivalent value in collateral (+ some fees) is removed from the account's collateral on line 133. The resulting debt to collateral ratio is hence (D-V)/(C-(V+F)), where V is the amount removed, D is the initial debt, F is the fee, and C is the initial collateral. This amount is not guaranteed to be healthy; the final ratio may still exceed 75%.


Users who call `liquidate()` are entitled to liquidate enough collateral until the account becomes healthy. A correct design should allow users to reach this condition in one call. With the current design, users may have to __call multiple times__, missing the best market values of collaterals, let alone wasting gas.

We demonstrate this bug in the following POC, which can be deployed and run.

#### Preconditions:
In order to run the POC, two preconditions need to be satisfied. The core reason for having these preconditions is that a `liquidationQueue` is necessary to invoke `_orderBookLiquidation`. However, to save space in the POC code which is already quite substantial, we apply these preconditions so that the code can run.
```solidity
... 
if (true) { //@audit: Change to true
    uint256 needed = 0;
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < maxBorrowParts.length; i++) {
        needed += maxBorrowParts[i];
    }
    if (true) {  //@audit: Change to true
        _orderBookLiquidation(
...
```
1. Change the conditions on line [43](https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-bar-audit/blob/2286f80f928f41c8bc189d0657d74ba83286c668/contracts/markets/singularity/SGLLiquidation.sol#L43) and line [45](https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-bar-audit/blob/2286f80f928f41c8bc189d0657d74ba83286c668/contracts/markets/singularity/SGLLiquidation.sol#L48) to true. These conditions essentially guard the `_orderBookLiquidation()` function call and do not have any impact on the vulnerability. For simplicity and space reasons, we remove them to save space for the actual POC code.

```solidity
...
uint256 returnedShare = yieldBox.balanceOf(address(this), assetId) -
            uint256(totalAsset.elastic);
uint256 extraShare = 0; //@audit: Change to 0
...
```
2. Additionally, change line [181](https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-bar-audit/blob/2286f80f928f41c8bc189d0657d74ba83286c668/contracts/markets/singularity/SGLLiquidation.sol#L181) to `extraShare = 0`. This is necessary, since the value on line 181 will be < 0, hence reverting the function call. The reason this happens is that `returnedShare`, which stores the amount that the collateral is stored for, returns 0 as the POC does not simulate buying the collateral. This also does not have any impact on the vulnerability.

Once the changes have been made, add the following test into the `singularity.test.ts` test in `tapioca-bar-audit/test`

#### Code:
