# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Tapioca [docs](https://docs.tapioca.xyz/tapioca/core-technologies/pearlayer-and-oracles#price-feeds-oracles) state that a combination oracle design consisting of both Chainlink and Uniswap V3 oracles is implemented for "data reliability and decentralization". However the current implementation is not resilient against Chainlink feeds becoming [inactive](https://ethereum.stackexchange.com/questions/133242/how-future-resilient-is-a-chainlink-price-feed/133843#133843) or [deprecated](https://docs.chain.link/data-feeds/deprecating-feeds#:~:text=Data%20Feeds%20without%20publicly%20known,incurred%20by%20Chainlink%20node%20operators.). Oracles that inherit from `ModuleChainlinkMulti`, such as `Seer`, are especially vulnerable, as if any one of the feeds in their circuit goes down then each call to `ChainlinkUtils#_readChainlinkFeed` would revert, rendering the oracle totally unusable.

## Proof of Concept

Reading from Chainlink price feeds is handled by [`ChainlinkUtils#_readChainlinkFeed`](https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-periph-audit/blob/023751a4e987cf7c203ab25d3abba58f7344f213/contracts/oracle/utils/ChainlinkUtils.sol#L33-L60):

```solidity
File: tapioca-periph-audit\contracts\oracle\utils\ChainlinkUtils.sol

33:     function _readChainlinkFeed(
34:         uint256 quoteAmount,
35:         AggregatorV3Interface feed,
36:         uint8 multiplied,
37:         uint256 decimals,
38:         uint256 castedRatio
39:     ) internal view returns (uint256, uint256) {
40:         if (castedRatio == 0) {
41:             (
42:                 uint80 roundId,
43:                 int256 ratio,
44:                 ,
45:                 uint256 updatedAt,
46:                 uint80 answeredInRound
47:             ) = feed.latestRoundData();
48:             if (
49:                 ratio <= 0 ||
50:                 roundId > answeredInRound ||
51:                 block.timestamp - updatedAt > stalePeriod
52:             ) revert InvalidChainlinkRate(); // @audit revert if stale/invalid price
53:             castedRatio = uint256(ratio);
54:         }
55:         // Checking whether we should multiply or divide by the ratio computed
56:         if (multiplied == 1)
57:             quoteAmount = (quoteAmount * castedRatio) / (10 ** decimals);
58:         else quoteAmount = (quoteAmount * (10 ** decimals)) / castedRatio;
59:         return (quoteAmount, castedRatio);
60:     }
```

The function checks that the returned price is strictly positive and not stale, and reverts otherwise. Usually this is best practice, however in this case it defeats the purpose of the combined oracle system. The intention was to not have to rely on either Chainlink feeds or Uniswap pools, but under the current implementation the oracles are still heavily reliant on Chainlink feeds remaining functional.

Oracles that implement multiple Chainlink feeds (such as [Seer.sol](https://github.com/Tapioca-DAO/tapioca-periph-audit/blob/023751a4e987cf7c203ab25d3abba58f7344f213/contracts/oracle/Seer.sol)) are more vulnerable as if any single feed in the circuit is unavailable, the entire transaction will revert.

```solidity
File: tapioca-periph-audit\contracts\oracle\modules\ModuleChainlinkMulti.sol

