# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Before I start with the description of the problem I would like to specify that the `ChainlinkUtils.sol` contract itself where we need to check for Active Sequencer is not in the audit scope. But everything starts from the `Seer.sol` functions that are in the scopes and from there it will return the wrong data which is in the audit scopes.

We can see that TapiocaDAO will be the deplane of Arbitrum (Host), Optimism, and Ethereum: 
https://docs.tapioca.xyz/tapioca/information/supported-chains

Unfortunately, we see that when taking the price from the Chainlink Oracle there is no check if Sequencer is active. 
If the L2 Sequencer goes down, oracle data will not be kept up to date, and thus could become stale.

## Proof of Concept

The invocation flow of the Functions is as follows:
In `Seer.sol` we have `get()`, `peek()` and `peekSpot()`. I will give example with `get()`:
```solidity
function get(
        bytes calldata
    ) external virtual returns (bool success, uint256 rate) {
        (, uint256 high) = _readAll(inBase);
        return (true, high);
    }
```
This function call `_readAll()`:
```solidity
function _readAll(
        uint256 quoteAmount
    ) internal view override returns (uint256, uint256) {
        uint256 quoteAmountUni = _quoteUniswap(quoteAmount);

        // The current uni rate is in `outBase` we want our rate to all be in base `BASE`
        quoteAmountUni = (quoteAmountUni * BASE) / outBase;
        // The current amount is in `inBase` we want our rate to all be in base `BASE`
        uint256 quoteAmountCL = (quoteAmount * BASE) / inBase;
        uint256 ratio;

        (quoteAmountCL, ratio) = _quoteChainlink(quoteAmountCL);

        if (uniFinalCurrency > 0) {
            quoteAmountUni = _changeUniswapNotFinal(ratio, quoteAmountUni);
        }
        if (quoteAmountCL <= quoteAmountUni) {
            return (quoteAmountCL, quoteAmountUni);
        } else return (quoteAmountUni, quoteAmountCL);
    }
```
Which call `_quoteChainlink()` and `_changeUniswapNotFinal()`. And they finally call `_readChainlinkFeed()`. We can see in this contract is missing a check if Sequencer is active.

If a sequencer becomes unavailable, it is impossible to access read/write APIs that consumers are using and applications on the L2 network will be down for most users without interacting directly through the L1 optimistic rollup contracts. The L2 has not stopped, but it would be unfair to continue providing service on your applications when only a few users can use them.
It is possible that the data that will be returned in `Seer.sol` which is in the audit scope to be wrong.
## Tools Used

Visual Studio Code
## Recommended Mitigation Steps

You should always check for sequencer availability when using Chainlink's Arbitrum or Optimism price feeds or another layer 2 chains. 

Check this example -> https://docs.chain.link/data-feeds/l2-sequencer-feeds#example-code


