# Vulnerability details

## Summary

The `setRange` function does not properly validate that the new range endTimestamp is greater than the previous range end time. This allows setting backwards or invalid historical prices.

## Vulnerability Details 

The `setRange` function takes a new `endTimestamp` and `dailyInterestRate` and appends a new range to the `ranges` array. However, it does not check that the `endTimestamp` is greater than the previous range's `end` time.

This allows a malicious setter to set an `endTimestamp` in the past, which could set incorrect historical prices.

## Code Snippet

```solidity
function setRange(uint256 endTimestamp, uint256 dailyInterestRate) external onlyRole(SETTER_ROLE) {

  // No validation of endTimestamp

  ranges.push(
    Range(lastRange.end, endTimestamp, dailyInterestRate, prevClosePrice) 
  );

}
```

## Proof of Concept

1. Assume the last range has an end time of 1670000000 (Jan 1, 2023) 
2. Attacker calls `setRange` with endTimestamp=1660000000 (Jan 1, 2022) and some dailyInterestRate
3. This inserts a range with invalid (backwards) timestamps
4. Historical prices can now be incorrectly looked up

## Impact

Allowing invalid historical prices could disrupt systems relying on the oracle for accurate data. 

Attackers could exploit this to manipulate perceived historical prices or interest rates.

## Tools Used

Manual code review.


## Recommended Mitigation Steps

1. In `setRange`, compare `endTimestamp` against the previous range's `end` time

```solidity
function setRange(uint256 endTimestamp, uint256 dailyInterestRate) external onlyRole(SETTER_ROLE) {

  Range storage lastRange = ranges[ranges.length - 1];

  require(endTimestamp > lastRange.end, "Invalid end timestamp");

  // Rest of function

}
```

2. Revert if `endTimestamp` is not greater

```solidity
function setRange(uint256 endTimestamp, uint256 dailyInterestRate) external onlyRole(SETTER_ROLE) {

  Range storage lastRange = ranges[ranges.length - 1];

  if (endTimestamp <= lastRange.end) {
    revert("End timestamp must be greater than previous range"); 
  }

  // Rest of function

}
```

This will prevent setting backwards or invalid ranges.


