# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The `RWADynamicOracle.simulateRange` function is used to simulate the derivation of the prices returned from the oracle, given a range and a timestamp. 

When using this function if we are simulating the first range, we must set `startTime` and `rangeStartPrice`. If we are simulating a range > 1st then `startTime` and `rangeStartPrice` values will be calculated internally. 

But the issue is, the `simulateRange` function does not check whether the provided input `endTime` (`The end time for the range to simulate`) is a valid value. 

As a result if the user provides a `endTime < startTime` or the `endTime < lastRange.end` (when we are simulating a range other than range 1) this will derive the wrong price inside the following for loop execution.

    for (uint256 i = 0; i < length + 1; ++i) {
      Range memory range = rangeList[(length) - i];
      if (range.start <= blockTimeStamp) {
        if (range.end <= blockTimeStamp) {
          return derivePrice(range, range.end - 1);
        } else {
          return derivePrice(range, blockTimeStamp);
        }
      }
    }

This is because the `range.end < range.start` which is wrong execution state, but the `for` loop will continue execution because  both the `if` statements shown below will pass, and provide a wrong derived price to the end user.

      if (range.start <= blockTimeStamp) {
        if (range.end <= blockTimeStamp) {

