# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Call to latestRoundData could potentially revert and make it impossible to
query any prices.

Add a event to notify offchain so that admin can take action to fix the issue.

Chainlink’s multisigs can immediately block access to price feeds at will.
Therefore, to prevent denial of service scenarios, it is recommended to
query Chainlink price feeds using a defensive approach with Solidity’s
try/catch structure. In this way, if the call to the price feed fails, the caller
contract is still in control and can handle any errors safely and explicitly.
The admin can set the below mapping to a price and by pass the chainlink feed.

prices[address(token)] = some value will by pass the call to chain link.

## Proof of Concept


```
if(_feed !=(address(0)){
  (, int256 answer, , uint256 updatedAt, ) = AggregatorV3Interface(feed)
            .latestRoundData();
}
else{
  // @audit fire offchain event
  // Admin to monitor such events to take action.
}
```

## Tools Used
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

a) step 1:
Switch the price feed to manual by setting the below to some non zero value
prices[address(token)]="some price";

step 2:
Wrap the chainlink call in a try catch block for better handling.
```
function getPrice(address _feed) external view returns (int256) {
        try AggregatorV3Interface(_feed).latestRoundData() returns (
            uint80,         // roundID
            int256 price,   // price
            uint256,        // startedAt
            uint256,        // timestamp
            uint80          // answeredInRound
        ) {
            return price;
        } catch Error(string memory) {            
            // handle failure here:
            // revert, call propietary fallback oracle, fetch from another 3rd-party oracle, etc.
        }
    }
```

