# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-moonwell/blob/main/src/core/Oracles/ChainlinkOracle.sol#L97-L113


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The logic to reading prices from Chain link does not account for age of the price. The logic assumes that if the price is greater than 0 and updated At time is not equal to zero, it is a valid price.

As the price is used in conversion of tokens, the price is a critical factor not only in terms of value, but also in the context of time and how long ago it was fetched. Prices few days back or even hours back could results in huge losses for the users or protocol.

It is absolutely necessary to monitor the age of price and fallback incase stale prices are being returned.

The chain link provides the round Id and updated At using which a mechanism could be build to ensure the protocol is not operating with stale prices.


## Proof of Concept
The below function does not track the roundId and updatedAt to identify a scenario where the feed for certain tokens is no more being refreshed in chainlink and hence older prices are returned.


```
 function getChainlinkPrice(
        AggregatorV3Interface feed
    ) internal view returns (uint256) {
        (, int256 answer, , uint256 updatedAt, ) = AggregatorV3Interface(feed)
            .latestRoundData();
        require(answer > 0, "Chainlink price cannot be lower than 0");
        require(updatedAt != 0, "Round is in incompleted state");

        // Chainlink USD-denominated feeds store answers at 8 decimals
        uint256 decimalDelta = uint256(18).sub(feed.decimals()); // @audit assuming token has 18 decimals as max.
        // Ensure that we don't multiply the result by 0
        if (decimalDelta > 0) {
            return uint256(answer).mul(10 ** decimalDelta);
        } else {
            return uint256(answer);
        }
    }
```
## Tools Used
Manual

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Implement the logic in getChainlinkPrice to maintain the lastUpdatedAt and roundId at feed level will allow to track if the price is stale. To judge, use a threshold which can be configured for the protocol or at feed level.

If the difference between updatedAt and lastUpdatedAt grows above threshold, notify  offchain about stale prices.






