# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-07-moonwell/blob/4aaa7d6767da3bc42e31c18ea2e75736a4ea53d4/src/core/router/WETHRouter.sol#L23-L27


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
This vulnerability results in the mToken contract having the authority to withdraw and spend any quantity of tokens from the WETHRouter contract at any time. This could potentially lead to a significant loss of tokens if a flaw in the mToken contract is leveraged by malicious actors.

## Proof of Concept
On deployment of the WETHRouter contract, it calls the approve function on the weth contract (WETH9 ERC20 Token), setting the maximum possible allowance for the mToken contract. This bestows the mToken with the ability to transfer any amount of weth tokens from the WETHRouter contract, making it a substantial potential vulnerability.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The approval amount should ideally be the exact necessary amount for specific operations. Also, having methods to revoke or decrease the approval is a favorable fallback strategy.

These specific changes could mitigate the vulnerability:

Change the approval line to only approve the amount necessary for operations.
Implement a function that can adjust the approved tokens amount accordingly to specific needs.
Perform a thorough security audit of the mToken before approval, to ensure it does not contain vulnerabilities that can be exploited.

Please note that these mitigations add complexity to the contract and might lead to elevated gas prices for users. Therefore, the most suitable approach should be adapted considering these aspects.

Always adhere to the principle of least authority (POLA). Contracts and addresses should only have the permissions they need to perform their actions, nothing more.

```
// Import the Ownable contract from the OpenZeppelin contracts library
import "@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol";

contract WETHRouter is Ownable {
    WETH9 public weth;
    MErc20 public mToken;
    uint256 public approvedAmount;

    constructor(WETH9 _weth, MErc20 _mToken, uint256 _initialApproval) {
        weth = _weth;
        mToken = _mToken;
        approvedAmount = _initialApproval;
        _weth.approve(address(_mToken), _initialApproval);
    }

    function increaseApproval(uint256 _additionalAmount) public onlyOwner {
        approvedAmount += _additionalAmount;
        weth.approve(address(mToken), approvedAmount);
    }

    function decreaseApproval(uint256 _reducedAmount) public onlyOwner {
        require(_reducedAmount <= approvedAmount, "Reduced amount is greater than approved amount");
        approvedAmount -= _reducedAmount;
        weth.approve(address(mToken), approvedAmount);
    }
}

```
In this modified contract:
1) The WETHRouter contract now inherits from the Ownable contract, which means it has an owner state variable and a onlyOwner modifier.

2) The increaseApproval and decreaseApproval functions now have the onlyOwner modifier, which means they can only be called by the owner of the contract.


