# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-08-goodentry/blob/71c0c0eca8af957202ccdbf5ce2f2a514ffe2e24/contracts/PositionManager/PositionManager.sol#L67


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
`PositionManager::getPoolAddresses` is utilized by all the functions of `OptionsPositionManager` which interact with Good Entry lending pools.

```solidity
function getPoolAddresses(uint poolId) 
    internal view 
    returns( ILendingPool lp, IPriceOracle oracle, IUniswapV2Router01 router, address token0, address token1) 
  {
    (address lpap, address _token0, address _token1, address r, ) = ROEROUTER.pools(poolId); // @audit - deprecated status should be checked here
    token0 = _token0;
    token1 = _token1;
    lp = ILendingPool(ILendingPoolAddressesProvider(lpap).getLendingPool());
    oracle = IPriceOracle(ILendingPoolAddressesProvider(lpap).getPriceOracle());
    router = IUniswapV2Router01(r);
}
```
The unused tuple element returned by the router is the given pool's deprecation status. Rather than removing a pool from the array when it is deprecated, this boolean is simply updated in the pool's struct.

```solidity
function deprecatePool(uint poolId) public onlyOwner {
    pools[poolId].isDeprecated = true;
    emit DeprecatePool(poolId);
}
```

As can be seen in the first snippet above, this is not checked anywhere which means that actions can be performed against a pool that was intended to be deprecated. Deprecation could be intended for multiple reasons, for example following an oracle-manipulation exploit or upgrade, so it is important to prevent the protocol from interfacing with potentially dangerous or outdated external contracts so as to prevent loss of user funds.

