# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-08-goodentry/blob/main/contracts/PositionManager/OptionsPositionManager.sol#L470-L484


# Vulnerability details


## Impact

The `swapExactTokensForTokens` function is used to performing a swap but the slippage protection is not implemented correctly, the allowed slippage is hardcoded to 1% which could make the swap impossible in a highly volatile market. Additionally, the execution deadline for the swap relies on `block.timestamp`, which is problematic as it depends on the execution time of the block in which the transaction is processed. Consequently, the transaction could stay in the mempool for a considerable duration, and when it is eventually executed, it will not revert because the deadline is based on the block's own timestamp

The result of this incorrect implementation of slippage protection is that users may encounter blockages in highly volatile markets, and their swap transactions might remain stuck in the mempool for an extended period.

## Proof of Concept

The issue occurs in the `swapExactTokensForTokens` function below :

```solidity
function swapExactTokensForTokens(
    IUniswapV2Router01 ammRouter,
    IPriceOracle oracle,
    uint amount,
    address[] memory path
) internal returns (uint256 received) {
    if (
        amount > 0 &&
        AmountsRouter(address(ammRouter)).getAmountsOut(amount, path)[1] > 0
    ) {
        checkSetAllowance(path[0], address(ammRouter), amount);
        uint[] memory amounts = ammRouter.swapExactTokensForTokens(
            amount,
            // @audit hardcoded 1% slippage, not good in volatile market
            (getTargetAmountFromOracle(oracle, path[0], amount, path[1]) *
                99) / 100, // allow 1% slippage
            path,
            address(this),
            // @audit hardcoded deadline, tx can stay in the mempool for a long time
            block.timestamp

        );
        received = amounts[1];
    }
}
```

As we can see both minimum amount received and the execution deadline are hardcoded to 1% and `block.timestamp` respectively which is never recommended as the transaction can revert in highly volatile market and it can also remain in the mempool for a long period before getting executed.

It's recommended to always give the option to the user to set his own minimum amount received and the execution deadline during a swap transaction to avoid any of the aforementioned issues.

## Tools Used

Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

To avoid this issue i recommend to allow the user to set his own `minAmount` and `deadline` parameters (use the protocol ones by default if user doesn't set his own), the function could be modified as follows :

```solidity
function swapExactTokensForTokens(
    IUniswapV2Router01 ammRouter,
    IPriceOracle oracle,
    uint amount,
    uint minAmount, // @audit add min amount argument
    uint deadline, // @audit add execution deadline argument
    address[] memory path
) internal returns (uint256 received) {
    if (
        amount > 0 &&
        AmountsRouter(address(ammRouter)).getAmountsOut(amount, path)[1] > 0
    ) {
        checkSetAllowance(path[0], address(ammRouter), amount);

        // @audit get the values of : minAmount, deadline
        uint _minAmount = minAmount != 0
            ? minAmount
            : (getTargetAmountFromOracle(oracle, path[0], amount, path[1]) *
                99) / 100;
        uint _deadline = deadline > block.timestamp && deadline != 0
            ? deadline
            : block.timestamp;

        uint[] memory amounts = ammRouter.swapExactTokensForTokens(
            amount,
            _minAmount, // slippage
            path,
            address(this),
            _deadline
        );
        received = amounts[1];
    }
}
```


