# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-08-goodentry/blob/main/contracts/GeVault.sol#L222
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-08-goodentry/blob/main/contracts/GeVault.sol#L230-L236


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
This behaviour is dangerous for other integrators, as they could account more value withdrawn then the actual value. It's a similar vulnerability commonly pointed out to fee on transfer tokens.

## Proof of Concept
Protocols integrating with vaults usually account for the transferred amount using the returned `uint256` variable. However, the `GeVault` returns the `amount + fee`, and only actually transfers the `amount`, as can be seen below:
```solidity
function withdraw(uint liquidity, address token) public nonReentrant returns (uint amount) {
    ...
    amount = valueX8 * 10**ERC20(token).decimals() / oracle.getAssetPrice(token);
    ...
    uint bal = amount - fee;

    if (token == address(WETH)){
      WETH.withdraw(bal);
      payable(msg.sender).transfer(bal);
    }
    else {
      ERC20(token).safeTransfer(msg.sender, bal);
    }
    ...
}
```
A sample integrator contract that would be vulnerable to this is:
```solidity
contract VulnerableVaultOnTopOfGeVault {
    ...
    function withdraw(uint256 shares_) external {
        uint256 liquidity_ = shares_ * totalLiquidity() / totalSupply();
        uint256 amount_ = geVault.withdraw(liquidity_, WETH);
        payable(msg.sender).call(value: amount_)("");
    }
    ...
}
```
Notice that the `VulnerableVaultOnTopOfGeVault` would send more value to the user than the actual withdrawn, which could revert or worse, spend already present balance in the contract.

## Tools Used
Vscode, Foundry

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Return the `bal` variable instead, such as:
```solidity
function withdraw(uint liquidity, address token) public nonReentrant returns (uint bal) {
    ...
    uint256 amount = valueX8 * 10**ERC20(token).decimals() / oracle.getAssetPrice(token);
    ...
    bal = amount - fee;
}
```


## Assessed type

Token-Transfer

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-08-goodentry/blob/main/contracts/GeVault.sol#L232


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Stuck tokens in a contract that spends more than 2300 gas on `receive()`.

## Proof of Concept
This is a known issue, see [this finding](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-04-backd-findings/issues/52) for a similar description.

In the `GeVault`, the `withdraw()` function, when the token to withdraw is `WETH`, first unwrapps it and then transfers to the `msg.sender` the amount, as can be seen below:
```solidity
function withdraw(uint liquidity, address token) public nonReentrant returns (uint amount) {
    ...
    if (token == address(WETH)){
      WETH.withdraw(bal);
      payable(msg.sender).transfer(bal); // TO SUBMIT, might cause frozen assets
    }
    else {
      ERC20(token).safeTransfer(msg.sender, bal);
    }
    ...
}
```
The vulnerability is that `transfer()` will only forward 2300 gas, which means that the transaction might revert due to `OutOfGas` if the smart contract has additional logic in the `receive()` function. An example of such a vulnerable smart contract can be seen below:
```solidity
contract SampleVulnerableContract {
    address public owner;

    constructor (address owner_) {
        owner = owner_;
    }

    function deposit(GeVault vault_, address token_, uint256 amount_) external payable {
        vault_.deposit{value: msg.value}(token_, amount_);
    }

    function withdraw(GeVault vault_, uint256 liquidity_, address token_) external {
        vault_.withdraw(liquidity_, token_);
    }

    receive() external payable {
        payable(owner).transfer(msg.value);
    }
}
```

A POC was carried out in Foundry, showing that the mentioned smart contract is vulnerable to this issue. The brownie setup was migrated to Foundry.
