# Vulnerability details

TokenisableRange compounds trading fees accrued by the underlying Uniswap V3 pool whenever `claimFee()` is directly called by the user, or whenever a liquidity event (`deposit` or `withdraw`) happens.

After the fees are reinvested, [there is a slippage check](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-08-goodentry/blob/71c0c0eca8af957202ccdbf5ce2f2a514ffe2e24/contracts/TokenisableRange.sol#L209) that allows for a maximum 5% deviation between the compounding result and what's expected given the oracle prices of the assets.

## Impact
When this deviation is higher than 5% and the pool holds fees enough to be reinvested, the pool's availability for depositing and more importantly withdrawing funds can be temporarily compromised. 

This seems an excessively strict check, given that:
- the cost is temporarily blocking funding and withdrawals
- the benefit is protecting 0.05% of the pool assets (5% of the fees, which are reinvested only above 1% of the pool value)  

## Proof of Concept
The following PoC shows how deposits and withdraws are all halted by this failing slippage check. The unavailability condition is transitory (under the assumption that oracle and UniswapV3 market prices eventually converge) but can probably be avoided:

```Solidity
    function testFeeSlippageCheckFail() external {
        vm.startPrank(tokenWhale);
        TokenisableRange tr = new TokenisableRange();

        // out of range: WETH is more valuable than that (about 1870 USDC on this block 17811921); 
        // the pool will hold 0 WETH
        tr.initProxy(AaveOracle, USDC, WETH, 500e10, 1000e10, "Test1", "T1", false);

        USDC.approve(address(tr), 100_000e6);
        tr.init(100_000e6, 0);

        // time passes, and the pool trades in range, accumulating fees
        uint256 fee0 = 1_200e6;
        uint256 fee1 = 2e18;

        vm.mockCall(address(UniswapV3UsdcNFPositionManager), 
            abi.encodeWithSelector(INonfungiblePositionManager.collect.selector), 
            abi.encode(fee0, fee1));

        vm.mockCall(address(AaveOracle),
            abi.encodeWithSelector(IAaveOracle.getAssetPrice.selector, address(WETH)),
            abi.encode(uint256(75000000000)));

        USDC.transfer(address(tr), fee0);
        WETH.transfer(address(tr), fee1);

        vm.expectRevert();
        tr.claimFee();

        vm.expectRevert();
        tr.withdraw(1e18, 0, 0);

        vm.expectRevert();
        tr.deposit(1_000e6, 0);
    }
```

## Tools Used
Code review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Instead of always reinvesting the fees and revering after if the slippage check fails, consider:
- previewing the fee liquidity injection
- reinvesting the fees only if the preview is reasonable
- doing nothing otherwise


