# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-08-goodentry/blob/main/contracts/GeVault.sol#L367-L378


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
poolMatchesOracle on GeVault using slot0 to calculate slippage tolerance is subject to DOS.

GeVault has a price check function which is implemented in poolMatchesOracle. The pool price is fetched from slot0, which is the latest price ratio of token 0 / token1. Then the function continues to fetch the oracle price and using the oracle price to compared against the price coming from slot0. If the price difference is bigger than 1% then it returns false.

poolMatchesOracle is used in rebalance, withdraw and deposit.

However, this is subject to DOS conside:
1. a tx of rebalance/withdraw/deposit is detected in mempool.
2. A malicious actor frontrun the tx by frontrunning a little bit to tilt the pool by 1% price.
3. The actor now bundle the tx target and make it revert based on the poolMatchesOracle logic.
4. The actor now backrun to trade back. Cost of attack is at worse, the roundtrip swap fee of the tradeQuantity to move the price by 1%.

```solidity
  function poolMatchesOracle() public view returns (bool matches){
    (uint160 sqrtPriceX96,,,,,,) = uniswapPool.slot0();

    uint decimals0 = token0.decimals();
    uint decimals1 = token1.decimals();
    uint priceX8 = 10**decimals0;
    // Overflow if dont scale down the sqrtPrice before div 2*192
    priceX8 = priceX8 * uint(sqrtPriceX96 / 2 ** 12) ** 2 * 1e8 / 2**168;
    priceX8 = priceX8 / 10**decimals1;
    uint oraclePrice = 1e8 * oracle.getAssetPrice(address(token0)) / oracle.getAssetPrice(address(token1));
    if (oraclePrice < priceX8 * 101 / 100 && oraclePrice > priceX8 * 99 / 100) matches = true;
  }
```
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-08-goodentry/blob/main/contracts/GeVault.sol#L367-L378

## Proof of Concept


## Tools Used

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider using the TWAP price from uniswapPool as a way to benchmark the price difference. For deposit/withdraw, allow the user to specify the slippage tolerance which can then be applied to the oracle checks too.


