# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The contract does not implement any access control mechanisms. As a result, anyone can call the **claimPrizes** function and potentially manipulate the prize claiming process.

## Proof of Concept
[LINK](https://github.com/GenerationSoftware/pt-v5-claimer/blob/57a381aef690a27c9198f4340747155a71cae753/src/Claimer.sol#L60-L83)

      function claimPrizes(
        Vault vault,
        uint8 tier,
        address[] calldata winners,
        uint32[][] calldata prizeIndices,
        address _feeRecipient
      ) external returns (uint256 totalFees) {
        uint256 claimCount;
        for (uint i = 0; i < winners.length; i++) {
          claimCount += prizeIndices[i].length;
        }


        uint96 feePerClaim = uint96(
          _computeFeePerClaim(
            _computeMaxFee(tier, prizePool.numberOfTiers()),
            claimCount,
            prizePool.claimCount()
          )
        );


        vault.claimPrizes(tier, winners, prizeIndices, feePerClaim, _feeRecipient);


        return feePerClaim * claimCount;
      }

Anyone can call the **claimPrizes** function and potentially manipulate the prize claiming process.

## Tools Used
VS Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

To address the lack of access control in the Claimer contract, we can implement a modifier and apply it to the sensitive functions that should only be called by authorized addresses. Like this : 

    contract Claimer is Multicall {
      // Define the role that has access to sensitive functions
      address public admin;

      constructor(PrizePool _prizePool, uint256 _minimumFee, uint256 _maximumFee, uint256 _timeToReachMaxFee, UD2x18 _maxFeePortionOfPrize) {
        prizePool = _prizePool;
        maxFeePortionOfPrize = _maxFeePortionOfPrize;
        decayConstant = LinearVRGDALib.getDecayConstant(LinearVRGDALib.getMaximumPriceDeltaScale(_minimumFee, _maximumFee, _timeToReachMaxFee));
        minimumFee = _minimumFee;
        timeToReachMaxFee = _timeToReachMaxFee;

        // Set the admin role to the contract deployer
        admin = msg.sender;
      }

      // Modifier to restrict access to admin only
      modifier onlyAdmin() {
        require(msg.sender == admin, "Only the admin can call this function");
        _;
      }

      // Function to transfer the admin role to a new address
      function transferAdmin(address newAdmin) external onlyAdmin {
        require(newAdmin != address(0), "Invalid address");
        admin = newAdmin;
      }

      // Add the `onlyAdmin` modifier to sensitive functions
      function claimPrizes(Vault vault, uint8 tier, address[] calldata winners, uint32[][] calldata prizeIndices, address _feeRecipient) external onlyAdmin returns (uint256 totalFees) {
        // Function body remains the same
      }

      // Add the `onlyAdmin` modifier to sensitive functions
      function computeTotalFees(uint8 _tier, uint _claimCount) external view onlyAdmin returns (uint256) {
        // Function body remains the same
      }

      // Rest of the contract...
    }

In the above code, I added an **admin** variable to keep track of the address that has administrative privileges. The **onlyAdmin** modifier restricts access to functions that are only callable by the **admin** address. The **transferAdmin** function allows the **admin** to transfer the role to a new address if needed.

By applying the onlyAdmin modifier to the **claimPrizes** and also **computeTotalFees* functions, we ensure that only the **admin** can execute those functions, providing access control to sensitive operations.

Another approach to addressing the lack of access control is by using the OpenZeppelin's **Ownable** contract, which provides a basic access control mechanism. Here is an example on how we can integrate **Ownable** into the **Claimer** contract:

    import "@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol";

    contract Claimer is Multicall, Ownable {
      // Rest of the contract...

      // Override the constructor to set the contract deployer as the owner
      constructor(PrizePool _prizePool, uint256 _minimumFee, uint256 _maximumFee, uint256 _timeToReachMaxFee, UD2x18 _maxFeePortionOfPrize) {
        // Contract initialization code...

        // Set the contract deployer as the owner
        transferOwnership(msg.sender);
      }

      // Override the onlyOwner modifier provided by Ownable
      modifier onlyOwner() {
        require(msg.sender == owner(), "Only the owner can call this function");
        _;
      }

      // Rest of the contract...
    }

In this updated code, the **Claimer** contract now inherits from the **Ownable** contract provided by OpenZeppelin. The **Ownable** contract manages the ownership of the contract and provides the **onlyOwner** modifier.

The **constructor** function is overridden to call the **transferOwnership** function from **Ownable**, setting the contract deployer as the owner.

By applying the **onlyOwner** modifier to sensitive functions, we restrict access to those functions only to the owner (contract deployer). For example:

    function claimPrizes(Vault vault, uint8 tier, address[] calldata winners, uint32[][] calldata prizeIndices, address _feeRecipient) external onlyOwner returns (uint256 totalFees) {
      // Function body remains the same
    }

    function computeTotalFees(uint8 _tier, uint _claimCount) external view onlyOwner returns (uint256) {
      // Function body remains the same
    }

With this implementation, only the owner (contract deployer) will have permission to call the **claimPrizes** and **computeTotalFees** functions, providing access control to these sensitive operations.










