# Vulnerability details

## Impact:

The parseDS function in RRUtils.sol does not check the length of the digest field in the DS RR. If the length of the digest field is not within expected ranges, it could lead to incorrect parsing of DS records. This could result in erroneous DNSSEC validation and a security vulnerability in the ENS registry.

## Proof of Concept:

Suppose an attacker provides a malicious DNSSEC response containing a DS record with a digest length greater than the expected maximum length. The parseDS function will not detect the length violation and will incorrectly parse the DS record, potentially leading to an incorrect DNSSEC validation.

      function parseDS(bytes memory rr) internal pure returns (DS memory) {
    require(rr.length >= 8, "invalid rr");
    DS memory ds;
    (ds.keyTag, ds.algorithm, ds.digestType) = rr.extractThreeUint16(0);
    ds.digest = rr.slice(6); // No length check
    return ds;
      }



## Mitigation:

To mitigate this issue, a length check should be added to the parseDS function to ensure that the digest field is within expected length ranges. The length of the digest field should be checked against the expected length based on the digest type specified in the DS record. If the digest length is outside the expected range, the function should revert with an error message.

      function parseDS(bytes memory rr) internal pure returns (DS memory) {
    require(rr.length >= 8, "invalid rr");
    DS memory ds;
    (ds.keyTag, ds.algorithm, ds.digestType) = rr.extractThreeUint16(0);
    uint8 expectedLength;
    if (ds.digestType == DigestType.SHA1) {
        expectedLength = 20;
    } else if (ds.digestType == DigestType.SHA256) {
        expectedLength = 32;
    } else {
        revert("unsupported digest type");
    }
    require(rr.length == expectedLength + 6, "invalid digest length");
    ds.digest = rr.slice(6);
    return ds;
      }

This check ensures that the digest field is of expected length and reduces the likelihood of incorrect parsing of DS records.

