# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Detailed description of the impact of this finding.
``memcpy()`` will have side effect  when ``src < dest`` but ``src + len > dest`` and results in incorrect copy operation.

## Proof of Concept
Provide direct links to all referenced code in GitHub. Add screenshots, logs, or any other relevant proof that illustrates the concept.

``memcpy()`` allows a user to copy bytes from one memory range to another. However, the function will be correct only if the two memory ranges do not overlap. When there is overlap, the current implementation will have side effect and the copy will result in incorrect copied content in the target memory. The function will not revert in this case, therefore, it will be a silent failure. 

[https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/45ea10bacb2a398e14d711fe28d1738271cd7640/contracts/dnssec-oracle/BytesUtils.sol#L273-L292](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/45ea10bacb2a398e14d711fe28d1738271cd7640/contracts/dnssec-oracle/BytesUtils.sol#L273-L292)

when ``src < dest`` but ``src + len > dest``, the problem is that the source memory range might be overlapping with the target memory range and part of the source information might be overwritten by ``memcpy`` before it is read. As a result, the final content of the target memory range will not be the same as the content of the source memory range. The result will be a silent failure and wrong copy, therefore very dangerous. 

The following POC confirms my findings. One case is a correct copy, and the second case is a wrong copy due to overlapping ranges. 

```javascript
  function testMemcpy() public {
        bytes memory mem = abi.encode("000011112222333344445555666677778888999900001111222233334444555566667777888899990000111122223333444455556666777788889999");
        uint256 dest;
        uint256 src;
        uint256 len = 40;
        uint256 destOffset = 0;
        uint256 srcOffset = 30;

        assembly {
            dest := add(add(mem, 32), destOffset)
            src := add(add(mem, 32), srcOffset)
        }

        bytes32 hash1 = keccak(mem, srcOffset, len);
        memcpy(dest, src, len);
        bytes32 hash2 = keccak(mem, destOffset, len);
        assertTrue(hash1 == hash2);  // the copy is successful

        destOffset = 30; 
        srcOffset = 0; 
        hash1 = keccak(mem, srcOffset, len);
        memcpy(dest, src, len);
        hash2 = keccak(mem, destOffset, len);
        assertTrue(hash1 != hash2); // the copy fails
    }
```


## Tools Used
VSCode

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The following solution detects overlapping cases and revert on these case. A more sophisticated implementation can be introduced to allowed overlapping ranges and still can copy correctly.

```diff
```diff
 function memcpy(uint256 dest, uint256 src, uint256 len) private pure {
+       if((src <= dest && src + len > dest) || dest <= src && dest + len > src) 
+             revert copyBetweenOverlappingMemoryRanges();

        // Copy word-length chunks while possible
        for (; len >= 32; len -= 32) {
            assembly {
                mstore(dest, mload(src))
            }
            dest += 32;
            src += 32;
        }

        // Copy remaining bytes
        unchecked {
            uint256 mask = (256 ** (32 - len)) - 1;
            assembly {
                let srcpart := and(mload(src), not(mask))
                let destpart := and(mload(dest), mask)
                mstore(dest, or(destpart, srcpart))
            }
        }
    }
```
