# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Detailed description of the impact of this finding.
``base32HexDecodeWord()`` will always break when ``len % 8 == 1, or 3, or 6``.  The main issue is that when considering adding the last decoded character, the function will revert when ``len % 8 == 1, or 3, or 6``. 

## Proof of Concept
Provide direct links to all referenced code in GitHub. Add screenshots, logs, or any other relevant proof that illustrates the concept.

base32HexDecodeWord() will decode unpadded base32 data of up to one word in length.

[https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/45ea10bacb2a398e14d711fe28d1738271cd7640/contracts/dnssec-oracle/BytesUtils.sol#L332-L377](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/45ea10bacb2a398e14d711fe28d1738271cd7640/contracts/dnssec-oracle/BytesUtils.sol#L332-L377)

However,  when the function  considers adding the last decoded character, the function will revert when ``len % 8 == 1, or 3, or 6``:

```javascript
    uint256 bitlen = len * 5;
        if (len % 8 == 0) {
            // Multiple of 8 characters, no padding
            ret = (ret << 5) | decoded;
        } else if (len % 8 == 2) {
            // Two extra characters - 1 byte
            ret = (ret << 3) | (decoded >> 2);
            bitlen -= 2;
        } else if (len % 8 == 4) {
            // Four extra characters - 2 bytes
            ret = (ret << 1) | (decoded >> 4);
            bitlen -= 4;
        } else if (len % 8 == 5) {
            // Five extra characters - 3 bytes
            ret = (ret << 4) | (decoded >> 1);
            bitlen -= 1;
        } else if (len % 8 == 7) {
            // Seven extra characters - 4 bytes
            ret = (ret << 2) | (decoded >> 3);
            bitlen -= 3;
        } else {
            revert();
        }
```

The following POC confirms my finding. One needs to make  ``base32HexDecodeWord()`` a public function to do this test. 

