        for (uint256 i = 0; i < len; i++) {
            bytes1 char = self[off + i];
            require(char >= 0x30 && char <= 0x7A);
            decoded = uint8(base32HexTable[uint256(uint8(char)) - 0x30]);
            require(decoded <= 0x20);
            if (i == len - 1) {
                break;
            }
            ret = (ret << 5) | decoded;
        }

        uint256 bitlen = len * 5;
        if (len % 8 == 0) {
            // Multiple of 8 characters, no padding
            ret = (ret << 5) | decoded;
        } else if (len % 8 == 2) {
            // Two extra characters - 1 byte
            ret = (ret << 3) | (decoded >> 2);
            bitlen -= 2;
        } else if (len % 8 == 4) {
            // Four extra characters - 2 bytes
            ret = (ret << 1) | (decoded >> 4);
            bitlen -= 4;
        } else if (len % 8 == 5) {
            // Five extra characters - 3 bytes
            ret = (ret << 4) | (decoded >> 1);
            bitlen -= 1;
        } else if (len % 8 == 7) {
            // Seven extra characters - 4 bytes
            ret = (ret << 2) | (decoded >> 3);
            bitlen -= 3;
        } else {
            revert();
        }
        return bytes32(ret << (256 - bitlen));
    }
```


## Tools Used
VScode

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The function needs to consider the other three cases as well: len % 8 = 1, 3, 6. One might also consider cases based on ``bitlen % 8`` instead of ``len %8``. This might improve the readability of the code.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/main/contracts/dnsregistrar/OffchainDNSResolver.sol#L65


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The resolveCallback function is vulnerable to an arbitrary callback attack. The function takes an arbitrary bytes parameter extraData, which is used to decode a tuple (name, query) and then further decode the response parameter. The function then executes a callback function with the decoded data using either IExtendedDNSResolver.resolve, IExtendedResolver.resolve, or by performing a staticcall to the callback address.
This means that an attacker could potentially pass a malicious callback function in the extraData parameter, which could perform unauthorized actions or return unexpected data. This could lead to serious security vulnerabilities, including the potential for loss of funds or sensitive data.

## Proof of Concept
To prove the existence of the arbitrary callback vulnerability, I have analyzed the code of the resolveCallback function and identified that it takes an arbitrary bytes parameter extraData, which is used to decode a tuple (name, query) and then further decode the response parameter. The function then executes a callback function with the decoded data using either IExtendedDNSResolver.resolve, IExtendedResolver.resolve, or by performing a staticcall to the callback address then crafted a proof-of-concept attack where passed a malicious callback function in the extraData parameter, which performed unauthorized actions or returned unexpected data. I was able to successfully execute the attack and demonstrate the vulnerability.

## Tools Used
To identify and exploit the arbitrary callback vulnerability, I have used a combination of manual code review and testing with a custom script (Remix)

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The vulnerability can be mitigated by changing the function's design to accept only predefined callback functions, instead of allowing arbitrary ones. Alternatively, the function can be updated to include checks and validations that ensure the callback function is legitimate and does not perform any unauthorized actions.
Here is an example code to mitigate the "Arbitrary Callback" vulnerability in the resolveCallback function:
function resolveCallback(
    bytes calldata response,
    bytes calldata extraData,
    address callbackContract,
    bytes4 callbackFunction
) external view returns (bytes memory) {
    (bytes memory name, bytes memory query) = abi.decode(
        extraData,
        (bytes, bytes)
    );
    DNSSEC.RRSetWithSignature[] memory rrsets = abi.decode(
        response,
        (DNSSEC.RRSetWithSignature[])
    );

    (bytes memory data, ) = oracle.verifyRRSet(rrsets);
    for (
        RRUtils.RRIterator memory iter = data.iterateRRs(0);
        !iter.done();
        iter.next()
    ) {
        // Ignore records with wrong name, type, or class
        bytes memory rrname = RRUtils.readName(iter.data, iter.offset);
        if (
            !rrname.equals(name) ||
            iter.class != CLASS_INET ||
            iter.dnstype != TYPE_TXT
        ) {
            continue;
        }

        // Look for a valid ENS-DNS TXT record
        (address dnsresolver, bytes memory context) = parseRR(
            iter.data,
            iter.rdataOffset,
            iter.nextOffset
        );

        // If we found a valid record, try to resolve it
        if (dnsresolver != address(0)) {
            if (
                IERC165(dnsresolver).supportsInterface(
                    IExtendedDNSResolver.resolve.selector
                )
            ) {
                return
                    IExtendedDNSResolver(dnsresolver).resolve(
                        name,
                        query,
                        context
                    );
            } else if (
                IERC165(dnsresolver).supportsInterface(
                    IExtendedResolver.resolve.selector
                )
            ) {
                return IExtendedResolver(dnsresolver).resolve(name, query);
            } else {
                // Mitigation: Only allow a predefined contract and function to be called
                require(
                    callbackContract != address(0) &&
                    callbackFunction != bytes4(0) &&
                    callbackContract == msg.sender &&
                    callbackFunction == callbackContract.resolveCallback.selector,
                    "Invalid callback"
                );

                (bool ok, bytes memory ret) = address(dnsresolver)
                    .staticcall(query);
                if (ok) {
                    return ret;
                } else {
                    revert CouldNotResolve(name);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // No valid records; revert.
    revert CouldNotResolve(name);
}
In this example, I have added two additional parameters callbackContract and callbackFunction to the function. The caller must pass the address of the contract and the function selector that are allowed to be called as a callback function.
Then we added a check in the code to make sure that the callback function is being called by the expected contract and function, and not by any arbitrary contract and function.
This mitigation ensures that only the predefined contract and function can be executed as a callback function, and mitigates the risk of an attacker providing a malicious callback function.

