# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In `RRUtils.readDNSKEY()` function, if the `length == 4` then the `self.publicKey` will be an empty bytes array. No check is performed to validata the `length` variable. This will return the `public key` of the `DNSKEY` as an empty bytes array.

Check should be performed to make sure `length > 4`, else the `keyrdata.length` is a stale value and the transaction should revert.

Same scenario applies for `self.digest` calculation in the `RRUtils.readDS()` function as well. If `length = DS_DIGEST = 4`, then the `length - DS_DIGEST = 0`. Which will assign empty bytes string to `self.digest`. 

Check should be performed to make sure `length > 4`, else the `self.digest` is a stale value and the transaction should revert.

Same scenario applies for the `self.data` calculation in the `RRUtils.readSignedSet()` function as well. If the `data.length = RRSIG_SIGNER_NAME + self.signerName.length`, then the ` self.data = data.substring(RRSIG_SIGNER_NAME + self.signerName.length,0)`. Hence the `self.data = bytes(0)`. This will be problematic in the `DNSSECImpl.verifyRRSet()` function's `proof = rrset.data;` line of code. Since the bytes(0) will be returned from the function. And there is no validation check performed to eliminate this scenario.

## Proof of Concept

