## Recommended Mitigation Steps

It is recommended to use the same domain name representation format for both `domain name` provided by the `user` and the `domain name` stored in the `RRSet`. Hence either should use `DNS wire format` or `DNS label-sequence format` for both occassions.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/45ea10bacb2a398e14d711fe28d1738271cd7640/contracts/dnsregistrar/OffchainDNSResolver.sol#L104


# Vulnerability details

## Description

In OffchainDNSResolver, `resolveCallback` parses resource  records received off-chain and extracts the DNS resolver address:
```
		// Look for a valid ENS-DNS TXT record
		(address dnsresolver, bytes memory context) = parseRR(
			iter.data,
			iter.rdataOffset,
			iter.nextOffset
		);
```

The contract supports three methods of resolution through the resolver:
1. IExtendedDNSResolver.resolve
2. IExtendedResolver.resolve
3. Arbitrary call with the `query` parameter originating in `resolve()`

Code is below:
```
            // If we found a valid record, try to resolve it
            if (dnsresolver != address(0)) {
                if (
                    IERC165(dnsresolver).supportsInterface(
                        IExtendedDNSResolver.resolve.selector
                    )
                ) {
                    return
                        IExtendedDNSResolver(dnsresolver).resolve(
                            name,
                            query,
                            context
                        );
                } else if (
                    IERC165(dnsresolver).supportsInterface(
                        IExtendedResolver.resolve.selector
                    )
                ) {
                    return IExtendedResolver(dnsresolver).resolve(name, query);
                } else {
                    (bool ok, bytes memory ret) = address(dnsresolver)
                        .staticcall(query);
                    if (ok) {
                        return ret;
                    } else {
                        revert CouldNotResolve(name);
                    }
                }
            }
```

The issue is that a resolver could support option (3), but execution would revert prior to the `query` call. The function uses `supportsInterface` in an unsafe way. It should first check that the contract implements ERC165, which will guarantee the call won't revert. Dynamic resolvers are not likely in practice to implement ERC165 as there's no specific signature to support ahead of time.

## Impact

Resolution with custom DNS resolvers are likely to fail.

## Tools Used

Manual audit

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Use the OZ `ERC165Checker.sol` library, which [addresses](https://github.com/OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts/blob/f959d7e4e6ee0b022b41e5b644c79369869d8411/contracts/utils/introspection/ERC165Checker.sol#L36) the issue:
```
    function supportsInterface(address account, bytes4 interfaceId) internal view returns (bool) {
        // query support of both ERC165 as per the spec and support of _interfaceId
        return supportsERC165(account) && supportsERC165InterfaceUnchecked(account, interfaceId);
    }
```

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/main/contracts/dnssec-oracle/BytesUtils.sol#L300-L318


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The `BytesUtils.substring` function accepts out of bound `offset` value and returns a valid response without reverting.
```solidity
    function substring(
        bytes memory self,
        uint256 offset,
        uint256 len
    ) internal pure returns (bytes memory) {
        require(offset + len <= self.length);

        bytes memory ret = new bytes(len);
        uint256 dest;
        uint256 src;
        assembly {
            dest := add(ret, 32)
            src := add(add(self, 32), offset)
        }
        memcpy(dest, src, len);
        return ret;
    }
```

The function contains a `require` statement which validates `offset + len <= self.length` condition but it does not revert when `offset` is provided equal to the `self.length` and the `len` is provided as `0`.

This is an unhandled edge case and a bug. The `substring` function is used at various places throughout the protocol. The contracts interacting with the `substring` function assumes that the function will revert on invalid inputs. Hence the non-reverting flow in the edge case breaks that assumption of other contracts and may lead to serious issues for ENS protocol.

## Proof of Concept
A mock contract was created to expose the `substring` function:
```solidity
pragma solidity ^0.8.4;
import "./BytesUtils.sol";

contract AuditBytesUtils {
    function substring(
        bytes memory self,
        uint256 offset,
        uint256 len
    ) public pure returns (bytes memory) {
        return BytesUtils.substring(self, offset, len);
    }
}
```

A new test file was created and ran using `yarn test path-to-file`
```javascript
