https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/45ea10bacb2a398e14d711fe28d1738271cd7640/contracts/dnsregistrar/OffchainDNSResolver.sol#L49-L63


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
`resolve` function is marked as `external` `view` and takes two bytes `calldata` parameters, `name` and `data`. However, the `data` parameter is not being used in the function, which may cause confusion for future developers or auditors reviewing the contract.

Furthermore, the `resolve` function is expected to revert when called, but its current implementation may not revert in all cases. This can lead to unexpected behavior, which can potentially be exploited by attackers.

## Proof of Concept
The issue is in the resolve function: [#L49-L63](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/45ea10bacb2a398e14d711fe28d1738271cd7640/contracts/dnsregistrar/OffchainDNSResolver.sol#L49-L63)
```solidity
    function resolve(
        bytes calldata name,
        bytes calldata data
    ) external view returns (bytes memory) {
        string[] memory urls = new string[](1);
        urls[0] = gatewayURL;


        revert OffchainLookup(
            address(this),
            urls,
            abi.encodeCall(IDNSGateway.resolve, (name, TYPE_TXT)),
            OffchainDNSResolver.resolveCallback.selector,
            abi.encode(name, data)
        );
    }
```
Let's look at this scenario:
An attacker can call the `resolve` function with arbitrary values for `name` and `data` parameters, and the function will revert as expected. However, if the attacker passes a non-empty value for the `data` parameter, the function will still revert, but the unused `data` parameter will not be included in the encoded function call that is passed to the `OffchainLookup` function.

## Tools Used

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The `resolve` function should be updated to either utilize the `data` parameter or remove it altogether. Furthermore, the function should be marked as `external` instead of `public` to clearly indicate that it can only be called from outside the contract.
Consider this Option:

The Original Code:
```solidity
    function resolve(
        bytes calldata name,
        bytes calldata data
    ) external view returns (bytes memory) {
        string[] memory urls = new string[](1);
        urls[0] = gatewayURL;


        revert OffchainLookup(
            address(this),
            urls,
            abi.encodeCall(IDNSGateway.resolve, (name, TYPE_TXT)),
            OffchainDNSResolver.resolveCallback.selector,
            abi.encode(name, data)
        );
    }
```

Updated Code:
```solidity
function resolve(bytes calldata name) external returns (bytes memory) {
  string[] memory urls = new string[](1);
  urls[0] = gatewayURL;

  revert OffchainLookup(
    address(this),
    urls,
    abi.encodeCall(IDNSGateway.resolve, (name, TYPE_TXT)),
    OffchainDNSResolver.resolveCallback.selector,
    abi.encode(name)
  );
}
```
In the updated code, the `data` parameter has been removed, and the function is marked as `external`.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/main/contracts/dnssec-oracle/RRUtils.sol#L329-L339


# Vulnerability details

# Impact

[RRUtils.serialNumberGte()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/45ea10bacb2a398e14d711fe28d1738271cd7640/contracts/dnssec-oracle/RRUtils.sol#L329-L339) implements RFC1982 based on comments, according to [RFC1982 docs](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1982), serial numbers are to be unsigned 32 bit integers - these numbers should wrap. This function casts `uint32` as `int32` @ [RRUtils.sol#L337](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/45ea10bacb2a398e14d711fe28d1738271cd7640/contracts/dnssec-oracle/RRUtils.sol#L337) in an `unchecked` block, which seems to conform to the spec but results in bad comparisons that do not revert. 

```solidity
/**
    * @dev Compares two serial numbers using RFC1982 serial number math.
    */
function serialNumberGte(
    uint32 i1,
    uint32 i2
) internal pure returns (bool) {
    unchecked {
        return int32(i1) - int32(i2) >= 0;
    }
}
```

The serialNumberGte() function is used to validate `RRSet` `expiration` and `inception` in [DNSSECImpl.sol#L154-L168](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/45ea10bacb2a398e14d711fe28d1738271cd7640/contracts/dnssec-oracle/DNSSECImpl.sol#L154-L168) as well as `inception` values in [DNSRegistrar.sol#L152-L154](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/45ea10bacb2a398e14d711fe28d1738271cd7640/contracts/dnsregistrar/DNSRegistrar.sol#L152-L154). 

Due to this, proofs that should be rejected due to their `inception` or `expiration` datetime's are not reverted. 

