# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/main/contracts/dnsregistrar/RecordParser.sol#L14-L40


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
RecordParser.readKeyValue will revert when offset is not 0 and there is no more " " sign

## Proof of Concept
`RecordParser.readKeyValue` function should read key and value from the bytes array. Function also receives `offset` and `len` variables, which are offset from where you need to start and length of array.
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/main/contracts/dnsregistrar/RecordParser.sol#L14-L40
```solidity
    function readKeyValue(
        bytes memory input,
        uint256 offset,
        uint256 len
    )
        internal
        pure
        returns (bytes memory key, bytes memory value, uint256 nextOffset)
    {
        uint256 separator = input.find(offset, len, "=");
        if (separator == type(uint256).max) {
            return ("", "", type(uint256).max);
        }


        uint256 terminator = input.find(
            separator,
            len + offset - separator,
            " "
        );
        if (terminator == type(uint256).max) {
            terminator = input.length;
        }


        key = input.substring(offset, separator - offset);
        value = input.substring(separator + 1, terminator - separator - 1);
        nextOffset = terminator + 1;
    }
```

The problem is inside finding `terminator`, which is space sign. To find it function uses `input.find` and provides `separator`, which is offset of `=` sign and `len + offset - separator` as amount of bytes that should be checked for space sign.

In case if `offset` param is 0, then this function will work fine, but when it's not, then `len + offset - separator` value will be bigger than `len - separator` and in case if bytes array is `len` length long, then `input.find` will revert as function will try to access elements [that are out of bounds of array](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/main/contracts/dnssec-oracle/BytesUtils.sol#L394).

RecordParser.readKeyValue function is not used currently by any other code. However, as it's in the scope, i suppose that it will be used later and can create problems for the protocol. That's why i suppose that it can be medium level bug. 
## Tools Used
VsCode
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Use this to finds terminator: 
```solidity
uint256 terminator = input.find(
            separator,
            len - separator,
            " "
        );
```

