# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In `DNSSECImpl`, checking RRSIG RR validity is done via the `verifyRRSet` function.

According to [`RFC4035`,  section 5.3.1. (Checking the RRSIG RR Validity)](https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc4035.txt) the first required check is:

```
   o  The RRSIG RR and the RRset MUST have the same owner name and the same class.
```

In code, this check is not done. Current logic only ensures that all RRs have the same name (the `validateRRs` function):

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/main/contracts/dnssec-oracle/DNSSECImpl.sol#L196-L207
```Solidity
   if (name.length == 0) {
         name = iter.name();
   } else {
         // Name must be the same on all RRs. We do things this way to avoid copying the name
         // repeatedly.
         if (
            name.length != iter.data.nameLength(iter.offset) ||
            !name.equals(0, iter.data, iter.offset, name.length)
         ) {
            revert InvalidRRSet();
         }
   }
```

and then _sets_ the owner name to this value, instead of comparing with it:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/main/contracts/dnssec-oracle/DNSSECImpl.sol#L152
```Solidity
        rrset.name = name;
```

This is a check to be done, not an enforcement to be applied.

`verifyRRSet`'s input data is bundled in a `RRSetWithSignature` which contain the raw data of a RRset (in `rrset`)

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/main/contracts/dnssec-oracle/DNSSEC.sol#L8-L11
```Solidity
    struct RRSetWithSignature {
        bytes rrset;
        bytes sig;
    }
```

Input should include the RRSIG owner name in order to validate it as per `RFC4035`. As it is, owner name validation does not exist.

This issues portraits to incorrect protocol business logic implementation, something to be careful of, as requested by the protocol team:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens
> - Describe any specific areas you would like addressed. E.g. Please try to break XYZ.": Accuracy of DNSSEC implementation compared to the standard, any failures to check critical conditions.

## Proof of Concept

Following is an example of an RRSIG record ([source](https://docs.infoblox.com/space/NAG8/22252229/RRSIG+Resource+Records)) that shows what elements are needed to be provided and validated:

![RRSIG record](https://docs.infoblox.com/download/attachments/22252229/Arrows5.png)

In the current codebase the owner name element is not passed anywhere in order for it to be compared. It is only set to the `RRset` name.

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-ens/blob/main/contracts/dnssec-oracle/DNSSECImpl.sol#L152
```Solidity
        rrset.name = name;
```

## Tools Used

Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Pass `RRSIG` `RR` owner name and have it verified with the `RRset` names to complete validation.

