# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Users will be able to claim and verify expired `RRsets` in about 15 years from now, breaking an invariant where the current time must be less than or equal to the expiration field.

Users can also break a similar invariant where the current time must be greater or equal than the `RRset` inception time.

## Proof of Concept

When an `RRSet` is validated, there is a check to ensure that the current time is less than or equal to the expiration field:
```solidity
// o  The validator's notion of the current time MUST be less than or
//    equal to the time listed in the RRSIG RR's Expiration field.
if (!RRUtils.serialNumberGte(rrset.expiration, uint32(now))) {
    revert SignatureExpired(rrset.expiration, uint32(now));
}
```

However, the comparison will break with the current values, as there will be an overflow if `i2 > int32.max`:

```solidity
function serialNumberGte(
    uint32 i1,
    uint32 i2
) internal pure returns (bool) {
    unchecked {
        return int32(i1) - int32(i2) >= 0;
    }
}
```

If the current timestamp is `2147483649` (Jan 19 2038) users will be able to validate the `RRset` even if it's actually expired, by setting the expiration to zero.

There is a similar issue with `inception`, as users are currently able to set a date far in the future (e.g `4294967295` Feb 07 2106), which will be considered valid for the same reason:

```solidity
// o  The validator's notion of the current time MUST be greater than or
//    equal to the time listed in the RRSIG RR's Inception field.
if (!RRUtils.serialNumberGte(uint32(now), rrset.inception)) {
    revert SignatureNotValidYet(rrset.inception, uint32(now));
}
```

## Tools Used
Remix

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Consider modifying the `serialNumberGte` function to avoid overflows when wrapping:

```solidity
function serialNumberGte(
    uint32 i1,
    uint32 i2
) internal pure returns (bool) {
    unchecked {
        return int64(uint64(i1)) - int64(uint64(i2)) >= 0;
    }
}
```

