# Vulnerability details

## Impact
This bug allows users to lose tokens when attempting a legitimate ERC20-to-NFT swap via a `PrivatePool` pool. If one deploys a `PrivatePool` via the factory contract whereby the base token is a "silent ERC20" (an ERC20 token that returns `false` rather than reverting when it's unable to transfer) users may lose tokens when swapping via the pool's `sell` method.

## Proof of Concept
**Note on the "direct interaction" warning:**

While comments **do warn** against swapping directly the same loss could occur when swapping through a router contract if the router implementation relies on the values returned by the `PrivatePool` and does not explicitly verify the returned ERC20 balance, similar to how the existing `EthRouter` operates.

**Actual PoC:**

Add the following test to `test/Factory/Create.t.sol` running it with `forge test --mt test_PoC_silentTokenAllowsLoss -vvvv` and deploying [this "silent ERC20" implementation](https://gist.github.com/Philogy/b0c360ec0eb11eb003393f5f0b86b1a5) as `silentToken` in the test file's `setuUp` method:
```solidity
    function test_PoC_silentTokenAllowsLoss() public {
        address poolOwner = makeAddr("PoolOwner");

        vm.prank(poolOwner);
        PrivatePool pool = factory.create(
            address(silentToken),
            address(milady),
            10e18,
            10e18,
            0,
            0,
            bytes32(0),
            false,
            false,
            bytes32(0),
            new uint[](0),
            0
        );

        // Enough for 1 sell but not 2.
        silentToken.mint(address(pool), 1.5e18);

        address user = makeAddr("user");

        // Define tokens to be traded
        uint256[] memory tokens = new uint[](2);
        tokens[0] = 1;
        tokens[1] = 2;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokens.length; i++) {
            milady.transferFrom(address(this), user, tokens[i]);
        }

        PrivatePool.MerkleMultiProof memory emptyProof = PrivatePool.MerkleMultiProof(new bytes32[](0), new bool[](0));
        uint256 totalWeight = pool.sumWeightsAndValidateProof(tokens, new uint[](0), emptyProof);
        (uint256 expectedOut,,) = pool.sellQuote(totalWeight);
        console2.log(unicode"Expected 🤔: %d", expectedOut);
        assertEq(silentToken.balanceOf(user), 0, "User shouldn't start with tokens");

        // Prepare & Attempt Swap
        vm.startPrank(user);
        milady.setApprovalForAll(address(pool), true);
        (uint256 reportedOut,,) = pool.sell(tokens, new uint[](0), emptyProof, new IStolenNftOracle.Message[](0));
        vm.stopPrank();
        // The call didn't revert and the `reportedOut` is as expected, all good right?
        assertEq(reportedOut, expectedOut);
        // Nope, user still has no tokens because transfer silently failed
        assertEq(silentToken.balanceOf(user), 0);
        // However the pool did take, and will keep the user's NFTs:
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < tokens.length; i++) {
            assertEq(milady.ownerOf(tokens[i]), address(pool));
        }
    }
```

The test shows that it's possible to have an entirely valid swap with a compliant ERC20 token that leads to unexpected user loss.

## Tools Used
Manual review.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Check the return value of ERC20 `transfer` / `transferFrom` functions everywhere. Replace [`ERC20(baseToken).transfer(msg.sender, netOutputAmount);`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-caviar/blob/main/src/PrivatePool.sol#L365) with `ERC20(baseToken).safeTransfer(msg.sender, netOutputAmount);`.

