# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The EthRouter provides an interface for buying or selling from multiple pools at the same time. However, there is no security preventing users from selling to pools which have a `baseToken` which is an ERC20 token. Thus, when the user sells their NFT using the EthRouter to such a pool, the funds get stuck in the EthRouter contract and there is no function to rescue them. In addition to this, the user also loses the NFTs that they sold to the pool

## Proof of Concept
I've create a POC of this issue using forge below. This can be added to the `EthRouter/Sell.t.sol` file

```solidity
function _addSellErc20() internal returns (EthRouter.Sell memory, uint256) {
    uint256[] memory empty = new uint256[](0);
    shibaInu.approve(address(factory), 100e18);
    privatePool = factory.create(
        address(shibaInu),
        address(milady),
        100e18,
        10e18,
        200,
        199,
        bytes32(0),
        true,
        false,
        bytes32(shibaInu.balanceOf(address(this))), // random between each call to _addBuy
        empty,
        100e18
    );

    uint256[] memory tokenIds = new uint256[](2);
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        milady.mint(address(this), i + totalTokens);
        tokenIds[i] = i + totalTokens;
    }

    totalTokens += 2;

    bytes32[][] memory publicPoolProofs = new bytes32[][](0);
    EthRouter.Sell memory sell = EthRouter.Sell({
        pool: payable(address(privatePool)),
        nft: address(milady),
        tokenIds: tokenIds,
        tokenWeights: new uint256[](0),
        proof: PrivatePool.MerkleMultiProof(new bytes32[](0), new bool[](0)),
        stolenNftProofs: new IStolenNftOracle.Message[](0),
        isPublicPool: false,
        publicPoolProofs: publicPoolProofs
    });

    (uint256 baseTokenAmount,,) = privatePool.sellQuote(tokenIds.length * 1e18);
    return (sell, baseTokenAmount);
}

function test_StuckERC20Tokens() public {
    // arrange
    deal(address(shibaInu), address(this), 200e18);
    EthRouter.Sell[] memory sells = new EthRouter.Sell[](2);
    (EthRouter.Sell memory sell1, uint256 outputAmount1) = _addSellErc20();
    (EthRouter.Sell memory sell2, uint256 outputAmount2) = _addSellErc20();
    minOutputAmount += outputAmount1 + outputAmount2;
    sells[0] = sell1;
    sells[1] = sell2;
    uint256 ethRouterBalanceBefore = shibaInu.balanceOf(address(ethRouter));
    console.log("user NFT balance before:", milady.balanceOf(address(this)));

    // act
    // User passes in minOutputAmount = 0
    ethRouter.sell(sells, 0, 0, false);

    uint256 userBalanceAfter = shibaInu.balanceOf(address(this));
    console.log("ethRouter balance:", shibaInu.balanceOf(address(ethRouter)));
    console.log("user balance:", userBalanceAfter);
    // assert
    // Assets are stuck in the eth router
    assertEq(
        shibaInu.balanceOf(address(ethRouter)) - ethRouterBalanceBefore, minOutputAmount, "Should have transferred output amount to caller"
    );
    assertEq(
        userBalanceAfter, 0, "Should have transferred output amount to caller"
    );

    console.log("user NFT balance after:", milady.balanceOf(address(this)));
}
```

These tests should not pass, but they do. The logs are shown below: 
```sh
  user NFT balance before: 4
  ethRouter SHIB balance after: 32670000000000000000
  user SHIB balance after: 0
  user NFT balance after: 0
```

As you can see, the behaviour is unexpected and all the tokens for the sale end up in the `EthRouter` contract. Since there is no functionality in the contract to resolve this issue, the funds are stuck and the users NFTs are gone.

## Tools Used
VS-code, foundry

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The EthRouter contract wants to concern itself with only private pools that use ETH as the base token. This is clear from the `buy` function not having any functionality to transfer ERC20 tokens. However, `sell` is capable of interacting with any pool regardless of `baseToken`. I'd suggest reverting the `sell` function of EthRouter if the `baseToken` of the pool is not ETH

```solidity
function sell(Sell[] calldata sells, uint256 minOutputAmount, uint256 deadline, bool payRoyalties) public {
    // check that the deadline has not passed (if any)
    if (block.timestamp > deadline && deadline != 0) {
        revert DeadlinePassed();
    }

    // loop through and execute the sells
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < sells.length; i++) {
        /*...*/
        } else {
            PrivatePool pool = PrivatePool(sells[i].pool);
            require(pool.baseToken() == address(0), "Router: base token must be ETH");
            // execute the sell against a private pool
            pool.sell(
                sells[i].tokenIds, sells[i].tokenWeights, sells[i].proof, sells[i].stolenNftProofs
            );
        }
    }
    /*...*/
}
```

This reverts as expected using my tests

