## Tools Used

Manual Analysis, Foundry



## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Before transferring feeAmount from msg.sender to the pool in the change function, there should be a check to confirm that the fee amount is greater than 0. This would prevent any zero transfers from occurring and allow the change() function to work as intended with tokens that revert on zero transfers.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-caviar/blob/main/src/PrivatePool.sol#L256


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
When a fee on transfer token is used as the base token, the owner of the pool will lose yield by using a part of the amount owed to pay the fee. Every time the buy() function is called using this type of base token, the pool's value will decrease. This means when a pool owner attempts to withdraw assets from the pool, they could be left with much less of the base token than what they put in while having the same amount or less NFTs from when they started.

In the worst-case scenario, a bad actor can repeatedly buy and sell NFTs, forcing the pool to pay the fee on buy(), which would slowly drain the pool. If there were no fees or royalties (or royalties go to the bad actor), then there would be no IL. The bad actor would need to pay the fees on the sell side of things so they could only drain as much as they are willing to lose. An extremely motivated attacker could drain virtually all the base tokens and all but one NFT in the current contract.

That is the worst-case scenario. Unlikely but devastating. The much more likely/inevitable case is that the pool owner will be the one paying the transaction fee whenever someone buys an NFT, leading to a loss of funds.

There is nothing preventing fee on transfer tokens from being used as base tokens. And there is nothing in the documentation that prohibits the use. It is very likely that a token like usdt will be used and when it is. This will occur. 
## Proof of Concept
By running this function, you will see that the balance in the pool is less than the inputted amount with fees and royalties being 0. The inputted amount is supposed to be how much the owner or private pool is receiving from the buyer. Because the amount in the pool is less than the inputted amount, this means that the owner effectively paid the transfer fee out of their earnings. This is a loss of funds/yield for the owner.


The easiest way to test this is to add this code snippet into ShibaInu.sol
``` solidity
file: ShibaInu.sol


contract TUSD is ERC20 {
    uint immutable fee;

    constructor() ERC20("Tether USD", "tUSD", 18) {
        fee = 1;
    }

    // --- Token ---
    function transferFrom(
        address src,
        address dst,
        uint wad
    ) public override returns (bool) {
        require(balanceOf[src] >= wad, "insufficient-balance");
        if (src != msg.sender && allowance[src][msg.sender] != type(uint).max) {
            require(
                allowance[src][msg.sender] >= wad,
                "insufficient-allowance"
            );
            allowance[src][msg.sender] = (allowance[src][msg.sender] - wad);
        }

        balanceOf[src] = (balanceOf[src] - wad);
        balanceOf[dst] = balanceOf[dst] + (wad - fee);
        balanceOf[address(0)] = (balanceOf[address(0)] + fee);

        return true;
    }

    function transfer(address dst, uint wad) public override returns (bool) {
        balanceOf[msg.sender] = (balanceOf[msg.sender] - wad);
        balanceOf[dst] = balanceOf[dst] + (wad - fee);
        balanceOf[address(0)] = (balanceOf[address(0)] + fee);

        return true;
    }
}
```

