# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-05-venus/blob/8be784ed9752b80e6f1b8b781e2e6251748d0d7e/contracts/Comptroller.sol#L324-L380
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-05-venus/blob/8be784ed9752b80e6f1b8b781e2e6251748d0d7e/contracts/Comptroller.sol#L354-L356


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
In the [preBorrowHook function](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-05-venus/blob/8be784ed9752b80e6f1b8b781e2e6251748d0d7e/contracts/Comptroller.sol#L324-L380), if the `borrowCap` is less than the total amount of borrowed tokens, the transaction is reverted, and this can lead to a potential vulnerability where an attacker can borrow tokens, which can cause the `borrowCap` to be exceeded, and then transfer the tokens to another address, making it impossible to repay the borrowed tokens.
```solidity
function preBorrowHook(
        address vToken,
        address borrower,
        uint256 borrowAmount
    ) external override {
        _checkActionPauseState(vToken, Action.BORROW);

        oracle.updatePrice(vToken);

        if (!markets[vToken].isListed) {
            revert MarketNotListed(address(vToken));
        }

        if (!markets[vToken].accountMembership[borrower]) {
            // only vTokens may call borrowAllowed if borrower not in market
            _checkSenderIs(vToken);

            // attempt to add borrower to the market or revert
            _addToMarket(VToken(msg.sender), borrower);
        }

        if (oracle.getUnderlyingPrice(vToken) == 0) {
            revert PriceError(address(vToken));
        }

        uint256 borrowCap = borrowCaps[vToken];
        // Skipping the cap check for uncapped coins to save some gas
        if (borrowCap != type(uint256).max) {
            uint256 totalBorrows = VToken(vToken).totalBorrows();
            uint256 nextTotalBorrows = totalBorrows + borrowAmount;
            if (nextTotalBorrows > borrowCap) {
                revert BorrowCapExceeded(vToken, borrowCap);
            }
        }

        AccountLiquiditySnapshot memory snapshot = _getHypotheticalLiquiditySnapshot(
            borrower,
            VToken(vToken),
            0,
            borrowAmount,
            _getCollateralFactor
        );

        if (snapshot.shortfall > 0) {
            revert InsufficientLiquidity();
        }

        Exp memory borrowIndex = Exp({ mantissa: VToken(vToken).borrowIndex() });

        // Keep the flywheel moving
        uint256 rewardDistributorsCount = rewardsDistributors.length;

        for (uint256 i; i < rewardDistributorsCount; ++i) {
            rewardsDistributors[i].updateRewardTokenBorrowIndex(vToken, borrowIndex);
            rewardsDistributors[i].distributeBorrowerRewardToken(vToken, borrower, borrowIndex);
        }
    }
```
If the `borrowCap` is not set to the maximum value, the function checks if the `nextTotalBorrows` is greater than `borrowCap`. If it is, the function reverts with the `BorrowCapExceeded` error.

If the code that sets the `borrowCap` value is vulnerable, an attacker could set the `borrowCap` value to a lower amount, tricking the `preBorrowHook` function into believing that there is enough room to borrow tokens. The attacker could then proceed to borrow tokens and cause the `nextTotalBorrows` value to exceed the `borrowCap`. This could lead to the `preBorrowHook` function reverting and the transaction failing.

It occurs specifically in the `if` statement starting on line [354:](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-05-venus/blob/8be784ed9752b80e6f1b8b781e2e6251748d0d7e/contracts/Comptroller.sol#L354-L356)
```solidity
if (nextTotalBorrows > borrowCap) {
    revert BorrowCapExceeded(vToken, borrowCap);
}
```
This `if` statement checks if the `nextTotalBorrows` value, which is the total amount of borrows after the current borrow operation is completed, is greater than the `borrowCap` value, which is the maximum amount of borrows allowed for this market. If the `nextTotalBorrows` value is greater than the borrowCap value, the function reverts and the transaction fails with the `BorrowCapExceeded` error.

The vulnerability occurs if an attacker is able to manipulate the `borrowCap` value to be lower than the actual amount of borrowed tokens. This would allow the attacker to borrow tokens up to the manipulated `borrowCap` limit and cause the `nextTotalBorrows` value to exceed the `borrowCap`, causing the function to revert with the `BorrowCapExceeded` error.

Therefore, it is crucial to ensure that the code that sets the `borrowCap` value is secure and cannot be manipulated by an attacker.

## Proof of Concept
* Assume Alice has identified the vulnerability in the `preBorrowHook` function of the lending protocol. Alice has discovered that she can manipulate the `borrowCap` value to be lower than the actual amount of borrowed tokens, allowing her to borrow tokens beyond the `borrowCap` limit.

* Alice creates a new market in the lending protocol and sets the `borrowCap` value to 100 tokens, even though the market actually has a total of 1000 tokens available for borrowing.

* Alice then deposits 10 tokens into the market and proceeds to borrow 100 tokens. Since the `borrowCap` is set to 100 tokens, the `preBorrowHook` function does not revert the transaction and allows Alice to borrow 100 tokens.

* Next, Alice transfers the borrowed 100 tokens to a different address that she controls, making it impossible for her to repay the borrowed tokens.

* Now, the `nextTotalBorrows` value for the market is 100 tokens, which exceeds the `borrowCap` value of 100 tokens. When someone else tries to borrow tokens from the market, the `preBorrowHook` function will revert with the `BorrowCapExceeded` error, preventing any further borrowing in the market.

* Meanwhile, Alice is able to keep the 100 borrowed tokens without having to repay them, as the lending protocol cannot enforce the repayment due to the manipulated `borrowCap` value, and clearly this results in a loss for the lending protocol and its users.

## Tools Used
Manual
VsCode

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Include a check for the amount of tokens currently borrowed by the borrower in question, in addition to the `totalBorrows`. If the sum of the borrower's current borrowed tokens and the tokens to be borrowed exceed the `borrowCap`, the transaction should be reverted. This would ensure that an attacker cannot borrow tokens that cause the `borrowCap` to be exceeded and then transfer them to another address.


