# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-llama/blob/9d641b32e3f4092cc81dbac7b1c451c695e78983/src/LlamaCore.sol#L429


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
There is no option to remove the deprecated(or bad) strategies. As a result, malicious creators might use that strategy to execute the actions.

## Proof of Concept
When users create actions using `createAction()`, the strategy must be [whitelisted](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-llama/blob/9d641b32e3f4092cc81dbac7b1c451c695e78983/src/LlamaCore.sol#L526).

Also, there is a `createStrategies()` function to create new strategies.

But once the strategy is added, there is no option to remove it and it might break the governance system.

1. A strategy with the `approvalRole` and `minApprovals` was created and it worked properly for a while. This strategy didn't have a disapproval option.
2. After a certain time, many users hold the `approvalRole` with high quantities so only 1 role holder can approve an action by passing the `minApprovals` threshold.
3. So malicious users who have this `approvalRole`, can create an action and execute it more freely than before.

I understand `_createAction()` checks the [permission](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-llama/blob/9d641b32e3f4092cc81dbac7b1c451c695e78983/src/LlamaCore.sol#L539) that contains the strategy and the permission can be disabled using [setRolePermission()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-llama/blob/9d641b32e3f4092cc81dbac7b1c451c695e78983/src/LlamaPolicy.sol#L207).

```solidity
function _createAction(
    address policyholder,
    uint8 role,
    ILlamaStrategy strategy,
    address target,
    uint256 value,
    bytes calldata data,
    string memory description
  ) internal returns (uint256 actionId) {
    if (target == address(executor)) revert CannotSetExecutorAsTarget();
    if (!strategies[strategy]) revert InvalidStrategy();

    PermissionData memory permission = PermissionData(target, bytes4(data), strategy);
    bytes32 permissionId = keccak256(abi.encode(permission));

    // Typically (such as in Governor contracts) this should check that the caller has permission
    // at `block.number|timestamp - 1` but here we're just checking if the caller *currently* has
    // permission. Technically this introduces a race condition if e.g. an action to revoke a role
    // from someone (or revoke a permission from a role) is ready to be executed at the same time as
    // an action is created, as the order of transactions in the block then affects if action
    // creation would succeed. However, we are ok with this tradeoff because it means we don't need
    // to checkpoint the `canCreateAction` mapping which is simpler and cheaper, and in practice
    // this race condition is unlikely to matter.
    if (!policy.hasPermissionId(policyholder, role, permissionId)) revert PolicyholderDoesNotHavePermission();
    ...
  }
```

But this function should be called so many times to disable the bad strategy for each role/target/data. (at most 256 times for the role, 26 times for target/data which has the onlyLlama modifier, so thousands of times).

Furthermore, `setRolePermission()` can be called by executing the action and it's practically very hard to remove the strategy using this function.

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
I think we should add a function to remove the deprecated strategy.


