# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The StaderOracle contract is exposed to the risk of gas limit exceeded errors and a potential Denial-of-Service (DoS) vulnerability due to the handling of the sdPrices array. Additionally, the contract may suffer from stale price data when the strict condition `(submissionCount == (2 * trustedNodesCount) / 3 + 1)` is not met. 

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-stader/blob/main/contracts/StaderOracle.sol#L290-L297

```solidity
        if ((submissionCount == (2 * trustedNodesCount) / 3 + 1)) {
            lastReportedSDPriceData = _sdPriceData;
            lastReportedSDPriceData.sdPriceInETH = getMedianValue(sdPrices);
            delete sdPrices;

            // Emit SD Price updated event
            emit SDPriceUpdated(_sdPriceData.sdPriceInETH, _sdPriceData.reportingBlockNumber, block.number);
        }
```
These issues can lead to failed transactions, susceptibility to DoS attacks, and outdated price information. 

## Proof of Concept
To demonstrate the issues:

1. Invoke the submitSDPrice function multiple times, ensuring that `(submissionCount == (2 * trustedNodesCount) / 3 + 1)` is not strictly met.
2. Monitor the behavior of the `sdPrices array` and the `lastReportedSDPriceData.sdPriceInETH` value.
3. Notice that the `sdPrices` array retains its values without being reset, potentially growing unbounded, leading to DoS when calling function `insertSDPrice`

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-stader/blob/main/contracts/StaderOracle.sol#L300-L310

```solidity
    function insertSDPrice(uint256 _sdPrice) internal {
        sdPrices.push(_sdPrice);
        if (sdPrices.length == 1) return;

        uint256 j = sdPrices.length - 1;
        while ((j >= 1) && (_sdPrice < sdPrices[j - 1])) {
            sdPrices[j] = sdPrices[j - 1];
            j--;
        }
        sdPrices[j] = _sdPrice;
    }
```

4. Observe that `lastReportedSDPriceData.sdPriceInETH` remains unchanged, causing the reported price to be stale and not reflecting the latest submitted value via [`getMedianValue(sdPrices)`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-06-stader/blob/main/contracts/StaderOracle.sol#L312-L315).

## Tools Used
Manual

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider:

1. Implementing an alternative sorting algorithm: Replace the insertion sort algorithm in the `insertSDPrice` function with a more efficient sorting algorithm, such as merge sort or quicksort. These algorithms have a time complexity of O(n log n) and can handle larger arrays more effectively.

2. Limiting the size of the array: Evaluate the necessity of maintaining the entire history of prices in the `sdPrices` array. If it is not crucial, consider implementing a mechanism to limit the size of the array. For example, you could store only the most recent prices within a fixed-size circular buffer, discarding older entries as new ones are added.

3. Using a data structure that maintains sorted order: Explore the possibility of using a data structure specifically designed to maintain sorted order, such as a binary search tree or a skip list. These data structures offer efficient insertion and retrieval operations while automatically maintaining the sorted order.

4. Reporting mechanism enhancement: Consider implementing a mechanism to track and report stale price data. This can involve monitoring the last updated timestamp and displaying a warning message or requesting a new price update if the data becomes stale beyond a certain threshold.



