# Vulnerability details

`EigenPod::_sendETH()` is called for any withdraw action, but it needs the `EigenPod` contract to have Ether in order to send it.

There is no way for the contract to receive the Ether needed for the withdrawals. So, it will always revert.

## Impact

Users won't be able to withdraw Ether staked via the Beacon Chain strategy.

## Proof of Concept

Contracts need either a `receive` or a `fallback` function in order to receive Ether.

The `EigenPod` contract has none of them, nor does it have any function to receive and keep any Ether.

The contract inherits from `IEigenPod`, `Initializable`, `ReentrancyGuardUpgradeable`, and `EigenPodPausingConstants`. None of these contracts implement a `receive` or `fallback` function either.

The `_sendETH()` function depends on the contract to have Ether, as it tries to send `{value: amountWei}`:

```solidity
    function _sendETH(address recipient, uint256 amountWei) internal {
        delayedWithdrawalRouter.createDelayedWithdrawal{value: amountWei}(podOwner, recipient);
    }
```

[Link to code](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-eigenlayer/blob/main/src/contracts/pods/EigenPod.sol#L464-L466)

Any withdraw functions from the contract calls `_sendETH()` to perform the withdrawal, but they will all fail because of the lack of Ether balance:

```solidity
    // @audit-info showing `withdrawRestakedBeaconChainETH()`, but it's valid for all the other withdraw functions
    function withdrawRestakedBeaconChainETH(
        address recipient,
        uint256 amountWei
    )
        external
        onlyEigenPodManager
    {
        // reduce the restakedExecutionLayerGwei
        restakedExecutionLayerGwei -= uint64(amountWei / GWEI_TO_WEI);

        emit RestakedBeaconChainETHWithdrawn(recipient, amountWei);

        // transfer ETH from pod to `recipient`
        _sendETH(recipient, amountWei);
    }
```

[Link to code](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-eigenlayer/blob/main/src/contracts/pods/EigenPod.sol#L437-L451)

The only `payable` function that allows the contract to receive Ether is `stake()`, which requires `msg.value == 32 ether` and all is sent to the `ethPOS` contract via `ethPOS.deposit{value : 32 ether}`:

```solidity
    function stake(bytes calldata pubkey, bytes calldata signature, bytes32 depositDataRoot) external payable onlyEigenPodManager {
        // stake on ethpos
        require(msg.value == 32 ether, "EigenPod.stake: must initially stake for any validator with 32 ether");
        ethPOS.deposit{value : 32 ether}(pubkey, _podWithdrawalCredentials(), signature, depositDataRoot);
        emit EigenPodStaked(pubkey);
    }
```

[Link to code](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-eigenlayer/blob/main/src/contracts/pods/EigenPod.sol#L158-L163)

The tests files in the codebase use Foundry cheatcodes to `deal` Ether to the contract, so that's the reason they didn't fail:

```solidity
    //simulate a withdrawal
    cheats.deal(address(pod), stakeAmount);
```

[Link to code](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-eigenlayer/blob/main/src/test/EigenPod.t.sol#L216-L217)

## POC Test

This test shows the inability of a pod to receive Ether.

Add this test to `test/EigenPod.t.sol` and run `forge test -m "testCannotSendEtherToPod"`:

```solidity
    function testCannotSendEtherToPod() public {
        // ./solidityProofGen "ValidatorFieldsProof" 61511 true "data/slot_209635/oracle_capella_beacon_state_209635.ssz" "withdrawalCredentialAndBalanceProof_61511.json"
        setJSON("./src/test/test-data/slashedProofs/notOvercommittedBalanceProof_61511.json");
        IEigenPod newPod = _testDeployAndVerifyNewEigenPod(podOwner, signature, depositDataRoot);

        // Assert that the `newPod` doesn't have any balance
        assertEq(address(newPod).balance, 0);

        // Assert that this address has balance
        assertGt(address(this).balance, 1);

        // The pod contract cannot receive Ether
        (bool success,) = address(newPod).call{value: 1}("");
        assertEq(success, false);
        assertEq(address(newPod).balance, 0);
    }
```

## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Add a way for the `EigenPod` contract to receive the Ether needed for the withdrawals. Be it the `receive()` function, or any specific `payable` function for that purpose.


## Assessed type

ETH-Transfer

