# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The default behavior of the `PriceFeed` contract is to fallback to the latest reported price by the Chainlink and Tellor oracles it supports. 

This behavior is ill-advised as an oracle reporting its prices improperly indicates a significant and potentially fatal market event. Similar implementations in use during the LUNA price crash suffered from significant bad debt, including:
- Venus Protocol: https://blog.venus.io/venus-protocol-official-statement-regarding-luna-6eb45c3cb058
- Blizz Finance: https://rekt.news/venus-blizz-rekt/

To note, those protocols were affected because the Chainlink oracle was paused thereby reporting the same stale price over and over. The exact behavior would result in the same outcome in the Ethos Reserve ecosystem as an oracle would be considered "frozen" in such a case and cause the contract to yield the latest "good price".

## Proof of Concept

The code below demonstrates how a price will be "static" if the Chainlink oracle of a token fails:
```sol
contract ImproperStalePrice {
    uint256 public price;
    uint256 public lastReport;

    // This parameter can be arbitrarily configured and showcases that any amount of time can elapse
    uint256 private constant STALENESS_WINDOW = 1 hours;

    /**
     * Simplified Assumptions:
     *
     * - Contract Is Owner of `PriceFeed`
     */
    function demonstrate(address collateral, IPriceFeed feed) external {
        // Setup: Add our Chainlink aggregator to illustrate a pause status
        SampleChainlink oracle = new SampleChainlink();
        feed.updateChainlinkAggregator(collateral, address(oracle));

        // Step 1: Acquire a valid price report from the oracle
        price = feed.fetchPrice(collateral);
        lastReport = block.timestamp;

        // Step 2: Disable oracle
        oracle.disable();
    }

    function demonstrate2(address collateral, IPriceFeed feed) external {
        // Step 3: After time elapses, request a price again
        require(block.timestamp - lastReport >= STALENESS_WINDOW && lastReport >= 0, "Insufficient Time Elapsed");

        uint256 _price = feed.fetchPrice(collateral);
        require(_price == price, "ISSUE: Price Equates w/o Error");
    }
}

// Custom Chainlink w/ Disable
contract SampleChainlink {
    bool internal _disable;

    // This function will cause price reports to be regarded as incorrect by `PriceFeed::fetchPrice`
    function disable() external {
        _disable = true;
    }

    function decimals() external view returns (uint8) {
        return 18;
    }

    function latestRoundData() external view returns (
            uint80 roundId,
            int256 answer,
            uint256 startedAt,
            uint256 timestamp,
            uint80 answeredInRound
    ) {
        return (
            1, 
            1 ether, 
            block.timestamp, 
            _disable ? 0 : block.timestamp, 
            1
        );
    }

    function getRoundData(uint256 roundId_) external view returns (
            uint80 roundId,
            int256 answer,
            uint256 startedAt,
            uint256 timestamp,
            uint80 answeredInRound
    ) {
        return (
            roundId_, 
            1 ether, 
            block.timestamp, 
            _disable ? 0 : block.timestamp, 
            1
        );
    }
}
```

## Tools Used

Manual review.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

We advise a staleness check to be associated with the last reported price within the contract, ensuring that it is not utilized at all when the price staleness window has elapsed.

The protocol becoming unavailable is more desirable than its assets being affected in their entirety as bad debt would be arbitrarily created if the oracles of an asset stop reporting prices.

