# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The impact is critical because it enables anyone to submit as many signatures as they want manipulating the governance. The probe relies that in the delegateBySig function, the nonce it is not checked upon msg.sender, it is checked upon the output for the recover with the ECDSA library. If users re-input the v,r,s, parameters from other users and change the delegate, the ECDSA will not give an error and the function will pass and as there is no check that signer == msg.sender, this random function will pass and you would have managed to vote extra. This can be repeated an "infinite" amount of times. Will show better in the PoC.


        function delegateBySig(
        address delegatee,
        uint256 nonce,
        uint256 expiry,
        uint8 v,
        bytes32 r,
        bytes32 s
       ) public {
        require(block.timestamp <= expiry, "ERC20Votes: signature expired");
        //@audit uncheked that signaer is not address 0
        address signer = ECDSAUpgradeable.recover(
            _hashTypedDataV4(keccak256(abi.encode(_DELEGATE_TYPEHASH, delegatee, nonce, expiry))),
            v,
            r,
            s
         );
        require(nonce == _useNonce(signer), "ERC20Votes: invalid nonce");
        _delegate(signer, delegated);
         }

## Proof of Concept
1 User votes calling delegateBySig  (this step is optional because anyone can call with their own v,r,s values)
2Attacker goes to etherscan and takes those values and adds them as a parameter in the same function but changes the delegatee.
3 Nonce will always be 0 because the hash will be different and once it goes through recover, it gives a different address.
4 Done all checks have been passed, this exploit could be repeated as many times as wanted to manipulate votes.

Proof by image:
Note that both delegates are different and it does not fail meanwhile v,r,s and nonce are correct.
https://imgur.com/a/MFd3TTZ

## Tools Used
Manual

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
There is a lot to improve in the function. But the main thing would be to check whether msg.sender==signer. This check it is currently not done, which can lead to passing arbitrary addresses as I demonstrated.



        require(block.timestamp <= expiry, "ERC20Votes: signature expired");
        //@audit uncheked that signaer is not address 0
        address signer = ECDSAUpgradeable.recover(
            _hashTypedDataV4(keccak256(abi.encode(_DELEGATE_TYPEHASH, delegatee, nonce, expiry))),
            v,
            r,
            s
         );
        require(signer == msg.sender);  //new require that would "solve the issue"
        require(nonce == _useNonce(signer), "ERC20Votes: invalid nonce");
        _delegate(signer, delegatee);
