# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/plugins/assets/CTokenFiatCollateral.sol#L47


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

```
return shiftl_toFix(rate, shiftLeft);
```
It should be noted that the function "shiftl_toFix" is not defined in the contract and not imported from any library, this means that the compiler will throw an error when trying to deploy the contract and this function can't be called.

The `CTokenFiatCollateral` contract uses a function called "shiftl_toFix" in the "refPerTok()" function to calculate the quantity of whole reference units per whole collateral tokens. 

## Proof of Concept

Attempt to compile the `CTokenFiatCollateral` contract using a Solidity compiler, such as Truffle or Remix.

Observe that the compiler throws an error, indicating that the "shiftl_toFix" function is not defined in the contract and not imported from any library.

Attempt to deploy the contract on the Ethereum network but observe that it fails as the contract is not compiled successfully.

Attempt to call the `refPerTok()` function but observe that it throws an error as it calls the `shiftl_toFix` function which is not defined.

Observe that the contract cannot be used as intended, and users will be unable to interact with it.

```
pragma solidity 0.8.9;

import "./CTokenFiatCollateral.sol";

contract Exploit {
    CTokenFiatCollateral cTokenFiatCollateral;

    constructor() public {
        cTokenFiatCollateral = new CTokenFiatCollateral();
    }

    function refPerTok() public view returns (uint192) {
        return cTokenFiatCollateral.refPerTok();
    }
}
```

As you can see, the contract "Exploit" will fail when trying to call the `refPerTok()` function from `CTokenFiatCollateral` contract because it contains an undefined function `shiftl_toFix`.


## Tools Used

Remix, Manual audit, Vs Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Define the `shiftl_toFix` function within the `CTokenFiatCollateral` contract and provide an implementation for it.

```
function shiftl_toFix(uint256 x, uint8 y) internal pure returns (uint192) {
    return uint192(x) << y;
}
```

Use this newly defined function in the `refPerTok()` function instead of the undefined one.

```
function refPerTok() public view override returns (uint192) {
    uint256 rate = ICToken(address(erc20)).exchangeRateStored();
    int8 shiftLeft = 8 - int8(referenceERC20Decimals) - 18;
    return shiftl_toFix(rate, shiftLeft);
}
```

Re-compile and re-deploy the contract on the Ethereum network after making these changes.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/plugins/aave/StaticATokenLM.sol#L100


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

In the [`StaticATokenLM`](https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/plugins/aave/StaticATokenLM.sol#L100) contract, users are allowed to deposit either `AToken` (yield bearing tokens from AAVE), or their underlying tokens.
The amount is converted to shares systematically, even if the depositor deposits shares.
As shares are usually worth more than assets, a depositor who is depositing shares will receive less StaticATokenLM because they are treated as assets.

## Proof of Concept

In the following POC, it is shown that the amount of StaticATokens is not converted when sending ATokens directly, which is the case for the assets.
At the end of the test, the assertion `assertEq(sa.balanceOf(address(this)), 150 * 10**9);` fails. The actual value is `100 * 10**9`.

```solidity
contract StaticTest is Test {
    function testDeposit() public {
        FakeAsset fa = new FakeAsset();
        FakeAToken ft = new FakeAToken();
        ft.setAsset(address(fa));

        AaveLendingPoolMock lp = new AaveLendingPoolMock(address(0));
        // 200%
        lp.setNormalizedIncome(address(fa), 2e18);
        StaticATokenLM sa = new StaticATokenLM(ILendingPool(address(lp)), address(ft), "", "");
        ft.mint(address(this), 100);
        fa.mint(address(this), 100);

        ft.approve(address(sa), type(uint256).max);
        fa.approve(address(sa), type(uint256).max);

        // We deposit in underlying directly, should take 100 and give 50
        // because the rate is 200% right now
        sa.deposit(address(this), 100, 0, true);
        assertEq(sa.balanceOf(address(this)), 50 * 10**9);

        // Depositing in ATokens, should be 1:1
        sa.deposit(address(this), 100, 0, false);
        assertEq(sa.balanceOf(address(this)), 150 * 10**9);
    }
}
```

Note: the full POC is available on this [gist](https://gist.github.com/iFrostizz/00efcc35c5bbecf867965b76f857ef96). It was trimmed for readability.

## Tools Used

Manual inspection

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Handle the deposit from `AToken` without converting them

```solidity
function _deposit(
    address depositor,
    address recipient,
    uint256 amount,
    uint16 referralCode,
    bool fromUnderlying
) internal returns (uint256) {
    require(recipient != address(0), StaticATokenErrors.INVALID_RECIPIENT);
    _updateRewards();

	uint256 amountToMint;

    if (fromUnderlying) {
        ASSET.safeTransferFrom(depositor, address(this), amount);
        LENDING_POOL.deposit(address(ASSET), amount, address(this), referralCode);
	amountToMint = _dynamicToStaticAmount(amount, rate());
    } else {
        ATOKEN.safeTransferFrom(depositor, address(this), amount);
	amountToMint = amount;
    }

    _mint(recipient, amountToMint);

    return amountToMint;
}
```

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/Distributor.sol#L61-L65


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Detailed description of the impact of this finding.
The ``distribute()`` function might suffer from serious rounding error since it calculates ``tokensPerShare`` that has a division rounding error, and then use ``uint256 transferAmt = tokensPerShare * numberOfShares;`` to calculate the ``transferAmt`` for each destination. In the worst case, ``tokensPerShare = 0`` and as a result, there is no distribution at all!

``
## Proof of Concept
Provide direct links to all referenced code in GitHub. Add screenshots, logs, or any other relevant proof that illustrates the concept.
The main problem of ``distribute()`` is that it calculates ``tokensPerShare`` first, which introduces a rounding error, and then use it to calculate ``transferAmt`` based on the following line:
```
uint256 transferAmt = tokensPerShare * numberOfShares;

```
This is a standard division-followed-by-multiplication rounding error. It should be corrected by multiplication-followed-by-division. 

To see this: suppose we need to distribute 9999 RSR tokens to 10,000 shares to Alice and Bob, each of them has 5000 shares:
1) tokensPerShare = 9999/10000 = 0; 
2)  transferAmt = tokensPerShare * numberOfShares = 0 * 5000 = 0
3) As a result, both Alice and Bob will receive zero RST tokens, although we expect they will each receive 4999.5. 
RSR tokens. 

We need to use the multiplication-followed-by-division to correct this: 
```
uint256 transferAmt =  amount*numberOfShares/totalShares;

```


## Tools Used
Remix

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Reimplement it as follows using the multiplication-followed-by-division to calculate distribution amount for each destination. 

```
function distribute(
        IERC20 erc20,
        address from,
        uint256 amount
    ) external notPausedOrFrozen {
        require(erc20 == rsr || erc20 == rToken, "RSR or RToken");
        bool isRSR = erc20 == rsr; // if false: isRToken

        {
            RevenueTotals memory revTotals = totals();
            uint256 totalShares = isRSR ? revTotals.rsrTotal : revTotals.rTokenTotal;
            require(totalShares > 0, "nothing to distribute");
        }

        // Evenly distribute revenue tokens per distribution share.
        // This rounds "early", and that's deliberate!

        Transfer[] memory transfers = new Transfer[](destinations.length());
        uint256 numTransfers;

        for (uint256 i = 0; i < destinations.length(); ++i) {
            address addrTo = destinations.at(i);

            uint256 numberOfShares = isRSR
                ? distribution[addrTo].rsrDist
                : distribution[addrTo].rTokenDist;
            if (numberOfShares == 0) continue;
            uint256 transferAmt = amount*numberOfShares/totalShares;

            if (addrTo == FURNACE) {
                addrTo = furnace;
            } else if (addrTo == ST_RSR) {
                addrTo = stRSR;
            }

            transfers[numTransfers] = Transfer({
                erc20: erc20,
                addrTo: addrTo,
                amount: transferAmt
            });
            numTransfers++;
        }
        emit RevenueDistributed(erc20, from, amount);

        // == Interactions ==
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < numTransfers; i++) {
            Transfer memory t = transfers[i];
            IERC20Upgradeable(address(t.erc20)).safeTransferFrom(from, t.addrTo, t.amount);
        }
    }
```

