## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Change the formula so that we first calculate the 'base' (i.e. the min amount of baskets the RToken can satisfy without trading):
```
base = basketsHeldBy(backingManager) // in the PoC's case it'd be 95
(diffLowValue, diffHighValue) = (0,0) 
for each collateral token:
    diff = collateralBalance - basketHandler.quantity(base) 
    (diffLowValue, diffHighValue) = diff * (priceLow, priceHigh)
addBasketsLow = diffLowValue / basketPriceHigh
addBasketHigh = diffHighValue / basketPriceLow
range.top = base + addBasketHigh
range.bottom = base + addBasketLow
```

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/interfaces/ITrading.sol#L49


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

```
/// Settle a single trade, expected to be used with multicall for efficient mass settlement
    /// @custom:refresher
    function settleTrade(IERC20 sell) external;
```
This function takes in only a single IERC20 token as a parameter, with no other information about the trade. This could lead to unintended trades being settled if it is possible for multiple trades to be open for the same sell token.


## Proof of Concept

Assume there are two open trades, Trade A and Trade B, that both involve the same sell token.

Trade A has the address of `0x123456` and is between `msg.sender` and another address.

Trade B has the address of `0x789012` and is between `msg.sender` and another address.

An attacker calls the `settleTrade()` function with the address of the sell token used in both Trade A and Trade B.

Since the function takes in only a single IERC20 token as a parameter, with no other information about the trade, it will settle any trade that is open for 
the token passed as an argument.

The attacker can then choose which trade he wants to settle and can call the function multiple times to settle the trade he wants to.

```
function exploit() public {
    // Assume Trade A has the address of 0x123456 and Trade B has the address of 0x789012
    address sellToken = ERC20_TOKEN_ADDRESS; // address of the sell token used in both trades
    ITrade tradeA = ITrade(0x123456);
    ITrade tradeB = ITrade(0x789012);
    // Checking the trade details before exploiting
    require(tradeA.sell() == sellToken, "Trade A doesn't involve the sell token");
    require(tradeB.sell() == sellToken, "Trade B doesn't involve the sell token");
    // Settle Trade A
    settleTrade(sellToken);
    // Check that Trade A is settled
    require(tradeA.isSettled(), "Trade A is not settled");
    // Settle Trade B
    settleTrade(sellToken);
    // Check that Trade B is settled
    require(tradeB.isSettled(), "Trade B is not settled");
}
```

`settleTrade()` function can be exploited by an attacker to settle any trade that is open for the same sell token, regardless of the trade's details, which could lead to unintended consequences such as the wrong trade being `settled`, or the wrong quantities of tokens being transferred.

## Tools Used

Manual audit, vs code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Add the address of the trade contract as a parameter. This would allow the function to directly access the trade contract and settle it.

```
function settleTrade(IERC20 sell, address trade) external {
    ITrade iTrade = ITrade(trade);
    require(iTrade.isOpen(), "Trade is already settled");
    // Settle the trade
    iTrade.settle();
}
```

Use a unique identifier for the trade as a parameter. This would allow the function to look up the trade contract using the unique identifier.

```
function settleTrade(IERC20 sell, bytes32 tradeId) external {
    ITrade iTrade = trades[tradeId];
    require(iTrade.isOpen(), "Trade is already settled");
    // Settle the trade
    iTrade.settle();
}
```

Use the address of the parties involved in the trade as a parameter. This would allow the function to determine which trade should be settled based on the parties involved.

```
