    ITrade iTrade;
    // Search for the open trade involving the seller and buyer
    for(uint i = 0; i < openTrades.length; i++) {
        iTrade = openTrades[i];
        if(iTrade.
```

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/StRSR.sol#L449-L456


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

While loop within the `endIdForWithdraw()` function.

```
while (left < right - 1) {
    // Loop invariants, because without great care a binary search is usually wrong:
    // - queue[left].availableAt <= time
    // - either right == queue.length or queue[right].availableAt > time
    test = (left + right) / 2; // left < test < right because left < right - 1
    if (queue[test].availableAt <= time) left = test;
    else right = test;
}
```
This loop can cause an infinite loop if the loop invariants are not maintained properly.

`endIdForWithdraw()` functions to consume an excessive amount of gas and cause the smart contract to stop responding to other transactions. This could also lead to a `denial-of-service` attack on the contract. The `endIdForWithdraw()` function will not return the expected result and the contract will not function properly.

## Proof of Concept

The `draftQueues[draftEra][account]` array is in such a way that the loop invariants are not met, causing the while loop to run indefinitely. This can be done by, for example, creating a scenario where the left and right pointers in the while loop are not updated correctly, or by manipulating the `availableAt` timestamp of the `CumulativeDraft` structs in the array so that the loop never exits.

1. An attacker creates a large number of drafts in the system and manipulates the `availableAt` timestamp of these drafts in such a way that the binary search always returns the same midpoint value.

2. The attacker then calls the `endIdForWithdraw()` function with their account, causing the while loop to run indefinitely, as the left and right pointers are not updated correctly.

3. This will cause the function to run indefinitely and consume all the gas provided for the transaction, resulting in a DoS attack on the contract.

This code snippet illustrates this proof of concept.

```
// Attacker creates a large number of drafts and manipulates the availableAt timestamp
for (uint i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
    draftQueues[draftEra][attacker].push(CumulativeDraft({
        availableAt: block.timestamp + i, 
        drafts: 1
    }));
}

// Attacker calls the endIdForWithdraw() function
contract.endIdForWithdraw(attacker);
```
This will cause the function to run indefinitely and consume all the gas provided for the transaction, resulting in a `DoS attack` on the contract.

## Tools Used

Manual audit, Vs code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Add a maximum `iteration` limit for the while `loop`, and have the function return an error if the loop reaches that maximum limit without finding a result.

```
function endIdForWithdraw(address account) external view returns (uint256) {
    uint256 time = block.timestamp;
    CumulativeDraft[] storage queue = draftQueues[draftEra][account];
    uint256 maxIterations = 100;
    uint256 iterations = 0;

    // Bounds our search for the current cumulative draft
    (uint256 left, uint256 right) = (firstRemainingDraft[draftEra][account], queue.length);

    // If there are no drafts to be found, return 0 drafts
    if (left >= right) return right;
    if (queue[left].availableAt > time) return left;

    // Otherwise, there *are* drafts with left <= index < right and availableAt <= time.
    // Binary search:
    uint256 test;
    while (left < right - 1) {
        // Check loop invariants
        require(queue[left].availableAt <= time, "Invariant failure: queue[left].availableAt > time");
        require(right == queue.length || queue[right].availableAt > time, "Invariant failure: queue[right].availableAt <= time");
        require(iterations < maxIterations, "Maximum iteration limit reached, function failed");
        // Perform binary search
        test = (left + right) / 2; // left < test < right because left < right - 1
        if (queue[test].availableAt <= time) left = test;
        else right = test;
        iterations++;
    }
    return right;
}
```

The code above added a maximum iteration limit and also checks for loop invariants and if it fails to find the value in a given limit it `reutns` an error. This would prevent the infinite loop while still allowing the function to work properly.

