https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/mixins/Auth.sol#L34


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
```
mapping(address => uint256) public longFreezes;

/* ==== Invariants ====
   0 <= longFreeze[a] <= LONG_FREEZE_CHARGES for all addrs a
   set{a has LONG_FREEZER} == set{a : longFreeze[a] == 0}
*/
```
This bug could allow an attacker to manipulate the values stored in the `longFreezes` mapping, by updating them in a way that does not respect the stated invariant of `0 <= longFreeze[a] <= LONG_FREEZE_CHARGES` for all addresses a. This could lead to unexpected or unintended behavior in the contract. For example, if the `longFreeze` value for an address is set to a value higher than LONG_FREEZE_CHARGES, the attacker may be able to exploit this to cause the contract to malfunction or behave in unexpected ways. Additionally, if there is no check in place to enforce the stated invariant, the bug could allow the attacker to bypass any logic or restrictions that rely on the `longFreezes` mapping. This could lead to unauthorized access or other security vulnerabilities.


## Proof of Concept

For an attacker to manipulate the values stored in the `longFreezes` mapping by calling functions that update the values without proper checks or validation. The attacker could then use this manipulation to gain unauthorized access to the contract's functions or resources.

```
contract Attacker {
    address targetContract;

    constructor(address _targetContract) public {
        targetContract = _targetContract;
    }

    function exploit() public {
        // Call the function that updates the longFreezes mapping and pass in an address and a value greater than LONG_FREEZE_CHARGES
        targetContract.call(abi.encodeWithSignature("updateLongFreezes(address,uint256)", address(this), LONG_FREEZE_CHARGES+1));
    }
}
```
The malicious contract `Attacker` is able to call the `updateLongFreezes` function of the target contract and set the value of the mapping for the address of the attacker, to a value greater than LONG_FREEZE_CHARGES. This would enable the attacker to potentially gain unauthorized access to the contract's functions or resources.


## Tools Used

Manual audit, Vs Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

add functions for updating the `longFreezes` mapping that "include" checks to ensure that the values being set are within the correct range. For example, a function to increment the value stored at a given address in the mapping could include a check that the current value plus the increment does not exceed `LONG_FREEZE_CHARGES`. Another option would be to add a modifier that can be applied to functions that update the mapping to ensure that the checks are performed.

```
function incrementLongFreeze(address _address) public {
    require(_address != address(0), "Invalid address");
    require(longFreezes[_address] + 1 <= LONG_FREEZE_CHARGES, "Exceeded maximum number of charges");
    longFreezes[_address]++;
}
```

Another instance of a modifier that ensures that the values in the mapping are within the correct range.

```
modifier checkLongFreezeRange(address _address) {
    require(longFreezes[_address] <= LONG_FREEZE_CHARGES, "Exceeded maximum number of charges");
    _;
}

function incrementLongFreeze(address _address) public checkLongFreezeRange(_address) {
    require(_address != address(0), "Invalid address");
    longFreezes[_address]++;
}
```
Another possible mitigation would be to give the ownership to a multi-sig address or a time-locked contract, so that the contract can be paused only after a certain condition is met or after a certain time.

```
// Example code for "transferOwnership" function
function transferOwnership(address newOwner) public {
    require(msg.sender == owner, "Only the current owner can transfer ownership.");
    require(isAddress(newOwner), "Invalid address for new owner.");
    owner = newOwner;
    emit OwnershipTransferred(msg.sender, newOwner);
}
```

```
// Example code for using timelock
modifier timelock(uint _timelock) {
    require(block.timestamp >= _timelock);
    _;
}

function pause() public timelock(now + 1 days) {
    require(msg.sender == owner, "Only the owner can pause the contract.");
    require(!paused, "The contract is already paused.");
    paused = true;
    emit Paused();
}
```

```
// Example code for using multisig
address[] public owners;

function addOwner(address _owner) public {
    require(msg.sender == owner, "Only the current owner can add new owners.");
    require(_owner != address(0), "Invalid address for new owner.");
    owners.push(_owner);
}

function pause() public {
    require(msg.sender == owner, "Only the owner can pause the contract.");
    require(!paused, "The contract is already paused.");
    require(isConfirmed(msg.sender, owners), "Transaction must be confirmed by multiple owners.");
    paused = true;
    emit Paused();
}
```

