https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/interfaces/IFurnace.sol#L13-L17


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
```
function init(
        IMain main_,
        uint48 period_,
        uint192 ratio_
    ) external;
```
The signature of the `init` function in the IFurnace contract. It takes in a `IMain` contract address as its first parameter, but it is not clear what the purpose or functionality of this contract is and whether it is properly initialized or checked for before being used in the `IFurnace` contract.

If the `IMain` contract is not properly initialized or is not in the expected state, it could cause the `IFurnace` contract to malfunction or behave unexpectedly.

If the `IMain` contract has any security vulnerabilities, it could be exploited by malicious actors to compromise the security of the `IFurnace` contract.

If the `IMain` contract is not designed to be used in conjunction with the `IFurnace` contract, it could cause the `IFurnace` contract to malfunction or behave unexpectedly.

If the `IMain` contract is not updated or maintained, it could cause the `IFurnace` contract to malfunction or behave unexpectedly.


## Proof of Concept

creating an implementation of the `IFurnace` contract and calling its `init` function with a malicious `IMain` contract address.

```
pragma solidity 0.8.9;

import "./IFurnace.sol";

contract MyFurnace is IFurnace {
    IMain main;
    uint48 period;
    uint192 ratio;

    function init(IMain main_, uint48 period_, uint192 ratio_) external {
        main = main_;
        period = period_;
        ratio = ratio_;
    }

    function period() external view returns (uint48) {
        return period;
    }

    function setPeriod(uint48 newPeriod) external {
        period = newPeriod;
        emit PeriodSet(period, newPeriod);
    }

    function ratio() external view returns (uint192) {
        return ratio;
    }

    function setRatio(uint192 newRatio) external {
        ratio = newRatio;
        emit RatioSet(ratio, newRatio);
    }

    function melt() external {
        // Code to perform melting of RTokens goes here
    }
}
```

`IFurnace` contract that stores the `IMain` contract address, period, and ratio as state variables and implements the functions as specified in the interface.

Then create a malicious `IMain` contract that has a function that allows an attacker to steal Ether from the `MyFurnace` contract.

```
contract MaliciousMain {
    function stealEther() external {
        msg.sender.transfer(address(this).balance);
    }
}
```

Then by creating an instance of `MaliciousMain` contract and passing its address to the `init` function of the `MyFurnace` contract, an attacker can steal Ether from the `MyFurnace` contract, since the `MaliciousMain` contract can be called from `MyFurnace` contract without any check.

```
MyFurnace myFurnace = new MyFurnace();
MaliciousMain maliciousMain = new MaliciousMain();

myFurnace.init(address(maliciousMain), 1000, 500);
```

## Tools Used

Manual audit, VSCODE

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Clearly document the purpose and functionality of the `IMain` contract and its expected state before being used in the `IFurnace` contract.

Before initializing the `IMain` contract in the `init` function of the `IFurnace` contract, add a check to verify that the `IMain` contract is properly initialized and in the expected state.

As a best practice, it is a good idea to verify the address of the `IMain` contract before initializing it, this can be done by comparing the address with a known address of the `IMain` contract, or by checking its code hash.

It is also a good idea to check that the address passed to the function is not a malicious address.

```
function init(IMain main_, uint48 period_, uint192 ratio_) external {
    require(main_.address == knownIMainAddress, "Invalid IMain contract address");
    require(keccak256(abi.encodePacked(main_.code)) == knownIMainCodeHash, "Invalid IMain contract code");
    main = main_;
    period = period_;
    ratio = ratio_;
}
```

The `init` function checks that the `IMain` contract address passed to it is equal to a known and valid address, and that the code hash of the `IMain` contract is equal to a known and valid code hash. This will ensure that only a legitimate and properly initialized `IMain` contract can be used in the `IFurnace` contract.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/Distributor.sol#L87-L137


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Distributor.distribute sends less amount that it should because of rounding. It first divides and then multiplies.

## Proof of Concept
When some amount of token is distributed using `Distributor.distribute` then it should be equally distributed among receivers.
https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/Distributor.sol#L87-L137
```solidity
    function distribute(
        IERC20 erc20,
        address from,
        uint256 amount
    ) external notPausedOrFrozen {
        require(erc20 == rsr || erc20 == rToken, "RSR or RToken");
        bool isRSR = erc20 == rsr; // if false: isRToken
        uint256 tokensPerShare;
        {
            RevenueTotals memory revTotals = totals();
            uint256 totalShares = isRSR ? revTotals.rsrTotal : revTotals.rTokenTotal;
            require(totalShares > 0, "nothing to distribute");
            tokensPerShare = amount / totalShares;
        }


        // Evenly distribute revenue tokens per distribution share.
        // This rounds "early", and that's deliberate!


        Transfer[] memory transfers = new Transfer[](destinations.length());
        uint256 numTransfers;


        for (uint256 i = 0; i < destinations.length(); ++i) {
            address addrTo = destinations.at(i);


            uint256 numberOfShares = isRSR
                ? distribution[addrTo].rsrDist
                : distribution[addrTo].rTokenDist;
            if (numberOfShares == 0) continue;
            uint256 transferAmt = tokensPerShare * numberOfShares;


            if (addrTo == FURNACE) {
                addrTo = furnace;
            } else if (addrTo == ST_RSR) {
                addrTo = stRSR;
            }


            transfers[numTransfers] = Transfer({
                erc20: erc20,
                addrTo: addrTo,
                amount: transferAmt
            });
            numTransfers++;
        }
        emit RevenueDistributed(erc20, from, amount);


        // == Interactions ==
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < numTransfers; i++) {
            Transfer memory t = transfers[i];
            IERC20Upgradeable(address(t.erc20)).safeTransferFrom(from, t.addrTo, t.amount);
        }
    }
```

After we know `totalShares`, which depends on if token is RToken or RSR, the function calculates `tokensPerShare`.
`tokensPerShare = amount / totalShares`.
In this moment we already lost precision because of rounding.

Later we use  `tokensPerShare` to know total amount of erc20 token that is needed to be send to recipient `uint256 transferAmt = tokensPerShare * numberOfShares`, but this value is already incorrect. And the amount that is sent to recipient is less than needed.

## Tools Used
VsCode
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Do not calculate `tokensPerShare` before. 
You can store `totalShares` variable and then later use `uint256 transferAmt = amount * numberOfShares / totalShares`

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/BackingManager.sol#L221-L235


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
BackingManager.handoutExcessAssets divides before multiplication. Because of that less amount of rewards is sent to rTokenTrader and rsrTrader.

## Proof of Concept
While calculating amount that should be sent to `rTokenTrader` and `rsrTrader` inside `handoutExcessAssets` function, BackingManager divides before multiplication and sends less amount of rewards because of rounding.

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/BackingManager.sol#L221-L235
```solidity
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
            IAsset asset = assetRegistry.toAsset(erc20s[i]);


            uint192 req = needed.mul(basketHandler.quantity(erc20s[i]), CEIL);
            if (asset.bal(address(this)).gt(req)) {
                // delta: {qTok}, the excess quantity of this asset that we hold
                uint256 delta = asset.bal(address(this)).minus(req).shiftl_toUint(
                    int8(IERC20Metadata(address(erc20s[i])).decimals())
                );
                // no div-by-0: Distributor guarantees (totals.rTokenTotal + totals.rsrTotal) > 0
                // initial division is intentional here! We'd rather save the dust than be unfair
                toRSR[i] = (delta / (totals.rTokenTotal + totals.rsrTotal)) * totals.rsrTotal;
                toRToken[i] = (delta / (totals.rTokenTotal + totals.rsrTotal)) * totals.rTokenTotal;
            }
        }
```

For example if totals.rTokenTotal == totals.rsrTotal = 100 and delta = 500 then both toRSR[i] and toRToken[i] should be 250, but they are 200.
As result less amount than needed is sent to contracts.

## Tools Used
VsCode
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Multiply before division.

