https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L475-L488


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
RToken.redeem doesn't have slippage protection. In case if it's not enough of erc20 token at the moment then less amount than should will be sent to user.

## Proof of Concept
When user redeems his rTokens, then first amount of baskets that will be exhanged is calculated and amount that should be paid to user according to basket price.

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L461-L488
```soldiity
        uint256 supply = totalSupply();
        uint192 baskets = uint192(mulDiv256(basketsNeeded_, amount, supply));
        emit Redemption(redeemer, amount, baskets);


        (address[] memory erc20s, uint256[] memory amounts) = basketHandler.quote(baskets, FLOOR);


        // ==== Prorate redemption ====
        // i.e, set amounts = min(amounts, balances * amount / totalSupply)
        //   where balances[i] = erc20s[i].balanceOf(this)


        uint256 erc20length = erc20s.length;


        // D18{1} = D18 * {qRTok} / {qRTok}
        // downcast is safe: amount <= balanceOf(redeemer) <= totalSupply(), so prorate < 1e18
        uint192 prorate = uint192((FIX_ONE_256 * amount) / supply);


        // Bound each withdrawal by the prorata share, in case we're currently under-collateralized
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < erc20length; ++i) {
            // {qTok}
            uint256 bal = IERC20Upgradeable(erc20s[i]).balanceOf(address(backingManager));


            // gas-optimization: only do the full mulDiv256 if prorate is 0
            uint256 prorata = (prorate > 0)
                ? (prorate * bal) / FIX_ONE // {qTok} = D18{1} * {qTok} / D18
                : mulDiv256(bal, amount, supply); // {qTok} = {qTok} * {qRTok} / {qRTok}


            if (prorata < amounts[i]) amounts[i] = prorata;
        }
```

It's possible that after switching the basket or for another reason it will be nor enough of some erc20 token inside BackingManager. In such case user will receive less amount of token than he should. `if (prorata < amounts[i]) amounts[i] = prorata;`

Currently user doesn't have ability to provide minimum amount of tokens that he wants to receive from redeem. So as result it's possible that user will be underpaid after tx.

## Tools Used
VsCode
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider a way for user to control minimum amount of each erc20 token of basket that he expects to receive.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/libraries/RedemptionBattery.sol#L44


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
``` require(amount <= charge, "redemption battery insufficient"); ```

This code only checks if the `amount` being redeemed is less than or equal to the current charge of the battery but does not check if it is greater than the total supply of the token.

This bug is severe. As the `discharge` function does not check if the "amount" being redeemed is greater than the total supply of the token, it allows an attacker to redeem an `arbitrary` large amount of tokens and drain the entire supply. This can result in a complete loss of the token for all other users.

An attacker could exploit this vulnerability by repeatedly calling the "discharge" function with large amounts of tokens until the entire supply is depleted. This could effectively render the token worthless and cause significant financial loss for any users or investors who hold the token.

The attacker can also use the same vulnerability to manipulate the token's value. For instance, if the token's value is determined by the total supply, the attacker can use this bug to artificially decrease the total supply and make the token's value increase, then sell the token at a higher price.

## Proof of Concept

This would involve creating a contract that utilizes the `RedemptionBatteryLib` library and calls the "discharge" function with a number of tokens that exceeds the total supply.

```
pragma solidity 0.8.9;

import "./RedemptionBatteryLib.sol";

contract TokenDrain {
    using RedemptionBatteryLib for RedemptionBatteryLib.Battery;
    RedemptionBatteryLib.Battery public battery;
    uint256 public totalSupply;

    constructor(uint256 _totalSupply) public {
        totalSupply = _totalSupply;
    }

    function drain() public {
        require(msg.sender == address(this), "only owner can drain the token");
        // Attacker calls the discharge function with a large amount of tokens
        // that exceeds the total supply, draining the entire supply
        battery.discharge(totalSupply, totalSupply + 1);
    }
}
```

the "drain" function can be called by the contract's owner to drain the entire supply of tokens, regardless of the current charge of the battery. The function calls the "discharge" function in the `RedemptionBatteryLib` library with a number of tokens that exceeds the total supply, allowing the attacker to drain the entire supply.

## Tools Used

Manual audit, Vs Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

The `discharge` function ensures that the amount being redeemed is not greater than the total supply of the token. This can be done by adding an additionally `require` statement that compares the amount being redeemed to the total supply.

```
function discharge(
        Battery storage battery,
        uint256 supply,
        uint256 amount
    ) internal {
        if (battery.redemptionRateFloor == 0 && battery.scalingRedemptionRate == 0) return;

        // {qRTok}
        uint256 charge = currentCharge(battery, supply);
        // check if the amount being redeemed is greater than the total supply
        require(amount <= supply, "redemption amount exceeds total supply");
        // A nice error message so people aren't confused why redemption failed
        require(amount <= charge, "redemption battery insufficient");

        // Update battery
        battery.lastBlock = uint48(block.number);
        battery.lastCharge = charge - amount;
    }
```

The `require` statement checks if the amount being redeemed is less than or equal to the total supply of the token. If the condition is not met, the function will revert and the redemption will not be executed.

