# Vulnerability details

## Impact
```
  function register(
        string calldata version,
        IDeployer deployer,
        bool makeLatest
    ) external;

    /// Unregister by version
    function unregister(string calldata version) external;

    /// @return The Deployer from the latest deployment
    function latestDeployment() external view returns (IDeployer);
}
```
This bug is a major security vulnerability because it allows anyone to call the `register`, `unregister`, and `latestDeployment` functions, which can potentially lead to unauthorized changes to the contract. Without proper access control or permission system in place, any malicious actor can call these functions and register, unregister, or access the latest deployment deployer contract, which can result in unexpected behavior, data loss, or even financial loss for the users of the contract.

A malicious actor could register a malicious version of the deployer contract, which could potentially be used to steal funds or perform other unauthorized actions. If a malicious actor unregisters a version that is currently in use, it could cause a malfunction in the contract, which could lead to financial loss. It could also allow malicious actors to access the latest deployment of the deployer contract, which could be used to steal funds or perform other unauthorized actions.

## Proof of Concept

This bug would involve creating a scenario where an unauthorized party can call the `register`, `unregister`, or `latestDeployment` functions of the contract. 
```
pragma solidity 0.8.9;

import "./IDeployer.sol";
import "truffle/Assert.sol";

interface IDeployerRegistry {
    event DeploymentUnregistered(string version, IDeployer deployer);
    event DeploymentRegistered(string version, IDeployer deployer);
    event LatestChanged(string version, IDeployer deployer);

    function register(
        string calldata version,
        IDeployer deployer,
        bool makeLatest
    ) external;

    function unregister(string calldata version) external;

    function latestDeployment() external view returns (IDeployer);
}

contract Attacker {
    IDeployerRegistry public registry;

    constructor(address _registry) public {
        registry = IDeployerRegistry(_registry);
    }

    function performAttack() public {
        // Create a malicious deployer contract
        address maliciousDeployer = new MaliciousDeployer();

        // Register the malicious deployer contract
        registry.register("1.0.0", maliciousDeployer, true);
        // Unregister a version that is currently in use
        registry.unregister("1.0.0");
        // Access the latest deployment
        address latestDeployment = registry.latestDeployment();

        // Assert that the malicious deployer was registered
        Assert.equal(maliciousDeployer, latestDeployment, "Malicious deployer was not registered");
    }
}
```
Up here I demonstrate how an attacker can create a malicious deployer contract, register it as the latest deployment, `unregister` a version that is currently in use, and access the latest deployment. With the lack of access control or permission system in place, the malicious deployer is registered and causes unexpected behavior and financial loss.
## Tools Used

Manual audit, Truffle, Vs Code.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Implement an access control mechanism, such as an `onlyOwner` modifier, that can be used to check that the `msg.sender` is the owner of the contract before executing critical functions like register, unregister, and `latestDeployment`.

```
pragma solidity 0.8.9;

import "./IDeployer.sol";

interface IDeployerRegistry {
    event DeploymentUnregistered(string version, IDeployer deployer);
    event DeploymentRegistered(string version, IDeployer deployer);
    event LatestChanged(string version, IDeployer deployer);

    /// Register a deployer address, keyed by a version.
    /// @dev Does not allow overwriting without deregistration
    /// @param version A semver version string
    /// @param makeLatest True iff this deployment should be promoted to be the latest deployment
    function register(
        string calldata version,
        IDeployer deployer,
        bool makeLatest
    ) external
```
Include the `onlyOwner` modifier.

```
modifier onlyOwner() {
    require(msg.sender == owner, "Only owner can call this function");
    _;
}

function register(
        string calldata version,
        IDeployer deployer,
        bool makeLatest
    ) external onlyOwner {
    // Function body here
}
```
Use the `require` statement in the function body to check that the `msg.sender` is the owner of the contract before executing critical functions like `register`, `unregister`, and `latestDeployment`

```
function register(
        string calldata version,
        IDeployer deployer,
        bool makeLatest
    ) external {
    require(msg.sender == owner, "Only owner can call this function");
    // Function body here
}
```

Create a whitelist of authorized addresses that can call the functions `register`, `unregister`, and `latestDeployment`. The whitelist can be implemented as a mapping or an array that keeps track of the authorized addresses, and then checks if the `msg.sender` is present in that data structure before proceeding with the function execution.

```
mapping(address => bool) public authorizedAddresses;

function register(
        string calldata version,
        IDeployer deployer,
        bool makeLatest
    ) external {
    require(authorizedAddresses[msg.sender], "Unauthorized address");
    // Function body here
}
```

