## Impact

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/interfaces/IAssetRegistry.sol#L55

`register()` function does not check if the passed-in `IAsset` contract has already been registered before adding it to the registry. This could allow an attacker to repeatedly register the same contract and potentially manipulate the state of the registry.

The vulnerability in the `register()` function occurs in the following line.

```
function register(IAsset asset) external returns (bool) {
    AssetRegistered(asset.token(), asset);
    return true;
}
```
Where there is no check to ensure that an `IAsset` contract is not already registered before adding it to the registry.

An attacker can repeatedly register the same contract, by repeatedly calling the `register()` function, this could lead to the manipulation of the state of the registry. This could allow the attacker to gain unauthorized access to assets or other malicious actions.

For example, an attacker could register a malicious contract multiple times to the registry, this could cause the malicious contract to be prioritized over other legitimate contracts, allowing the attacker to perform unauthorized actions.

Furthermore, this vulnerability could also lead to the creation of `multiple` copies of the same contract, which could cause confusion and errors in the system. The attacker could also register a `malicious` contract and use it to steal assets or perform other malicious actions.

Let's demonstrates how the attacker can repeatedly register the same contract.


```
contract Attacker {
    IAssetRegistry registry;
    IAsset maliciousAsset;

    function registerAsset() public {
        // The maliciousAsset is passed in the register function
        registry.register(maliciousAsset); 
    }
}
```

The attacker contract has a function `registerAsset()` that repeatedly calls the `register(IAsset asset)` function of the `IAssetRegistry` contract, passing in the malicious contract `maliciousAsset` as the argument. Since the `register(IAsset asset)` function does not check if the contract has already been registered, the malicious contract is added to the registry multiple times.


## Proof of Concept

This vulnerability would involve creating a malicious contract and repeatedly registering it using the `register()` function. Here's a possible scenario.

An attacker creates a malicious contract that has the capability to steal assets from the smart contract's registry.

The attacker repeatedly calls the `register()` function, passing in the malicious contract as the `IAsset` argument.

Since the `register()` function does not check if the contract has already been registered, the malicious contract is added to the registry multiple times.

The attacker can now use multiple copies of the malicious contract in the registry to steal assets or perform other malicious actions.

As the attacker can repeatedly register the same contract, the attacker can continuously add more malicious contracts to the registry and potentially compromise the security and integrity of the smart contract.

## Tools Used

Manual audit, vs code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

It is recommended to add a check to ensure that an `IAsset` contract is not already registered before adding it to the registry in the `register()` function. Here is an example that could be used to implement this check.

```
function register(IAsset asset) external returns (bool) {
    require(!isRegistered(asset.token()), "Asset already registered");
    assets[asset.token()] = asset;
    AssetRegistered(asset.token(), asset);
    return true;
}
```
This code adds a new requirement statement, which checks if the passed-in `IAsset` contract is already registered by calling the `isRegistered()` function with the `asset.token()` as an argument. If the contract is already registered, the function will revert with the message "Asset already registered". If the contract is not registered, the code proceeds to add the contract to the registry and emit the `AssetRegistered` event.

This mitigation step will prevent an attacker from repeatedly registering the same contract, thus preventing them from manipulating the state of the registry.







