Consider implementing a form of access control to restrict who can call the fallback function.

Here is an example of a fallback function that can be added to the `IAsset` and `ICollateral` interfaces to handle unexpected calls.

```
contract IAsset is IRewardable {
    address payable private safeAccount;
    event FallbackCalled(address sender, uint value);

    function() external payable {
        // Do something with the unexpected ether, e.g. store it in a safe account or refund it
        safeAccount.transfer(msg.value);
        emit FallbackCalled(msg.sender, msg.value);
    }
}

contract ICollateral is IAsset {
    function() external payable {
        // Do something with the unexpected ether, e.g. store it in a safe account or refund it
        safeAccount.transfer(msg.value);
        emit FallbackCalled(msg.sender, msg.value);
    }
}
```

The fallback function in both `IAsset` and `ICollateral` interfaces will handle unexpected calls to the contract. It first transfers the ether received to a safe account, where it can be stored or refunded.
It also `emits` an event `FallbackCalled`, which logs the address of the sender and the value of ether sent, this can be used for monitoring the contract and track who is calling the fallback function and when.

