VIM

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
prevent early manipulation of the PPS

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/plugins/trading/GnosisTrade.sol#L81-L94


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

`init()` function is missing a `require` statement to check if the input variable `sellAmount_` is less than or equal to the contract's balance of sell tokens. This means that the contract is allowing the user to `initiate` an auction with a sell amount greater than the contract's balance, which could lead to unexpected behavior and loss of funds.

```
function init(
        IBroker broker_,
        address origin_,
        IGnosis gnosis_,
        uint48 endTime_,
        IERC20Metadata sell_,
        uint256 sellAmount_,
        IERC20Metadata buy_,
        uint192 minBuyAmount_,
        uint192 worstCasePrice_
    )
        public
        stateTransition(TradeStatus.NOT_STARTED, TradeStatus.OPEN)
        returns (bool)
    {
        require(endTime_ > now, "endTime must be in the future");
        require(sellAmount_ < 2**96, "sellAmount out of bounds");
        require(minBuyAmount_ < 2**96, "minBuyAmount out of bounds");
        // MISSING REQUIRE STATEMENT
    }
```
This missing `require` statement is the vulnerability that allows the smart contract to proceed with the auction even when it does not have enough balance to complete the transaction, this could lead to unexpected behavior and potential loss of funds.


## Proof of Concept

To exploit this vulnerability we'll create a malicious contract that calls the `init()` function of the vulnerable contract, passing in a `sell` amount greater than the contract's balance of sell tokens.

Let's assume the contract's balance of sell tokens is 50, and the malicious contract calls the `init()` function with a sell amount of 100 tokens.

```
// Malicious contract
contract Attacker {
    GnosisTrade public victim;

    function attack() public {
        victim.init(..., 100, ...);
    }
}
```
As the `init()` function does not have a require statement to check if the input variable `sellAmount_` is less than or equal to the contract's balance of sell tokens, the contract would proceed with the auction and attempt to sell 100 tokens, even though it only has 50 tokens available. This would result in the user losing 50 tokens because the contract does not have enough balance to complete the transaction.

Additionally, this vulnerability could also be `exploited by an attacker to drain` the contract's balance by initiating multiple auctions with large sell amounts.


## Tools Used

Manual audit, Vs Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

The `init()` function should be updated to include a `require` statement that checks if the input variable `sellAmount_` is less than or equal to the contract's balance of sell tokens. This will ensure that the contract only proceeds with the auction if it has enough balance to complete the transaction.

```
function init(
        IBroker broker_,
        address origin_,
        IGnosis gnosis_,
        uint48 endTime_,
        IERC20Metadata sell_,
        uint256 sellAmount_,
        IERC20Metadata buy_,
        uint192 minBuyAmount_,
        uint192 worstCasePrice_
    )
        public
        stateTransition(TradeStatus.NOT_STARTED, TradeStatus.OPEN)
        returns (bool)
    {
        require(endTime_ > now, "endTime must be in the future");
        require(sellAmount_ < 2**96, "sellAmount out of bounds");
        require(minBuyAmount_ < 2**96, "minBuyAmount out of bounds");
        require(sellAmount_ <= sell.balanceOf(address(this)), "Not enough balance");
    }
```

`init()` function ensures that the contract only proceeds with the auction if it has enough balance to complete the transaction, preventing unexpected behavior and potential loss of funds.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L186-L340


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
It has following impacts.
1. `backingManager` would be transferred with lesser than `deposits`
2. Recorded `liabilities` will be more
3. collateral deposited into the `RToken` contract would be lesser than `deposits`

## Proof of Concept
Any one can mint RToken by depositing backet of valid collaterals. The issue here is with collateral that would charge upon transferring.

Some ERC20 token implementations have a fee that is charged on each token transfer. This means that the transferred amount isn't exactly what the receiver will get. A call to IERC20(token).transfer(recipient, 100) with a fee-on-transfer (FOT) of 5% will entitle the recipient with 95 tokens.

The main issue then lies in that the protocol will pull funds from arbitrary ERC20 token implementation using transferFrom, assuming that the receiving amount matches the amount sent as the parameter to the transfer call, which is not always the case.

Fee on Transfer
Some tokens take a transfer fee (e.g. STA, PAXG), some do not currently charge a fee but may do so in the future (e.g. USDT, USDC).

The STA transfer fee was used to drain $500k from several balancer pools (more details).

https://github.com/d-xo/weird-erc20#:~:text=Fee%20on%20Transfer,%3A%20TransferFee.sol

In RToken contract, through `issue` function , time-delayed issuance of RToken is done for basket collateral.

based on the `RToken` is going to be minted and the `totalSupply()`, the amtBaskets is calculated as shown below,

        uint192 amtBaskets = uint192(
            totalSupply() > 0 ? mulDiv256(basketsNeeded, amtRToken, totalSupply()) : amtRToken
        );

the amount is going to be deposited is calculated from `basketHandler.quote`

        (address[] memory erc20s, uint256[] memory deposits) = basketHandler.quote(
            amtBaskets,
            CEIL
        );

For the `amtBaskets`, the deposit amount will be decided in Baskehandler.sol

    function quote(uint192 amount, RoundingMode rounding)
        external
        view
        returns (address[] memory erc20s, uint256[] memory quantities)
    {
        uint256 length = basket.erc20s.length;
        erc20s = new address[](length);
        quantities = new uint256[](length);


        for (uint256 i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
            erc20s[i] = address(basket.erc20s[i]);


            // {qTok} = {tok/BU} * {BU} * {tok} * {qTok/tok}
            quantities[i] = quantity(basket.erc20s[i]).mul(amount, rounding).shiftl_toUint(
                int8(IERC20Metadata(address(basket.erc20s[i])).decimals()),
                rounding
            );
        }
    }

After deciding the deposit amount from above process, it will be transferred to `backingManager` . For fee on trasfer token, the actual amount is transferred is lesser than the deposit value.

            for (uint256 i = 0; i < erc20s.length; ++i) {
                IERC20Upgradeable(erc20s[i]).safeTransferFrom(
                    issuer,
                    address(backingManager),
                    deposits[i]
                );
            }

liabilities are updated based on the deposit value. 

        // Increment liabilities
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < basketSize; ++i) {
            liabilities[IERC20(erc20s[i])] += deposits[i];
        }

Finally , the collaterals are deposited  into RToken contract.

        // == Interactions: accept collateral ==
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < basketSize; ++i) {
            IERC20Upgradeable(erc20s[i]).safeTransferFrom(issuer, address(this), deposits[i]);
        }

If the collateral is chagring fee on transfer than the actual deposited value would be different than the contract received, the value updated as liabilities.

## Tools Used

Manual code review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Improve support for fee on transfer type of ERC20. When pulling funds from the user using transferFrom the usual approach is to compare balances pre/post transfer, like so:

something similar to given below.

uint256 balanceBefore = IERC20(token).balanceOf(address(this));
IERC20(token).transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
uint256 transferred = IERC20(token).balanceOf(address(this)) - balanceBefore;
// use transferred value as deposit

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/plugins/assets/RTokenAsset.sol#L44-L55


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
`basketRange()` in the contract. The function `tryPrice()` calls the function `basketRange()` from the library RecollateralizationLibP1, which is not defined in the contract. As a result, when `tryPrice()` is executed, it will cause the contract to revert and throw an error, preventing it from continuing execution. This means that any functionality that relies on `tryPrice()` will also fail to execute.

## Proof of Concept
```
pragma solidity 0.8.9;

import "./RTokenAsset.sol";

contract Attacker {
    RTokenAsset public rTokenAsset;

    constructor(address rTokenAssetAddress) {
        rTokenAsset = RTokenAsset(rTokenAssetAddress);
    }

    function exploit() public {
        // Call the tryPrice function in the RTokenAsset contract
        rTokenAsset.tryPrice();
    }
}
```

The code above creates a contract called "Attacker" that takes the address of an `RTokenAsset` contract as an input to its constructor. The exploit function calls the `tryPrice()` function of the `RTokenAsset` contract. Because the `tryPrice()` function in the `RTokenAsset` contract calls the `basketRange()` function, which is not defined, calling `exploit()` will cause the `RTokenAsset` contract to revert and fail, preventing it from functioning correctly.

To exploit this vulnerability, the attacker could deploy this contract and call its `exploit()` function, causing the `RTokenAsset` contract to fail. This will cause a significant impact on the usability and functionality of the contract, making it unusable for its intended purpose.

## Tools Used

Manual audit, Vs Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

* Define the function `basketRange()` in the contract. 

```
function basketRange() internal view returns (RecollateralizationLibP1.BasketRange memory) {
    //implement the function here
}
```
This will ensure that the function is defined and can be called by `tryPrice()` without causing the contract to revert.

* Import the correct library that defines the function.

```
import "path/to/RecollateralizationLibP1.sol";
```
This will allow the contract to access the `basketRange()` function defined in the library.

* Remove the call to `basketRange()` function from `tryPrice()`. If the function is not needed in the `tryPrice()` function and it is causing the contract to revert, it can be removed.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/BackingManager.sol#L67-L77
https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L439-L443


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Function `redeem()` in RToken contract, redeems RToken for basket collateral and it's callable when protocol is paused. but function `redeem()` transfers basket tokens from BackingManager contract to redeemer and it requires spending allowance of the BackingManger tokens which is granted by calling `grantRTokenAllowance()` for each basket tokens. function `grantRTokenAllowance()` is not callable when protocol is paused. so if for any one of the basket tokens function `grantRTokenAllowance()` didn't get called (newly added tokens to basket) then when protocol is paused `grantRTokenAllowance()` can't be called and `redeem()` won't be possible.

## Proof of Concept
This is `grantRTokenAllowance()` code:
```
    function grantRTokenAllowance(IERC20 erc20) external notPausedOrFrozen {
        require(assetRegistry.isRegistered(erc20), "erc20 unregistered");
        // == Interaction ==
        IERC20Upgradeable(address(erc20)).safeApprove(address(main.rToken()), 0);
        IERC20Upgradeable(address(erc20)).safeApprove(address(main.rToken()), type(uint256).max);
    }
```
As you can see it gives RToken spending allowance and it can't be called when protocol is Paused.
This is `redeem()` code:
```
function redeem(uint256 amount) external notFrozen {
        require(amount > 0, "Cannot redeem zero");
.........
.........
.........
        // Accept and burn RToken, reverts if not enough balance to burn
        _burn(redeemer, amount);

        bool allZero = true;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < erc20length; ++i) {
            if (amounts[i] == 0) continue;
            if (allZero) allZero = false;

            // Send withdrawal
            IERC20Upgradeable(erc20s[i]).safeTransferFrom(
                address(backingManager),
                redeemer,
                amounts[i]
            );
        }

        if (allZero) revert("Empty redemption");
    }
```
As you can see it can be called when protocol is paused and it transfers tokens from BackingManager to redeemer and require that RToken has spending allowance of BackingManager tokens so function `grantRTokenAllowance()` most be called for basket tokens before.
the problem is that if there were newly added token in the basket and `grantRTokenAllowance()` isn't called for that token and protocol was paused then user won't be able to `redeem()` because `grantRTokenAllowance()` is not callable during Paused time and `redeem()` won't be possible in Paused time. so as docs says that `redeem()` should be possible when protocol is paused but it won't happen in all cases and redeem() would be blocked.

## Tools Used
VIM

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
allow `grantRTokenAllowance()` to be called when contract is paused or call it when basket changes from contract.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/fdd9f81fe58953d758dbea62beed169a74523de1/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L420


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
This is similar to the "high" vulnerability I submitted, but also shows a similar exploit can be done if a user isn't a whale, and isn't issuing anything. 

A user can send a redeem TX and an evil actor can make it so they get almost nothing back during recollateralization. This requires ordering transactions, or just getting very unlucky with the order of your transaction. 

## Proof of Concept
- UserA is looking to redeem their rToken for tokenA (the max the battery will allow, let's say 100k)
- A basket refresh is about to be triggered
- Evil user wants the protocol to steal UserA's funds
- UserA sends redeem TX to the mempool, but Evil user move transactions around before it hits

- Evil user calls refreshbasket in same block as original collateral (tokenA) is disabled, kicking in backupconfig (tokenB)
- Protocol is now undercollateralized but collateral is sound (tokenB is good)
- Evil sends 1tokenB to backingManager to UserA's redeem has something to redeem
- UserA's redemption tx lands, and redeems 100k rTokens for a fraction of tokenB! 

UserA redeems and has nothing to show for it!
Evil user only had to buy 1 tokenB (or even less) to steal 100k of their rToken

## Tools Used

Hardhat

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Disallow redemptions/issuance during undercollateralization

