# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L186


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Users can get loss from the slippage due to various potential reasons.

## Proof of Concept
Users can issue new RTokens by depositing the necessary collaterals using the function `issue()`.
The function `issue()` has two parameters specifying the `recipient` and `amtRToken`.
Note that it does not provide a parameter to specify the maximum spending of the actual collateral tokens.

```solidity
RToken.sol
186:     function issue(address recipient, uint256 amtRToken)
187:         public
188:         notPausedOrFrozen
189:         returns (uint256)
190:     {
191:         require(amtRToken > 0, "Cannot issue zero");
192:
193:         // == Refresh ==
194:         assetRegistry.refresh();
195:
196:         address issuer = _msgSender(); // OK to save: it can't be changed in reentrant runs
197:
198:         uint48 basketNonce = basketHandler.nonce();
199:         IssueQueue storage queue = issueQueues[recipient];
200:
201:         // Refund issuances against old baskets
202:         if (queue.basketNonce > 0 && queue.basketNonce != basketNonce) {
203:             // == Interaction ==
204:             // This violates simple CEI, so we have to renew any potential transient state!
205:             refundSpan(recipient, queue.left, queue.right);
206:
207:             // Refresh collateral after interaction
208:             assetRegistry.refresh();
209:
210:             // Refresh local values after potential reentrant changes to contract state.
211:             basketNonce = basketHandler.nonce();
212:             queue = issueQueues[recipient];
213:         }
214:
215:         // == Checks-effects block ==
216:         CollateralStatus status = basketHandler.status();
217:         require(status == CollateralStatus.SOUND, "basket unsound");
218:
219:         furnace.melt();
220:
221:         // AT THIS POINT:
222:         //   all contract invariants hold
223:         //   furnace melting is up-to-date
224:         //   asset states are up-to-date
225:         //   queue.basketNonce = basketHandler.nonce()
226:
227:         // Compute the whole issuance span. We want to accumulate the issuance:
228:         // iss = {when: vestingEnd' - vestingEnd, amtRToken, amtBaskets, deposits}
229:
230:         // amtBaskets: the BU change to be recorded by this issuance
231:         // D18{BU} = D18{BU} * {qRTok} / {qRTok}
232:         // Downcast is safe because an actual quantity of qBUs fits in uint192
233:         uint192 amtBaskets = uint192(
234:             totalSupply() > 0 ? mulDiv256(basketsNeeded, amtRToken, totalSupply()) : amtRToken
235:         );
236:
237:         (address[] memory erc20s, uint256[] memory deposits) = basketHandler.quote(
238:             amtBaskets,
239:             CEIL
240:         );
241:
242:         // Add amtRToken's worth of issuance delay to allVestAt
243:         uint192 vestingEnd = whenFinished(amtRToken); // D18{block number}
244:
245:         // ==== If the issuance can fit in this block, and nothing is blocking it, then
246:         // just do a "quick issuance" of iss instead of putting the issuance in the queue:
247:         // effects and actions if we go this way are the combined actions to create and vest iss:
248:         //   basketsNeeded += iss.amtBaskets
249:         //   mint(recipient, iss.amtRToken)
250:         //   for each token index i, erc20s[i].transferFrom(issuer, backingManager, iss.deposits[i])
251:         if (
252:             // D18{blocks} <= D18{1} * {blocks}
253:             vestingEnd <= FIX_ONE_256 * block.number &&
254:             queue.left == queue.right &&
255:             status == CollateralStatus.SOUND
256:         ) {
257:             // Fixlib optimization:
258:             // D18{BU} = D18{BU} + D18{BU}; uint192(+) is the same as Fix.plus
259:             uint192 newBasketsNeeded = basketsNeeded + amtBaskets;
260:             emit BasketsNeededChanged(basketsNeeded, newBasketsNeeded);
261:             basketsNeeded = newBasketsNeeded;
262:
263:             // Note: We don't need to update the prev queue entry because queue.left = queue.right
264:             emit Issuance(recipient, amtRToken, amtBaskets);
265:
266:             // == Interactions then return: transfer tokens ==
267:             // Complete issuance
268:             _mint(recipient, amtRToken);
269:
270:             for (uint256 i = 0; i < erc20s.length; ++i) {
271:                 IERC20Upgradeable(erc20s[i]).safeTransferFrom(
272:                     issuer,
273:                     address(backingManager),
274:                     deposits[i]
275:                 );
276:             }
277:
278:             // All RTokens instantly issued
279:             return amtRToken;
280:         }
...
```

The necessary collateral amounts `deposits` are calculated using the function `BasketHandler.quote()`

```solidity
BasketHandler.sol
407:     function quote(uint192 amount, RoundingMode rounding)
408:         external
409:         view
410:         returns (address[] memory erc20s, uint256[] memory quantities)
411:     {
412:         uint256 length = basket.erc20s.length;
413:         erc20s = new address[](length);
414:         quantities = new uint256[](length);
415:
416:         for (uint256 i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
417:             erc20s[i] = address(basket.erc20s[i]);
418:
419:             // {qTok} = {tok/BU} * {BU} * {tok} * {qTok/tok}
420:             quantities[i] = quantity(basket.erc20s[i]).mul(amount, rounding).shiftl_toUint(
421:                 int8(IERC20Metadata(address(basket.erc20s[i])).decimals()),
422:                 rounding
423:             );
424:         }
425:     }
```
The function `quote()` uses the function `quantity()` again.
```solidity
    function quantity(IERC20 erc20) public view returns (uint192) {
        try assetRegistry.toColl(erc20) returns (ICollateral coll) {
            if (coll.status() == CollateralStatus.DISABLED) return FIX_ZERO;

            uint192 refPerTok = coll.refPerTok(); // {ref/tok}
            if (refPerTok > 0) {
                // {tok/BU} = {ref/BU} / {ref/tok}
                return basket.refAmts[erc20].div(refPerTok, CEIL);
            } else {
                return FIX_MAX;
            }
        } catch {
            return FIX_ZERO;
        }
    }
```
Note that this function is using the collateral's `refPerTok()` to get the {ref/tok}.
The function `refPerTok()` is defined differently in the asset plugin and it's possible that this exchange rate fluctuates.
So it means the actually necessary collateral amounts can change according to the fluctuation of `refPerTok()`.
If the function `refPerTok()` is compromised in any way (whatever external things), issuers are vulnerable and will need to pay more.
This becomes more problematic if the user (or the caller contract) already approved more than necessary.

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Following the best practices and the other well-known protocols, consider adding a slippage control parameter in the function `issue()` and allow the caller to specify the maximum amount of collaterals to be spent.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/fdd9f81fe58953d758dbea62beed169a74523de1/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L420


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
This is similar to the "high" vulnerability I submitted but shows a similar exploit can be done if a user isn't a whale, stealing a smaller amount of funds. This is potentially a "high" risk depending on how easy you think execution is. I think it's probably "high" but I'm being conservative. 

A user can send a redeem TX and an evil actor can make it so they get almost nothing back during recollateralization. This requires ordering transactions, or just getting very unlucky with the order of your transaction. 

IE: You hit an innocuous `redeem` function, and a malicious actor spends almost no resources to steal funds not greater than the redemption battery allowance.

Difficulty: High/Med. They can build a bot the just waits for this scenario, which wouldn't be too uncommon. They could also spread FUD to tell people to redeem near a recollateralization time.
Impact: High. You  can steal a "small" amount of funds (up to the battery redemption rate), but depending on the rToken supply this can be very high (potentially tens or hundreds of thousands of dollars).

## Proof of Concept
- UserA is looking to redeem their rToken for tokenA (the max the battery will allow, let's say 100k)
- A basket refresh is about to be triggered
- Evil user wants the protocol to steal UserA's funds
- UserA sends redeem TX to the mempool, but Evil user move transactions around before it hits

- Evil user calls refreshbasket in same block as original collateral (tokenA) is disabled, kicking in backupconfig (tokenB)
- Protocol is now undercollateralized but collateral is sound (tokenB is good)
- Evil user issues a 1 RToken for 1 tokenB, now the protocol has 1 tokenB for redeemers
- UserA's redemption tx lands, and redeems 100k rTokens for a fraction of tokenB! 

UserA redeems and has nothing to show for it!
Evil user only had to buy 1 tokenB (or even less) to steal 100k of their rToken

## Tools Used

Hardhat

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Disallow issuance/redemptions during undercollateralization

## Proof of Code
To run:
1. git clone https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol.git
2. cd protocol
3. Copy paste the below code to a file like `/tmp/changes.patch`
4. git apply /tmp/changes.patch
5. yarn
6. yarn hardhat test test/submission-test.test.ts

```
