# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Detailed description of the impact of this finding.
When ``setPeriod()`` and ``setRatio()`` are called, the two parameters, ``period`` and ``ratio`` will change the behavior of ``melt()`` function retroactively since the last recorded ``lastPayout``. Meanwhile, a user can front-run these two functions and call ``melt()`` (anybody can call) and melt RTokens and bring ``lastPayout`` up to date, making ``period`` and ``ratio`` only effective prospectively. As a result, the consequence of ``setPeriod()`` and ``setRatio()`` is unpredictable, the new parameters might take effect for the past or for the future. 


## Proof of Concept
Provide direct links to all referenced code in GitHub. Add screenshots, logs, or any other relevant proof that illustrates the concept.
The problem lies in two facts: 1) when the new parameters of ``setPeriod()`` and ``setRatio()`` are set, they will take effect retroactively since ``lastPayout``; 2) melt() can be called by anyone and thus used to front-run ``setPeriod()`` and ``setRatio()``.  Depending on whether front-running happen or not, the behavior of the system could be one way or the other: new parameters will take effect for the past or for the future. 

## Tools Used
Remix

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
We can define the behavior of the system to be that new parameters only take effect for the future. All we need to do is to call ``_melt()`` first before setting the new parameters. 
```
function setPeriod(uint48 period_) public governance {
        _melt();
        require(period_ > 0 && period_ <= MAX_PERIOD, "invalid period");
        emit PeriodSet(period, period_);
        period = period_;
    }

    /// Ratio setting
    /// @custom:governance
    function setRatio(uint192 ratio_) public governance {
        _melt();
        require(ratio_ <= MAX_RATIO, "invalid ratio");
        // The ratio can safely be set to 0
        emit RatioSet(ratio, ratio_);
        ratio = ratio_;
    }

  function melt() external notPausedOrFrozen {
        _melt();
    }

 function _melt() internal {
        if (uint48(block.timestamp) < uint64(lastPayout) + period) return;

        // # of whole periods that have passed since lastPayout
        uint48 numPeriods = uint48((block.timestamp) - lastPayout) / period;

        // Paying out the ratio r, N times, equals paying out the ratio (1 - (1-r)^N) 1 time.
        uint192 payoutRatio = FIX_ONE.minus(FIX_ONE.minus(ratio).powu(numPeriods));

        uint256 amount = payoutRatio.mulu_toUint(lastPayoutBal);

        lastPayout += numPeriods * period;
        lastPayoutBal = rToken.balanceOf(address(this)) - amount;
        if (amount > 0) rToken.melt(amount);
    }
```

