# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L205
https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L393


# Vulnerability details

### Details
The function `issue(address recipient, uint256 amtRToken)` allows an `issuer` to mint RTokens for a `recipient` which can be a different account.

The `issuer` is set to the caller of the function
```sol
address issuer = _msgSender();
```
and is responsible for depositing collateral
```sol
// == Interactions: accept collateral ==
for (uint256 i = 0; i < basketSize; ++i) {
	IERC20Upgradeable(erc20s[i]).safeTransferFrom(issuer, address(this), deposits[i]);
}
```
If there is a basket change, the collateral will be refunded.
`issue(address recipient, uint256 amtRToken)`
```sol
	// Refund issuances against old baskets
	if (queue.basketNonce > 0 && queue.basketNonce != basketNonce) {
		...
		refundSpan(recipient, queue.left, queue.right);
		...
	}
```
`vest(address account, uint256 endId)`: (`account` will be the recipient)
```sol
...
if (queue.basketNonce != basketNonce) {
	refundSpan(account, queue.left, queue.right);
...
```

However, the collateral is actually refunded to the recipient instead of the issuer.

`cancel(...)` is also interesting since the recipient is the one in the issueQueues `IssueQueue storage queue = issueQueues[recipient];`

```sol
    function cancel(uint256 endId, bool earliest) external notFrozen {
        address account = _msgSender();
        IssueQueue storage queue = issueQueues[account];

        require(queue.left <= endId && endId <= queue.right, "out of range");

        // == Interactions ==
        if (earliest) {
            refundSpan(account, queue.left, endId);
        } else {
            refundSpan(account, endId, queue.right);
        }
    }
```
We can see `account = _msgSender()` so a user calling this function can only cancel issuances for their own account.

If issuer and recipient are seperate entities, then it means the recipient of the RToken gets to `cancel(...)` the issuance, but not the issuer of the RToken.


### Impact
The depositor(issuer) is not the refundee. Which I believe is not the intended functionality. 

The issuer should also be the one to cancel issuances made, not the recipient.

A loss of funds scenario I can think of is that the recipient is actually a smart contract designed only to handle the RToken. Because of a basket change, the collateral tokens unexpectedly are sent to the contract(recipient) when `vest(...)` is called which has no way of transferring collateral tokens out, resulting in loss of funds.

^ hypothetical scenario so will submit as Medium
### Recommendations
I think it makes sense for the issuer to be the refundee - since a _refund_ means to "return funds" to the original funder. Decision to cancel issuances should also be made by issuer, not recipient.

Fun note: The protocol went through 4 audits so I was curious why this wasn't caught, turns out it used to be refunded to the issuer for all 4 audits. Bug was introduced because of search and replace O_O https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/commit/003df52bdf3c254826967f5f418367eb19e9f6c3. Perhaps tests should also cover this case to prevent future issues.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/mixins/ComponentRegistry.sol#L19


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
```
function __ComponentRegistry_init(Components memory components_) internal onlyInitializing {
```
The first line of code is where the function `__ComponentRegistry_init` is marked as `onlyInitializing` which means that it can only be called once during contract deployment.

```
function __ComponentRegistry_init(Components memory components_) internal onlyInitializing {
```

The second line of code is where the function `__ComponentRegistry_init` is marked as internal which means that it can only be called by other functions within the same contract.

The `__ComponentRegistry_init` function is marked as `onlyInitializing` which means it can only be called once during the contract deployment. This means that if the contract is ever upgraded, the address of the components can't be modified. This can be a major issue as the contract may rely on the correct address of the components for proper functioning, and if the address is ever changed, the contract would no longer function as intended.

The `__ComponentRegistry_init` function is also marked as internal, which means it can only be called by other functions within the same contract. This may limit the ability to initialize the contract if the function is not called by the contract's deployer or another function with the appropriate access. This could cause the contract to be deployed in an uninitialized state and would not function as intended.

The combination of the above two bugs creates a major issue where the contract is deployed with the correct address of the components, but the address can't be modified in case of an upgrade, which would mean the contract would no longer function as intended.


## Proof of Concept

We'll Create a new version of the contract that changes the address of a component, but is unable to call the `__ComponentRegistry_init` function to update the address in the contract.

For example, the attacker could deploy a new version of the contract with a malicious version of a component, but since the `__ComponentRegistry_init` function is marked as `onlyInitializing`, it can only be called once during contract deployment and can't be called again to update the address of the component.

Another attack vector would be if the attacker creates a malicious version of the contract and deploy it in a way that the `__ComponentRegistry_init` function is not called, this will lead to the contract being deployed in an uninitialized state and would not function as intended.

```
pragma solidity 0.8.9;

contract Attacker {
    // Address of the original contract
    address componentRegistry;

    constructor(address _componentRegistry) public {
        componentRegistry = _componentRegistry;
    }

    function upgrade(address _newComponent) public {
        // Attempting to upgrade the component address
        // but the __ComponentRegistry_init function is marked as onlyInitializing
        // so this call will fail
        IComponentRegistry(componentRegistry).__ComponentRegistry_init(_newComponent);
    }
}
```

The attacker contract that attempting to upgrade the component address, but the `__ComponentRegistry_init` function is marked as `onlyInitializing`, so this call will fail, and the component address will not be updated.

Another example would be if the attacker creates a malicious version of the contract and deploy it in a way that the `__ComponentRegistry_init` function is not called, this will lead to the contract being deployed in an uninitialized state and would not function as intended.

```
contract MaliciousDeployer {
    // Address of the original contract
    address componentRegistry;

    constructor() public {
        // Deploying the component registry contract but not calling the __ComponentRegistry_init function
        componentRegistry = new ComponentRegistry();
    }
}
```

## Tools Used

Manual audit, Vs Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Remove the `onlyInitializing` modifier from the `__ComponentRegistry_init` function. This will allow the function to be called multiple times, allowing the component addresses to be updated in the case of a contract upgrade.

```
function __ComponentRegistry_init(Components memory components_) internal {
    // existing code
}
```

Add a public function to update the component addresses and use a guard condition to check if the new address is different from the existing one, this would prevent infinite loops.

```
function updateRTokenTrader(address _newRTokenTrader) public {
    require(_newRTokenTrader != address(rTokenTrader), "RTokenTrader address is already set to the same address");
    _setRTokenTrader(_newRTokenTrader);
}
```

Make the `__ComponentRegistry_init` function external and call it from the constructor or from a function that is called by the deployer.

```
function __ComponentRegistry_init(Components memory components_) external {
    // existing code
}
```

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/mixins/Auth.sol#L74-L75


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
```
address msgSender = _msgSender();
_grantRole(OWNER, msgSender);
```
The `msg.sender` is granted the OWNER role and there is no clear way for this role to be transferred to other addresses in the future.

This means that the initial owner of the contract is `hardcoded` to the address that calls the `__Auth_init` function and there is no provision for transferring ownership to other addresses. This could be a problem if the initial owner loses access to their account or if there is a need for multiple parties to have control over the contract.

In addition, It will also limit the flexibility of the contract as the owner will not be able to transfer ownership to other parties.

This may lead to a lack of control over the contract and can lead to various issues like the inability to upgrade the contract, inability to freeze/unfreeze the contract and lack of control over the access of the contract.

Consequently, this can lead to the contract being stuck in a state where it can no longer be updated or controlled by anyone, making it a serious issue.

## Proof of Concept

A malicious user can exploit this vulnerability by creating a new contract that inherits from the Auth contract, and then calling the `__Auth_init` function to become the owner. Once they are the owner, they can control the contract and make arbitrary changes to it.

```
pragma solidity 0.8.9;

import "@openzeppelin/contracts-upgradeable/access/AccessControlUpgradeable.sol";
import "../interfaces/IMain.sol";

contract Attacker is Auth {
    function attack() public {
        // Calling the __Auth_init function and becoming the owner
        __Auth_init(100, 100);
        // Now the msg.sender (the address that called this function) is the owner of the contract
        // and can make arbitrary changes to it
    }
}
```
This will allow the attacker to take ownership of the contract and make arbitrary changes to it.

## Tools Used

Manual audit, vs Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

add a function to the contract that allows the `OWNER` role to be transferred to another address. This function should have appropriate access control measures in place, such as requiring the caller to be the current OWNER or have a specific role.

```
function transferOwnership(address newOwner) public onlyOwner {
    require(newOwner != address(0));
    _transferRole(OWNER, msg.sender, newOwner);
}
```

The `transferOwnership` function takes in a new owner address as a parameter. The function uses a `require` statement to ensure that the new owner address is not the `null address (0x0)`. The function then uses the `_transferRole` function provided by the `AccessControl` library to transfer the OWNER role from the current owner to the new owner address. The `onlyOwner` modifier is used to ensure that only the current owner can call this function.

Additionally, it's recommended to add a guard condition to the `__Auth_init` function to check if the `msg.sender` is already an owner.

```
require(!_hasRole(OWNER, msg.sender), "msg.sender is already an owner");
```

This way, the contract prevents the possibility of an owner role being assigned to multiple addresses.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/mixins/Auth.sol#L157


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The vulnerability would be located in the absence of the following functions.

```
    function unfreeze() public onlyOwner{
        // Unfreeze logic
    }
    function unpause() public onlyOwner{
        // Unpause logic
    }
```

This vulnerability can be seen as the missing of these functions in the contract, as the contract has the ability to freeze and pause but there's no way to `unfreeze` or `unpause`.

The contract's functionality is in that it has the ability to `freeze` and `pause`, but there is no provided function to `unfreeze` or `unpause` the contract. This can prevent the contract from functioning as intended and may lead to unexpected behavior or errors. Additionally, the contract may become stuck in a frozen or paused state, which would prevent any transactions from being processed. This can cause significant issues for users of the contract and may lead to financial loss or other negative consequences.

This bug may also lead to security concerns, as an attacker may be able to freeze or pause the contract and prevent legitimate transactions from being processed. This could potentially lead to a loss of funds or other security issues. Additionally, the inability to `unfreeze` or `unpause` the contract could lead to a loss of control over the contract by the contract owner.


## Proof of Concept

This bug could involve an attacker freezing or pausing the contract using the provided functions and then preventing it from being unfrozen or `unpaused`. This could be done by exploiting a vulnerability in the contract, such as by calling the freeze or pause function repeatedly, or by creating a malicious contract that calls the `freeze` or `pause` function on the vulnerable contract.

```
contract Attacker {
    address target;

    function setTarget(address _target) public {
        target = _target;
    }

    function freeze() public {
        // call the freeze function on the target contract repeatedly
        for (uint i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            target.call(bytes4(keccak256("freeze()")));
        }
    }
}
```

The attacker creates a contract called `Attacker` that takes a target contract address and repeatedly calls the freeze function on that target contract. This can cause the target contract to become stuck in a frozen state, preventing any transactions from being processed.

Another example is an attacker `creates` a malicious contract that calls the freeze or pause function on the vulnerable contract, and then the malicious contract takes control of the vulnerable contract, it can be used as a malicious contract that can steal the user's assets.

## Tools Used

Manual audit, vs code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Add an `unfreeze` function that can only be called by the owner or an authorized party. This function should set the `unfreezeAt` variable to a value that is less than the current block time, effectively unfreezing the contract.

```
    function unfreeze() public onlyOwner {
        unfreezeAt = block.timestamp - 1;
    }
```

Add an `unpause` function that can only be called by the owner or an authorized party. This function should set the paused variable to false, effectively unpausing the contract.

```
    function unpause() public onlyOwner {
        paused = false;
    }
```

Add a modifier to the functions that are not supposed to be called when the contract is frozen or paused.

```
    modifier whenNotFrozenOrPaused() {
        require(!isFrozen() && !paused);
        _;
    }
    function myFunction() public whenNotFrozenOrPaused {
        // function's body
    }
```

Use a time delay mechanism such as `OpenZeppelin's TimedCrowdsale` or `TimedCrowdsaleUpgradeable` to unfreeze the contract after a specific period of time.

Add a timer for the freeze function so that the `freeze` state will end automatically after a specific period of time.

Use Role Based Access Control (RBAC) to limit `access` to these functions to only authorized parties.

Add event logs to the contract to keep track of the freeze and unfreeze actions and make it possible for the users to track the state of the contract.

Consider adding a limit on the number of times the freeze function can be called before it is automatically unfrozen.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/mixins/Auth.sol#L36
