# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/9196c85b980e1ef48d04779e4b4d2b6279c0433d/contracts/mixins/Auth.sol#L120


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Trusting on the admin of the role `SHORT_FREEZER` can be a risk. Because if is not working properly (out of access), no address is going to be assigned to the role `SHORT_FREEZER`. So, in case the last `SHORT_FREEZER` is revoked, the protocol is left without any `SHORT_FREEZER`.

## Proof of Concept

When a `SHORT_FREEZER` detects a bug, and calls the function `freezeShort`, it will be revoked.
https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/9196c85b980e1ef48d04779e4b4d2b6279c0433d/contracts/mixins/Auth.sol#L120

The admin of the role `SHORT_FREEZER` is responsible to assign a new address to this role.

If the admin of the role `SHORT_FREEZER` is compromised, malicious, or not working properly, a new address will not be assigned to `SHORT_FREEZER` role. This leaves the protocol without `SHORT_FREEZER` and can be a risk to the protocol. Because there is no `SHORT_FREEZER` to take an immediate action in case of a detected bug.

The main vulnerability is that the protocol does not check how many `SHORT_FREEZER` is active at the moment. Suppose, there are 3 addresses assigned to the role `SHORT_FREEZER`. First and second `SHORT_FREEZER` detect a bug, and after short-freezing the protocol, they are revoked. Only one `SHORT_FREEZER` is left. Suppose, another bug is detected, and this last `SHORT_FREEZER` short-freezes the protocol. Since the protocol does not check that this is the last active `SHORT_FREEZER`, the protocol revokes it. Since, the admin of the role `SHORT_FREEZER` is compromised or malicious, no new addresses is going to be assigned this role. So, the protocol is left without any `SHORT_FREEZER`.

The same story can be applied to the role `LONG_FREEZER`.

The scenario explained above is for a good case that there are 3 addresses assigned to the role `SHORT_FREEZER`. But, if only one address is assigned to this role and the admin is not working properly, the risk will be more. 

## Tools Used

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
### Solution 1:
The protocol should track the number of active `SHORT_FREEZER`. So, if it is the last one, it should not be revoked. So that, we ensure that there is always at least one `SHORT_FREEZER` available.
```
    uint numberOfShortFreezer;

    function freezeShort() external onlyRole(SHORT_FREEZER) {
        if(numberOfShortFreezer > 1){
            // Revoke short freezer role after one use
            _revokeRole(SHORT_FREEZER, _msgSender());
            freezeUntil(uint48(block.timestamp) + shortFreeze);
            numberOfShortFreezer--;
        }
    }
```
### Solution 2:
Having a backup `SHORT_FREEZER` can be a safe security measure. So, in case the last `SHORT_FREEZER` is going to be revoked, the backup one should be assigned to this address automatically and alerts the owner that there is something wrong with the availability of the `SHORT_FREEZER`.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L344-L370
https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L251-L280


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Rtoken should issue new tokens according to the issuance rate, however it can't do that correct and as result users that should receive their funds at current block should wait.

## Proof of Concept
When user wants to mint RToken he calls `issue` function.
RToken is limited to issue new tokens in a block according to the `issuanceRate`.
Minimum rate is 10_000 tokens.
To know at which block amount of RTokens that users wants to receive can be minted `whenFinished` function [is called](https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L243).

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/p1/RToken.sol#L344-L370
```solidity
    function whenFinished(uint256 amtRToken) private returns (uint192 finished) {
        // Calculate the issuance rate (if this is the first issuance in the block)
        if (lastIssRateBlock < block.number) {
            lastIssRateBlock = block.number;


            // D18{rTok/block} = D18{1/block} * D18{rTok} / D18{1}
            // uint192 downcast is safe, max value representations are 1e18 * 1e48 / 1e18
            lastIssRate = uint192((issuanceRate * totalSupply()) / FIX_ONE);
            // uint192(<) is equivalent to Fix.lt
            if (lastIssRate < MIN_BLOCK_ISSUANCE_LIMIT) lastIssRate = MIN_BLOCK_ISSUANCE_LIMIT;
        }


        // make `before` be the fractional-block when this issuance should start;
        // before = max(allVestAt, block.number - 1)
        uint192 before = allVestAt; // D18{block number}
        // uint192 downcast is safe: block numbers are smaller than 1e38
        uint192 nowStart = uint192(FIX_ONE * (block.number - 1)); // D18{block} = D18{1} * {block}
        if (nowStart > before) before = nowStart;


        // finished: D18{block} = D18{block} + D18{1} * D18{RTok} / D18{rtok/block}
        // uint192() downcast here is safe because:
        //   lastIssRate is at least 1e24 (from MIN_ISS_RATE), and
        //   amtRToken is at most 1e48, so
        //   what's downcast is at most (1e18 * 1e48 / 1e24) = 1e38 < 2^192-1
        finished = before + uint192((FIX_ONE_256 * amtRToken + (lastIssRate - 1)) / lastIssRate);
        allVestAt = finished;
    }
```

This function calculates current allowed amount of new tokens per block(`lastIssRate`) using totalSupply of RToken.
In case if `lastIssRate` is less than MIN_BLOCK_ISSUANCE_LIMIT, then rate is MIN_BLOCK_ISSUANCE_LIMIT(10_000).
Later function calculates `before` which is last block where all previously ordered tokens will be minted.

Using this variables function can calculate `finished` - block at which ordered amount of current user will be minted.
It calculates in following way: last block where all previously ordered tokens will be minted + amount of blocks that is needed to mint RTokens of current user. `finished = before + uint192((FIX_ONE_256 * amtRToken + (lastIssRate - 1)) / lastIssRate);`

Pls, note that `uint192((FIX_ONE_256 * amtRToken + (lastIssRate - 1)) / lastIssRate)` will be always at least 1, because `+ (lastIssRate - 1)` is rounding here.
That means that for example if issuanceRate is 10_000 and user mints only 5_000, then `allVestAt` is increased by 1 block. And that means that if user wants to mint extra 5_000 in same block he will not be able to do that.

Scenario.
1.block = 25. 2 persons at start time(block 25) want to mint 5000 Rtok
2.first one starts. as it's start time, then lastIssRate becomes 10000, lastIssRateBlock = 25
before=0; nowStart = block-1=24; nowStart > before => before = nowStart = 24
first user will receive finish time (block-1 + 1 block); finished = 25 -1 +1 = 25. allVestAt = 25
that means that this tokens will be minted in this block.
3.second user in same block 25 wants to mint 5000 rtok.
as it's same block, then lastIssRate is still 10000
before=allVestAt=25; nowStart(block 24) < before(block 25), so before = block 25
finished is computed as 25 + 1 = 26; allVestEnd = 26;
that means that this 5000 tokens will be minted in next block, however they should be minted in this

