## Tools Used
VS Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Since it is hard to ensure that ERC777 token will never be added to a basket, it is recommended to improve the code to completely avoid this attack risk:
1. Add ReentrancyGuard to the RToken, and make important methods (`issue`, `vest`, `redeem`, `mint`, `setBasketsNeeded`) nonReentrant.
2. Add a flag to RToken to revert functions in other contracts (like `BasketHandler.manageTokens()`) when RToken is executing issue/vest/redeem.

# Lines of code

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/libraries/RedemptionBattery.sol#L53-L56


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
```
function currentCharge(Battery storage battery, uint256 supply)
        internal
        view
        returns (uint256 charge)
```
The code is missing the `return charge` statement, which means that the function does not return a value. This can cause an issue with the usage of this function.

The function `currentCharge` will not return any value, this could cause issues in the functionality of the smart contract that uses this function.

The function `currentCharge` is called by the `discharge` function to calculate the current charge of the battery, but if this function does not return a value, it means that the function does not execute correctly, this can cause the contract to break or not work as intended.

For example, if the contract uses the `currentCharge` value to determine whether there is sufficient charge to complete a redemption, and the function does not return a value, it will cause the redemption to fail even if there is sufficient charge.


## Proof of Concept
We'll create a contract that utilizes the `RedemptionBatteryLib` library and calls the function `currentCharge`, but the function does not return any value.

```
pragma solidity 0.8.9;

import "./RedemptionBatteryLib.sol";

contract BrokenCurrentCharge {
    using RedemptionBatteryLib for RedemptionBatteryLib.Battery;
    RedemptionBatteryLib.Battery public battery;
    uint256 public totalSupply;

    constructor(uint256 _totalSupply) public {
        totalSupply = _totalSupply;
    }

    function checkCharge() public view returns(uint256){
        // The call to currentCharge function will not return any value
        return battery.currentCharge(totalSupply);
    }
}
```
this example, the function `checkCharge` uses the `currentCharge` function to determine the current charge of the battery. But, in this case, the `currentCharge` function is not defined to return any value, this will cause the `checkCharge` function to not return any value as well, breaking the functionality of the contract.


## Tools Used

Manual audit, Vs Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

add a return statement in the `currentCharge` function to return the calculated charge.

```
function currentCharge(Battery storage battery, uint256 supply)
        internal
        view
        returns (uint256 charge)
    {
        // {qRTok/hour} = {qRTok} * D18{1/hour} / D18
        uint256 amtPerHour = (supply * battery.scalingRedemptionRate) / FIX_ONE_256;

        if (battery.redemptionRateFloor > amtPerHour) amtPerHour = battery.redemptionRateFloor;

        // {blocks}
        uint48 blocks = uint48(block.number) - battery.lastBlock;

        // {qRTok} = {qRTok} + {qRTok/hour} * {blocks} / {blocks/hour}
        charge = battery.lastCharge + (amtPerHour * blocks) / BLOCKS_PER_HOUR;

        uint256 maxCharge = amtPerHour > supply ? supply : amtPerHour;
        if (charge > maxCharge) charge = maxCharge;
        // add a return statement
        return charge;
    }
```

the return `statement` is added at the end of the function to return the `calculated` charge. This will allow the function to return the correct value, which can then be used in other parts of the contract, fixing the issue and restoring the intended functionality of the contract.

