# Vulnerability details

## Impact

https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/interfaces/IAsset.sol#L16-L48
https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/interfaces/IAsset.sol#L75
https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/interfaces/IAsset.sol#L90
https://github.com/reserve-protocol/protocol/blob/df7ecadc2bae74244ace5e8b39e94bc992903158/contracts/interfaces/IAsset.sol#L93

```
interface IAsset is IRewardable {
    function refresh() external;
    function price() external view returns (uint192 low, uint192 high);
    function lotPrice() external view returns (uint192 lotLow, uint192 lotHigh);
    function bal(address account) external view returns (uint192);
    function erc20() external view returns (IERC20Metadata);
    function erc20Decimals() external view returns (uint8);
    function isCollateral() external view returns (bool);
    function maxTradeVolume() external view returns (uint192);
}

interface ICollateral is IAsset {
    function targetName() external view returns (bytes32);
    function status() external view returns (CollateralStatus);
}
```
This allows an attacker to call these functions without sending any funds to the contract, which can lead to unexpected behavior and potentially unintended changes to the contract's state.

External functions without payable modifier can be called without sending ether to the contract which can lead to unexpected behavior and potentially unintended changes to the contract's state.

Without the payable modifier, the contract can be invoked without paying for the gas cost of the transaction, causing the contract owner to pay for the gas cost.

Without the payable modifier, the contract will not receive any funds, which can cause a lack of revenue for the contract owner.


## Proof of Concept

Not including the "payable" modifier on "external" functions in the `IAsset` and `ICollateral` interfaces is as follows.

```
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

import "./IAsset.sol";

contract Attacker {
    IAsset public asset;

    constructor(address _asset) public {
        asset = IAsset(_asset);
    }

    function callRefresh() public {
        // Calling the refresh() function without sending any ether
        asset.refresh();
    }
}
```

In this, the attacker contract is able to call the `refresh()` function of an `IAsset` contract without sending any ether. This can lead to unexpected behavior and potentially unintended changes to the contract's state.

Additionally, an attacker can deploy another contract that calls the non-payable external functions of `ICollateral` contract, causing the same vulnerability.

## Tools Used

Manual audit, vs code.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

"payable" modifier on "external" functions in the `IAsset` and `ICollateral` interfaces can be mitigated by adding the payable keyword to all external functions.


```
interface IAsset is IRewardable {
    function refresh() external payable;
    function price() external view returns (uint192 low, uint192 high);
    function lotPrice() external view returns (uint192 lotLow, uint192 lotHigh);
    function bal(address account) external view returns (uint192);
    function erc20() external view returns (IERC20Metadata);
    function erc20Decimals() external view returns (uint8);
    function isCollateral() external view returns (bool);
    function maxTradeVolume() external view returns (uint192);
}

interface ICollateral is IAsset {
    function targetName() external view returns (bytes32);
    function status() external view returns (CollateralStatus);
}
```

By adding the payable keyword to all `external` functions, it ensures that any calls to these functions will require the sender to include some funds to cover the gas cost of the transaction. This can prevent unexpected behavior and unintended changes to the contract's state.

