# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/main/src/PoolV3.sol#L529-L576


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
- If the pool incurs bad debt, some users may lose part or all of their deposits.  
## Vulnerability Details
A position with bad debt is expected to be liquidated using function `liquidatePositionBadDebt`.  
See: [CDPVault.liquidatePositionBadDebt](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/main/src/CDPVault.sol#L579-L632)
```solidity
function liquidatePositionBadDebt(address owner, uint256 repayAmount) external whenNotPaused{
    ...
    ... snipped
    ...
    position = _modifyPosition(
        owner,
        position,
        0,
        debtData.cumulativeIndexNow,
        -toInt256(takeCollateral),
        totalDebt
    );

    pool.repayCreditAccount(debtData.debt, 0, loss); // U:[CM-11]
    // transfer the collateral amount from the vault to the liquidator
    token.safeTransfer(msg.sender, takeCollateral);

    int256 quotaRevenueChange = _calcQuotaRevenueChange(-int(debtData.debt));
    if (quotaRevenueChange != 0) {
        IPoolV3(pool).updateQuotaRevenue(quotaRevenueChange); // U:[PQK-15]
    }
}
```  
This function calls `pool.repayCreditAccount` at the end to record the loss from bad debt.  
The pool then accounts for the loss:  
- It burns shares from treasury as much as possible to cover the loss
- The remaining loss is incurred to the pool, deducted from `expectedLiquidity`  

See: [PoolV3.repayCreditAccount](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/main/src/PoolV3.sol#L529-L576)
```solidity
function repayCreditAccount(
    uint256 repaidAmount,
    uint256 profit,
    uint256 loss
)
    external
    override
    creditManagerOnly // U:[LP-2C]
    whenNotPaused // U:[LP-2A]
    nonReentrant // U:[LP-2B]
{
    uint128 repaidAmountU128 = repaidAmount.toUint128();

    DebtParams storage cmDebt = _creditManagerDebt[msg.sender];
    uint128 cmBorrowed = cmDebt.borrowed;
    if (cmBorrowed == 0) {
        revert CallerNotCreditManagerException(); // U:[LP-2C,14A]
    }

    if (profit > 0) {
        _mint(treasury, convertToShares(profit)); // U:[LP-14B]
    } else if (loss > 0) {
        address treasury_ = treasury;
        uint256 sharesInTreasury = balanceOf(treasury_);
        uint256 sharesToBurn = convertToShares(loss);
        if (sharesToBurn > sharesInTreasury) {
            unchecked {
                emit IncurUncoveredLoss({
                    creditManager: msg.sender,
                    loss: convertToAssets(sharesToBurn - sharesInTreasury)
                }); // U:[LP-14D]
            }
            sharesToBurn = sharesInTreasury;
        }
        _burn(treasury_, sharesToBurn); // U:[LP-14C,14D]
    }

    _updateBaseInterest({
        expectedLiquidityDelta: -loss.toInt256(),
        availableLiquidityDelta: 0,
        checkOptimalBorrowing: false
    }); // U:[LP-14B,14C,14D]

    _totalDebt.borrowed -= repaidAmountU128; // U:[LP-14B,14C,14D]
    cmDebt.borrowed = cmBorrowed - repaidAmountU128; // U:[LP-14B,14C,14D]

    emit Repay(msg.sender, repaidAmount, profit, loss); // U:[LP-14B,14C,14D]
}
```
However, this function lacks the mechanism to socialize or distribute the loss to shareholders in the pool.  
As the exchange rate for this pool is fixed at 1:1 ratio, meaning that anyone is expected to withdraw the full amount they've deposited.  

See: [PoolV3.sol#L427-L439](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/main/src/PoolV3.sol#L427-L439)
```solidity
function _convertToShares(uint256 assets) internal pure returns (uint256 shares) {
    return assets; 
}

function _convertToAssets(uint256 shares) internal pure returns (uint256 assets) {
    return shares;
}
```

Consequently, if the pool incurs losses, some users may not be able to withdraw in full amount due to the lack of funds.  

### Example scenario to demonstrate the issue  
Supposed that there are 3 actors interacting with the protocol  
- **ALICE** as a depositor
- **EVE** as another depositor
- **BOB** as a borrower  

1. ALICE deposits 10 ETH
2. EVE deposits 100 ETH
3. BOB borrows 110 ETH
4. Supposed that BOB's posistion is underwater and incurring bad debt and BOB's position is liquidated with a loss of 10 ETH  
5. EVE successfully withdraw her 100 ETH
6. ALICE won't be able to withdraw her 10 ETH due to the lack of funds  

## Recommended Mitigations
- Consider implementing mechanism to distribute incurred loss evenly.  

## Additional Notes
This finding stems from a different root cause compared to the following issues:  
- [WP-H4](https://notes.watchpug.com/p/18ea3089e2esgBHp#n_17)  
- [Re:WP-H4](https://notes.watchpug.com/p/1909aa8a565HVvGe#n_8)  

It is clear that 1:1 exchange rate is intended because the fix for accrued interest distribution is fixed by introducing a mechanism for distribution.  
The root cause of this finding is the lack of mechanism to distribute losses arising from bad debt.  

## Proof-of-Concept
The following PoC utilizes the existing test suite to demonstrate the issue.  
It attemps to show a situation where:  
- The test contract `address(this)` owns 1_000_000 ETH in **PoolV3** ([TestBase.sol#createCore](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/main/src/test/TestBase.sol#L159-L191))  
- BOB owns 80 ETH in **PoolV3**  
- Bad debt arises and liquidated  
- The test contract withdraws 1_000_000 ETH in full
- BOB is not able to withdraw his full 80 ETH because of the bad debt  

### Steps
1. Add a new test file, `CDPVaultNoLoss.sol`, in` 2024-07-loopfi/src/test/unit/` directory.  
2. Paste the below content in the file.  
3. Run `forge t --match-contract CDPVaultNoLoss --match-test test_no_loss_distribution -vv`  
4. Observe that BOB fails to withdraw in full amount
