# Vulnerability details

## Summary & Impact
Due to a lack of facade address and position whitelisting of the `facade` address argument of the `GearBoxV3:executeCommands` function, `CreditFacadeV3` addresses addresses can be set in internal calls to `SafeERC20:forceApprove`, giving malicious contracts  used in strategies the ability to drain the GearBoxV3 connector of deposited asset through the inherited call to `BaseConnector:_approveOperations`.

Malicious actors may engineer enticing strategies implying high returns which involve malicious CreditFacadeV3 contracts. Strategies may also be directly created by malicious token owners. 

AI agent or human Strategy Managers may therefore inadvertedly select individual or batched strategies involving such maliocus tokens for use against the GearBoxV3, based on the implication of high returns. It is also possible for such transactions to be hidden between batches of attractive, legitimate trades which may be executed in a batch manner via the `BalancerFlashLoan:makeFlashLoan` function if supported.

The efficacy of Watchers or other AI agents cannot be relied upon to prevent abuse of this issue, as the NOYA documentation at the time of audit states that all roles are [managed by the NOYA team](https://docs.noya.ai/technical-architecture/technical-architecture/roles-in-noya#watchers). Therefore, the current technical controls of the `GearBoxV3` contract do not sufficiently protect against external threats from abusing this issue. 

## Root Cause - Missing `facade` address verification in `GearBoxV3:executeCmmands`
Most other Connector functions invokable by Strategy Managers only allowed whitelisted positions to be executed, via calls to `Registry:updateHoldingPosition` using a calculated `positionId` hash from `Registry:calculateHoldingPosition`. Other connectors instead relied on token whitelisting, or a trusted receiver address, to achieve a similar level of protection against potentially harmful strategies being executed. However, the `GearBoxV3:executeCommands` function lacked any protection against malicious strategy execution, and as such an arbitrary spender address can be set in an internal call to the inherited `BaseConnector:_approveOperations` function.  

A malicious contract implementing a function for `multicall` may then transfer the approved asset to an arbitrary address. This is necessary as the `BaseConnector:_revokeApproval` function is called immediately following the multicall.

```solidity

//File:contracts/connectors/GearBoxV3.sol

    /**
     * @notice Execute multiple commands on a credit account
     * @param facade - CreditFacade address
     * @param creditAccount - credit account address
     * @param calls - array of multicalls
     * @param approvalToken - token to approve
     * @param amount - amount to approve
     */

    function executeCommands(
        address facade,
        address creditAccount,
        MultiCall[] calldata calls,
        address approvalToken,
        uint256 amount
    ) public onlyManager nonReentrant {
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < calls.length; i++) {
            if (calls[i].target != facade) revert IConnector_InvalidTarget(calls[i].target);
            bytes4 method = bytes4(calls[i].callData[:4]);

            if (method == ICreditFacadeV3Multicall.enableToken.selector) {
                (address token) = abi.decode(calls[i].callData[4:], (address));
                _updateTokenInRegistry(token);
            }
        }
        if (approvalToken != address(0)) {
            _approveOperations(approvalToken, ICreditFacadeV3(facade).creditManager(), amount);
        }
        ICreditFacadeV3(facade).multicall(creditAccount, calls);
        if (approvalToken != address(0)) {
            _revokeApproval(approvalToken, ICreditFacadeV3(facade).creditManager());
        }
        emit ExecuteCommands(facade, creditAccount, calls, approvalToken, amount);
    }

//File:contracts/helpers/BaseConnector.sol

    /// @notice is used to approve the _spender to spend the _amount of the _token
    /// @dev It uses the forceApprove function so it's safe to use it with tokens with special approval logic (USDT)
    function _approveOperations(address _token, address _spender, uint256 _amount) internal virtual {
        uint256 currentAllowance = IERC20(_token).allowance(address(this), _spender);
        if (currentAllowance >= _amount) {
            return;
        }
        IERC20(_token).forceApprove(_spender, _amount);
    }
```

## Proof of Concept

The following Forge PoC test demonstrates this issue. The modifications can be inserted into the GearBoxV3.t.sol test file.

Test items: 
* `test_audit_GearBoxV3ConnectorDirectKeeper` : simulates the Keepers contract executing a malicious strategy on behalf of Strategy Managers directly.
* `MalContract` : simulates a malicious ICreditFacade contract.

<details>

```solidity

