# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/main/contracts/connectors/UNIv3Connector.sol#L140


# Vulnerability details

## Description
The getPositionTVL public view function at the UNIv3Connector contract utilizes the instant price of a uniswap v3 pool to calculate the position TVL. As can be seen in the following snippet:
```solidity
function _getPositionTVL(HoldingPI memory p, address base) public view override returns (uint256 tvl) {
        PositionBP memory positionInfo = registry.getPositionBP(vaultId, p.positionId);
        uint256 tokenId = abi.decode(p.data, (uint256));
        (address token0, address token1) = abi.decode(positionInfo.data, (address, address));
        uint256 amount0;
        uint256 amount1;
        (int24 tL, int24 tU, uint24 fee) = abi.decode(p.additionalData, (int24, int24, uint24));
        {
...
            (uint160 sqrtPriceX96,,,,,,) = pool.slot0();
...
}
```

While this is technically not wrong, it represents a price-manipulation attack surface for protocols, as AMM instant pricing can be easily manipulated through flash-loans.
This implementation also does contradict the UniswapValueOracle implementation made by the protocol - as the latter does utilize the TWAP while the former doesn't.
## Impact
Currently the TVL() function is very important for the protocol, as it limits the max amount of assets deposited at a vault:
```solidity
if (TVL() > depositLimitTotalAmount) {
            revert NoyaAccounting_TotalDepositLimitExceeded();
        }
```

This function utilizes the getPositionTVL function at each of the connectors.

It determines the amount of shares to be emitted to someone at the calculateDepositShares function:
```
function _convertToShares(uint256 assets, Math.Rounding rounding) internal view virtual returns (uint256) {
        return assets.mulDiv(totalSupply() + 10 ** _decimalsOffset(), totalAssets() + 1, rounding);
    }

 function totalAssets() public view override returns (uint256) {
        return TVL();
    }
```

Manipulating instant prices on AMMs is a common attack. Such an attack can be utilized to  without true increase/decrease in TVL (through mechanisms like deposits, withdraws, interest accrual and liquidations) can be catastrophic as it can not only brick deposits due to falsely reaching a deposit limit total amount but also it can inflate/deflate shares for a user in the calculateDepositShares execution context.

## Tools Used
Manual review
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Make sure to utilize TWAP oracles to mitigate the impact of price-manipulation attack vectors in the context of the UNIv3Connector.


