# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/main/contracts/connectors/MorphoBlueConnector.sol#L132
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/main/contracts/accountingManager/AccountingManager.sol#L627


# Vulnerability details

## Description
The getPositionTVL public view function at the MorphoBlueConnector accounts borrows in TVL:
```solidity
function _getPositionTVL(HoldingPI memory p, address base) public view override returns (uint256 tvl) {
        ...
            uint256 borrowAmount =
                uint256(pos.borrowShares).toAssetsUp(market.totalBorrowAssets, market.totalBorrowShares);
        ...  
            tvl = _getValue(
                params.loanToken,
                base,
                supplyAmount + borrowAmount + convertCToL(pos.collateral, params.oracle, params.collateralToken)
            );
        }
    }
```

This is not entirely correct, as the borrowed amount isn’t "locked" in the same way that collateral or supplied assets are. The borrower is free to use these assets outside of the lending protocol, meaning they aren’t contributing to the value directly controlled or secured by the protocol.
That can effectively make any borrow inflate the TVL returned by this connector.
## Impact
I have written this TVL impact summary and placed it on TVL-related submissions. This is to help optimize similar impacts related to different protocol integration issues. 
Currently the TVL() function is very important for the protocol, as it limits the max amount of assets deposited at a vault:
```solidity
if (TVL() > depositLimitTotalAmount) {
            revert NoyaAccounting_TotalDepositLimitExceeded();
        }
```

This function utilizes the getPositionTVL function at each of the connectors.

It determines the amount of shares to be emitted to someone at the calculateDepositShares function:
```
function _convertToShares(uint256 assets, Math.Rounding rounding) internal view virtual returns (uint256) {
        return assets.mulDiv(totalSupply() + 10 ** _decimalsOffset(), totalAssets() + 1, rounding);
    }

 function totalAssets() public view override returns (uint256) {
        return TVL();
    }
```

Inflating its value without true increase in TVL (through borrows) can not only brick deposits due to falsely reaching a deposit limit total amount but also it can deflate shares for a user in the calculateDepositShares execution context.
If the manager attempts to do leveraged positions - supplying collateral to a pool, utilizing this supplied collateral to borrow certain amount then supplying this borrowed amount back to the pool over and over - the impact can be further increased.
## Tools Used
Manual review
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Avoid accounting the borrow amount as part of the TVL as it breaks accounting by accounting twice values that were utilized as collateral. 



