# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/valueOracle/NoyaValueOracle.sol#L81-L93
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/valueOracle/NoyaValueOracle.sol#L76


# Vulnerability details

According to the docs under the `Technical Architecture` the `Value Oracle` 
> This contract is allowing us to calculate the value of a token based on another. We can define routes in this contract (we have a price source for tokens A-B, B-C, C-D and we want to get the price of A-D. we can define the route of [B,C] in the smart contract and it loops through the route to get the price of A-D.

This means the price of `A-D` will be derived by converting an amount from token `A-B` then `B-C` and finally from `C-D`. However, this mechanism for conversion between tokens is not properly implemented in  [NoyaValueOracle._getValue()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/valueOracle/NoyaValueOracle.sol#L81-L93) leading to wrong conversion between tokens.
```solidity
     /// @notice Gets the value of an asset in terms of a base Token, considering a specific number of sources
    /// @param asset The address of the asset
    /// @param baseToken The address of the base Token
    /// @param amount The amount of the asset
    /// @return The value of the asset in terms of the base Token


    function getValue(address asset, address baseToken, uint256 amount) public view returns (uint256) {
        if (asset == baseToken || amount == 0) {
            return amount;
        }


      @>  address[] memory sources = priceRoutes[asset][baseToken];


        return _getValue(asset, baseToken, amount, sources);
    }
```
The public [getValue](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/valueOracle/NoyaValueOracle.sol#L76) function derives the `priceRoutes` for the conversion. That is an array of sources to convert through to derive the value of an amount of token A in terms of token D. 

In other to get the value in token D, amount of token A is converted to token B, then that amount of B is converted to token C and finally, the amount of token C is converted to token D.
The issue here is that the conversion route is not implemented correctly in `_getValue()` 
```solidity
    function _getValue(address asset, address baseToken, uint256 amount, address[] memory sources)
        internal
        view
        returns (uint256 value)
    {
        uint256 initialValue = amount;
        address quotingToken = asset;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < sources.length; i++) {
          @> initialValue = _getValue(asset, sources[i], initialValue);
            quotingToken = sources[i];
        }
        return _getValue(quotingToken, baseToken, initialValue);
    }
```
During the loop of conversions through the `sources` array that is the `priceRoutes`, the initial `asset` (that's the token A) is used to run through the entire loop leading to conversions from 
* Token A to Token B ......first loop
* Token A to Token C ...... second loop
* Token A to Token D ...... final conversion

During the loop the initial asset (Token A) is first used to convert to the first source (Token B), therefore the `initialValue` returned during the first loop is in terms of the first token address in the `sources[0]` array, this token address is stored in `quotingToken`, in the second loop instead of using the saved token address from the previous loop `quotingToken` the same `asset` is used again to generate the  new value against `sources[1]` the next element in the sources array, which shouldn't be as the `oracle` may not exist leading to a revert or the generation of wrong values. Rather the token address `sources[0]` from the previous loop should used against the current token address `sources[1]` to derive the new value in the `priceRoutes`

The correct flow of conversions should be;
* Token A to Token B ......first loop
* Token B to Token C ...... second loop
* Token C to Token D ...... final conversion

As stated by the docs.


## Impact
The function will be bricked and always revert anytime the conversion is attempted as there would be no oracle address for conversion between the wrong token pairs like from Token A to Token C or Token A to Token D.

Considering that the `getValue()` function is a critical function used throughout the protocol, a constant revert of this function will be detrimental to the proper functioning of the protocol.

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The loop in `_getValue()` should be corrected to update the token address passed in after each loop by using the `quotingToken` variable introduced in the function before the loop.
```diff
    function _getValue(address asset, address baseToken, uint256 amount, address[] memory sources)
        internal
        view
        returns (uint256 value)
    {
        uint256 initialValue = amount;
        address quotingToken = asset;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < sources.length; i++) {
--            initialValue = _getValue(asset, sources[i], initialValue);
++            initialValue = _getValue(quotingToken, sources[i], initialValue);
            quotingToken = sources[i];
        }
        return _getValue(quotingToken, baseToken, initialValue);
    }
```



