# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Attacker can force the protocol into a DoS state for user withdrawals when the protocol executes a batch's reset thereby forcing all user withdrawal fulfillment to fail.

## Proof of Concept
The Noya Protocol has implemented a safeguard for resetting the middle index of the deposit or withdraw queue. As can be seen from the `resetMiddle` function NATSPEC, its use reads: "in case of a price manipulation, we can reset the middle index of the deposit or withdraw queue to prevent the users from getting more shares than they should" but since during a reset for the withdrawal queue, the state variable `currentWithdrawGroup.totalCBAmount` wasn't mutated to not account for the nullified withdrawals in the current withdraw group, the `neededAssets` returned from `neededAssetsForWithdraw()` function will not match the `totalCBAmount` stored value in the current withdraw group, hence the current withdraw group will not be finalized and withdrawals will not proceed because of this check below in the `fulfillCurrentWithdrawGroup()` function:

```solidity
if (neededAssets != 0 && amountAskedForWithdraw != currentWithdrawGroup.totalCBAmount) {
            revert NoyaAccounting_NOT_READY_TO_FULFILL();
        }
```

You can add a console.log inside the function to see the inconsistent values stored.

```solidity
FILE: AccountingManager.sol

