Token-Transfer

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/accountingManager/AccountingManager.sol#L428
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/accountingManager/AccountingManager.sol#L304


# Vulnerability details

# Impact
`AccountingManager.sol`:: a DoS attack may leave all withdrawal requests unfulfillable forever

### ~ Preface ~

*The `AccountingManager` contract utilizes the order book pattern, *i.e.* the user **has to request a withdrawal first**, and then [when the manager account decides so] the withdrawal requests will be fulfilled when the **manager account** runs the `AccountingManager:executeWithdraw` function*.

The users' requests are kept within the structure `WithdrawQueue` which has the following properties:

```solidity
/*
* @notice WithdrawQueue is a struct that holds the withdraw queue
* @param queue is a mapping that holds the WithdrawRequest structs
* @param first is the first index of the queue (indicates the index that is waiting for the tvl to be updated)
* @param middle is the middle index of the queue (indicates the index that is waiting for calculation)
* @param last is the last index of the queue (indicates the index that is waiting for execution)
* @param totalSharesWaitingForWithdraw is the total amount of shares that is waiting for execution
*/

struct WithdrawQueue {
mapping(uint256 => WithdrawRequest) queue;
uint256 first; // NOTE!!! --> = 0 by default
uint256 middle;
uint256 last;
}
```

- As noted above, the `withdrawQueue.first` property is *with the current code* always `0` by default.

### The problem
Though there's no check that the `receiver` address is an allowed address, i.e. it's ***not** blacklisted*.

- It's worth noting that the `withdraw` function does have a check verifying that the amount is > 0, so does the `deposit` function, so the total cost of the DoS and making the AccountingManager withdrawal queue unusable is 1 wei share of the underlying token + the gas fees *(which are pretty low on L2s)*.


## The attack vector
1. Having successfully deposited at least 1 wei share of the underlying token, call the `withdraw` function to request a withdrawal;
2. Specify the `receiver` as a user that is blacklisted by the **`USDC` contract**.

## The result
1. Prior to when the **MANAGER** claims the `withdrawQueue`'s requests by running the `executeWithdraw` function, the `calculateWithdrawShares` function is called;
2. Then the account with the **MANAGER** role will attempt to run `executeWithdraw`, but it will always fail because the specified receiver of one (or more) of the queue items is a blacklisted account, and the USDC token will revert any attempts to transfer to the blacklisted address.




