# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/BaseConnector.sol#L204-L219
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/SwapHandler/Implementaions/LifiImplementation.sol#L77-L102


# Vulnerability details

## Summary

The calculation of slippage, off-chain, can lead to frequent reverts in the Swap flow and potentially make the protocol vulnerable to high losses.

## Vulnerability Details

Swaps between two tokens are initiated by `Keepers` calling [`swapHoldings()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/BaseConnector.sol#L204-L219).
Subsequently, two slippage params are passed to [`performSwapAction()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/SwapHandler/Implementaions/LifiImplementation.sol#L77-L102) which performs a check on them in [`verifySwapData()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/SwapHandler/Implementaions/LifiImplementation.sol#L110C1-L125C6) before sending the swap request to `Li.fi`.

One of the slippage params, `receivingAmount`, is calculated off-chain and stored in the callData field of the swap request.

The other, `_request.minAmount`, is calculated on-chain in the same transaction, see [`executeSwap()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/SwapHandler/GenericSwapAndBridgeHandler.sol#L90-L120), and is based on a slippage tolerance away from the Oracle's current price.

```
    function executeSwap(SwapRequest memory _swapRequest)
        // SOME CODE

        if (_swapRequest.checkForSlippage && _swapRequest.minAmount == 0) {
            // set minAmount so that slippage can be checked
            uint256 _slippageTolerance = slippageTolerance[_swapRequest.inputToken][_swapRequest.outputToken];
            if (_slippageTolerance == 0) {
                _slippageTolerance = genericSlippageTolerance;
            }
            INoyaValueOracle _priceOracle = INoyaValueOracle(valueOracle);
>>>         uint256 _outputTokenValue =
>>>             _priceOracle.getValue(_swapRequest.inputToken, _swapRequest.outputToken, _swapRequest.amount);

            _swapRequest.minAmount = (((1e6 - _slippageTolerance) * _outputTokenValue) / 1e6);
        }

        // SOME CODE
```

In [`verifySwapData()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/SwapHandler/Implementaions/LifiImplementation.sol#L110-L125) if the off-chain param, `receivingAmount`, is less than the on-chain param `_request.minAmount`, the transaction reverts.

Given that `receivingAmount` will have been calculated minutes or even hours before `_request.minAmount` using a different price, there is likely to be a discrepancy between the two parameters; `receivingAmount` only needs to be 1 wei less than for the function to revert.

```
    function verifySwapData(SwapRequest calldata _request) public view override returns (bool) {

        bytes4 selector = bytes4(_request.data[:4]);
        if (selector != LI_FI_GENERIC_SWAP_SELECTOR) {
            revert InvalidSelector();
        }

>>>     (address sendingAssetId, uint256 amount, address from, addr ess receivingAssetId, uint256 receivingAmount) =
>>>         ILiFi(lifi).extractGenericSwapParameters(_request.data);

        if (from != _request.from) revert InvalidReceiver(from, _request.from);
>>>     if (receivingAmount < _request.minAmount) revert InvalidMinAmount();
        if (sendingAssetId != _request.inputToken) revert InvalidInputToken();
        if (receivingAssetId != _request.outputToken) revert InvalidOutputToken();
        if (amount != _request.amount) revert InvalidAmount();

        return true;
    }
```

## Impact

Frequent reverts are lkely to occur in the Swap functionality due to normal market volatility. This can prevent the protocol from operating efficiently, potentially impacting it's ability to fund certain actions and restricting the protocols availability.

Additionally if there is a big price change; `receivingAmount` may be set far too low which leaves the protocol open to massive losses.

## Tools Used

Manual Review
Foundry Testing

## Recommendations

Use an on-chain slippage calculation and construct the callData on-chain before sending to Li.fi.


