# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The `ChainlinkOracleConnector` contract currently verifies that prices are greater than zero but fails to check for prices at extreme boundaries defined by minAnswer and maxAnswer in the Chainlink aggregator. This oversight could lead to significant mispricing issues if the actual market price of an asset falls below the minimum threshold or exceeds the maximum threshold set by the aggregator. Such a scenario could allow malicious actors to exploit the system by interacting with the protocol as if the asset were still valued within the erroneous range, potentially leading to substantial financial discrepancies and losses.

## Proof of Concept
`getValueFromChainlinkFeed` retrieves and processes price data from Chainlink without considering whether the returned price might be artificially constrained by the aggregator's minAnswer or maxAnswer limits

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/main/contracts/helpers/valueOracle/oracles/ChainlinkOracleConnector.sol#L123

```solidity
(, price,, updatedAt,) = source.latestRoundData();
if (block.timestamp - updatedAt > chainlinkPriceAgeThreshold) {
    revert NoyaChainlinkOracle_DATA_OUT_OF_DATE();
}
if (price <= 0) {
    revert NoyaChainlinkOracle_PRICE_ORACLE_UNAVAILABLE(address(source), address(0), address(0));
}
```

## Tools Used
Manual

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Introduce checks for price values against predefined minAnswer and maxAnswer thresholds, ensuring that the prices are within acceptable limits. This could involve storing these limits within the contract and comparing the retrieved price against them:
```solidity
(, price,, updatedAt,) = source.latestRoundData();
if (block.timestamp - updatedAt > chainlinkPriceAgeThreshold) {
    revert NoyaChainlinkOracle_DATA_OUT_OF_DATE();
}
if (price <= 0 || price < minAnswer || price > maxAnswer) {
    revert NoyaChainlinkOracle_PRICE_ORACLE_UNAVAILABLE(address(source), address(0), address(0));
}
```


