118:             
120:             uint256 _outputTokenValue =
121:                 _priceOracle.getValue(_swapRequest.inputToken, _swapRequest.outputToken, _swapRequest.amount);
122:           
123:             _swapRequest.minAmount = (((1e6 - _slippageTolerance) * _outputTokenValue) / 1e6);

129:         _amountOut = ISwapAndBridgeImplementation(swapImplInfo.route).performSwapAction(msg.sender, _swapRequest);
```

**To summarize:**
For pricefeeds that have higher heartbeats, having a smaller threshold would be wrong since the price has technically not gone stale yet. AAVE/USD has 3600 seconds as heartbeat, so the price has gone stale for 1 hour but it's still being used. Same can be said about 1Inch/ETH which has 24 hours as heartbeat but price is reported as stale in 2 hours. Some other price feeds with different staleness thresholds:
 - AMPL/USD - 48 hours
 - COMP/USD, DAI/USD, ETH/USD, FRAX/USD, GRT/ETH, LUSD/USD, MKR/USD, MATIC/USD, StETH/USD, SUSHI/USD, UNI/USD, USDP/USD, ZRX/USD, YFI/USD - 1 hour
 - LINK/ETH - 6 hours. 

Note these values are from Ethereum mainnet only. Other chains have different values being used. For BNB chain (in scope as per contest README), some feeds like 1Inch/USD, AAVE/USD are 900 seconds only. For MATIC chain, it is even lower with 1inch/USD and AAVE/USD having 27s only. The list goes on. [See here](https://docs.chain.link/data-feeds/price-feeds/addresses?network=ethereum&page=1).

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Instead of using a single threshold, consider using multiple thresholds specific to each price feed.





