# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/accountingManager/AccountingManager.sol#L317-L355
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/accountingManager/AccountingManager.sol#L547-L573


# Vulnerability details

## ISSUE

There are issues that arises if a user/grifter transfers an amount of the base token to the [`AccountingManager`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/main/contracts/accountingManager/AccountingManager.sol) contract before the withdrawal process starts.

**Issue 1**

The first issue is that the amount of asset (base token) calculated for redemption when [`AccountingManager.calculateWithdrawShares()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/accountingManager/AccountingManager.sol#L226-L250) is called by the vault manager returns more assets than it is supposed to.

```solidity
function calculateWithdrawShares(uint256 maxIterations) public onlyManager nonReentrant whenNotPaused {
        // ...
        while (
            //...
        ) {
            i += 1;
            WithdrawRequest storage data = withdrawQueue.queue[middleTemp];
            uint256 assets = previewRedeem(data.shares);    //@audit-issue - If shares = 1.5e18 when `baseToken.balanceOf(address(this))` = 1e18, assets = 2.249e18 instead of 1.5e18
            data.amount = assets;
            data.calculationTime = block.timestamp;
            //...
        }
        //...
    }
```

The issue lies in the [`previewRedeem()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/accountingManager/AccountingManager.sol#L239) call. `previewRedeem()` calls the internal `_convertToAssets()` to convert the number of shares to an equivalent amount of base tokens (asset) for withdrawal.

```solidity
    // ERC4626.sol

    /** @dev See {IERC4626-previewDeposit}. */
    function previewDeposit(uint256 assets) public view virtual returns (uint256) {
        return _convertToShares(assets, Math.Rounding.Floor);
    }

    //...

        /**
     * @dev Internal conversion function (from assets to shares) with support for rounding direction.
     */
    function _convertToShares(uint256 assets, Math.Rounding rounding) internal view virtual returns (uint256) {
        return assets.mulDiv(totalSupply() + 10 ** _decimalsOffset(), totalAssets() + 1, rounding); //<-- Uses `totalAssets()` in `AccountingManager` contract for computation
    }

```

The assets are calculated as:

$$
\text{assets} = \frac {\text{shares}\times{\text{totalAssets()}}} {\text{totalSupply()}}
$$

`shares` = number of shares to be converted.

`totalAssets()` - overrides the existing [`totalAssets()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/node_modules/@openzeppelin/contracts-5.0/token/ERC20/extensions/ERC4626.sol#L110-L113) ERC-4626 function and uses the TVL (which includes both directly held assets and those managed through external strategies/connectors) for calculation, instead of the total amount of the underlying asset that is held by vault (i.e. `asset.balanceOf(vault)`).

`totalSupply()` - returns the total supply of the tokens managed/held by the vault (that have been "deposited", assigned to the deposit queue and their equivalent shares "minted". Does not include those "transferred" explicitly to the vault, as direct minting is not allowed to account for the shares).

**Example:**

- Alice deposits 2e18 of the base token (e.g. `WETH`) to the vault contract (`AccountingManager`). They gets an equivalent amount of shares (2e18). The system calculates, then executes their deposit to a connector (e.g Morpho Blue).
- Suppose another user or grifter (say Bob) transfers 1e18 of the base token to the contract before Alice tries to withdraw.
- After some time passes and Alice is ready to withdraw about 1.5e18 of their deposit, they call `AccountingManager.withdraw()` which adds their requests to the withdraw queue.
- During the withdraw flow, the vault manager calls `AccountingManager.calculateWithdrawShares()` to compute the amount of tokens to be withdrawn using accounting from the withdraw queue. At some point during the calculation, `previewRedeem()` is called. This does some internal calculation using an equivalent formula above.

Using these values gotten from each parameter:

_shares_ = 1.5e18

_totalAssets()_ = (getTVL() + baseToken.balanceOf(address(vault)) - depositQueue.totalAWFDeposit) = 2e18 + 1e18 - 0 = 3e18

- getTVL() - the vault's TVL (explained above)

- baseToken.balanceOf(address(vault)) - current amount of the base token in vault (i.e. Bob's transferred tokens)

- depositQueue.totalAWFDeposit - Assuming no deposit awaiting execution

_totalSupply()_ = 2e18 (explained above)

$$
\text{assets} = \frac {{1.5^{18}}\times{{3^{18}}}} {{2^{18}}}
$$

This returns approximately 2.25e18 (test case returns 2.249e18). Thus a user that deposits 2e18 and wants to withdraw 1.5e18 of their assets is likely to receive 2.25e18 of the base token which is far more than their initial deposit. If they decide to withdraw the entire 2e18, the calculated amount will be 2.999e18 (approx. 3e18).

---

**Issue 2**

The same issue with `previewRedeem()` (transfer before withdrawal) results in another issue in which retrieving tokens for withdrawal fails and reverts. The calculated withdraw shares return an inflated token amount. This results in incorrect internal accounting of variables and functions that uses this value. Using the same example and values above, the call to [`AccountingManager.retrieveTokensForWithdraw()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/accountingManager/AccountingManager.sol#L547-L573) will revert at the last check. This is because amount asked for withdrawal is greater than the needed assets.

```solidity
    //AccountingManager.sol

    //...
    //This check evaluates to true since amountAskedForWithdraw_temp > neededAssets. This causes a revert when trying to retrieve tokens.
    if (amountAskedForWithdraw_temp > neededAssets) {
        revert NoyaAccounting_INVALID_AMOUNT(); //<-- Call reverts here
    }
    //...
```

A flow of the issue is shown in the code snippets below. The comments describe each step.

```solidity
function calculateWithdrawShares(uint256 maxIterations) public onlyManager nonReentrant whenNotPaused {
        //...
        while (
            //...
        ) {
            i += 1;
            WithdrawRequest storage data = withdrawQueue.queue[middleTemp];
            uint256 assets = previewRedeem(data.shares);    //1. This returns incorrect value for `assets` (from previous example: 2.249e18)
            data.amount = assets;
            data.calculationTime = block.timestamp;
            assetsNeededForWithdraw += assets;  //2. `assets` is added to `assetsNeededForWithdraw`
            processedShares += data.shares;
            emit CalculateWithdraw(middleTemp, data.owner, data.receiver, data.shares, assets, block.timestamp);

            middleTemp += 1;
        }
        currentWithdrawGroup.totalCBAmount += assetsNeededForWithdraw;  //3. `totalCBAmount` -> total amount of the base token awaiting execution. This returns an incorrect `assetsNeededForWithdraw` value (= 2.249e18)
        withdrawQueue.middle = middleTemp;
    }

    //...

    function neededAssetsForWithdraw() public view returns (uint256) {
        uint256 availableAssets = baseToken.balanceOf(address(this)) - depositQueue.totalAWFDeposit;    //4. `availableAssets` is non-zero (1e18 - 0 = 1e18). Recall Bob deposited 1e18 of the base token
        if(currentWithdrawGroup.isStarted == false || currentWithdrawGroup.isFullfilled == true
                || availableAssets >= currentWithdrawGroup.totalCBAmount
        ) {
            return 0;
        }
        return currentWithdrawGroup.totalCBAmount - availableAssets;    //5. If withdraw group has started, this executes.  (Total base token awaiting withdraw execution - available assets) = (2.249e18 - 1e18 = 1.249e18)
    }

```

The call reverts on the last check.

```solidity
function retrieveTokensForWithdraw(RetrieveData[] calldata retrieveData) public onlyManager nonReentrant {
        uint256 amountAskedForWithdraw_temp = 0;
        uint256 neededAssets = neededAssetsForWithdraw();   //6. This returns less assets than intended (= 1.249e18)
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < retrieveData.length; i++) {
            if (!registry.isAnActiveConnector(vaultId, retrieveData[i].connectorAddress)) {
                continue;
            }
            uint256 balanceBefore = baseToken.balanceOf(address(this));
            uint256 amount = IConnector(retrieveData[i].connectorAddress).sendTokensToTrustedAddress(
                address(baseToken), retrieveData[i].withdrawAmount, address(this), retrieveData[i].data
            );
            uint256 balanceAfter = baseToken.balanceOf(address(this));
            if (balanceBefore + amount > balanceAfter) revert NoyaAccounting_banalceAfterIsNotEnough();
            amountAskedForWithdraw_temp += retrieveData[i].withdrawAmount;  //7. Amount retrieved from `retrieveData` is withdrawal amount of the user (Alice wants to withdraw 1.5e18 of the base token)
            emit RetrieveTokensForWithdraw(
                retrieveData[i].withdrawAmount,
                retrieveData[i].connectorAddress,
                amount,
                amountAskedForWithdraw + amountAskedForWithdraw_temp
            );
        }
        amountAskedForWithdraw += amountAskedForWithdraw_temp;
        if (amountAskedForWithdraw_temp > neededAssets) {   //8. This check evaluates to true since 1.5e18 > 1.249e18. This causes a revert when trying to retrieve tokens.
            revert NoyaAccounting_INVALID_AMOUNT();
        }
    }
```
![transfer-before-withdrawal](transfer-before-withdraw.png)

The first issue results in inflated redeem amount for the user trying to withdraw after the transfer. The second issue can lead to DOS for users withdrawing.

## POC

```solidity
pragma solidity 0.8.20;

import "forge-std/Test.sol";
import {IERC20} from "lib/forge-std/src/interfaces/IERC20.sol";

import {AccountingManager, AccountingManagerConstructorParams, RetrieveData} from "contracts/accountingManager/AccountingManager.sol";
import {NoyaValueOracle, INoyaValueOracle} from "contracts/helpers/valueOracle/NoyaValueOracle.sol";
import {PositionRegistry} from "contracts/accountingManager/Registry.sol";
