# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/valueOracle/oracles/ChainlinkOracleConnector.sol#L124


# Vulnerability details

## Summary

**Context:**
Function [getValueFromChainlinkFeed()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/valueOracle/oracles/ChainlinkOracleConnector.sol#L115) in ChainlinkOracleConnector retrieves the price of an asset in terms of a base token from a price feed contract.

**Issue:**
Currently the function [getValueFromChainlinkFeed()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/valueOracle/oracles/ChainlinkOracleConnector.sol#L115) performs a `price <= 0` check to ensure the price returned for the asset is not negative since the price feed returns an int256 variable. The issue is that the int256 price is first typecasting to uint256 and then checked to be greater than equal to 0. This is a problem since if the int256 price variable is negative, typecasting it to uint256 would cause an underflow and return an incorrect value.

**Impact:**
Due to this, the price returned by this oracle is consumed by the connectors and the swap handler.

 - [Connectors](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/tree/main/contracts/connectors) - Uses oracle to retrieve the holding position TVL in terms of the base token. Due to this issue, the TVL would be reported as higher or lower than expected when updating the TVL from normal chains to the base chain.
 - [GenericSwapAndBridgeHandler](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/main/contracts/helpers/SwapHandler/GenericSwapAndBridgeHandler.sol#L90) - Uses oracle to determine the best output token amount, which is used to calculate the minimum amount out for slippage when swapping through LiFi. If the price returned is higher than expected, the minAmountOut is set higher than the swap amount, causing a revert. If the price returned is lower than expected, the minAmountOut is set extremely low, causing loss of funds to the connector and thus overall TVL and users eventually. 

## Proof of Concept
Here is the whole process:

1. The [getValueFromChainlinkFeed()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/9c79b332eff82011dcfa1e8fd51bad805159d758/contracts/helpers/valueOracle/oracles/ChainlinkOracleConnector.sol#L115) function (called through the NoyaValueOracle.sol contract) is ultimately called either through a connector or the GenericSwapAndBridgeHandler contract.
 - On Line 127, the price is retrieved as an int256 variable. This means it is technically possible for it to hold a negative value.
 - On Line 128, we directly perform the uint256 typecast instead of applying the check on Line 133 before.
 - Due to this, the typecast causes an underflow.
 - On Line 138, if isInverse is true, we would round down to 0 mainly since denominator > numerator i.e. uintPrice is extremely high due to the underflow.
 - On Line 144, if isInverse if false, we multiply by uintPrice, thus causing the value returned to be higher than expected.
```solidity
File: ChainlinkOracleConnector.sol
119:     function getValueFromChainlinkFeed(
120:         AggregatorV3Interface source,
121:         uint256 amountIn,
122:         uint256 sourceTokenUnit,
123:         bool isInverse
124:     ) public view returns (uint256) {
125:         int256 price;
126:         uint256 updatedAt;
127:         (, price,, updatedAt,) = source.latestRoundData();
128:         uint256 uintprice = uint256(price);
129:         if (block.timestamp - updatedAt > chainlinkPriceAgeThreshold) {
130:             revert NoyaChainlinkOracle_DATA_OUT_OF_DATE();
131:         }
132:         
133:         if (price <= 0) {
134:             revert NoyaChainlinkOracle_PRICE_ORACLE_UNAVAILABLE(address(source), address(0), address(0));
135:         }
136:         
138:         if (isInverse) {
139:             return (amountIn * sourceTokenUnit) / uintprice;
140:         }
141:         
144:         return (amountIn * uintprice) / (sourceTokenUnit);
145:     }
```

2. As an example, if we take the [StargateConnector.sol](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-noya/blob/main/contracts/connectors/StargateConnector.sol) contract, we can see that it queries the price from the NoyaValueOracle on the last line (which queries the price from ChainlinkOracleConnector). This incorrect price is returned to the OmnichainNormalChainManager that reports it to the base chain as TVL.

```solidity
