The test above shows 1997 USDC is locked in the Accounting Manager contract plus the 10,000 USDC deposited into the connector vault as those cannot be withdrawn because the users who facilitated the deposits, Bob and George both have only 3 shares. Since Bob has a share size of 0, the withdrawal request he makes will revert right away as well as George's.

There is a `rescue` function available for the emergency address to skim tokens off the accounting manager contract but keeping in mind the protocol has a 30-minute deposit waiting time and a call to `calculateDepositShares()` will very much happen every 30 minutes and then immediately followed by `executeDeposit()` transaction call, any attacker can still trigger this issue because even if the protocol does realize the issue a couple of minutes after executing the deposits to the connector vault and using the `rescue()` function, the 1000 USDC deposited into the connector vault cannot be withdrawn because the initiating users got minted 0 shares and even if the protocol does use Alice's pulled USDC to reimburse part of the affected users, the protocol ends up losing the amount in the connector + supplemented asset to wholly reimburse.

## Tools Used
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The issue lies in the way the Accounting Manager tries to mimic 1:1 asset denomination for user deposits according to the data the `ERC4646` `_convertToShares` returns. One way to fix this is for the protocol to treat deposited tokens via the `deposit()` function as the determinant of `totalAsset` by using a variable such as `depositedAssets` and using that for calculation of the shares to mint rather than the `totalAsset()` function/variable which tracks `token.balanceOf(address(this))`














