# Vulnerability details

The [RToken](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-reserve/blob/3f133997e186465f4904553b0f8e86ecb7bbacbf/contracts/p1/RToken.sol) contract implements an issuance and redemption throttle mechanism. This throttle is designed to restrict issuance to an upper bound of a certain percentage of the total supply per hour. However, the current implementation allows for compounding of this percentage, resulting in a significantly higher effective issuance rate than intended.

The issuance throttle is implemented in [`Throttle.useAvailable()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-reserve/blob/3f133997e186465f4904553b0f8e86ecb7bbacbf/contracts/libraries/Throttle.sol#L37), which calculates the maximum amount of tokens that can be issued in the current block. This function updates the available amount based on the time elapsed since the last issuance and the hourly limit.

The core issue lies in the fact that the hourly limit is recalculated based on the current total supply for each issuance, rather than being fixed to the initial supply at the start of the hour. This allows for a compounding effect, where each issuance increases the total supply, which in turn increases the limit for the next issuance.

The issue also affects the redemption throttle, seeing as the underlying implementation is the same, albeit in the opposite direction: redeeming multiple times in an hour will effectively decrease the redemption limit.

## Impact
The compounding effect of the issuance throttle allows for a much higher token issuance rate than intended. This undermines the intended rate-limiting effect of the throttle mechanism and can lead to rapid inflation of the token supply.

## Proof of Concept
The below POC demonstrates the issue as follows:

1. Set the issuance throttle to 10% per hour.
2. Verify that it is not possible to issue more than 10% at once.
3. Simulate issuance every minute for an hour.
4. Show that the final supply increase is more than 21.5%, which is significantly higher than the intended 10% limit.

The test case can be added to the `Issuance` section of `RToken.test.ts`:
```typescript
it.only('Compounds issuance throttle over multiple blocks', async function () {
  // Initial setup
  await Promise.all(tokens.map((t) => t.connect(addr1).approve(rToken.address, initialBal)))
  await rToken.connect(addr1).issue(fp('1000000'))

  const initialSupply = await rToken.totalSupply()

  // Set Issuance Throttle Params to 10% per hour
  const newIssuanceThrottleParams = {
	amtRate: fp('1'), // Set to minimum to not interfere with percentage-based throttling
	pctRate: fp('0.1') // 10% per hour
  }
  await rToken.connect(owner).setIssuanceThrottleParams(newIssuanceThrottleParams)

  // Reset throttling by advancing past an hour
  await advanceTime(60 * 60 + 1)

  // Attempt to issue more than 10%, which should revert
  const moreThan10Percent = initialSupply.mul(11).div(100) // 11% of current supply
  await expect(rToken.connect(addr1).issue(moreThan10Percent))
	.to.be.revertedWith('supply change throttled')

  // Simulate issuance every minute for an hour
  for (let i = 0; i < 60; i++) {
	await advanceTime(60)
	const available = await rToken.issuanceAvailable()
	await rToken.connect(addr1).issue(available)
  }

  // Check final supply and calculate effective rate
  const finalSupply = await rToken.totalSupply()
  const actualIncrease = finalSupply.sub(initialSupply)
  const simpleExpectedIncrease = initialSupply.mul(10).div(100) // Simple 10% increase
  const effectiveHourlyRate = actualIncrease.mul(fp('1')).div(initialSupply)

  // Actual increase is larger than defined
  expect(actualIncrease).to.be.gt(simpleExpectedIncrease)
  // Effective rate is > 20% (!) issuance in 1 hour
  expect(effectiveHourlyRate).to.be.gt(fp('0.215'))
})
```

## Tools Used
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
The issuance throttle should be based on a fixed reference point rather than the continuously updating total supply. Here are two potential approaches:

1. Calculate the hourly limit based on the total supply each time the hour changes since the last call, and use this fixed limit for all issuances within that hour.
2. Implement a sliding window mechanism that tracks the total supply at checkpoints within the past hour and uses the oldest checkpoint to calculate the throttle.



