# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The problem here is that if the user gives approval to the TradingContract via the `setApprovalForAll` , the approval remain forever. Once the `TradingContract` becomes compromised or the owner becomes malicious, all the NFTs of the users could be stolen because the approval isn’t revoked. Even when the transfer is happens, the approval which was given via `setApprovalForAll` isn’t cleared, that’s why the potential bug could occur.

## Proof of Concept

```solidity
function test_maliciousApprovals() public{
            //Step1 - ordinary user mint the nft
            address user = address(111);
            deal(address(user), 1 ether);
            bytes32[] memory arr = new bytes32[](1);
            arr[0] = "0x";
            vm.prank(address(user));
            forgeNft.mintToken{value: 0.2 ether}(arr);

            //Step2 - User list the nft in the EntityTrading and then decide to cancelListing
            vm.startPrank(address(user));
            forgeNft.setApprovalForAll(address(trading), true);
            trading.listNFTForSale(1, 0.1 ether);
            trading.cancelListing(1);
            vm.stopPrank();

            //Step3 - The Contract becomes compromised and it could drain the users
            vm.prank(address(trading));
            forgeNft.transferFrom(address(user), address(trading), 1);

        }
```

## Tools Used

Manual review/Foundry

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Consider clearing the approvals once the user `cancelListing` or the nft is transferred out of the protocol.


