# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-benddao/blob/main/src/PriceOracle.sol#L122-L125


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Lack of the Chainlink aggregator answer staleness validation may result in incorrect assets pricing and losses for the protocol. 

## Proof of Concept
The BendDao protocol uses the `PriceOracle.sol` contract to fetch assets prices. It consists of two oracles, one for ERC20 assets and one for NFTs. Chainlink price feeds are used to receive ERC20 assets prices in the base currency:
```solidity
  function getAssetPriceFromChainlink(address asset) public view returns (uint256) {
    AggregatorV2V3Interface sourceAgg = assetChainlinkAggregators[asset];
    require(address(sourceAgg) != address(0), Errors.ASSET_AGGREGATOR_NOT_EXIST);

    (uint80 roundId, int256 answer, , uint256 updatedAt, uint80 answeredInRound) = sourceAgg.latestRoundData();
    require(answer > 0, Errors.ASSET_PRICE_IS_ZERO);
>>  require(updatedAt != 0, Errors.ORACLE_PRICE_IS_STALE);
    require(answeredInRound >= roundId, Errors.ORACLE_PRICE_IS_STALE);

    return uint256(answer);
  }
```
However, checking that `updated != 0` is not enough, since the `updatedAt` could be greater than zero and still be out of date.

## Tools Used
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider checking that `updatedAt` is in the specified time range:
```solidity
require(updatedAt + timeLimit > block.timestamp, Errors.ORACLE_PRICE_IS_STALE);
```


