# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-optimism/blob/70556044e5e080930f686c4e5acde420104bb2c4/packages/contracts-bedrock/src/dispute/FaultDisputeGame.sol#L453


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
A `fault dispute game` is initialized with an `output root` corresponding to the state of `L2` at a specific `L2 block number`. 
`Execution trace subgames` at `SPLIT_DEPTH + 1` are initialized with a `claim` that commits to the entire `execution trace` between two consecutive `output roots` (a `block n` -> `n+1` state transition).
However, in some cases, the `claim` at `SPLIT_DEPTH + 1` can incorrectly commit to the `execution trace` between the same `block`, i.e., `block n` => `n` state transition. 
This is a really weird situation. 
Additionally, an incorrect disputed `L2 block number` can be saved to the `preimage oracle`.

## Proof of Concept
Let me give an example:
The `SPLIT_DEPTH` is `4`, and the `startingOutputRoot` is `{ l2BlockNumber: 5, root: root }`.
A `bad proposer` submits an invalid `claim` `C` for `block 14`.
The `nodes` at `SPLIT_DEPTH` are `16`, `17`, ..., `31`, and these `nodes` represent individual `blocks`.
`Node 16` represents `block 6`, `node 17` represents `block 7`, and `nodes 24` to `31` represent `block 14`.
An honest `challenger` detects that the `claim` for `block 14` is incorrect and responds with a correct `claim` `D` for `block 13`, moving to `node 2` (`trace index` `7` for `block 13`). 
The `proposer` then `defends` with a correct `claim` `E` for `block 14` and moves to `node 6` (`(2 + 1) * 2`).
If the `challenger` does not make any further `moves`, the invalid `claim` will win. 
Therefore, the `challenger` must `defend` `node 6`, as they can't `attack` it since it's a correct `claim` for `block 14`.
The game then moves to `node 14` (`(6 + 1) * 2`), and the `claim` at this `node` is also the correct `claim` `E` for `block 14`. 
The `proposer` `attacks` `node 14` with the original incorrect `claim` `C` for `block 14` and moves to `node 28` (`14 * 2`).
This means that both players agree on `node 27` but not on `node 28`, even though both `nodes` represent the same `block 14`. 
Additionally, in the `addLocalData` function, the `disputed L2 block number` is incorrectly calculated as `18` instead of `14`. 
```
function addLocalData(uint256 _ident, uint256 _execLeafIdx, uint256 _partOffset) external {
    if (status != GameStatus.IN_PROGRESS) revert GameNotInProgress();
    (Claim starting, Position startingPos, Claim disputed, Position disputedPos) =
        _findStartingAndDisputedOutputs(_execLeafIdx);
    Hash uuid = _computeLocalContext(starting, startingPos, disputed, disputedPos);
    IPreimageOracle oracle = VM.oracle();
    if (_ident == LocalPreimageKey.L1_HEAD_HASH) {
    } else if (_ident == LocalPreimageKey.STARTING_OUTPUT_ROOT) {
    } else if (_ident == LocalPreimageKey.DISPUTED_OUTPUT_ROOT) {
    } else if (_ident == LocalPreimageKey.DISPUTED_L2_BLOCK_NUMBER) {
        uint256 l2Number = startingOutputRoot.l2BlockNumber + disputedPos.traceIndex(SPLIT_DEPTH) + 1; // @audit, here

        oracle.loadLocalData(_ident, uuid.raw(), bytes32(l2Number << 0xC0), 8, _partOffset);
    } else if (_ident == LocalPreimageKey.CHAIN_ID) {
        oracle.loadLocalData(_ident, uuid.raw(), bytes32(L2_CHAIN_ID << 0xC0), 8, _partOffset);
    } else {
        revert InvalidLocalIdent();
    }
}
```
These incorrect values saved to the `preimage oracle` will be used in the `step` function in `MIPS`.
## Tools Used

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Prevent users from making different `claims` for the same `block` by checking the `trace index`. 
Additionally, in the `addLocalData` function, upload the `minimum` between the calculated `disputed L2 block number` and the `proposed L2 block number`.


