# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Users will not be able to get tokens more than 3e18 while whitelisting system is on

## Proof of Concept

When whitelisting is on users have to pass a special check while transfering tokens:

```solidity
    function _beforeTokenTransfer(address from, address to, uint256 amount) internal override {
        if (_whitelistContract != address(0)) {
@>            IWhitelist(_whitelistContract).checkWhitelist(from, to, amount);
        }
        super._beforeTokenTransfer(from, to, amount);
    }


    function checkWhitelist(address from, address to, uint256 amount) external onlyVultisig {
        if (from == _pool && to != owner()) {
            ...

            // // Calculate rough ETH amount for VULT amount
            uint256 estimatedETHAmount = IOracle(_oracle).peek(amount);
@>            if (_contributed[to] + estimatedETHAmount > _maxAddressCap) {
                revert MaxAddressCapOverflow();
            }

            _contributed[to] += estimatedETHAmount;
        }
    }
```

`_maxAddressCap` is set in constructor and equals to 3 ether:

```solidity
    constructor() {
        _maxAddressCap = 3 ether;
        _locked = true;
    }
```

If this option remains unchenged users will not be able to receive tokens with more than 3 ether.

I guess, if I transfer tokens to the Vault it may be ok for a protocol system to keep a restriction, but what if a user wants to transfer to his another account? 

## Tools Used
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
I guess the best option is to check whether the transfer is to Vault or to user, and remove the check for the second option.


