# Vulnerability details

## Impact
A malicious subject creator can DoS all of his/her buyers and prevent them selling their subject tokens.

## Proof of Concept
Token creators create their Curves subject tokens in this protocol, and users can buy other people's subject tokens.

At the moment there is not restriction in terms of a subject token address being a contract or EOA. Alice can create a subject token with her `alice.eth` EAO account and her friends might buy a Curves token with Alice's subject. However, another user can do the same with a contract address and this user might want to act malicious.

I'll create a scenario below but now let's check the `sellCurvesToken` function.  
[https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-curves/blob/516aedb7b9a8d341d0d2666c23780d2bd8a9a600/contracts/Curves.sol#L282C1-L293C6](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-curves/blob/516aedb7b9a8d341d0d2666c23780d2bd8a9a600/contracts/Curves.sol#L282C1-L293C6)

```solidity
    function sellCurvesToken(address curvesTokenSubject, uint256 amount) public {
        uint256 supply = curvesTokenSupply[curvesTokenSubject];
        if (supply <= amount) revert LastTokenCannotBeSold();
        if (curvesTokenBalance[curvesTokenSubject][msg.sender] < amount) revert InsufficientBalance();

        uint256 price = getPrice(supply - amount, amount);

        curvesTokenBalance[curvesTokenSubject][msg.sender] -= amount;
        curvesTokenSupply[curvesTokenSubject] = supply - amount;

-->     _transferFees(curvesTokenSubject, false, price, amount, supply);
    }
```

It performs some check, decreases user balance & total supply, and the calls the `_transferFees` function, which is the function that transfers not only fees but also the selling income. Here is the [\_transferFees](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-curves/blob/516aedb7b9a8d341d0d2666c23780d2bd8a9a600/contracts/Curves.sol#L218C1-L261C6) function:

```solidity
    function _transferFees(
        address curvesTokenSubject,
        bool isBuy,
        uint256 price,
        uint256 amount,
        uint256 supply
    ) internal {
        (uint256 protocolFee, uint256 subjectFee, uint256 referralFee, uint256 holderFee, ) = getFees(price);
        {
            bool referralDefined = referralFeeDestination[curvesTokenSubject] != address(0);
            {
                address firstDestination = isBuy ? feesEconomics.protocolFeeDestination : msg.sender;
                uint256 buyValue = referralDefined ? protocolFee : protocolFee + referralFee;
                uint256 sellValue = price - protocolFee - subjectFee - referralFee - holderFee;
-->             (bool success1, ) = firstDestination.call{value: isBuy ? buyValue : sellValue}(""); //@audit-info transfers income to the seller. 
                if (!success1) revert CannotSendFunds();
            }
            {
-->             (bool success2, ) = curvesTokenSubject.call{value: subjectFee}(""); //@audit transfers subjectFee to the subject. What if subject always reverts?
                if (!success2) revert CannotSendFunds();
            }

        // skipped for brevity.

    }
```

As we can see above, this function both transfers the income to the seller and the `subjectFee` to the subject address.

As I mentioned above, the subject address might be a contract. It is not enforced to be an EOA. If that contract reverts, users can never sell their Curves tokens.

**Attack scenario:**

Let's imagine a crypto celebrity (Alice) with hundreds of thousands of followers on Twitter. She decides to join Curves.

1. Alice announces she joined the Curves and will sell her subject tokens.

2. She says she will not use her daily account for this because this is some kind of fun experiment or business.

3. She says to her thousands of followers she will use a smart contract account for this.

4. She creates an upgradeable smart contract.

5. Initial implementation of this contract works perfectly fine. Users check the smart contract but don't suspect anything because the implementation is not malicious.

6. Buying/selling begins and everyone seamlessly perform actions. Everything works fine.

7. Then, she changes the implementation.  
    The upgraded contract can not accept ether. No one can sell tokens.

8. If she wants to sell tokens herself, she performs multicall.  
    Upgrades the contract to normal version, sells her tokens, and upgrades to the malicious version again.


One can argue that this is user mistake and people should not invest in crypto without knowing. However, this is not solely a user mistake or a user being reckless. The main issue here is allowing smart contracts to be subject, and transferring both selling price and the creator fee in the same transaction. 

## Tools Used
Manual review.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
One option is not allowing contract addresses to be a subject.

The other option is that the protocol should store users' selling prices, and accumulated fees for subjects and referrals as separate variables instead of transferring all of them via external calls in one single transaction. Users and fee earners should withdraw their stored amount of incomes separately.


