# Vulnerability details


## Impact

Pyth price feed currently fetches the value of baseToken with a 5 minute time threshold. However, this is too short for some of the tokens/chains that the Predy protocol supports, and would cause the price feed to revert.

## Bug Description

If the `priceFeed` field is set for a PairStatus, when calculating the vaule of a userPosition (vault), it will use the baseToken/quoteToken value from `priceFeed` oracle.

The `PriceFeed.sol` implementation uses Pyth Oracle to calculate the value for the baseToken. However, it uses the `getPriceNoOlderThan()` API with a 5 minute threshold. There are several issues with this:

1. For some of the tokens/chains that the Predy protcol supports, the price feed has been out of date for a long time. An example is the DAI/USD on BASE chain. The price feed contract for BASE is `0x8250f4aF4B972684F7b336503E2D6dFeDeB1487a` (can be found [here](https://docs.pyth.network/price-feeds/contract-addresses/evm)), and the id for DAI/USD is `0xb0948a5e5313200c632b51bb5ca32f6de0d36e9950a942d19751e833f70dabfd` (can be found [here](https://pyth.network/developers/price-feed-ids)). We can look up id on [basescan](https://basescan.org/address/0x8250f4aF4B972684F7b336503E2D6dFeDeB1487a#readProxyContract), and currently the latest update timestamp for DAI/USD is 1691959366, which is 10 months ago. Calling `getPriceNoOlderThan()` with a 5 minute threshold would end up with a revert.

2. Looking up the Pyth docs https://docs.pyth.network/price-feeds/api-reference/evm/get-price-no-older-than, we can know that the price feed is not periodically updated onchain, but users need to call `updatePriceFeeds()` to manually update it. However, no such mechanism is mentioned for the Predy protocol.

> This function reverts with a PriceFeedNotFound error if the requested feed id has never received a price update. This error could either mean that the provided price feed id is incorrect, or (more typically) that this is the first attempted use of that feed on-chain. In the second case, calling updatePriceFeeds will solve this problem.

Concluding the above, using a 5 minute threshold for Pyth oracle and not explicitly calling `updatePriceFeeds()` beforehand would likely cause a revert, especially for tokens/chains that aren't as popular.

