As mentioned earlier, Chainlink's price aggregators have pre-determined `minAnswer` and `maxAnswer` price ranges they will return. In a flash crash event, an asset price can fall significantly below the pre-determined `minAnswer`. However, the aggregator will continue to report its `minAnswer`. For more, refer to the case of [Venus Protocol and Blizz Finance in the crash of LUNA](https://rekt.news/venus-blizz-rekt/).

For example, the following lists some assets the protocol supports (see [docs](https://dittoeth.com/technical/oracles#oracles)) and their pre-defined `minAnswer`/`maxAnswer` (represented by 8 decimals precision).
- The `XAU/USD` price aggregator has the `minAnswer` == [10000000000](https://etherscan.io/address/0xea5f70faa03f5C30B96029635C8d431d1A3cD1b8#readContract#F19) and `maxAnswer` == [1000000000000](https://etherscan.io/address/0xea5f70faa03f5C30B96029635C8d431d1A3cD1b8#readContract#F18).
- The `EUR/USD` price aggregator has the `minAnswer` == [10000000](https://etherscan.io/address/0x02F878A94a1AE1B15705aCD65b5519A46fe3517e#readContract#F19) and `maxAnswer` == [10000000000](https://etherscan.io/address/0x02F878A94a1AE1B15705aCD65b5519A46fe3517e#readContract#F18).
- The `JPY/USD` price aggregator has the `minAnswer` == [100000](https://etherscan.io/address/0x01A1F73b1f4726EB6EB189FFA5CBB91AF8E14025#readContract#F19) and `maxAnswer` == [10000000](https://etherscan.io/address/0x01A1F73b1f4726EB6EB189FFA5CBB91AF8E14025#readContract#F18).

I noticed that the `getOraclePrice()` does not detect such a flash crash event. If the market crashes, the `price` and/or `basePrice` reported by Chainlink can be higher than the actual asset price (below the `minAnswer`). 

As a result, the incorrectly reported `price` or `basePrice` leads to miscalculating the `priceInEth`.

### 3. Lack of Detecting Chainlink Aggregators' `minAnswer` and `maxAnswer`

This sub-section shows that the `getOraclePrice()` does not detect or handle the Chainlink aggregators' `minAnswer` and `maxAnswer`.

After calculating the `priceInEth`, the [third](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-dittoeth/blob/91faf46078bb6fe8ce9f55bcb717e5d2d302d22e/contracts/libraries/LibOracle.sol#L33-L46) code path of the `getOraclePrice()` validates the obtained `price` and `basePrice` by invoking the [`oracleCircuitBreaker()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-dittoeth/blob/91faf46078bb6fe8ce9f55bcb717e5d2d302d22e/contracts/libraries/LibOracle.sol#L44) (see `@3.1` below). 

As you can see, the `oracleCircuitBreaker()` does not verify [both price variables](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-dittoeth/blob/91faf46078bb6fe8ce9f55bcb717e5d2d302d22e/contracts/libraries/LibOracle.sol#L125-L126) against their corresponding aggregators' `minAnswer` or `maxAnswer` (see `@3.2`). Therefore, the function cannot be aware of the flash crash events.

