# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The LibOracle library contains a hardcoded conversion factor that assumes Chainlink oracles always return price data with 8 decimal places. This assumption leads to an inflexible design that may not accurately reflect prices if the Chainlink oracle's decimal precision changes. In the context of financial and trading applications, incorrect price data can lead to significant financial inaccuracies, mispricing of assets, unintended liquidations, or arbitrage opportunities, potentially causing users to experience financial loss or erode trust in the platform. The severity of this issue is rated as Medium because, while the likelihood of `basePrice` getting its 8 decimal places altered by Chainlink is low, the impact is high. Such a change would not be reflected in the contract's calculations, leading to incorrect conversions and potential financial discrepancies.

## Proof of Concept
In the try logic of `getOraclePrice()` below, the returned `basePrice` multiplies `C.BASE_ORACLE_DECIMALS`, i.e. [10**10](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-dittoeth/blob/main/contracts/libraries/Constants.sol#L66-L68), statically normalizing `basePrice` from 8 decimals to 18 decimals prior to getting it inverted. This implementation does not account for potential variations in the decimal precision of the Chainlink oracle.

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-dittoeth//blob/main/contracts/libraries/LibOracle.sol#L27-L32

```solidity
        try baseOracle.latestRoundData() returns (uint80 baseRoundID, int256 basePrice, uint256, uint256 baseTimeStamp, uint80) {
            if (oracle == baseOracle) {
                // @dev multiply base oracle by 10**10 to give it 18 decimals of precision
                uint256 basePriceInEth = basePrice > 0 ? uint256(basePrice * C.BASE_ORACLE_DECIMALS).inv() : 0;
                basePriceInEth = baseOracleCircuitBreaker(protocolPrice, baseRoundID, basePrice, baseTimeStamp, basePriceInEth);
                return basePriceInEth;
```
This conversion assumes that the decimal precision of the Chainlink oracle is always 8, which might not hold true for `basePrice` in the future, should Chainlink or the specific oracle used change its decimal precision.

Let's dive into the verified contract of ETH / USD price feed. As seen in the current code snippet, decimals() of the price feed contract is dependent on currentPhase.aggregator.decimals():

https://etherscan.io/address/0x5f4eC3Df9cbd43714FE2740f5E3616155c5b8419#code#L431

```solidity
  /**
   * @notice represents the number of decimals the aggregator responses represent.
   */
  function decimals()
    external
    view
    override
    returns (uint8)
  {
    return currentPhase.aggregator.decimals();
  }
```
And notably, the contract owner is allowed to propose and confirm/change the aggregator:

https://etherscan.io/address/0x5f4eC3Df9cbd43714FE2740f5E3616155c5b8419#code#L481

```solidity
  /**
   * @notice Allows the owner to propose a new address for the aggregator
   * @param _aggregator The new address for the aggregator contract
   */
  function proposeAggregator(address _aggregator)
    external
    onlyOwner()
  {
    proposedAggregator = AggregatorV2V3Interface(_aggregator);
  }

  /**
   * @notice Allows the owner to confirm and change the address
   * to the proposed aggregator
   * @dev Reverts if the given address doesn't match what was previously
   * proposed
   * @param _aggregator The new address for the aggregator contract
   */
  function confirmAggregator(address _aggregator)
    external
    onlyOwner()
  {
    require(_aggregator == address(proposedAggregator), "Invalid proposed aggregator");
    delete proposedAggregator;
    setAggregator(_aggregator);
  }
```
Consequently, `currentPhase` will be changed by invoking `setAggregator()` in the code block above:

https://etherscan.io/address/0x5f4eC3Df9cbd43714FE2740f5E3616155c5b8419#code#L495

```solidity
  function setAggregator(address _aggregator)
    internal
  {
    uint16 id = currentPhase.id + 1;
    currentPhase = Phase(id, AggregatorV2V3Interface(_aggregator));
    phaseAggregators[id] = AggregatorV2V3Interface(_aggregator);
  }
```
Supposing after the change, `decimals()` returns 18 instead of the previous 8, this is going to make the price of ETH in USD `10**10` more and `getOraclePrice()` will have to permanently depend on the integrity of the fallback Uniswap `TWAP`. 

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-dittoeth/blob/main/contracts/libraries/LibOracle.sol#L80-L91

```solidity
        bool priceDeviation = protocolPrice > 0 && chainlinkDiff.div(protocolPrice) > 0.5 ether;

        // @dev if there is issue with chainlink, get twap price. Verify twap and compare with chainlink
        if (invalidFetchData) {
            return twapCircuitBreaker();
        } else if (priceDeviation) {
            // Check valid twap price
            try IDiamond(payable(address(this))).estimateWETHInUSDC(C.UNISWAP_WETH_BASE_AMT, 30 minutes) returns (uint256 twapPrice)
            {
                if (twapPrice == 0) {
                    return chainlinkPriceInEth;
                }
```

And if `twapPrice == 0`, the severely deflated `chainlinkPriceInEth` will be returned messing up with the accounting logic on all calling functions from other contracts dependent on `getOraclePrice()`, specifically when `LibOrders._updateOracleAndStartingShort()` will have [s.bids[asset][C.HEAD].ercAmount = uint80(oraclePrice)](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-dittoeth/blob/main/contracts/libraries/LibOracle.sol#L151) so erroneously set. 

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-dittoeth/blob/main/contracts/libraries/LibOrders.sol#L728-L735

```solidity
    function _updateOracleAndStartingShort(address asset, uint16[] memory shortHintArray) private {
        AppStorage storage s = appStorage();
        uint256 oraclePrice = LibOracle.getOraclePrice(asset);
        uint256 savedPrice = asset.getPrice();
        asset.setPriceAndTime(oraclePrice, getOffsetTime());
        if (oraclePrice == savedPrice) {
            return; // no need to update startingShortId
        }
```

## Tools Used
Manual

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
To mitigate this issue and enhance the contract's flexibility and accuracy in handling price data from Chainlink oracles, it is recommended to dynamically fetch the oracle's decimal precision at runtime and adjust the conversion factor accordingly. The `getOraclePrice` function should be modified to include a step where it queries the Chainlink oracle for its current decimal precision and calculates the conversion factor based on this value. Here is a suggested implementation:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-dittoeth/blob/main/contracts/libraries/LibOracle.sol#L30

```diff
-                uint256 basePriceInEth = basePrice > 0 ? uint256(basePrice * C.BASE_ORACLE_DECIMALS).inv() : 0;
+                uint256 basePriceInEth = basePrice > 0 ? uint256(basePrice * (18 - baseOracle.decimals()).inv() : 0;
```








