```
  function estimateTWAP(uint128 amountIn, uint32 secondsAgo, address pool, address baseToken, address quoteToken)
        internal
        view
        returns (uint256 amountOut)
    {
        if (secondsAgo <= 0) revert Errors.InvalidTWAPSecondsAgo();

        uint32[] memory secondsAgos = new uint32[](2);
        secondsAgos[0] = secondsAgo;
        secondsAgos[1] = 0;

        // @dev Returns the cumulative tick and liquidity as of each timestamp secondsAgo from the current block timestamp
        (int56[] memory tickCumulatives,) = IUniswapV3Pool(pool).observe(secondsAgos);

        int56 tickCumulativesDelta = tickCumulatives[1] - tickCumulatives[0];
        int24 tick = int24(tickCumulativesDelta / int32(secondsAgo));  <----@audit

        // Always round to negative infinity
        if (tickCumulativesDelta < 0 && (tickCumulativesDelta % int32(secondsAgo) != 0)) {
            tick--;
        }

        // @dev Gets price using this formula: p(i) = 1.0001**i, where i is the tick
        amountOut = getQuoteAtTick(tick, amountIn, baseToken, quoteToken);
```
The estimate Twap is used to get the Time weighed price which was forked from [uniswapOracleLibrary](https://github.com/Uniswap/v3-periphery/blob/main/contracts/libraries/OracleLibrary.sol) but there is slight difference in the tick calculation which can lead precision loss. High CVD values at specific levels can indicate significant buying and selling interest. By looking at the cumulative bars, one can gauge the intensity of buying or selling selling aggression.

> Rapidly increasing positive bars may signal strong buying interest, while sharp negative bars indicate strong selling interest.
An exhaustion signal occurs when the indicator reaches an extreme level, indicating a possible overbought oversold condition. 

*In rare cases*
***If the cumulative delta reaches a very high positive value, this means that buyers are overextended and may run out of steam soon.***

Delta always the change between 2 values.
Delta y = y1 - y0
y is the change


***Consider a scenario where secondsAgo is set to 1800 (30 minutes). If tickCumulativesDelta is a large positive or negative value, such as 2^30 (1073741824), the division operation tickCumulativesDelta / int32(secondsAgo) would result in a significant loss of precision due to the data type conversion. This loss of precision could lead to inaccuracies in the calculated tick value, affecting the reliability of the Oracle data.***


