# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-panoptic/blob/833312ebd600665b577fbd9c03ffa0daf250ed24/contracts/PanopticPool.sol#L905-L946


# Vulnerability details

## Description
The Uniswap V3 pool observations used in the fast and slow oracle tick calculations may attempt to influence the observed prices by strategically placing trades or manipulating the pool's liquidity.

   The `computeMedianObservedPrice` function relies on a fixed number of observations (`FAST_ORACLE_CARDINALITY` and `SLOW_ORACLE_CARDINALITY`) and a fixed period between observations (`FAST_ORACLE_PERIOD` and `SLOW_ORACLE_PERIOD`). If these constants are not carefully chosen, an attacker may be able to manipulate the median price by influencing a significant portion of the observations.

   Additionally, the `MAX_SLOW_FAST_DELTA` threshold is used to determine whether to check the solvency at both the fast and slow oracle ticks. An attacker may try to exploit this threshold by manipulating the price to keep the difference between the fast and slow oracle ticks just below the threshold, potentially maintaining an insolvent position without being detected by the slow oracle tick check.

LoC: https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-panoptic/blob/833312ebd600665b577fbd9c03ffa0daf250ed24/contracts/PanopticPool.sol#L905-L946
```solidity
        int24 fastOracleTick = PanopticMath.computeMedianObservedPrice(
            _univ3pool,
            observationIndex,
            observationCardinality,
            FAST_ORACLE_CARDINALITY,
            FAST_ORACLE_PERIOD
        );


        int24 slowOracleTick;
        if (SLOW_ORACLE_UNISWAP_MODE) {
            slowOracleTick = PanopticMath.computeMedianObservedPrice(
                _univ3pool,
                observationIndex,
                observationCardinality,
                SLOW_ORACLE_CARDINALITY,
                SLOW_ORACLE_PERIOD
            );
        } else {
            (slowOracleTick, medianData) = PanopticMath.computeInternalMedian(
                observationIndex,
                observationCardinality,
                MEDIAN_PERIOD,
                s_miniMedian,
                _univ3pool
            );
        }


        // Check the user's solvency at the fast tick; revert if not solvent
        bool solventAtFast = _checkSolvencyAtTick(
            user,
            positionIdList,
            currentTick,
            fastOracleTick,
            buffer
        );
        if (!solventAtFast) revert Errors.NotEnoughCollateral();


        // If one of the ticks is too stale, we fall back to the more conservative tick, i.e, the user must be solvent at both the fast and slow oracle ticks.
        if (Math.abs(int256(fastOracleTick) - slowOracleTick) > MAX_SLOW_FAST_DELTA)
            if (!_checkSolvencyAtTick(user, positionIdList, currentTick, slowOracleTick, buffer))
                revert Errors.NotEnoughCollateral();
    }
```
User may be able to manipulate the fast and slow oracle ticks by influencing the Uniswap V3 pool observations. By strategically placing trades or manipulating the pool's liquidity could potentially bypass the solvency checks and maintain insolvent positions. If the attacker succeeds in keeping the difference between the fast and slow oracle ticks below the MAX_SLOW_FAST_DELTA threshold, they may be able to avoid triggering the slow oracle tick check, which could have detected their insolvent position.

## Impact
It may allow users to maintain insolvent positions without being detected by the protocol's solvency checks. This could lead to increased risk for the protocol and its users, as insolvent positions may not be properly liquidated or managed. 

## Tools Used
Manual audit

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider the following measures carefully choose the values for the `FAST_ORACLE_CARDINALITY`, `FAST_ORACLE_PERIOD`, `SLOW_ORACLE_CARDINALITY`, and `SLOW_ORACLE_PERIOD` constants based on a thorough analysis of the system's requirements and potential attack scenarios.

