# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-panoptic/blob/833312ebd600665b577fbd9c03ffa0daf250ed24/contracts/CollateralTracker.sol#L350
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-panoptic/blob/833312ebd600665b577fbd9c03ffa0daf250ed24/contracts/tokens/ERC20Minimal.sol#L49-L55


# Vulnerability details

## Summary
In the context of `ERC-20 tokens`, approvals are a mechanism that allows one address (`spender`) to spend tokens from another address (`owner`) up to a specified limit. This approval is typically set by the `owner` and can be adjusted or revoked as needed.

As per the [protocol](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-panoptic/blob/833312ebd600665b577fbd9c03ffa0daf250ed24/contracts/CollateralTracker.sol#L346-L348), if a user has `open positions`, their `tokens`(assets) are essentially locked as `collateral` until those positions are closed or settled. If they were to transfer tokens away while their collateral is still committed, it could lead to `under-collateralization`.

However, this DoS'es approved users who may not have knowledge of the `owner`'s open option positions.

## Proof of Concept

### Scenario:
1. `Approval Mechanism`: 
Initially, the `owner` of the `shares`, (`User A`) approves another user (`User B`) to spend a certain amount of tokens on their behalf using the [approve()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-panoptic/blob/833312ebd600665b577fbd9c03ffa0daf250ed24/contracts/tokens/ERC20Minimal.sol#L49-L55) function.

```solidity
    function approve(address spender, uint256 amount) public returns (bool) {
        allowance[msg.sender][spender] = amount;


        emit Approval(msg.sender, spender, amount);


        return true;
    }
```
This approval allows `User B` to call the `transferFrom()` function to transfer `shares` from `User A`'s account to another account, up to the approved limit.

2. `Unawareness of Open Positions`: 
`User B`, who has been approved to spend tokens by `User A`, might not be aware of whether `User A` has any `open positions` in the protocol.
3. `Attempted Transfer by User B`: 
`User B`, with the `approval` granted by `User A`, attempts to transfer shares from `User A`'s account to another account using the [transferFrom()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-panoptic/blob/833312ebd600665b577fbd9c03ffa0daf250ed24/contracts/CollateralTracker.sol#L341-L353) function.

```solidity
    function transferFrom(
        address from,
        address to,
        uint256 amount
    ) public override(ERC20Minimal) returns (bool) {
        // make sure the caller does not have any open option positions
        // if they do: we don't want them sending panoptic pool shares to others
        // as this would reduce their amount of collateral against the opened positions


        if (s_panopticPool.numberOfPositions(from) != 0) revert Errors.PositionCountNotZero();


        return ERC20Minimal.transferFrom(from, to, amount);
    }
```

Since `User B` is unaware of `User A`'s open positions, they proceed with the transfer assuming it will succeed.

4. `Transaction Reverted`: 
However, when the `transferFrom()` function is called, the code checks whether `User A` has any open positions.

```solidity
if (s_panopticPool.numberOfPositions(from) != 0) revert Errors.PositionCountNotZero();
```

If `User A` does have open positions, the transaction is reverted, and an error is returned. This is because allowing the transfer could potentially harm `User A`'s ability to maintain their positions adequately, leading to risks such as under-collateralization or liquidation.

#### Coded PoC
To reproduce this, locate the test already implemented by the Dev team inside `test/foundry/core/CollateralTracker.t.sol` and run the test in foundry with the following command:
`forge test --match-test -vvv test_Fail_transferFrom_positionCountNotZero`

([Here is the source](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-panoptic/blob/833312ebd600665b577fbd9c03ffa0daf250ed24/test/foundry/core/CollateralTracker.t.sol#L1052-L1111))

```solidity
    // transferFrom fail Errors.PositionCountNotZero()
    function test_Fail_transferFrom_positionCountNotZero(
        uint256 x,
        uint256 widthSeed,
        int256 strikeSeed,
        uint128 positionSizeSeed
    ) public {
        _initWorld(x);


        // Invoke all interactions with the Collateral Tracker from user Bob
        vm.startPrank(Bob);


        // give Bob the max amount of tokens
        _grantTokens(Bob);


        // approve collateral tracker to move tokens on Bob's behalf
        IERC20Partial(token0).approve(address(collateralToken0), type(uint256).max);
        IERC20Partial(token1).approve(address(collateralToken1), type(uint256).max);


        // award corresponding shares
        _mockMaxDeposit(Bob);


        {
            // call will be minted in range
            (width, strike) = PositionUtils.getOTMSW(
                widthSeed,
                strikeSeed,
                uint24(tickSpacing),
                currentTick,
                1
            );


            // sell as Bob
            tokenId = TokenId.wrap(0).addPoolId(poolId).addLeg(0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0, strike, width);
            positionIdList.push(tokenId);


            positionSize0 = uint128(bound(positionSizeSeed, 2, 2 ** 104));
            _assumePositionValidity(Bob, tokenId, positionSize0);


            panopticPool.mintOptions(positionIdList, positionSize0, 0, 0, 0);
        }


        // approve Alice to move shares on Bob's behalf
        IERC20Partial(address(collateralToken0)).approve(Alice, type(uint256).max);
        IERC20Partial(address(collateralToken1)).approve(Alice, type(uint256).max);


        uint256 bal0 = collateralToken0.balanceOf(Bob);
        uint256 bal1 = collateralToken1.balanceOf(Bob);


        // redeem from Alice on behalf of Bob
        vm.startPrank(Alice);


        // Check if test reverted
        vm.expectRevert(Errors.PositionCountNotZero.selector);
        collateralToken0.transferFrom(Bob, Alice, bal0);


        // Check if test reverted
        vm.expectRevert(Errors.PositionCountNotZero.selector);
        collateralToken1.transferFrom(Bob, Alice, bal1);
    }
```

## Impact
Denial of Service (DoS) Risk: 
As a result of the transaction being reverted, `User B`'s attempt to transfer tokens is unsuccessful. This will remain so until when the `owner, User A` chooses to close all their positions.

During this time, all `approvals` will remain useless. 


## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
`Conditional Approvals`: 
Implement a mechanism where `approvals` granted by the owner (`User A`) to the approved user (`User B`) are conditional upon the `absence of open positions`. When `User A` grants `approval`, the smart contract checks for `any open positions associated with User A's account`. 

If open positions exist, the `approval` is either not granted or automatically revoked until the positions are closed.

















