Oracle

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/519e518f2d8dec9acf6482b84a181e403070d22d/contracts/Bridge/L2/xRenzoDeposit.sol#L330-L359
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/519e518f2d8dec9acf6482b84a181e403070d22d/contracts/Bridge/L2/xRenzoDeposit.sol#L310-L313


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The updatePrice function in the xRenzoDeposit contract plays a critical role in determining the price of `ezETH`, which subsequently influences the minting rate of the `xezETH` tokens. The function currently checks for price deviations greater than 10% compared to the last updated price. While this check intends to safeguard against drastic fluctuations and potential manipulations, it might not work in a highly volatile market or against sophisticated manipulation tactics. 
Sophisticated attackers might exploit this threshold by structuring price manipulations in increments just below 10%, gradually but consistently pushing the price to an erroneous value. Over time, these small, "acceptable" changes could accumulate, significantly deviating from the true market price without ever triggering the safeguard. 


## Proof of Concept

Github Links: https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/519e518f2d8dec9acf6482b84a181e403070d22d/contracts/Bridge/L2/xRenzoDeposit.sol#L330-L359

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/519e518f2d8dec9acf6482b84a181e403070d22d/contracts/Bridge/L2/xRenzoDeposit.sol#L310-L313

Here's a breakdown of the specific components within the code that relate to the vulnerability:


   function updatePrice(uint256 _price, uint256 _timestamp) external override {
       if (msg.sender != receiver) revert InvalidSender(receiver, msg.sender);
       _updatePrice(_price, _timestamp);
   }


   function _updatePrice(uint256 _price, uint256 _timestamp) internal {
       if (_price == 0) {
           revert InvalidZeroInput();
       }

       // Check for price divergence - more than 10%
       if ((_price > lastPrice && (_price - lastPrice) > (lastPrice / 10)) ||
           (_price < lastPrice && (lastPrice - _price) > (lastPrice / 10))) {
           revert InvalidOraclePrice();
       }

       if (_timestamp <= lastPriceTimestamp) {
           revert InvalidTimestamp(_timestamp);
       }

       if (_timestamp > block.timestamp) {
           revert InvalidTimestamp(_timestamp);
       }

       lastPrice = _price;
       lastPriceTimestamp = _timestamp;
       emit PriceUpdated(_price, _timestamp);
   }


The function checks if the new price `_price` is zero, which correctly prevents disruptive zero pricing. It then validates the price against a fixed 10% deviation threshold, which might not suit all market conditions.

## Tools Used
Visual Studio Code.
Manual Inspection.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Introduce a dynamic deviation threshold based on historical volatility data or a moving average of past price updates. This approach allows the contract to adjust its sensitivity based on market conditions.



## Assessed type

Other

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Withdraw/WithdrawQueue.sol#L206


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The withdraw method in WithdrawQueue accepts the assetOut address 

and asset amount to place order for .

However , it fails to account for slippage scenarios due to calculating of assetOut 

amount to be returned by an oracle and the prices returned by oracles vary 

plus there can be scenarios when the transactions are just delayed due to network 

congestion or other factors which might lead users to placing orders of receiving 

less assetOut than anticipated at the time of transaction submission .


## Proof of Concept

Here is the code for withdraw 

```solidity
 function withdraw(uint256 _amount, address _assetOut) external nonReentrant {
        // check for 0 values
        if (_amount == 0 || _assetOut == address(0)) revert InvalidZeroInput();

        // check if provided assetOut is supported
        if (withdrawalBufferTarget[_assetOut] == 0) revert UnsupportedWithdrawAsset();

        // transfer ezETH tokens to this address
        IERC20(address(ezETH)).safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), _amount);

        // calculate totalTVL
        (, , uint256 totalTVL) = restakeManager.calculateTVLs();

        // Calculate amount to Redeem in ETH
        uint256 amountToRedeem = renzoOracle.calculateRedeemAmount(
            _amount,
            ezETH.totalSupply(),
            totalTVL
        );

        // update amount in claim asset, if claim asset is not ETH
        if (_assetOut != IS_NATIVE) {
            // Get ERC20 asset equivalent amount
            amountToRedeem = renzoOracle.lookupTokenAmountFromValue(
                IERC20(_assetOut),
                amountToRedeem
            );
        }

        // revert if amount to redeem is greater than withdrawBufferTarget
        if (amountToRedeem > getAvailableToWithdraw(_assetOut)) revert NotEnoughWithdrawBuffer();

        // increment the withdrawRequestNonce
        withdrawRequestNonce++;

        // add withdraw request for msg.sender
        withdrawRequests[msg.sender].push(
            WithdrawRequest(
                _assetOut,
                withdrawRequestNonce,
                amountToRedeem,
                _amount,
                block.timestamp
            )
        );

        // add redeem amount to claimReserve of claim asset
        claimReserve[_assetOut] += amountToRedeem;

        emit WithdrawRequestCreated(
            withdrawRequestNonce,
            msg.sender,
            _assetOut,
            amountToRedeem,
            _amount,
            withdrawRequests[msg.sender].length - 1
        );
    }

```

Take following scenario .

1. Alice submits a transaction of withdraw amount 100 input tokens with current tokenA being returned , according to market value , to be 100 
2. The transaction gets delayed due to several factors i.e network congestion.
3. When the the transaction is executed , the price has surged and now the exchange 

of 100 input tokens to 5 output tokens.

4. The transaction gets executed and Alice incurs a loss worth of 95 output tokens.

The loss escalates when the price surge is high and a lot of assetOut is involved

like 2 billion.

Due to lack of minimum amount out and deadline parameter withdraw 

the user has incurred a significant loss of out tokens.

## Tools Used
Manual review
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Add slippage parameters like `minAmountOut` and `deadline`

and only initiates withdrawal requests if 

`amountToBeRedeemed  >= minAmountOut`

and 

`block.timestamp<=deadline`

otherwise, revert the withdraw order.





