# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Oracle/RenzoOracle.sol#L26
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-renzo/blob/main/contracts/Bridge/L2/Oracle/RenzoOracleL2.sol#L13


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

`RenzoOracle` and `RenzoOracleL2` use a hard-coded time interval for all feeds.

## Proof of Concept

The heartbeat in both contracts is hard-coded to the same value but price feeds often have different heartbeats:

```solidity
/// @dev The maxmimum staleness allowed for a price feed from chainlink
uint256 constant MAX_TIME_WINDOW = 86400 + 60; // 24 hours + 60 seconds

function lookupTokenValue(IERC20 _token, uint256 _balance) public view returns (uint256) {
    AggregatorV3Interface oracle = tokenOracleLookup[_token];
    if (address(oracle) == address(0x0)) revert OracleNotFound();

    (, int256 price, , uint256 timestamp, ) = oracle.latestRoundData();
    if (timestamp < block.timestamp - MAX_TIME_WINDOW) revert OraclePriceExpired();
    if (price <= 0) revert InvalidOraclePrice();

    // Price is times 10**18 ensure value amount is scaled
    return (uint256(price) * _balance) / SCALE_FACTOR;
}
```

The oracle should account for this fact by using the correct heartbeat for each feed, otherwise the staleness check is not complete and there's a risk of the integrators consuming a stale price.

The heartbeats can be found here:
https://docs.chain.link/data-feeds/price-feeds/addresses/?network=ethereum&page=1
(check `Show more details`)

## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Use a different heartbeat for each feed in accordance to the values provided from Chainlink.



