# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/main/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L205-L228
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/main/src/core/Dyad.sol#L38-L48


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Any user who takes on another user's `NOTE` liquidation begins a cycle where another user needs to now liquidate them or forgo their asset which was rolled over from taking on the very first liquidation.

## Proof of Concept
```solidity
function liquidate(
    uint id,
    uint to
  ) 
    external 
      isValidDNft(id)
      isValidDNft(to)
    {
      uint cr = collatRatio(id);
      if (cr >= MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO) revert CrTooHigh();
      dyad.burn(id, msg.sender, dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id));

      uint cappedCr               = cr < 1e18 ? 1e18 : cr;
      uint liquidationEquityShare = (cappedCr - 1e18).mulWadDown(LIQUIDATION_REWARD);
      uint liquidationAssetShare  = (liquidationEquityShare + 1e18).divWadDown(cappedCr);

      uint numberOfVaults = vaults[id].length();
      for (uint i = 0; i < numberOfVaults; i++) {
          Vault vault      = Vault(vaults[id].at(i));
          uint  collateral = vault.id2asset(id).mulWadUp(liquidationAssetShare);
          vault.move(id, to, collateral);
      }
      emit Liquidate(id, msg.sender, to);
  }
```
- Let's assume `wETH` price is USD 1000
- Suppose user A provided USD 1500 in `wETH`, then minted USD 1000 `DYAD`
- user B provided USD 3000 then minted USD 1000 `DYAD`
- Price of `wETH` dips to USD 800
- user A's position is undercollateralized, so user B decides to liquidate them (keep in mind a third party can & should be able to liquidate others as long as the CR is below 150%)
- user B's USD 1000 `DYAD` tokens are burnt (any DYAD minted against any `NOTE` can be used)
- user A's `mintedDyad` against his `NOTE` is now 0 because they are liquidated
- user B's `mintedDyad` against their own `NOTE` is still USD 1500 because, during the `DYAD` token burn, user A's internal accounting was erased while user B paid
- Technically, user B should be able to withdraw the rolled-over collateral and redeem from their USD 3000 deposit because now the vault holds USD 4500 in `wETH` but only `1000` DYAD in the `mintedDyad` overall
- But what essentially happens is that user B now needs to find a way to get specific `DYAD` matching his `NOTE` ID, and redeem them to reduce his internal accounting to 0 so he can get his asset back
- What essentially happens is that user B ends up minting `DYAD` again while supplying surplus collateral or finding some other user who will liquidate them and take on the burden of user B
- This then starts the cycle of leaving the last person to liquidate to keep holding the bag.
- user 2 can also decide to withdraw USD 3000 (1500 from his 3000 + 1500 from liquidation assuming he liquidated below 100% CR with no reward)
- USD 1500 would still be locked in the protocol which if another user takes on, they encounter the same issue user B encountered. We can already see how this is going to end.

Following the function calls below, we can take a look at the relevant parts of the `liquidate` and `burn` code to understand the issue better:

Liquidation from `NOTE` 1 to `NOTE` 2
```solidity
function liquidate(
    uint id,
    uint to
  ) 
    external 
      isValidDNft(id)
      isValidDNft(to)
    {
      ...
@>    dyad.burn(id, msg.sender, dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id)); // burn

      ...
  }
```

DYAD burn implementation
```solidity
function burn(
      uint    id, 
      address from,
      uint    amount
  ) external 
      licensedVaultManager 
    {
      _burn(from, amount); // @note as can be seen, the caller provides the DYAD
      mintedDyad[msg.sender][id] -= amount; // and the mintedDyad of VaultManagerv2 is for NOTE ID 1 is reduced. This means, any NOTE can be used to liquidate any other NOTE
  }
```

Redeem DYAD implementation
```solidity
function redeemDyad(
    uint    id,
    address vault,
    uint    amount,
    address to
  )
    external 
      isDNftOwner(id)
    returns (uint) { 
      dyad.burn(id, msg.sender, amount); // @note at this point user B, doesn't have any DYAD after the previous liquidation so this will fail
      ...
      withdraw(id, vault, asset, to);
      emit RedeemDyad(id, vault, amount, to);
      return asset;
  }
```

Also, if the protocol intends to migrate user assets and data from the currently deployed v1 vault to the v2 vault, this issue will be reached a lot sooner for undercollateralized `NOTE` positions.

