# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/cd48c684a58158de444b24854ffd8f07d046c31b/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L205-L228
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/cd48c684a58158de444b24854ffd8f07d046c31b/src/core/Dyad.sol#L38-L47


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

When a liquidator liquidates someone, the liquidator's **DYAD** is burned, but their `mintedDyad` does not decrease, causing a discrepancy between the actual minted DYAD and that in the `mintedDyad` mapping.

Therefore, when **`Liquidator1`** liquidates **`User1`**, he will burn his tokens but not reduce the `mintedDyad` mapping, then **`Liquidator2`** liquidates **`Liquidator1`** and will have to burn more tokens than **`Liquidator1`** actually has because it gets them from the `mintedDyad` mapping, and so on to **`Liquidator3`**, causing a **snowball** effect where each subsequent liquidation of the previous liquidator will cost much more, and can also block them from recovering some of their collateral.

[VaultManagerV2.sol#L205-L228](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/cd48c684a58158de444b24854ffd8f07d046c31b/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L205-L228)

```solidity
function liquidate(
  uint id,
  uint to
) 
  external 
    isValidDNft(id)
    isValidDNft(to)
  {
    uint cr = collatRatio(id);
    if (cr >= MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO) revert CrTooHigh();
    dyad.burn(id, msg.sender, dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id));

    uint cappedCr               = cr < 1e18 ? 1e18 : cr;
    uint liquidationEquityShare = (cappedCr - 1e18).mulWadDown(LIQUIDATION_REWARD);
    uint liquidationAssetShare  = (liquidationEquityShare + 1e18).divWadDown(cappedCr);

    uint numberOfVaults = vaults[id].length();
    for (uint i = 0; i < numberOfVaults; i++) {
        Vault vault      = Vault(vaults[id].at(i));
        uint  collateral = vault.id2asset(id).mulWadUp(liquidationAssetShare);
        vault.move(id, to, collateral);
    }
    emit Liquidate(id, msg.sender, to);
}
```

[Dyad.sol#L38-L47](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/cd48c684a58158de444b24854ffd8f07d046c31b/src/core/Dyad.sol#L38-L47)

```solidity
function burn(
    uint    id, 
    address from,
    uint    amount
) external 
    licensedVaultManager 
  {
    _burn(from, amount);
    mintedDyad[msg.sender][id] -= amount;
}
```

## Proof of Concept

Will explain it with 3 tables for each participant.

Read the example below and follow the table for better understanding.

> ***Note:** The liquidation bonuses below are calculated, when the liquidation bonus logic is fine, because now there is issues with it also, which we described in another issues. We choose to use the 20% fixed logic in the example for easies calculation. Although even with the wrong one, it will just send less collateral to the liquidator in term of $20-30 less, but in the end the issue still the same.*
> 

UserA

| Operation | Dyad.balanceOf | mintedDyad | Collateral in USD | Collateral ratio |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 100 | 100 | $150 | 150% |
| 2 | 100 | 100 | $135 | 135% |
| 3 | 100 | 0 | $15 | liquidated |

Liquidator1

| Operation | Dyad.balanceOf | mintedDyad | Collateral in USD | Collateral ratio |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 200 | 200 | $400 | 200% |
| 2 | 100 | 200 | $520 | 260% |
| 3 | 100 | 200 | $290 | 145% |
| 4 | 100 | 0 | $50 | liquidated |

Liquidator2

| Operation | Dyad.balanceOf | mintedDyad | Collateral in USD | Collateral ratio |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 600 | 600 | $1000 | 167% |
| 2 | 400 | 600 | $1240 | 206% |
1. UserA deposits **$150** worth of collateral and mints **100** Dyad. (User Operation 1)
2. Liquidator1 deposits **$400** worth of collateral and mints **200** Dyad. (Liquidator1 Operation 1)
3. Liquidator2 deposits **$1000** worth of collateral and mints **600** Dyad. (Liquidator2 Operation 1)
4. UserA’s collateral drops to **$135** leading him to **liquidation** state. (User Operation 2)
5. Liquidator1 liquidates UserA, burning their **100** Dyad and receiving ($100 + 20% bonus collateral from UserA) = $120 in collateral. (Liquidator1 Operation 2 and User Operation 3)
    1. However, Liquidator1's `**mintedDyad**` remains unchanged is what causes the issue.
6. Liquidator1's collateral decreases to **$290**, putting them in a liquidatable state. (Liquidator1 Operation 3)
7. Liquidator2 liquidate Liquidator1, but needs to burn **200** Dyad since it take them from the `**mintedDyad**` mapping, when the actual DYAD amount that Liquidator1 has is **100**.
    1. Liquidator2 will burn **200 Dyad** and will receive **$240** from the Liquidator1 collateral, **burning twice more DYAD than the actual 100 DYAD that Liquidator1 has**. (Liquidator2 Operation 2 and Liquidator1 Operation 4)
8. Liquidator2 will experience the same when he become liquidatable, and so on causing a snowball effect.
9. Liquidator1 and so on, may experience losing of collateral, because if we look at the step 6. Liquidator has **$290** collateral but only **100** DYAD, which means he is with **290% cr**, but he was actually liquidated, and ended with **$50** collateral, but he may want to redeem them. If he had called `redeemDyad` there, he will be able to get his collateral but he ended liquidated.

