# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Attacker can manipulate the collateralization ratio, to mint infinite Dyad tokens
Attacker can exploit the price oracle to inflate the value of their collateral. 
VaultManagerV2 has no way to verify the accuracy of price data provided by vaults when calculating the collateralization ratio.
It inherently trusts that any vault added to the system will provide accurate pricing information.


## Proof of Concept

```solidity
function add(
      uint    id,
      address vault
  ) 
    external
      isDNftOwner(id)
  {
    if (vaults[id].length() >= MAX_VAULTS) revert TooManyVaults();
    if (!vaultLicenser.isLicensed(vault))  revert VaultNotLicensed();
    if (!vaults[id].add(vault))            revert VaultAlreadyAdded();
    emit Added(id, vault);
  }
```
Only checks for a valid license but doesn't perform any check on contract code.

Here's how can attacker inflated the collateral ratio to mint large amounts of Dyad tokens

Attacker can deploys a malicious vault contract with an inflated ```stEthPerToken``` function to manipulate the asset price (inherited) from IWstETH interface

```solidity
interface IWstETH {
  function stEthPerToken() external view returns (uint256);
  function getStETHByWstETH(uint256 wstETHAmount) external view returns (uint256);
}
```
To determine the exchange rate between stETH and wstETH when calculating the collateralization ratio
There's too much trust in the interface any contract that looks like a vault (by just having the right functions) it will be accepted as a legitimate vault.

Here's how can attacker set the functions on maliciousvault 

Instead of implementing ```stEthPerToken``` to call the legitimate Lido oracle to get the actual stETH/wstETH exchange rate, the attacker overrides the function with their own malicious logic.

```solidity
function stEthPerToken() external view override returns (uint) {
    return 1000 ether; // inflating the value
}
```
The VaultManagerV2 assumes any vault added,will provide accurate and honest price, so now attacker bypass the ```Add``` function checks as the function only check for a valid license but does not perform any verification of the contract code.so this will allow attacker to bypass the check. 

There's no check that verify whether the provided vault is valid or not.

Now attacker can deposits any amount of wstETH into the malicious vault as the deposit function takes a vault address as an arguments. So attacker can provide the address of their malicious vault.


```solidity
function deposit(
    uint    id,
    address vault,
    uint    amount
  ) 
    external 
      isValidDNft(id)
  {
    idToBlockOfLastDeposit[id] = block.number;
    Vault _vault = Vault(vault);
    _vault.asset().safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(vault), amount);
    _vault.deposit(id, amount);
  }
```
To manipulate the calculations, in other to mint unlimited amount of Dyad 

So MaliciousVaultWstEth has a function 
```stEthPerToken``` function) that return a manipulate price as the ```getUsdValue``` depends on the vault's price calculation


https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/cd48c684a58158de444b24854ffd8f07d046c31b/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L119



VaultManagerV2 contract now will calculates a high collateralization ratio.
The logic of the malicious vault (i.e the inflated ```assetPrice``` calculation) is hidden from the VaultManagerV2 at the time of deposit. The problem arises when the deposited asset's value is needed for calculating the collateralization ratio.

Since that the VaultManagerV2 doesn't have any check that validate the price data, the contract will accepts the inflated value from the maliciousvault, leading to the incorrect collateralization ratio calculation.


