## Assessed type

Context

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/cd48c684a58158de444b24854ffd8f07d046c31b/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L134


# Vulnerability details

**Description:** The `VaultManagerV2` lacks input validation checks for the address passed as `vault` address inside the `withdraw` function. This function only checks that the caller is the owner of the `DNft` with the corresponding ID and that the `deposit` function wasn't called in the same block. That's to prevent flashloan attacks.

However, it does not check that the address of the `vault` parameter is actually a whitelisted vault and if it's part of the vaults mapping `mapping(uint => EnumerableSet.AddressSet) internal vaults;` or not.

Because of this an attacker can craft a malicious Vault contract, that implements the `IVault` interface, and they can get to execute arbitrary logic and influence the internal accounting of the `VaultManagerV2` contract.

**Impact:** An attacker could manipulate the value of the `value` variable inside the `VaultManagerV2::withdraw` function and make it so that it always returns 0. Because of this, the attacker will be able to drain tokens from the vault. Here is how. The attacker can bypass the check in the contract that looks to see if the USD value of the ExoCollateral is smaller than `dyadMinted`.

Here is the line that I refer to `if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) - value < dyadMinted) revert NotEnoughExoCollat();` --> this will always pass if the value of `value` is 0.

After that, the `VaultManagerV2::withdraw` function calls the `_vault.withdraw(...);` function on the malicious vault contract. Here, the attacker can execute arbitrary logic, and they can call the `VaultManagerV2::burnDyad` function to make the amount of minted Dyad tokens for that particular NFT zero. This will help them pass the last check.

The `VaultManagerV2::withdraw` function will then proceed with `if (collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO) revert CrTooLow();` which will pass because all the Dyad tokens were burned. The `VaultManagerV2::collatRatio` function will return `typetype(uint).max;` for an NFT if there are no Dyad tokens minted to its name, and since we burned them all earlier, the `_dyad` variable will be zero.

```javascript
    function collatRatio(uint id) public view returns (uint) {
        uint _dyad = dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id);
@>      if (_dyad == 0) return type(uint).max;
        return getTotalUsdValue(id).divWadDown(_dyad);
    }
```

The function call will end, and the result is that an attacker can steal tokens from the vault.

Imagine this scenario. Alice mints DNft with ID 0. Alice deposits `10000$` worth of ETH into a legit vault and mints `5000$` worth of Dyad for DNft at ID 0. She crafts a malicious vault contract whose `assetPrice` function will always return 0.
She also changes the malicious vault `withdraw` function so that it calls the VaultManagerV2 burn function to burn the Dyad tokens minted for DNft ID 0.
She calls the `VaultManagerV2::widthraw` function with the DNft id 0, her malicious vault address, amount 100000$, and her address as the receiver address.

<summary>Withdraw function code below</summary>
<details>

```javascript

    function withdraw(uint id, address vault, uint amount, address to) public isDNftOwner(id) {
        if (idToBlockOfLastDeposit[id] == block.number) revert DepositedInSameBlock();
        uint dyadMinted = dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id);
        Vault _vault = Vault(vault);
        uint value = (amount * _vault.assetPrice() * 1e18) /
            10 ** _vault.oracle().decimals() /
            10 ** _vault.asset().decimals();
        if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) - value < dyadMinted) revert NotEnoughExoCollat();
        _vault.withdraw(id, to, amount);
        //@audit reentrancy?
        if (collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO) revert CrTooLow();
    }
```

</details>

`dyadMinted` will be 5000. The value of `value` will be zero because `_vault.assetPrice()` will return 0.
When calculating the value of the `value` variable, the amount `100000` will be multiplied by `0` and it will return 0.

On the line `if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) - value < dyadMinted) revert NotEnoughExoCollat();` the `getNonKeroseneValue` function will calculate the amount of collateral that the user has inside its vaults. Since Alice deposited in one real vault, this will return `10000`.

Therefore the check will look like this `if(10000 - 0 < 5000) == true, revert`. Since the check is not true, because 10000 > 5000 this will pass, and it will not revert.

The contract will then call `_vault.withdraw` which calls the burn function on the VaultManagerV2 contract. The `Dyad` tokens minted for the DNft at ID 0 will be burned.

On the last line when we check for `if (collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO) revert CrTooLow();` since now the amount of minted Dyad is 0 this will return `type(uint).max;` and the check will pass.

**Proof of Concepts:** I set up a Malicious Vault contract below by following the legit Vault contract template. I then added it to the `Deploy.V2.s.sol` script and I created a new instance of it. Then I wrote a minimal PoC just to show that anyone can call the withdraw function and pass this address, and the transaction will not revert. My test will revert because I didn't do it all the way, but I think the flaw is obvious based on what I wrote so far.

<summary>PoC. Click the details button below.</summary>

<details>

```javascript

// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity =0.8.17;

import {IVaultManager} from "../interfaces/IVaultManager.sol";
import {IDNft} from "../interfaces/IDNft.sol";
import {IVault} from "../interfaces/IVault.sol";
import {IAggregatorV3} from "../interfaces/IAggregatorV3.sol";

