# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Since there's no gap between the `maximal LTV` and the `liquidation LTV`, user positions may be liquidated as soon as `maximal minting` or `maximum collateral withdrawal` is done. Without leaving room for `collateral value fluctuations`, users have no chance to add more collateral or reduce debt before being liquidated and therefore lose funds. 

## Proof of Concept
######  Minting DYAD
> [mintDyad()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/4a987e536576139793a1c04690336d06c93fca90/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L156-L169)
```solidity
  function mintDyad(
    uint    id,
    uint    amount,
    address to
  )
    external 
      isDNftOwner(id)
  {
    uint newDyadMinted = dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id) + amount;
    if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) < newDyadMinted)     revert NotEnoughExoCollat();
    dyad.mint(id, to, amount);
    if (collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO) revert CrTooLow(); 
    emit MintDyad(id, amount, to);
  }
```
Here, the function does this check:
```solidity
    if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) < newDyadMinted)     revert NotEnoughExoCollat();
```
This means that if `getNonKeroseneValue(id) == newDyadMinted` the function would execute the minting utilizing the entire collateral available.

Also, the function will only revert if `collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO` which is 150% and therefore this means that if `collatRatio(id) == MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO`, the user will use the entire callateral to mint maximumly.

###### Collateral withdrawal
> [withdraw()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/cd48c684a58158de444b24854ffd8f07d046c31b/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L134-L153)
```solidity
  function withdraw(
    uint    id,
    address vault,
    uint    amount,
    address to
  ) 
    public
      isDNftOwner(id)
  {
    if (idToBlockOfLastDeposit[id] == block.number) revert DepositedInSameBlock();
    uint dyadMinted = dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id);
    Vault _vault = Vault(vault);
    uint value = amount * _vault.assetPrice() 
                  * 1e18 
                  / 10**_vault.oracle().decimals() 
                  / 10**_vault.asset().decimals();
    if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) - value < dyadMinted) revert NotEnoughExoCollat();
    _vault.withdraw(id, to, amount);
    if (collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO)  revert CrTooLow(); 
  }
```
Here, the function does this check:
```solidity
    if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) - value < newDyadMinted)     revert NotEnoughExoCollat();
```
This means that if `getNonKeroseneValue(id) == newDyadMinted` the function would execute the withdrawal utilizing the entire collateral available.

Also, the function will only revert if `collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO` which is 150% and therefore this means that if `collatRatio(id) == MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO`, the user will withdraw maximum callateral.


According to [Documentation](https://dyadstable.notion.site/DYAD-design-outline-v6-3fa96f99425e458abbe574f67b795145):
> If a Note’s collateral value in USD drops below 150% of its DYAD minted balance, it faces liquidation.

It therefore follows that if a user utilizes all their collateral value such that each minted `DYAD` tokens is  backed by exactly `$1.50` of exogenous collateral with no allowance whatsoever, then any `fluctuation in the collateral price` will drop it below `150%` of its `DYAD` minted balance.

## Impact
As such, the users would  `mint/withdraw` themselves right into `liquidation` in the same block.

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider having different `LTV thresholds`. The difference allows price and collateral value fluctuations and protects users from liquidations caused by high volatility.

Such as:

```solidity
if (collatRatio(id) <= MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO) revert CrTooLow(); 
```










