# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Deposited Kerosine tokens cannot be withdrawn.

## Proof of Concept
The `VaultManagerV2.sol#withdraw()` function is as follows.
```solidity
  function withdraw(
    uint    id,
    address vault,
    uint    amount,
    address to
  ) 
    public
      isDNftOwner(id)
  {
    if (idToBlockOfLastDeposit[id] == block.number) revert DepositedInSameBlock();
    uint dyadMinted = dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id);
    Vault _vault = Vault(vault);
    uint value = amount * _vault.assetPrice() 
                  * 1e18 
                  / 10**_vault.oracle().decimals() 
                  / 10**_vault.asset().decimals();
150:if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) - value < dyadMinted) revert NotEnoughExoCollat();
    _vault.withdraw(id, to, amount);
    if (collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO)  revert CrTooLow(); 
  }
```
As you can see, the withdrawal check is performed at #L150.
However, When the DNftOwner attempts to withdraw Kerosine, Kerosine cannot be withdrawn because an operation is performed between the USD value of Non Kerosine and the USD value of the Kerosine token to be withdrawn.
Let's look at the example below.
The protocol strives to maintain the value of the DYAD token at $1, and the issuance amount of the DYAD token is determined by the TVL of the deposited Non Kerosine asset. 
So let's say `dyadMinted = 3e18` and the TVL of the deposited Non Kerosine asset (i.e. `getNonKeroseneValue(id)) is 3.1 * 1e18. Also, let's say the amount of deposited Kerosine tokens is 2e18.
If the DNftOwner attempts to withdraw 0.5 * 1e18 Kerosine,
>`getNonKeroseneValue(id) - value = 3.1 * 1e18 - 0.5 * 1e18 = 2.6 * 1e18`
>dyadMinted = 3e18
Therefore, withdraw() function is reverted.
Since the value of Kerosine token is an asset to maintain 150% of the value of DYAD token, the value of Non Kerosine will be maintained similar to the value of DYAD token.
You can burn DYAD and withdraw Kerosine assets, but in this case, you may not be able to withdraw Non-Kerosine assets.

