# Vulnerability details

## Impact
An NFT owner can call `liquidate` to move other NFT's assets to his own, and this action can be done without any DYAD tokens.

## Proof of Concept
In both normal vault and Kerosine vault, the `move` function simply moves the assets from one ID to another:

```solidity
  function move(
    uint from,
    uint to,
    uint amount
  )
    external
      onlyVaultManager
  {
    id2asset[from] -= amount;
    id2asset[to]   += amount;
    emit Move(from, to, amount);
  }
```

Such function is only called in `VaultManager.liquidate`:

```solidity
  function liquidate(
    uint id,
    uint to
  ) 
    external 
      isValidDNft(id)
      isValidDNft(to)
    {
      uint cr = collatRatio(id);
      if (cr >= MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO) revert CrTooHigh();
      dyad.burn(id, msg.sender, dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id));

      uint cappedCr               = cr < 1e18 ? 1e18 : cr;
      uint liquidationEquityShare = (cappedCr - 1e18).mulWadDown(LIQUIDATION_REWARD);
      uint liquidationAssetShare  = (liquidationEquityShare + 1e18).divWadDown(cappedCr);

      uint numberOfVaults = vaults[id].length();
      for (uint i = 0; i < numberOfVaults; i++) {
          Vault vault      = Vault(vaults[id].at(i));
          uint  collateral = vault.id2asset(id).mulWadUp(liquidationAssetShare);
          vault.move(id, to, collateral);
      }
      emit Liquidate(id, msg.sender, to);
  }
```

In this function, first, all DYAD balance will be burnt, then, all assets under the `id` ID will be moved to `to` ID. The function caller are expected to provide valid ID for both `id` and `to`. 

On the other side, the `withdraw` function allows an NFT owner to withdraw assets from any vaults:

```solidity
  function withdraw(
    uint    id,
    address vault,
    uint    amount,
    address to
  ) 
    public
      isDNftOwner(id)
  {
    // @note this can be intentionally block by deposit 0 tokens
    if (idToBlockOfLastDeposit[id] == block.number) revert DepositedInSameBlock();
    uint dyadMinted = dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id);
    Vault _vault = Vault(vault);
    uint value = amount * _vault.assetPrice() 
                  * 1e18 
                  / 10**_vault.oracle().decimals() 
                  / 10**_vault.asset().decimals();
    if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) - value < dyadMinted) revert NotEnoughExoCollat();
    _vault.withdraw(id, to, amount);
    if (collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO)  revert CrTooLow(); 
  }

```

At the end of the function, it makes sure the collateral ratio is above the healthy value. However, when an NFT owner owns 0 DYAD token, such ratio will be set to `type(uint256).max`:

```solidity
  function collatRatio(
    uint id
  )
    public 
    view
    returns (uint) {
      uint _dyad = dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id);
      if (_dyad == 0) return type(uint).max;
      return getTotalUsdValue(id).divWadDown(_dyad);
  }
```

Suppose Bob owns an DYAD NFT, and sees other NFTs are doing well, and calls `liquidate` to move all their assets to his own. Then, he withdraws all assets from those vaults under his NFT, his action will be successful because Bob has no DYAD tokens and can bypass all checks in `withdraw`, moreover, Bob also doesn't lose much as he can do all of those without even needing to have DYAD tokens.

## Tools Used
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider only liquidate the amount which caller has in DYAD balance.



