# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Anyone with DNft token can add vault to the VaultManager. As the valuation of the assets under one NFT Id is based on the vault Manager's getNonKeroseneValue() 
function which relies on the vault's getUsdValue(id).

If the owner of the DNft manipulates the USD valuation for one or more of his vaults, there is a possibility of boosting the getNonKeroseneValue() to a level much higher than actual value.

```
  usdValue = vault.getUsdValue(id);
```

Based on this, the DNft owner can mint DYAD tokens using this wrong valuation inflating the current supply of DYAD tokens and thus diluting the valuation of DYAD tokens over time.

## Proof of Concept
1) Mint DNft token and become a valid owner 
Any one can mint a DNft and become an owner of a valid NFT token.
   As long as sufficient ether is available, any one can mint the NFT
```
  function mintNft(address to)
    external 
    payable
    returns (uint) {
      uint price = START_PRICE + (PRICE_INCREASE * publicMints++);
      if (msg.value < price) revert InsufficientFunds();
      uint id = _mintNft(to);
      if (msg.value > price) to.safeTransferETH(msg.value - price);
      emit MintedNft(id, to);
      return id;
  }
```


2) Add vaults to the VaultManager as below. 
   As a valid owner of DNft token, anyone can add a vault with any underlying 
   asset. The vault can be a custom implementation and could have custom implementation on how getUsdValue() functionality is implement.
```
 function add(
      uint    id,
      address vault
  ) 
    external
      isDNftOwner(id)
  {
    if (vaults[id].length() >= MAX_VAULTS) revert TooManyVaults();
    if (!vaultLicenser.isLicensed(vault))  revert VaultNotLicensed();
    if (!vaults[id].add(vault))            revert VaultAlreadyAdded();
    emit Added(id, vault);
  }
```
As such the DNft owner() can boost the value of the USD value returned by one or more of the vaults deployed under the DNft id.

3) Mint Dyad based on getNonKeroseneValue() for DNft id.
As the valuation of asset in USD can be manipulated by the DNft owner, it is possible to boost the getNonKeroseneValue() and their by provision for additional minting of DYAD tokens using the mintDyad function.

If the getNonKeroseneValue() is larger than minted Dyad, new tokens can be minted into the circulation. The extent of how much can be minted additionally is based on the extend of price manipulation in the vault while returning getUsdValue().

```
 function mintDyad(
    uint    id,
    uint    amount,
    address to
  )
    external 
      isDNftOwner(id)
  {
    uint newDyadMinted = dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id) + amount;
    if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) < newDyadMinted)     revert NotEnoughExoCollat();
    dyad.mint(id, to, amount);
    if (collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO) revert CrTooLow(); 
    emit MintDyad(id, amount, to);
  }
```


## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
a) Currently, the implementation is not enforcing that the vault added to the vault manager is same as vault contract in the code base. The vault manager accepts address of vault contract.

b) Vault and underlying assets should be restricted to a pre approved list.
Only a valid list of assets can be added as vaults by DNft users. Since that restriction is not implemented, it is possible to add vault with any underlying token.

c) As the asset list is valid and the oracles providing the prices are also restricted, this risk will be reduced.


