# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The protocol utilizes Chainlink data feeds, in the vault functions where they fetch  the price they have the following checks:
```solidity
if (block.timestamp > updatedAt + STALE_DATA_TIMEOUT) revert StaleData();
```
In case Chainlink data feeds, return stale prices the ``assetPrice()`` functions will revert. When a user position is undercollateralized, it should get liquidated immediately so the protocol doesn't incur any bad debt. In order for this to happen the [liquidate()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/main/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L205-L228) function is called, and in order to get the current collateral ratio the [collatRatio()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/main/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L230-L239) function is called, which in turn calls the [getTotalUsdValue()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/main/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L241-L248) function where [getNonKeroseneValue()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/main/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L250-L267) and [getKeroseneValue()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/main/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L269-L286) functions are called. 

```solidity
  function getNonKeroseneValue(uint id) public view returns (uint) {
      uint totalUsdValue;
      uint numberOfVaults = vaults[id].length(); 
      for (uint i = 0; i < numberOfVaults; i++) {
        Vault vault = Vault(vaults[id].at(i));
        uint usdValue;
        if (vaultLicenser.isLicensed(address(vault))) {
          usdValue = vault.getUsdValue(id);        
        }
        totalUsdValue += usdValue;
      }
      return totalUsdValue;
  }
```
As can be seen from the above code snippet, the ``getUsdValue()`` function will be called for each vault the user has added to the list of vaults, he intends to deposit collateral into. Note that it doesn't matter if the user has deposited funds into those vaults. ``getUsdValue()`` internally calls the ``assetPrice()`` function which will revert if the returned price is stale. If the protocol is unable to liquidate undercollateralized positions, it can easily go underwater especially in black swan events, when typically prices are decreasing rapidly and data feeds may not function properly. 
