# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/main/src/core/Vault.sol#L100
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/main/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L250-L267
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/main/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L146


# Vulnerability details

## Description
The `assetPrice` method in `Vault` relies on the Chainlink Price feed to get the current price of the assets in the vaults by calling `latestRoundData()` of the `IAggregatorV3` interface.

This `assetPrice()` is used by `VaultManagerV2` to get the `totalUsdValue` of assets.

This function should check for the minimum and maximum amount return to prevent some cases from happening, as Chainlink aggregators have a built-in circuit breaker if the price of an asset goes outside of a predetermined price band. 

The result is that if an asset experiences a huge drop in value (e.g., a LUNA crash), the oracle's price will continue to return the `minAnswer` instead of the actual price of the asset. 
This would result in false collateral values for users (in case of implosion, much higher values than the actual values) in case of exogenous vaults.

Something similar happened when [Venus on Bsc when LUNA Imploded](https://rekt.news/venus-blizz-rekt/).

If a case like LUNA happens, then the oracle will return the minimum price, not the crashed price.

## Proof of Concept
Inside `VaultManagerV2` we have `getNonKeroseneValue()`, which is used to obtain the total collateral value of exogenous tokens in vaults for a given `id` corresponding to `dNft`

```solidity
 function getNonKeroseneValue(uint id) public view returns (uint) {
        uint totalUsdValue;
        uint numberOfVaults = vaults[id].length();
        for (uint i = 0; i < numberOfVaults; i++) {
            Vault vault = Vault(vaults[id].at(i));
            uint usdValue;
            if (vaultLicenser.isLicensed(address(vault))) {
                usdValue = vault.getUsdValue(id); // @audit -> calls to chainlink Oracle
            }
            totalUsdValue += usdValue;
        }
        return totalUsdValue;
    }
```
Now, if we take a look at the `getUsdValue` of the `Vault`, then it is implemented as:

```solidity
function getUsdValue(uint id) external view returns (uint) {
    return
        (id2asset[id] * assetPrice() * 1e18) /
        10 ** oracle.decimals() /
        10 ** asset.decimals();
}
```

Where `assetPrice` is implemented as:

```solidity
function assetPrice() public view returns (uint) {
      (
        ,
        int256 answer,
        , 
        uint256 updatedAt, 
      ) = oracle.latestRoundData();
      // @audit no check for minAnswer and maxAnswer
      if (block.timestamp > updatedAt + STALE_DATA_TIMEOUT) revert StaleData();
      return answer.toUint256();
  }
```

As per the implementation, we can observe there is no price feed check performed on `answer`.

Chainlink Aggregators have `minAnswer` and `maxAnswer` circuit breakers built into them. This means that if the asset's price drops below the minPrice, the protocol will continue to value the token at the minPrice instead of its actual value.


Example: TokenA has a minimum price of USD 1. When its price drops to USD 0.10, the aggregator still returns USD 1, allowing the user to mint DYAD as if it were USD 1, which is 10x its actual value.

## Impact
Methods like `mintDyad`, `withdraw`, `collatRatio`, `liquidate`, and `redeemDyad` in `VaultManagerV2` will be affected as they all depend on either `getNonKeroseneValue`, which internally depends on `assetPrice()` of non-exogenous vaults or they directly call `vault.assetPrice()`. 

This will inflate the value of Collateral that the users have.

Impact: High
Likelihood: Low
Severity: Medium

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
ChainlinkAdapterOracle should check the returned answer against the minAnswer/maxAnswer and revert if the answer is outside of the bounds; also additional check for `roundId` should be encouraged:

```diff
function assetPrice() public view returns (uint) {
      (
        uint80 roundId,
        int256 answer,
        , 
        uint256 updatedAt, 
      ) = oracle.latestRoundData();
      if(roundId == 0) revert();
      if (block.timestamp > updatedAt + STALE_DATA_TIMEOUT) revert StaleData();
+    if (answer >= maxAnswer or answer <= minAnswer) revert();
      return answer.toUint256();
```

**_Note:_**
`minAnswer` and `maxAnswer` is defined in the [OffchainAggregator](https://github.com/smartcontractkit/libocr/blob/master/contract/AccessControlledOffchainAggregator.sol#L33-L34)

For example `minAnswer` of stETH can be found in the aggregator contract of MAINNET_CHAINLINK_STETH. 

MAINNET_CHAINLINK_STETH = [0xCfE54B5cD566aB89272946F602D76Ea879CAb4a8](https://etherscan.io/address/0xCfE54B5cD566aB89272946F602D76Ea879CAb4a8#readContract)
Aggregator of stETH = [0xdA31bc2B08F22AE24aeD5F6EB1E71E96867BA196](https://etherscan.io/address/0xdA31bc2B08F22AE24aeD5F6EB1E71E96867BA196#readContract)

`minAnswer` returned by aggregator = 1e9 i.e 10 USD (as aggregator has 8 decimals).

Reference for other valid medium submissions:
1. https://code4rena.com/reports/2023-07-moonwell#m-02-missing-check-for-the-maxmin-price-in-the-chainlinkoraclesol-contract












