# Vulnerability details

## Impact 
For Kerosine vaults, if any of them are added to `KerosineManager`, when `assetPrice` is called, there can be infinite recursion and lead to OOG DoS. 

## Proof of Concept 
Let's take a look at the `assetPrice` function in unbounded Kerosine vault: 

```solidity
  function assetPrice() 
    public 
    view 
    override
    returns (uint) {
      uint tvl;
      address[] memory vaults = kerosineManager.getVaults();
      uint numberOfVaults = vaults.length;
      for (uint i = 0; i < numberOfVaults; i++) {
        Vault vault = Vault(vaults[i]);
        tvl += vault.asset().balanceOf(address(vault)) 
                * vault.assetPrice() * 1e18
                / (10**vault.asset().decimals()) 
                / (10**vault.oracle().decimals());
      }
      uint numerator   = tvl - dyad.totalSupply();
      uint denominator = kerosineDenominator.denominator();
      return numerator * 1e8 / denominator;
  } 
``` 

We see it loops through all vaults registered in `KerosineManager` and gets the `assetPrice` value. If the unbounded Kerosine vault itself is in the list of `KerosineManager`, this can cause infinite recursion, and make the transaction revert due to OOG. 

This happens because there is no check on what vaults can be added to the manager contracts, either the normal vault or the Kerosine vault can be added. This can be solved by removing the Kerosine vault from the manager, but had to be done by the NFT owner, until then, all functions related to getting price will be disabled. 

## Tools Used 
Manual review 

## Recommended Mitigation Steps 
If Kerosine vaults are not meant to be added to managers, add a check, otherwise, maybe consider rewrite the fetch price logic so this doesn't happen recursively.


