# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Users may get less collateral than they should when withdrawing or redeeming, users may get less Dyad than they should when making a mint and users may burn more Dyad than they should when burning

## Proof of Concept

1. `withdraw()`

```solidity
  function withdraw(
    uint    id,
    address vault,
    uint    amount,
    address to
  ) 
    public
      isDNftOwner(id)
  {
    if (idToBlockOfLastDeposit[id] == block.number) revert DepositedInSameBlock();
    uint dyadMinted = dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id);
    Vault _vault = Vault(vault);
    uint value = amount * _vault.assetPrice() 
                  * 1e18 
                  / 10**_vault.oracle().decimals() 
                  / 10**_vault.asset().decimals();
    if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) - value < dyadMinted) revert NotEnoughExoCollat();
    _vault.withdraw(id, to, amount);
    if (collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO)  revert CrTooLow(); 
  }
```

`withdraw()` has a function to withdraw the remaining collateral that the user has in the vault and still maintain the collateral ratio that the user has. The problem here is that there is no checking whether the user receives collateral according to what was requested or not. Checks are only carried out to maintain Dyad price stability but do not check the amount received by users. If the USD value of the collateral changes significantly but the collateral ratio is still maintained, then it is possible that the user will receive less collateral than requested.

2. `mintDyad()`

```solidity
  function mintDyad(
    uint    id,
    uint    amount,
    address to
  )
    external 
      isDNftOwner(id)
  {
    uint newDyadMinted = dyad.mintedDyad(address(this), id) + amount;
    if (getNonKeroseneValue(id) < newDyadMinted)     revert NotEnoughExoCollat();
    dyad.mint(id, to, amount);
    if (collatRatio(id) < MIN_COLLATERIZATION_RATIO) revert CrTooLow(); 
    emit MintDyad(id, amount, to);
  }
```

`mintDyad()` has a function to mint Dyad according to the amount input by the user. The main problem here is that the protocol does not check how many Dyads the user receives. Is it in accordance with what the user requested or not. Checks are only carried out to maintain the stability of the user's collateral ratio and so that the Dyad value remains stable.

3. `redeemDyad()`

```solidity
  function redeemDyad(
    uint    id,
    address vault,
    uint    amount,
    address to
  )
    external 
      isDNftOwner(id)
    returns (uint) { 
      dyad.burn(id, msg.sender, amount);
      Vault _vault = Vault(vault);
      uint asset = amount 
                    * (10**(_vault.oracle().decimals() + _vault.asset().decimals())) 
                    / _vault.assetPrice() 
                    / 1e18;
      withdraw(id, vault, asset, to);
      emit RedeemDyad(id, vault, amount, to);
      return asset;
  }
```

`mintDyad()` has a function to burn the amount of Dyad owned by the user and withdraw collateral. Just like the two issues above, this function also does not check whether the collateral the user will receive is in accordance with what the user requested.

4. `burnDyad()`

```solidity
  function burnDyad(
    uint id,
    uint amount
  ) 
    external 
      isValidDNft(id)
  {
    dyad.burn(id, msg.sender, amount);
    emit BurnDyad(id, amount, msg.sender);
  }
```

`burnDyad()` has a function to burn the amount of Dyad owned by the user. The main problem here, protocol does not check if burned Dyad according to what the user inputs, so there is a possibility that the user will burn Dyad more than what was requested.

