# Vulnerability details

## Impact
`UnboundedKerosineVault::assetPrice()` returning stale price.
## Proof of Concept
The current implementation of `Vault.sol` has a constant defining the stale time of the oracle, as of the time writing this the protocol has already deployed a Vault that uses the ETH/USD oracle with stale time of 90 miuntes. 

[Dyad's WETH vault](https://etherscan.io/address/0xcF97cEc1907CcF9d4A0DC4F492A3448eFc744F6c#readContract)
[ETH/USD Oracle](https://data.chain.link/feeds/ethereum/mainnet/eth-usd)

However, as we can see from the links provided above the oracle's actual hearbeat is 60 minutes not 90. In that case, if the price were to go stale, the protocol will continue to operate normally and return stale price for the next 30 minutes. 

Kerosene price is calculated by getting the TVL of all of the vaults subtracting the total supply of dyad to get the overcollateralized usd and then divide it by the total supply of kerosene.


```javascript
  function assetPrice() 
    public 
    view 
    override
    returns (uint) {
      uint tvl;
      address[] memory vaults = kerosineManager.getVaults();
      uint numberOfVaults = vaults.length;
      for (uint i = 0; i < numberOfVaults; i++) {
        Vault vault = Vault(vaults[i]);
        //@review - Get the total usd value of the vault
        tvl += vault.asset().balanceOf(address(vault)) 
                * vault.assetPrice() * 1e18
                / (10**vault.asset().decimals()) 
                / (10**vault.oracle().decimals());
      }
      //@review - Get the total overcollateralization of the protocol (TVL - totalSupply)
      uint numerator   = tvl - dyad.totalSupply();
      uint denominator = kerosineDenominator.denominator();

      //@review - return the price with precision of 8 decimals.
      return numerator * 1e8 / denominator;
  }

```

The vault's TVL is calculated by getting it's total balance of tokens and multiplying it by the asset price, which we are getting from the chainlink oracle.

This is where the price can go stale and the protocol will continue to work normally even with a stale price.

```javascript 
 function assetPrice() public view returns (uint) {
        (, int256 answer, , uint256 updatedAt, ) = oracle.latestRoundData();
        //@review - This is using wrong value for checking the heartbeat
        if (block.timestamp > updatedAt + STALE_DATA_TIMEOUT)
            revert StaleData();
        return answer.toUint256();
    }
```


## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Since the `Vault.sol` contract is OOS and some of them are already deployed and there is no way to update them or redeploy them(assuming they have collateral deposited). 
Consider adding a new heartbeat check inside of `Vault.kerosine.unbouded.sol::assetPrice()`, and if the price is stale either revert or call a fallback oracle.

