# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-04-dyad/blob/44becc2f09c3a75bd548d5ec756a8e88a345e826/src/core/VaultManagerV2.sol#L59-L65


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The `VaultManagerV2.sol` contract has a function `setKeroseneManager` which has the `initializer` modifier and thus can only be called once. Generally, when using this modifier, the initializer function needs to be called in the same transaction as the contract deployment, which is generally handled via another contract or a factory contract. This is not the case here.

As seen in the deploy script, the contract is deployed, then other contracts are deployed, and then the initializer is called.

```solidity
//@audit vault deployed here
VaultManagerV2 vaultManager = new VaultManagerV2(
      DNft(MAINNET_DNFT),
      Dyad(MAINNET_DYAD),
      vaultLicenser
    );

    // weth vault
    Vault ethVault = new Vault(
      vaultManager,
      ERC20        (MAINNET_WETH),
      IAggregatorV3(MAINNET_WETH_ORACLE)
    );
    // wsteth vault
    VaultWstEth wstEth = new VaultWstEth(
      vaultManager,
      ERC20        (MAINNET_WSTETH),
      IAggregatorV3(MAINNET_CHAINLINK_STETH)
    );
    KerosineManager kerosineManager = new KerosineManager();
    kerosineManager.add(address(ethVault));
    kerosineManager.add(address(wstEth));

// @audit initialized here, several blocks later
    vaultManager.setKeroseneManager(kerosineManager);
```

Foundry deployment script execute each line as a separate transaction, so there can be multiple blocks difference between when the kerosene manager contract is deployed, and when it is initialized. Within this time interval, bots can call the `setKeroseneManager` on the deployed contract setting it to some garbage value, which would force the contracts to be redeployed.

This is a griefing attack vector, and is thus a medium severityissue. This can be used to cause unnecesary costs to the protocol due to multiple deployments.

## Proof of Concept

Contract is deployed in block #100. In block #101, an MEV bot calls `setKeroseneManager` in the contract, setting it to some garbage value. In block #105, the foundry script tries to call the same function and reverts.

Script will be needed to be run again, incurring more gas costs for deployment.

## Tools Used

Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

The `keroseneManager` contract should be deployed first as it has no dependencies. The `keroseneManager` address can then just be passed in the constructor of the vault manager, removing the need to use initializer.



