# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/457230945a49878eefdc1001796b10638c1e7584/src/V3Oracle.sol#L432-L450


# Vulnerability details


## Proof of Concept

Take a look at https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/457230945a49878eefdc1001796b10638c1e7584/src/V3Oracle.sol#L432-L450

```solidity
    // calculate position amounts given current price/tick
    function _getAmounts(PositionState memory state)
        internal
        view
        returns (uint256 amount0, uint256 amount1, uint128 fees0, uint128 fees1)
    {
        if (state.liquidity > 0) {
            state.sqrtPriceX96Lower = TickMath.getSqrtRatioAtTick(state.tickLower);
            state.sqrtPriceX96Upper = TickMath.getSqrtRatioAtTick(state.tickUpper);
            //@audit
            (amount0, amount1) = LiquidityAmounts.getAmountsForLiquidity(
                state.sqrtPriceX96, state.sqrtPriceX96Lower, state.sqrtPriceX96Upper, state.liquidity
            );
        }

        (fees0, fees1) = _getUncollectedFees(state, state.tick);
        fees0 += state.tokensOwed0;
        fees1 += state.tokensOwed1;
    }

```

This function is used to calculate the position amounts using the current price/tick, issue here is with querying the `LiquidityAmounts.getAmountsForLiquidity()`.

Now note that this function getAmountsForLiquidity() will trigger an arithmetic underflow whenever sqrtRatioX96 is smaller than sqrtRatioAX96, causing this functions to revert until this ratio comes back in range and the math no longer overflows.

These price conditions are also very likely to happen in real market conditions, and they can be permanent (i.e. one asset permanently appreciating over the other one).

The root cause of this issue is that the current solidity version used has under/overflow protection (i.e ^ 0.8.0); the `LiquidityAmounts` library, however expects otherwise so it won't work properly when compiled in v0.8.0 or later:

As stated, V3Oracle.sol is compiled with version of pragma solidity bigger than 0.8.0 as can be seen here
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/457230945a49878eefdc1001796b10638c1e7584/src/V3Oracle.sol#L1-L3
same case for FullMath.sol with the version ^0.8.0, which then causes the mulDiv to revert on overflows.

## Impact

This essentially breaks protocol's pricing logic cause this function is very essential while getting the value of an LP position, i.e "\_getAmounts() -> getPositionBreakdown() -> getValue()" is how the `LiquidityAmounts.getAmountsForLiquidity()` gets queried and note that this could revert for real life cases.

The above leads to different impacts, one of which is that it then causes protocol to allow borrowers to have bad loans, cause they can't get liquidated, this is cause liquidating wouldn't work [since it makes a call to ` _checkLoanIsHealthy()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/457230945a49878eefdc1001796b10638c1e7584/src/V3Vault.sol#L700-L703) which would also [revert due to its call to the oracle's `getValue()` ](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/457230945a49878eefdc1001796b10638c1e7584/src/V3Vault.sol#L1275-L1277).

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Restore the original library so that it can now compile with solc versions earlier than 0.8.0.



## Assessed type

Uniswap

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/435b054f9ad2404173f36f0f74a5096c894b12b7/src/utils/Swapper.sol#L138


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The function sets price limits for the swap using `TickMath.MIN_SQRT_RATIO + 1 or TickMath.MAX_SQRT_RATIO - 1`, depending on the swap direction `(params.swap0For1)`. These represent the minimum and maximum possible prices in the pool. An attacker will frontrunt this leading to slippage losses for users.

`https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/435b054f9ad2404173f36f0f74a5096c894b12b7/src/utils/Swapper.sol#L138`

`    function _poolSwap(PoolSwapParams memory params) internal returns (uint256 amountInDelta, uint256 amountOutDelta) {
        if (params.amountIn > 0) {
            (int256 amount0Delta, int256 amount1Delta) = params.pool.swap(
                address(this),
                params.swap0For1,
                int256(params.amountIn),
                (params.swap0For1 ? TickMath.MIN_SQRT_RATIO + 1 : TickMath.MAX_SQRT_RATIO - 1),
                abi.encode(
                    params.swap0For1 ? params.token0 : params.token1,
                    params.swap0For1 ? params.token1 : params.token0,
                    params.fee
                )
            );
            amountInDelta = params.swap0For1 ? uint256(amount0Delta) : uint256(amount1Delta);
            amountOutDelta = params.swap0For1 ? uint256(-amount1Delta) : uint256(-amount0Delta);


            // amountMin slippage check
            if (amountOutDelta < params.amountOutMin) {
                revert SlippageError();
            }
        }
    }`

## Proof of Concept
1. An attacker observes the pool and waits for an opportunity when a large swap is likely to occur or manipulates the market to create such a condition.

2. The attacker performs transactions that shift the pool's price closer to the extreme limit that the upcoming swap will use. This could involve adding or removing liquidity in a way that moves the price or executing swaps that push the price in the desired direction.

3. Unaware of the manipulation or unable to counteract it in time, the victim executes the large swap using `_poolSwap`. Due to the extreme price limits, the swap goes through but at a rate that is significantly worse than the market rate or intended by the victim.

4. The attacker then performs subsequent transactions to reverse the initial manipulation or exploit the new pool state created by the large swap. This could involve selling tokens at the now-inflated prices or buying back tokens at depressed prices, realizing a profit at the expense of the victim who executed the swap.

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Instead of using static extremes, dynamically calculate price limits based on current market conditions, historical price data, or oracle prices. This could help ensure swaps are executed within a reasonable range of the market price.


