Uniswap

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/457230945a49878eefdc1001796b10638c1e7584/src/transformers/AutoCompound.sol#L100-L196


# Vulnerability details


## Proof of Concept

Take a look at https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/457230945a49878eefdc1001796b10638c1e7584/src/transformers/AutoCompound.sol#L100-L196

Most especially [while getting state.amountInDelta & state.amountOutDelta](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/457230945a49878eefdc1001796b10638c1e7584/src/transformers/AutoCompound.sol#L139-L152) via the pool swap.

```solidity
                if (amountIn > 0) {
                    // no slippage check done - because protected by TWAP check
                    //@audit
                    (state.amountInDelta, state.amountOutDelta) = _poolSwap(
                        Swapper.PoolSwapParams(
                            pool, IERC20(state.token0), IERC20(state.token1), state.fee, params.swap0To1, amountIn, 0
                        )
                    );
                    state.amount0 =
                        params.swap0To1 ? state.amount0 - state.amountInDelta : state.amount0 + state.amountOutDelta;
                    state.amount1 =
                        params.swap0To1 ? state.amount1 + state.amountOutDelta : state.amount1 - state.amountInDelta;
                }
```

Evidently, one can see that while swapping via the pool there is no slippage whatsoever applied, which is because protocol assumes that the TWAP provided price is enough "protection", contrary to protocol's believe a TWAP check is used just to ensure that the pricing data returned from the oracle before the swap is correct, but slippage works a different thing, i.e it's placed to confirm that a specification of the minimum acceptable amount is done, cause at current implementation whereas a twap check exists, the swap would still return an unfair amount of tokens.

These three values have been set, i.e "amount0Min: 0, amount1Min: 0, deadline: block.timestamp" which is a wrong logic cause now the transaction can stay for as long as possible in the mempool and would still be valid, conjucting this with the fact that there are no slippage protections, then this attempt would lead to having very little amounts returned protection.

## Impact

High, lack of slippage protection leads to leak of value, this is due to protocol wrong assumption that using the right TWAP price is enough swap protection, but this price is returned before the swap is done, so slippage is still needed.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Regardless of TWAP, slippage protection should still be applied for swaps.



