Under/Overflow

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/main/src/V3Vault.sol?plain=1#L1143-L1149


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Revert assumes that a Uniswap V3 NFT position will always have liquidity in both token0 and token1. However, a NFT position can have liquidity in just one token. When calculating the collateral factor for a NFT position, Revert chooses the smaller of the two token collateral factors, regardless if the position actually owns the token. This can result in lenders receiving an unfair advantage when it comes to the collateral factor for NFT positions that are 100% one-sided on token0 and Revert considers token1 to have a smaller collateral factor than token0.


## Proof of Concept

As you can see below, _calculateTokenCollateralFactorX32 calculates the collateral factor based on which token has a smaller collateral factor:

```solidity
function _calculateTokenCollateralFactorX32(uint256 tokenId) internal view returns (uint32) {
    (,, address token0, address token1,,,,,,,,) = nonfungiblePositionManager.positions(tokenId);
    uint32 factor0X32 = tokenConfigs[token0].collateralFactorX32;
    uint32 factor1X32 = tokenConfigs[token1].collateralFactorX32;
    return factor0X32 > factor1X32 ? factor1X32 : factor0X32;
}
```

Unfortunately there are no checks to see if the NFT position has liquidity on both tokens. A user can create a NFT position where liquidity is applied to just one of the tokens, then deposit the NFT position into the vault, and finally borrow assets against that NFT position. However, the borrower will have an unfair collateral factor applied to their loan if the zero-balance NFT position token has a lower collateral factor. 

We can see how the collateral factor is unfairly applied in the following forge test:

```solidity

function testOneSidedNFTCollateralFactorBug() external {
    vault.setTokenConfig(address(WETH), uint32(Q32 * 8 / 10), type(uint32).max); // 80% collateral factor / max 100% collateral value

    // lend 10 USDC
    _deposit(10000000, WHALE_ACCOUNT);

    // Create one-sided NFT position
    INonfungiblePositionManager.MintParams memory mintParams = INonfungiblePositionManager.MintParams({
        token0: address(DAI),
        token1: address(WETH),
        tickLower: int24(200),
        tickUpper: int24(1000),
        fee: uint24(10000),
        recipient: TEST_NFT_ACCOUNT,
        amount0Desired: 10e18,
        amount1Desired: 0,
        amount0Min: 0,
        amount1Min: 0,
        deadline: block.timestamp + 15 minutes
    });

    vm.startPrank(TEST_NFT_ACCOUNT);
    deal(address(DAI), TEST_NFT_ACCOUNT, 30e18);
    deal(address(WETH), TEST_NFT_ACCOUNT, 30e18);

    // AUDIT: 0xa80964C5bBd1A0E95777094420555fead1A26c1e matches the DAI/WETH uniswap v3 pool
    DAI.approve(0xa80964C5bBd1A0E95777094420555fead1A26c1e, 20e18);
    WETH.approve(0xa80964C5bBd1A0E95777094420555fead1A26c1e, 20e18);

    (uint mintedTokenId ,,uint daiInNFTPosition, uint wethInNFTPosition) = NPM.mint(mintParams);

    // add collateral
    NPM.approve(address(vault), mintedTokenId);
    vault.create(mintedTokenId, TEST_NFT_ACCOUNT);
    (address token0, address token1,,, uint amount0Owed, uint amount1Owed,,) = oracle.getPositionBreakdown(TEST_NFT);
    (, uint256 fullValue, uint256 collateralValue,,) = vault.loanInfo(mintedTokenId);

    // borrow
    vault.borrow(mintedTokenId, collateralValue);

    // check results
    (uint32 token0collateralFactor,,) = vault.tokenConfigs(address(DAI));
    (uint32 token1collateralFactor,,) = vault.tokenConfigs(address(WETH));

    console.log("token 0 collateral factor: ", token0collateralFactor);
    console.log("token 1 collateral factor: ", token1collateralFactor);

    // AUDIT: as we can see below, the collateral factors are different between token0 and token1. When _calculateTokenCollateralFactorX32 is called, it will utilize token1's lower collateral factor.
    // token 0 collateral factor:  3865470566
    // token 1 collateral factor:  3435973836
}
```


## Tools Used

Manual inspection.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Revert should determine that liquidity exists in both tokens. If it doesn't, then Revert should apply the collateral factor to the token that contains 100% of the liquidity in the NFT position.






