https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/main/src/V3Vault.sol?plain=1#L1270-L1279
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/main/src/V3Vault.sol?plain=1#L1143-L1148


# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The owner of the vault can update a token config's collateral factor. Within the function is a check that the collateral factor must not exceed the max collateral factor which is set to 90%. However, the function does not include checks that the collateral factor is being lowered from the current level. When a collateral factor is lowered, loans can become instantly liquidatable which is unfair to loaners.

It's important to emphasis that normally admin function vulnerabilities are typically considered LOW. However, because Revert has added an additional check that the collateral factor does not reach above a certain level, Revert is assuming that any range value from `0 - max collateral factor` can be used for a token's collateral factor whenever setTokenConfig is called. This is why I believe this should be considered a MED.


## Proof of Concept

Let's first look at how the collateral factor is updated:

```solidity
function setTokenConfig(address token, uint32 collateralFactorX32, uint32 collateralValueLimitFactorX32)
    external
    onlyOwner
{
    if (collateralFactorX32 > MAX_COLLATERAL_FACTOR_X32) {
        revert CollateralFactorExceedsMax();
    }
    tokenConfigs[token].collateralFactorX32 = collateralFactorX32;
    tokenConfigs[token].collateralValueLimitFactorX32 = collateralValueLimitFactorX32;
    emit SetTokenConfig(token, collateralFactorX32, collateralValueLimitFactorX32);
}
```

As you can see, the only check that exists is that the collateralFactorX32 hasn't reached the max collateral factor.

Now let's see how a loan's healthiness is calculated:

```solidity
function _checkLoanIsHealthy(uint256 tokenId, uint256 debt)
    internal
    view
    returns (bool isHealthy, uint256 fullValue, uint256 collateralValue, uint256 feeValue)
{
    (fullValue, feeValue,,) = oracle.getValue(tokenId, address(asset));
    uint256 collateralFactorX32 = _calculateTokenCollateralFactorX32(tokenId);
    collateralValue = fullValue.mulDiv(collateralFactorX32, Q32);
    isHealthy = collateralValue >= debt;
}

function _calculateTokenCollateralFactorX32(uint256 tokenId) internal view returns (uint32) {
    (,, address token0, address token1,,,,,,,,) = nonfungiblePositionManager.positions(tokenId);
    uint32 factor0X32 = tokenConfigs[token0].collateralFactorX32;
    uint32 factor1X32 = tokenConfigs[token1].collateralFactorX32;
    return factor0X32 > factor1X32 ? factor1X32 : factor0X32;
}
```

To calculate the collateral factor of the loan, Revert checks which NFT position's token collateral factor is the lowest and then utilizes that value as the collateral factor. Let's say we have a current loan out such that:

```notes
token0 collateral factor: 75%
token1 collateral factor: 70%
``` 

If an admin changes token0 collateral factor from 75% to 65%, then the loan's collateral factor will move from 70% to 65%. This can result in the loan receiving an immediate unhealthy state after the collateral factor is updated. 


## Tools Used

Manual inspection.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

The protocol should check that the updated collateralFactor is not lower than the old collateralFactor within the setTokenConfig function. This will ensure that the protocol never lowers a collateralFactor resulting in immediate liquidations.

```solidity
function setTokenConfig(address token, uint32 collateralFactorX32, uint32 collateralValueLimitFactorX32)
    external
    onlyOwner
{
    if (collateralFactorX32 > MAX_COLLATERAL_FACTOR_X32) {
        revert CollateralFactorExceedsMax();
    }

    if (collateralFactorX32 < tokenConfigs[token].collateralFactorX32) {
        revert CollateralFactorLowerThanCurrentFactor();
    }
    tokenConfigs[token].collateralFactorX32 = collateralFactorX32;
    tokenConfigs[token].collateralValueLimitFactorX32 = collateralValueLimitFactorX32;
    emit SetTokenConfig(token, collateralFactorX32, collateralValueLimitFactorX32);
}
```




## Assessed type

Invalid Validation

