# Vulnerability details

## Detailed Explanation
The liquidate function relies on _checkLoanIsHealthy to know if the NFT position is eligible for liquidation. In order to do that, it will call the oracle.getValue (line 1410) to know the value of collateral nft position and compare it against the accumulated debt to know if it is healthy or not.
````
File: V3Vault.sol
1405:     function _checkLoanIsHealthy(uint256 tokenId, uint256 debt)
1406:         internal
1407:         view
1408:         returns (bool isHealthy, uint256 fullValue, uint256 collateralValue, uint256 feeValue)
1409:     {
1410:         (fullValue, feeValue,,) = oracle.getValue(tokenId, address(asset));
1411:         uint256 collateralFactorX32 = _calculateTokenCollateralFactorX32(tokenId);
1412:         collateralValue = fullValue.mulDiv(collateralFactorX32, Q32);
1413:         isHealthy = collateralValue >= debt;
1414:     }
1415: 
````

As you can see, the getValue function is very important in liquidation process, so we need to check on the possibility that it can fail or revert. I found this part of the function pertaining to _getReferenceTokenPriceX96. 

````
File: V3Oracle.sol
106:         (price0X96, cachedChainlinkReferencePriceX96) =
107:             _getReferenceTokenPriceX96(token0, cachedChainlinkReferencePriceX96);
108:         (price1X96, cachedChainlinkReferencePriceX96) =
109:             _getReferenceTokenPriceX96(token1, cachedChainlinkReferencePriceX96);

````
Then, let's check the details of _getReferenceTokenPriceX96 in which it returns the price for a token using the selected oracle mode given as reference token value
The price is calculated using Chainlink, Uniswap v3 TWAP, or both based on the mode
````
File: V3Oracle.sol
298:         if (usesChainlink) {
299:             uint256 chainlinkPriceX96 = _getChainlinkPriceX96(token);
300:             chainlinkReferencePriceX96 = cachedChainlinkReferencePriceX96 == 0
301:                 ? _getChainlinkPriceX96(referenceToken)
302:                 : cachedChainlinkReferencePriceX96;
303: 
304:             chainlinkPriceX96 = (10 ** referenceTokenDecimals) * chainlinkPriceX96 * Q96 / chainlinkReferencePriceX96
305:                 / (10 ** feedConfig.tokenDecimals);
306: 
307:             if (feedConfig.mode == Mode.TWAP_CHAINLINK_VERIFY) {
308:                 verifyPriceX96 = chainlinkPriceX96;
309:             } else {
310:                 priceX96 = chainlinkPriceX96;
311:             }
````
If it uses chainlink mode, price data will be retrieve via using _getChainlinkPriceX96 (see below). We can see in this function below (line 337) that it is calling the latestRoundData from price data feed contract.
````
File: V3Oracle.sol
328:     // calculates chainlink price given feedConfig
329:     function _getChainlinkPriceX96(address token) internal view returns (uint256) {
330:         if (token == chainlinkReferenceToken) {
331:             return Q96;
332:         }
333: 
334:         TokenConfig memory feedConfig = feedConfigs[token];
335: 
336:         // if stale data - revert
337:         (, int256 answer,, uint256 updatedAt,) = feedConfig.feed.latestRoundData();
338:         if (updatedAt + feedConfig.maxFeedAge < block.timestamp || answer < 0) {
339:             revert ChainlinkPriceError();
340:         }
341: 
342:         return uint256(answer) * Q96 / (10 ** feedConfig.feedDecimals);
343:     }
````
The price data feed contract here pertains to the proxy contract with AggregatorV3Interface to access the logic contract aggregator functions in order to retrieve prices from various sources.
Since this is proxy contract, there will be upgrades to be implemented and each upgrade can be subject to failure. Another issue on call reverts to the chainlink oracle is the Chainlink multisigs can immediately block access to price feeds at will, if this happens, retrieval of price data can be reverted which can cause the whole liquidation process to be cancelled.

There is another case also that the chainlink data feed can produce incorrect prices and very different in reality. Please take a look on this [case](https://blog.chain.link/improving-and-decentralizing-chainlinks-feature-release-and-network-upgrade-process/?ref=blog.synthetix.io), in which a human error occurred that requested a gold price (XAU) instead of the silver price (XAG).

If the case is incorrect prices, we can have a problem on this requirement of max difference line 324. This will revert also the whole liquidation prices if price difference between TWAP and Chainlink exceeded the max allowed.
````
File: V3Oracle.sol
323:         if (feedConfig.mode == Mode.CHAINLINK_TWAP_VERIFY || feedConfig.mode == Mode.TWAP_CHAINLINK_VERIFY) {
324:             _requireMaxDifference(priceX96, verifyPriceX96, feedConfig.maxDifference);
325:         }
````

## Impact
Liquidation can't be executed due to chainlink price data feed failure or big discrepancy with TWAP which can cause bad debt to protocol.

## Proof of Concept
Invoking Oracles might lead to reversals, risking a total service shutdown for reliant smart contracts. Since Chainlink multisigs have the power to cut off price feed access at any time, the current functionality of a price feed doesn't guarantee its future availability. To mitigate this, smart contracts should:

 - Encapsulate Oracle calls within try/catch blocks to manage errors effectively,
 - Include mechanisms to alter or refresh Oracle feeds once set.

Should an Oracle feed fail or stop working, and the smart contract lacks a backup data source or the capability to update its data sources, the contract will become irreversibly non-functional.

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
If the case is losing or failed access to price feed. It is recommended to query Chainlink price feeds using a defensive approach with Solidity’s try/catch structure. In this way, if the call to the price feed fails, the caller contract is still in control and can handle any errors safely and explicitly.

If the case is incorrect prices with big discrepancy against TWAP, can't recommend the best way approach because it is contrary on the intentiion of _requiremaxdifference.








