# Vulnerability details

## Impact
AutoExit and AutoRange contracts execution process can be DoSed by frontruning with a maliciuos transaction

## Proof of Concept

AutoExit contract lets a v3 position to be automatically removed (limit order) or swapped to the opposite token (stop loss order) when it reaches a certain tick.

AutoRange contract allows operator (Revert controlled bot) to change range for configured positions.

Both of these functions have an `execute()` function that forward a call to the Automator contract to validate a swap:

```
AutoExit.sol#execute()

 function execute(ExecuteParams calldata params) external nonReentrant {

...
    // checks if price in valid oracle range and calculates amountOutMin
    (state.amountOutMin,,,) = _validateSwap(
      !state.isAbove,
      state.swapAmount,
      state.pool,
      TWAPSeconds,
      maxTWAPTickDifference,
      state.isAbove ? config.token1SlippageX64 : config.token0SlippageX64
    );
...
}

Automator.sol#_validateSwap()

    function _validateSwap(
        bool swap0For1,
        uint256 amountIn,
        IUniswapV3Pool pool,
        uint32 twapPeriod,
        uint16 maxTickDifference,
        uint64 maxPriceDifferenceX64
    ) internal view returns (uint256 amountOutMin, int24 currentTick, uint160 sqrtPriceX96, uint256 priceX96) {
        // get current price and tick
@>        (sqrtPriceX96, currentTick,,,,,) = pool.slot0(); 

        // check if current tick not too far from TWAP
        if (!_hasMaxTWAPTickDifference(pool, twapPeriod, currentTick, maxTickDifference)) {
            revert TWAPCheckFailed();
        }

        // calculate min output price price and percentage
        priceX96 = FullMath.mulDiv(sqrtPriceX96, sqrtPriceX96, Q96);
        if (swap0For1) {
            amountOutMin = FullMath.mulDiv(amountIn * (Q64 - maxPriceDifferenceX64), priceX96, Q96 * Q64);
        } else {
            amountOutMin = FullMath.mulDiv(amountIn * (Q64 - maxPriceDifferenceX64), Q96, priceX96 * Q64);
        }
    }
```

The problem is on the line `(sqrtPriceX96, currentTick,,,,,) = pool.slot0();`. 

The slot0 has the most recent pool data that is extremely easy to manipulate.

So a maliciuos user can watch a mempool and frontran a desired tx by changing a values in the slot0 to break the check in the  `if (!_hasMaxTWAPTickDifference(pool, twapPeriod, currentTick, maxTickDifference))` to revert the full tx.

In that way he can DoS the contract to get the results that can damage the protocol.

## Tools Used
Manual review.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Use TWAP oracle prices instead of slot0 values. 


