# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/435b054f9ad2404173f36f0f74a5096c894b12b7/src/V3Vault.sol#L1032-L1073


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The `V3Vault.sol` is vulnerable to a liquidation slippage issue due to the lack of slippage protection during the liquidation process, which could lead to unexpected outcomes if the market price changes between the time the liquidation( the `liquidate function` calls the `_sendPositionValue` function: https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/435b054f9ad2404173f36f0f74a5096c894b12b7/src/V3Vault.sol#L735 ) is initiated and when it is executed.

Users who liquidate their positions could potentially face losses greater than their tolerance for price movement. If the market price changes significantly during the liquidation process, the user could end up receiving less of their borrowed token than expected, leading to a loss.

## Proof of Concept

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/435b054f9ad2404173f36f0f74a5096c894b12b7/src/V3Vault.sol#L1032-L1073


The `_sendPositionValue` function, which is responsible for liquidating a position. This function calculates the liquidation value and then calls `nonfungiblePositionManager.decreaseLiquidity` and `nonfungiblePositionManager.collect` to perform the liquidation.

Here is the relevant code snippet from `V3Vault.sol`:

```solidity
function _sendPositionValue(
    uint256 tokenId,
    uint256 liquidationValue,
    uint256 fullValue,
    uint256 feeValue,
    address recipient
) internal returns (uint256 amount0, uint256 amount1) {
    uint128 liquidity;
    uint128 fees0;
    uint128 fees1;

    // if full position is liquidated - no analysis needed
    if (liquidationValue == fullValue) {
        (,,,,,,, liquidity,,,,) = nonfungiblePositionManager.positions(tokenId);
        fees0 = type(uint128).max;
        fees1 = type(uint128).max;
    } else {
        (,,, liquidity,,, fees0, fees1) = oracle.getPositionBreakdown(tokenId);

        // only take needed fees
        if (liquidationValue < feeValue) {
            liquidity = 0;
            fees0 = uint128(liquidationValue * fees0 / feeValue);
            fees1 = uint128(liquidationValue * fees1 / feeValue);
        } else {
            // take all fees and needed liquidity
            fees0 = type(uint128).max;
            fees1 = type(uint128).max;
            liquidity = uint128((liquidationValue - feeValue) * liquidity / (fullValue - feeValue));
        }
    }

    if (liquidity > 0) {
        nonfungiblePositionManager.decreaseLiquidity(
            INonfungiblePositionManager.DecreaseLiquidityParams(tokenId, liquidity, 0, 0, block.timestamp)
        );
    }

    (amount0, amount1) = nonfungiblePositionManager.collect(
        INonfungiblePositionManager.CollectParams(tokenId, recipient, fees0, fees1)
    );
}
```

Issue here is that the `liquidity` and `fees` are calculated without considering the current market conditions, which could lead to unexpected outcomes if the market price changes between the time the liquidation is initiated and when it is executed.

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
`_sendPositionValue` function could be modified to include a slippage tolerance or use an oracle to estimate a fair price for the liquidation.
Here is an example of how the function could be updated to include a slippage tolerance:

```solidity
function _sendPositionValue(
    uint256 tokenId,
    uint256 liquidationValue,
    uint256 fullValue,
    uint256 feeValue,
    uint256 slippageTolerance, // New parameter for slippage tolerance
    address recipient
) internal returns (uint256 amount0, uint256 amount1) {
    // ...

    // Calculate the expected price range based on the slippage tolerance
    uint256 expectedPriceRange = calculateExpectedPriceRange(slippageTolerance);

    // Check if the current market price is within the expected price range
    if (!isPriceWithinRange(expectedPriceRange)) {
        revert PriceOutOfRange();
    }

    // ...
}
```

In this example above, `calculateExpectedPriceRange` and `isPriceWithinRange` are placeholder functions that would need to be implemented to calculate the expected price range based on the slippage tolerance and to check if the current market price is within that range.This would ensure that the liquidation process is fair and secure for the users, preventing potential losses due to unexpected price movements.





