# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Using Chainlink in L2 chains such as Arbitrum requires to check if the sequencer is down to avoid prices from looking like they are fresh although they are not.

The bug could be leveraged by malicious actors to take advantage of the sequencer downtime.

The impact of this can be on multiple levels. For example:

- Users can get better borrows if the price is above the actual price
- Users can avoid liquidations if the price is under the actual price


## Proof of Concept
Chainlink prices in the protocol are fetched through `V3Oracle::_getChainlinkPriceX96()`:

```
// calculates chainlink price given feedConfig
    function _getChainlinkPriceX96(address token) internal view returns (uint256) {
        if (token == chainlinkReferenceToken) {
            return Q96;
        }

        TokenConfig memory feedConfig = feedConfigs[token];

        // if stale data - revert
        (, int256 answer,, uint256 updatedAt,) = feedConfig.feed.latestRoundData();
        if (updatedAt + feedConfig.maxFeedAge < block.timestamp || answer < 0) {
            revert ChainlinkPriceError();
        }

        return uint256(answer) * Q96 / (10 ** feedConfig.feedDecimals);
    }
```
However there is no check inside if the sequencer updating the prices feeds on Arbitrum is currently down.

Chainlink have a dedicated section describing why this is needed -> https://docs.chain.link/data-feeds/l2-sequencer-feeds

Sponsors have confirmed on Discord that Arbitrum will be the first chain they will deploy the protocol on

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Chainlink has provided an example of how to implement the check: https://docs.chain.link/data-feeds/l2-sequencer-feeds#example-code


