# Vulnerability details

## Description
NOTE: this issue is different from the issue titled "Transformer Approval Persists After Position Transfer" as seen in the HYDN audit. While that issue is "fixed" as seen by the modification of the `transformApprovals` mapping from `transformApprovals[tokenId][target]` to `transformApprovals[msg.sender][tokenId][target]`, it seems loan position approval issues like below still persist. 


# Impact
when a user approves another address to call transform on its behalf, after each sucessfull transform action, the approval is not revoked. This allows the approved address to do multiple transforms (more than one) on the old loan position and new loan position, which is unintended by the user. 

### Attack Scenario
- a tranformation can modify or totally change a loan, this means a transformed loan position is not the same as it previously was. 
- user approves address A to transform its not so profitable loan position on its behalf via `approveTransform()`
- transform() is called by address A for user, the loan transform is sucessfull, new loan position is much more profitable.
- because a tranformation can happen and the `newTokenId` can be the same as the old `tokenID`, this previous approval by user A will still be valid for the new profitable transformed position. So it's a case of user gave approval for the old loan positon but it still works on the new (transformed) loan position because they have the same `tokenID`. 
-  `transform()` is then called AGAIN by address A for user, the transform is sucessful. This new action is  unintended by the user because the original intention was to give approval to transform the first original loan position, not the new (profitable) transformed one. This unintended transform can then turn the position into an unprofitable one.




