# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The `FightingFarm.transferFrom/safeTransferFrom` use the private `_ableToTransfer` function. While they check approvals on $msg.sender$ through `_isApprovedOrOwner(msg.sender, tokenId)` , it is never checked that the specified from parameter is actually the owner of the NFT.
An attacker can decrease other users' NFT balances, making them unable to cancel or claim their NFTs and locking users' funds. The attacker, who has been approved by the owner to use their tokens can transfer their owner's NFT passing the victim i.e owner's address as $from$ by calling `transferFrom(from=victim,to=attackerAccount,tokenId=attackerTokenId)`. This passes the `_isApprovedOrOwner` check, but reduces from's balance
Sp,If a delegate becomes compromised/untrustworthy after granting approval but before transfer then the owner will lose its NFT because there is no mechanism to revoke the approval that was granted earlier.

## Proof of Concept

This are the following lines of code where the actual vulnerability is happening
```
 function _ableToTransfer(uint256 tokenId, address to) private view returns(bool) {
        return (
          _isApprovedOrOwner(msg.sender, tokenId) //@audit checks for both & passes &&
          balanceOf(to) < MAX_FIGHTERS_ALLOWED &&
          !fighterStaked[tokenId]
        );
    }
```
```
  function transferFrom(
        address from, 
        address to, 
        uint256 tokenId
    ) 
        public 
        override(ERC721, IERC721)
    {
        require(_ableToTransfer(tokenId, to));
        _transfer(from, to, tokenId);
    }
```
```
    function safeTransferFrom(
        address from, 
        address to, 
        uint256 tokenId
    ) 
        public 
        override(ERC721, IERC721)
    {
        require(_ableToTransfer(tokenId, to));
        _safeTransfer(from, to, tokenId, "");
    }
```

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Add this line in `_ableTotransfer`
`require(ownerOf(tokenId) == from, Errors.ACCESS)`





