# Vulnerability details

## Impact
In scope, there are two `getOUSGPrice()` functions [ousgInstantManager.getOUSGPrice()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-ondo-finance/blob/main/contracts/ousg/ousgInstantManager.sol#L479-L485) and [rOUSG.getOUSGPrice()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-ondo-finance/blob/main/contracts/ousg/rOUSG.sol#L378-L380). The former has a protection against oracle malfunction: if the price returned by the oracle is too low, the transaction reverts. The latter doesn't have this protection, as we reported in another issue.

However, both of these functions don't care about price timestamps. This means that in case of oracle malfunction (if oracle doesn't keep updating the price value), the functionalities of `ousgInstantManager` and `rOUSG` keep working, computing their values on an old price value.

## Proof of concept

As described inside the Discord channel, this is the oracle contract: [RWAOracleExternalComparisonCheck](https://etherscan.deth.net/address/0x0502c5ae08E7CD64fe1AEDA7D6e229413eCC6abe). So, this is the `oracle.getPriceData()` function:

```
L115-L128

/**
* @notice Retrieve the last set RWA price data
*
* @dev `price` is in 18 decimals, `timestamp` is unix seconds since epoch
*/
function getPriceData()
external
view
override
returns (uint256 price, uint256 timestamp)
{
    price = uint256(rwaPrice);
    timestamp = priceTimestamp;
}
```

So, the oracle returns the timestamp value. However, inside [ousgInstantManager.getOUSGPrice()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-ondo-finance/blob/main/contracts/ousg/ousgInstantManager.sol#L479-L485):

```
479    function getOUSGPrice() public view returns (uint256 price) {
480      (price, ) = oracle.getPriceData();
481      require(
482        price > MINIMUM_OUSG_PRICE,
483        "OUSGInstantManager::getOUSGPrice: Price unexpectedly low"
484      );
485    }
```

and [rOUSG.getOUSGPrice()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-ondo-finance/blob/main/contracts/ousg/rOUSG.sol#L378-L380):

```
378     function getOUSGPrice() public view returns (uint256 price) {
379         (price, ) = oracle.getPriceData();
380     }
```

the returned value of the timestamp is ignored.

