# Vulnerability details


# Pausing `RWADynamicOracle` Inability to call `transferShares(_recipient,_sharesAmount)` even if rOUSG remains unpaused

If the `RWADynamicOracle` is paused the admin function `transferShares(_recipient,_sharesAmount)` will cease to work.
This is not ideal as the oracle is only needed in instances in which rOUSG amount is used rather than shares.

```contracts/ousg/rOUSG.sol:L#397
  function transferShares(
    address _recipient,
    uint256 _sharesAmount
  ) public returns (uint256) {
    _transferShares(msg.sender, _recipient, _sharesAmount);
    emit TransferShares(msg.sender, _recipient, _sharesAmount);
    uint256 tokensAmount = getROUSGByShares(_sharesAmount);
    emit Transfer(msg.sender, _recipient, tokensAmount);
    return tokensAmount;
  }
```

The `emit Transfer(msg.sender, _recipient, tokensAmount);` will cause this function to revert it the `RWADynamicOracle` is paused, even while rOUSG itself remains unpaused. 

## **Recommendation** 
Remove dependence on the `RWADynamicOracle` w/n the `transferShares` function. 

eg:
```contracts/ousg/rOUSG.sol:L#397
  function transferShares(
    address _recipient,
    uint256 _sharesAmount
  ) public returns (uint256) {
    _transferShares(msg.sender, _recipient, _sharesAmount);
    emit TransferShares(msg.sender, _recipient, _sharesAmount);
    return _sharesAmount;
  }
```
Alternatively you could implement some pause hierarchy w/n oracle contract like so:
```
  function pauseOracle() external onlyRole(PAUSER_ROLE) {
    require(
      rOUSG.paused() == true, 
      "rOUSG must be paused before Oracle"
    );
    _pause();
  }
```

It is unclear why the nominal amount of rOUSG is needed w/n this function given that the user is electing to transfer shares rather than rOUSG nominal amount. Shares ought to be transferable even if the oracle is in a paused state.

## Impact 
Shares will be unable to be transfered if `RWADynamicOracle` Oracle is in a paused state.

