# Vulnerability details

## Impact
`anchor` Anchors the latest L1 block details on the Taiko L2 chain. Inconsistencies between the L1 and L2 states could lead to the acceptance of invalid or fraudulent cross-layer messages. 

## Proof of Concept
The actual behavior of the anchor function may deviate from the expected behavior in the following way:
 * If an attacker manages to forge or manipulate the L1 block details provided as input parameters, the function may anchor invalid or inconsistent data on the L2 chain.
 * This could lead to discrepancies between the L1 and L2 states, causing issues in the cross-layer message verification process.
 * The inconsistencies may allow for the acceptance of invalid or fraudulent cross-layer messages, compromising the security and integrity of the system.

If the anchor transaction is not properly validated, it could lead to inconsistencies between the L1 and L2 states.

 - Code: [TaikoL2.sol#L71-L132](https://github.com/taikoxyz/taiko-mono/blob/main/packages/protocol/contracts/L2/TaikoL2.sol#L71-L132)

It does not verify the authenticity and integrity of the L1 block details provided as input parameters. This could lead to discrepancies between the L1 and L2 states, compromising the cross-layer message verification process.
```solidity


    /// @notice Initializes the contract.
    /// @param _owner The owner of this contract. msg.sender will be used if this value is zero.
    /// @param _addressManager The address of the {AddressManager} contract.
    /// @param _l1ChainId The ID of the base layer.
    /// @param _gasExcess The initial gasExcess.
    function init(
        address _owner,
        address _addressManager,
        uint64 _l1ChainId,
        uint64 _gasExcess
    )
        external
        initializer
    {
        __CrossChainOwned_init(_owner, _addressManager, _l1ChainId);


        if (block.chainid <= 1 || block.chainid > type(uint64).max) {
            revert L2_INVALID_CHAIN_ID();
        }


        if (block.number == 0) {
            // This is the case in real L2 genesis
        } else if (block.number == 1) {
            // This is the case in tests
            uint256 parentHeight = block.number - 1;
            l2Hashes[parentHeight] = blockhash(parentHeight);
        } else {
            revert L2_TOO_LATE();
        }


        gasExcess = _gasExcess;
        (publicInputHash,) = _calcPublicInputHash(block.number);
        __currentBlockTimestamp = uint64(block.timestamp);
    }


    /// @notice Anchors the latest L1 block details to L2 for cross-layer
    /// message verification.
    /// @param _l1BlockHash The latest L1 block hash when this block was
    /// proposed.
    /// @param _l1StateRoot The latest L1 block's state root.
    /// @param _l1BlockId The latest L1 block height when this block was proposed.
    /// @param _parentGasUsed The gas used in the parent block.
    function anchor(
        bytes32 _l1BlockHash,
        bytes32 _l1StateRoot,
        uint64 _l1BlockId,
        uint32 _parentGasUsed
    )
        external
        nonReentrant
    {
        if (
            _l1BlockHash == 0 || _l1StateRoot == 0 || _l1BlockId == 0
                || (block.number != 1 && _parentGasUsed == 0)
        ) {
            revert L2_INVALID_PARAM();
        }


        if (msg.sender != GOLDEN_TOUCH_ADDRESS) revert L2_INVALID_SENDER();


        uint256 parentId;
```
It first validates the input parameters and checks if the sender is the `GOLDEN_TOUCH_ADDRESS`. If the validation fails, it reverts with the appropriate error message. 

## Tools Used
Manual audit

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Use secure and trusted sources to obtain the L1 block details, such as directly from the L1 blockchain or through a decentralized oracle system. And consider implementing additional security measures, such as multi-party computation or threshold signatures, to ensure the validity and consensus of the anchored L1 block details.


