# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-salty/blob/53516c2cdfdfacb662cdea6417c52f23c94d5b5b/src/stable/CollateralAndLiquidity.sol#L154


# Vulnerability details

# Context
The liquidateUser function calls the decreaseUserShare function with the parameter `useCooldown` set to true. Thus, requiring the cooldown time to pass so the user can be liquidated, otherwise, the transaction will revert. 
```solidity
_decreaseUserShare( wallet, collateralPoolID, userCollateralAmount, true );
```

The problem with setting it to true is that now the following scenarios can be exploited: 

1. Attackers deposit BTC/ETH as collateral and borrow max USDS and once they become eligible for liquidation they front-run liquidators by increasing a small amount(dust) by depositing more collateral and becoming again ineligible for liquidation due to the cooldown.

2. Attackers maximize profits by cyclically borrowing USDS and converting it into WBTC or WETH, which is then used as collateral for further USDS loans. This leveraging process is repeated to its limit. Part of the borrowed USDS is kept as ETH, WBTC, or WETH to cover transaction fees and strengthen their collateral, reducing liquidation risk. This will cause a huge impact in terms of bad debt because the leveraged and borrowed USDS is also used to increase their shares/avoid liquidation, and expose themselves to receive more share rewards from the protocol which will keep providing the "reserves" for keeping a large and undercollaterized position opened.

3. All price oracles are susceptible to front-running as their prices tend to lag behind an asset's real-time price. More specifically:

Chainlink oracles are updated after the change in price crosses a deviation threshold, (eg. 2.5% in ETH / USD), which means a price feed could return a value slightly smaller/larger than an asset's actual price under normal conditions.
Uniwap V3 TWAP returns the average price over the past X number of blocks, which means it will always lag behind the real-time price.

Even though the protocol has also a 3rd option(the collateral pool reserves) oracle, it uses the closest price comparison between two prices. By having Uniswap TWAP and chainlink return lagged prices, attackers will front-run those oracles updates to be able to avoid liquidation by increasing a small dust into their collateral through the depositCollateralAndIncreaseShare function and become invalid for liquidation over the next hour, while using the same strategy mentioned on the 2nd issue above, they will reuse their rewards on top of their increased liquidity to keep avoiding liquidation "for free". 

4 - (Additional loss) If those attackers also manage to extract rewards from calling the Upkeep function, this will be another source provided by the protocol to manage those undercollaterized positions. 

As you can see above, extracting assets from the own protocol while using it for the advantage of having large undercollaterized positions is easily possible due to the low price paid for not being liquidated while using the funds provided by the protocol itself. 

## Impact
Undercollaterilized positions are not liquidated(especially large ones).
Bad debt will accumulate exponentially.
The protocol will become undercollaterized. 


## Proof of Concept
Add the following POC in CollateralAndLiquidity.t.sol:
