# Vulnerability details

## Impact

PriceAggregator.getPrice can be manipulated, which can cause bad debt and steal some of the value of USDS.

Also, the price can be manipulated to cause liquidation.

## Details

PriceAggregator.getPrice uses the average of the two closest price oracle values (Chainlink price feed, Uniswap V3 TWAP, Salty reserve) as the price. Therefore, to manipulate aggregatePrice, it is required to manipulate two values.

Among the three prices, **Salty reserve can be easily manipulated through swap in WBTC/USDS and WETH/USDS pools.**

```solidity
function getPriceETH() external view returns (uint256)
		{
                 // @audit can manipulate
                (uint256 reservesWETH, uint256 reservesUSDS) = pools.getPoolReserves(weth, usds);

		if ( ( reservesWETH < PoolUtils.DUST ) || ( reservesUSDS < PoolUtils.DUST ) )
			return 0;

		return ( reservesUSDS * 10**18 ) / reservesWETH;
		}
```

However, even if this value is manipulated, the Chainlink price feed and TWAP are difficult to manipulate, so the average of the two values will be returned as price.

If either value returns zero, this can be exploited to create a favorable price.

These issues are included in the cases described in the Attack Idea below.

> **Any issue that would prevent the accurate reporting of BTC and ETH price from the PriceAggregator.**

Currently, CoreChainlinkFeed returns 0 for price if the data in latestRoundData is past MAX_ANSWER_DELAY(3600s). This is to avoid reflecting stale prices in situations such as when the chainlink service is shut down. However, when I looked at the actual values in the price feed, **I found that they often exceeded MAX_ANSWER_DELAY**.

```solidity
function latestChainlinkPrice(AggregatorV3Interface chainlinkFeed) public view returns (uint256)
		{
		int256 price = 0;

		try chainlinkFeed.latestRoundData()
		returns (uint80, int256 _price, uint256, uint256 _answerTimestamp, uint80)
			{
			uint256 answerDelay = block.timestamp - _answerTimestamp;

@>			if ( answerDelay <= MAX_ANSWER_DELAY )
				price = _price;
			else
				price = 0;
			}
		catch (bytes memory) // Catching any failure
			{
			// In case of failure, price will remain 0
			}

@>		if ( price < 0 )
			return 0;

		// Convert the 8 decimals from the Chainlink price to 18 decimals
		return uint256(price) * 10**10;
		}
```

I was asked about this in a Chainlink Telegram. It's possible that the network is temporarily congested or that price updates are delayed for various reasons.
> If you're seeing a Chainlink price feed with a 3600s heartbeat not updating its latestRoundData for over an hour, it might be because of a few reasons. First, if the market for that asset is very stable, with little volatility, the feed might not update because the price hasn't changed enough to trigger a new round. Second, Chainlink relies on multiple data sources to ensure accuracy, and if there's a delay or issue with these external providers, it can affect the update frequency. Lastly, network congestion, either on the blockchain Chainlink operates on or within the Chainlink network itself, could cause delays. These feeds are designed to balance timeliness with cost and network efficiency, so updates may not always align perfectly with the heartbeat under certain conditions.

I could also see it through Etherscan.

tx: https://etherscan.io/tx/0xba8875a702b6490ed74d7510e91baf97a54f60203519b48ac8280b63416bd7e0
This is a tx that updates BTC/USD. It ran at 10:55:23 in block 19097454.

tx: https://etherscan.io/tx/0x859017b3259ed9f1541e2f233a263558076778085c046d85f42086f4e1f503be
However, the next update was executed at 11:55:35 in the [19097752](https://etherscan.io/block/19097752) block.

> This means that if we frontrun the price update tx in the 19097752 block, the chainlink price will return 0 because the answer delay is 3612.

The amazing thing is that this opportunity exists `almost every hour` (3600s). 

This allows us to manipulate the PriceAggregator's price almost every hour with a price of `TWAP price +- 2.5%`.

In this case, TWAP is relatively more inaccurate than the chainlink's value because it reflects past prices. Therefore, it is possible to make a larger difference than expected. (+5%)

An attacker can use this to make the value of the collateral smaller so that they can liquidate another user's loan and be rewarded.

Although almost impossible, it is possible to create bad debt and steal USDS in situations where TWAP is priced strangely.

