# Vulnerability details

## Impact
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-salty/blob/main/src%2Fprice_feed%2FCoreUniswapFeed.sol#L49-L73

The function `_getUniswapTwapWei()` calculates the `Time-Weighted Average Price (TWAP) of a Uniswap V3 pool` using the `Time-Weighted Average Tick (TWAT)` method. 

Let's break down the function:

The function takes two parameters: an instance of `IUniswapV3Pool` and a `uint256` representing the `TWAP` interval.
An array `secondsAgo` is declared with two elements, representing the start and end times of the `TWAP` calculation period.

The `observe()` function of the `Uniswap V3 pool` is called with the `secondsAgo` array to get the historical tick data. Ticks represent `price changes` in `Uniswap V3` and are stored in a cumulative form.
The difference between the tick cumulatives at the `start and end of the TWAP interval` is calculated and divided by the `TWAP interval` to get the average tick change per second.

The `TickMath.getSqrtRatioAtTick()` function is used to convert this average tick change into a `square root price`.
The `square root price` is then squared to get the `actual price`. The price is scaled by `FixedPoint96.Q96` to account for the fact that prices on `Uniswap V3` are always scaled up by `2^96`.

The function then retrieves the decimal values of the two tokens involved in the pool.

Depending on the `relative decimal values of the two tokens`, the function performs different calculations to convert the `price` into a quantity of `token0`. The result is returned as the output of the function.

This `_getUniswapTwapWei()` will trigger an `arithmetic underflow` whenever `sqrtPriceX96` is smaller than `FixedPoint96.Q96`, causing these function to revert until this price comes back in range and the math no longer overflows.

Such oracle price conditions are not only possible but also likely to happen in real market conditions, and they can be permanent (i.e. one asset permanently appreciating over the other one).

Then `getUniswapTwapWei()` function calls the `_getUniswapTwapWei()` in a try-catch block assigning:

```Solidity
twap=result;
```

The `getTwapWBTC()` function then calls `getUniswapTwapWei()` function to obtain `uniswapBTC_WETH and uniswapETH_USDC`:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-salty/blob/main/src%2Fprice_feed%2FCoreUniswapFeed.sol#L99-L100

```Solidity
            uint256 uniswapWBTC_WETH = getUniswapTwapWei( UNISWAP_V3_WBTC_WETH, twapInterval );
        uint256 uniswapWETH_USDC = getUniswapTwapWei( UNISWAP_V3_WETH_USDC, twapInterval );
```
Similarly, `getTwapWETH()` function also calls `getUniswapTwapWei()` function to obtain `uniswapWETH_USDC`:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-salty/blob/main/src%2Fprice_feed%2FCoreUniswapFeed.sol#L119

```Solidity
        uint256 uniswapWETH_USDC = getUniswapTwapWei( UNISWAP_V3_WETH_USDC, twapInterval );
```

Finally, the `getPriceBTC()` and `getPriceETH()` calls `getTwapWBTC()` and `getTwapWETH()` respectively to return their respective prices which are the 30 minutes TWAP by default and this could already be having errors due to the underflow above . 

## Proof of Concept
The root cause of this issue is that the `FullMath.sol library`, imported from `UniswapV3` was altered to build with `solidity v0.8.x`, which has `under/overflow protection`; the `library`, however, makes use of these by design, so it won’t work properly when compiled in `v0.8.0 or later`. 

###### Here is the actual test for thay can be used to demonstrate this issue:

- https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-salty/blob/main/src%2Fprice_feed%2Ftests%2FCoreUniswapFeed.t.sol

```Solidity

