# Lines of code

https://github.com/perspectivefi/spectra-core/blob/fec59dc6720fb4861b07b30845ef2c1a42f947bf/src/tokens/PrincipalToken.sol#L151


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
EIP-4626 does not require the decimals must be the same as the underlying tokens' decimals, and when it's not, previewRedeem may return an inflated or deflated `ibtRate`.

## Proof of Concept
In the current implementation, IERC4626(ibt).decimals() is used as the IERC4626(token).asset()'s decimals to calculate the `ibtRate` in the following formula.

`_ibtDecimals = IERC4626(_ibt).decimals();`

`ibtUnit = 10 ** _ibtDecimals;`

`ibtRate = IERC4626(ibt).previewRedeem(ibtUnit).toRay(_assetDecimals);`

The value of `ibtRate` will be significantly underestimated when the underlying token's decimals > ERC4626's decimals, and be significantly overestimated when the underlying token's decimals < ERC4626's decimals.

This will then cause issues when using the `ibtRate` to calculate how many shares passed to as an argument to withdraw from the ERC4626 as seen in the code below.

```
    function _withdrawShares(
        uint256 _ibts,
        address _receiver,
        address _owner,
        uint256 _ptRate,
        uint256 _ibtRate
    ) internal returns (uint256 shares) {
        if (_ptRate == 0) {
            revert RateError();
        }
        // convert ibts to shares using provided rates
        shares = _ibts.mulDiv(_ibtRate, _ptRate, Math.Rounding.Ceil);
        // burn owner's shares (YT and PT)
        if (block.timestamp < expiry) {
            IYieldToken(yt).burnWithoutUpdate(_owner, shares);
        }
        _burn(_owner, shares);
        emit Redeem(_owner, _receiver, shares);
    }
```

## Tools Used

Manul Review.


## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Set the `ibtUnit` to the decimals of the underlying token `assetDecimals` instead of the ibt token.


