# Vulnerability details

## Prerequisite knowledge
**ERC777 Hooks**: ERC777 hooks act as callback functions that are executed whether a token owner is trying to send ERC777 tokens or a token receiver is receiving ERC777 tokens. ERC20 tokens just update balances during transfers. But ERC777 tokens do it this way:
1. Make a hook call to an address chosen by the token sender.
2. Update balances.
3. Make a hook call to an address chosen by the token receiver.

    ![ERC777 Hooks](https://thumb.tildacdn.com/tild3138-6331-4330-b666-663033316335/-/format/webp/image_1.png)

**Registry and implementers:** All ERC777 tokens are linked to the registry contract https://etherscan.io/address/0x1820a4B7618BdE71Dce8cdc73aAB6C95905faD24. This address is used by ERC777 tokens to store preferred hook receivers. These preffered hook receivers are called "interface implementers".

It means that Alice can choose Bob as her interface implementer. Bob will receive hooks if Alice receives or sends ERC777 tokens.

Alice can manage different hook types. So, she can choose Bob as an interface implementer as long as Alice sends tokens and she can choose Tom only when Alice receives tokens.

In the majority of cases, she can also choose different interface implementers for different tokens.

For mofe info on the details of ERC777 and it's features, please check: https://ethereum.org/developers/docs/standards/tokens/erc-777


## Overview of the `UTBExecutor.sol` contract

The [`UTBExecutor`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol) contract is the contract responsible for executing any arbitrary calls in the project. There are two ways it can get called.

**Way #1**: The user calls the [`UTB::swapAndExecute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTB.sol#L108) function. The [`UTB::swapAndExecute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTB.sol#L108) function is one of the entry points of this protocol. It simply conducts a swap operation then executes an arbitrary call all on the same chain.

*Example of it's usage*: The user, has 1,000 USDC and he wants to mint an NFT in the same chain which costs 1000 DAI. The user will call the [`UTB::swapAndExecute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTB.sol#L108) function which will in turn, swap the user's 1,000 USDC with 1,000 DAI through uniswap and then execute an arbitrary call which would mint the NFT for the 1,000 DAI. The user gets to specify the `target` of the arbitrary call as well as the calldata he sends to it, as well as specify the details of the swap operation (address of tokenIn, address of tokenOut, amount of tokenIn, and amount of tokenOut).

**Way #1**: The user calls the [`UTB::bridgeAndExecute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/011f62059f3a0b1f3577c8ccd1140f0cf3e7bb29/src/UTB.sol#L259) function. The [`UTB::bridgeAndExecute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/011f62059f3a0b1f3577c8ccd1140f0cf3e7bb29/src/UTB.sol#L259) function is also one of the entry points of this protocol. It executes two swap operations (a swap pre-bridging and another swap post-bridging) and then executes an arbitrary call on the destination chain. The function responsible for the execution of the arbitrary call is the [`UTBExecutor::execute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol#L41) function which exists in the [`UTBExecutor`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol) contract.

*Example of it's usage*: The user, in Polygon has 1,000 USDC and he wants to mint an NFT in Optimism which costs 1000 DAI. The user will call the [`UTB::bridgeAndExecute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/011f62059f3a0b1f3577c8ccd1140f0cf3e7bb29/src/UTB.sol#L259) function which will in turn, conduct a pre-bridge swap operation and swap the user's 1000 USDC with 0.5 WETH. Then it will bridge the resulting WETH to Optimism and conduct a post-bridge swap operation to swap the resulting bridged 0.5 WETH to 1000 DAI. It will finally, execute an arbitrary call to mint the NFT. The user gets to specify the `target` of the arbitrary call as well as the calldata he sends to it, as well as specify the details of the pre-bridge swap operation, and partially the post-bridge swap operation as well. The function responsible for the execution of the arbitrary call is the [`UTBExecutor::execute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol#L41) function which exists in the [`UTBExecutor`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol) contract.


## The vulnerability

The [`UTBExecutor::execute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol#L41) function does not check if the `target` of the arbitrary call is the EIP1820 registry or not, neither does it check if the function selector of the calldata to be sent is the function selector of the `setInterfaceImplementer()` function.

This allows an attacker to set the interface implementer of address the [`UTBExecutor`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol) contract to be the address of a malicious contract he controls. When the attacker does that, anytime the [`UTBExecutor`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol) contract tries to send or receive ERC777 tokens, the malicious contract will be called.

If the [`UTBExecutor`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol) contract tries to send ERC777 tokens, the hook function `tokensToSend()` will be called on the malicious contract, and if the [`UTBExecutor`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol) contract received any ERC777 tokens, the hook function `tokensReceived()` will be called on the malicious contract. 

This allows for a reentrancy attack vector allowing the hijacking of ANY ERC777 tokens flowing through the protocol. Additionally, if the attacker wants, he can cause a huge disruption by making his hook functions revert, causing any operation involving ERC777 in the protocol to revert and causing permanent fund loss in the case that the operation was a cross-chain ones initiated using the [`UTB::bridgeAndExecute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/011f62059f3a0b1f3577c8ccd1140f0cf3e7bb29/src/UTB.sol#L259) function.

## Proof of concept

**Step 1** -> The attacker will make a call to the [`UTB::swapAndExecute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTB.sol#L108) function to conduct a swap between 1 USDC -> 1 DAI (useless swap, but needed to reach the point where we can execute an arbitrary call). and then instruct it to make an arbitrary call to the EIP1820 registry and set the interface implementer for the [`UTBExecutor`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol) contract to be a malicious contract created by the attacker. Note that the contract executing the arbitrary call would be the [`UTBExecutor`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol) contract inside the [`UTBExecutor::execute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol#L41) function, so the attacker would set the interface implementer of the [`UTBExecutor`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol) contract on it's behalf. The resulting 1 DAI from the swap will be then be refunded to the attacker as it has not been used in the arbitrary call. Also note that the `UTB` contract approves the `UTBExecutor` contract to spend any funds resulting from the swap.

