Right now, at any point in time the `UTBExecutor` contract receives ERC777 tokens from the `UTB`, the attacker's malicious contract will be called.

**Step 2** -> Suppose the victim has 1,000 USDC and he wants to mint an NFT which costs a 1,000 ERC777 tokens. The victim will approve the UTB to spend his USDC and call the [`UTB::swapAndExecute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTB.sol#L108) function and instruct it to swap his USDC for the ERC777 tokens (which is the payment method for the NFT), and then execute an arbitrary call to mint the NFT

**Step 3** -> After the [`UTB::swapAndExecute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTB.sol#L108) function swaps the 1,000 USDC for the 1,000 ERC777 tokens, it will approve the `UTBExecutor` contract to spend the ERC777 tokens and then call the [`UTBExecutor::execute()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol#L41) function in the `UTBExecutor` contract which will execute the arbitrary call to mint the NFT

