**Step 9** -> The UTBExecutor will approve the `paymentOperator`, in this case, the NFT contract the victim wants to mint, 1,000 ERC777 tokens so that the victim mints the NFT. The `approve()` call will not revert even though the `UTBExecutor` contract doesn't have 1,000 ERC777 tokens. The `approve()` function allows you to approve someone to spend 1,000,000 tokens on behalf of you even if you own 0 tokens.


**Step 10** -> The arbitrary call to the NFT contract will take place, but it will fail. However, the `execute()` function itself won't revert, because it was a low-level arbitrary call. 

**Step 11** -> The `execute()` function will then check if there any leftover ERC777 tokens from the arbitrary call by compaing [`initBalance`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/011f62059f3a0b1f3577c8ccd1140f0cf3e7bb29/src/UTBExecutor.sol#L59) and [`remainingBalance`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/011f62059f3a0b1f3577c8ccd1140f0cf3e7bb29/src/UTBExecutor.sol#L73) but the result will be zero. Although the NFT arbitrary call failed, the ERC777 tokens were already hijacked/drained from the `UTBExecutor`.


## Impact
This vulnerability allows an attacker to hijack any ERC777 tokens flowing through the protocol or cause a DoS allowing for permanent fund loss for any cross-chain operation involving ERC777 tokens in addition to preventing any same-chain operation involving ERC777 take place


