# Vulnerability details

## Impact
In [UTBExecutor.sol](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/main/src/UTBExecutor.sol), No validation on payload size/parameters. Risk of gas limit DoS. Payload execution cannot be avoided after input validation since there is no validation. No validation happening between entry point (execute call) and payload execution.

The UTBExecutor's `execute()` function allows arbitrary payload execution from the msg.sender without validation. This enables denial-of-service from resource exhaustion or potential funds draining if target contracts are vulnerable. UTBExecutor's `execute()` accepts an untrusted calldata payload and forwards it directly to the target contract address for execution

The [UTBExecutor.execute()](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-decent/blob/011f62059f3a0b1f3577c8ccd1140f0cf3e7bb29/src/UTBExecutor.sol#L19-L29) accepts an untrusted calldata payload from the msg.sender without checking

1. Size of the payload 
2. Contents/parameters of the payload

This opens the contract up to gas limit DoS attacks and unexpected code execution.

```solidity
function execute(
    address target,
    address paymentOperator,
    bytes memory payload, // unchecked! <@Notice
    address token,
    uint amount,
    address payable refund,
    uint extraNative
) public onlyOwner {
    bool success;
    if (token == address(0)) {
        (success, ) = target.call{value: amount}(payload);
        if (!success) {
            (refund.call{value: amount}(""));
        }
        return;
    }


    uint initBalance = IERC20(token).balanceOf(address(this));


    IERC20(token).transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
    IERC20(token).approve(paymentOperator, amount);


    if (extraNative > 0) {
        (success, ) = target.call{value: extraNative}(payload);
        if (!success) {
            (refund.call{value: extraNative}(""));
        }
    } else {
        (success, ) = target.call(payload);
    }
```
An attacker could construct a malicious payload that:

1. Is very large, causing the gas cost to exceed the gas limit leading to a DoS.

2. Calls back into the contract's state-changing functions in an attempt to drain funds or update ownership by taking advantage of reentrancy. 

For example:

```solidity 
execute(
  this,
  abi.encodeWithSignature("drainFunds()") // callback!
)
```

**Impact**

The unchecked payload execution has two main consequences:

1. **Denial of Service**: By passing large payloads, an attacker can make execute function calls use up the entire gas limit. This prevents the contract from processing further legitimate transactions.

2. **Arbitrary Code Execution**: Malicious callback payloads can potentially drain funds or update contract ownership by reentering state changing functions, provided the target contracts themselves are vulnerable.

In essence, by not validating inputs, UTBExecutor passes on the responsibility of secure code execution to the target contracts. So the impact is proportional to the security guarantees of the contracts being called.

## Proof of Concept
The main issue here is that UTBExecutor's execute() function blindly passes the payload provided by the `msg.sender` directly to the target contract without any checks. This means:

1. attacker can provide a very large payload that uses up all available gas. This will trigger out-of-gas exceptions and deny service to legitimate users:

```solidity
attacker -> execute(target, LARGE_PAYLOAD)

UTBExecutor -> target.call(LARGE_PAYLOAD) -> RUN OUT OF GAS 
```

2. The attacker can provide specially crafted payloads that invoke callbacks into UTBExecutor or dependencies to drain funds or take over ownership. This is possible if the target contracts themselves are vulnerable:


```solidity
attacker -> execute(vulnerableTarget, drainFundsPayload())  

UTBExecutor -> vulnerableTarget.call(drainFundsPayload()) 
            -> calls back drainFunds() -> funds drained
```

Essentially, you are trusting that all target contracts are securely coded without reentrancy or callback vulnerabilities. **Any vulnerabilities in targets can be exploited by a malicious user** providing the specially crafted payload.

## Tools Used
Manual

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Should add:

1. Payload size checking to prevent DoS attacks
2. Payload parameter validation to prevent unexpected callback instructions
3. Reentrancy guards around payload execution



