# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The DecentBridgeExecutor contract contains an unbounded approval limit in the _executeWeth function. This unbounded limit can potentially allow a malicious or compromised target contract to withdraw more WETH than necessary, leading to fund drainage.

We have seen numerous cases in which users are exploited due to improperly set approve functions. As such, it is important to only allow token approvals till what is necessary, and/or probably reset it after its usage.

## Proof of Concept
In the DecentBridgeExecutor contract, the _executeWeth function is designed to handle WETH transactions. Before executing a transaction, the function sets an allowance for the target contract by calling weth.approve(target, amount). This approval grants the target contract permission to transfer up to the specified amount of WETH from the DecentBridgeExecutor contract.

A malicious or vulnerable target contract could exploit this unbounded approval to repeatedly withdraw WETH up to the approved amount, draining funds from the DecentBridgeExecutor.

```solidity
    function _executeWeth(
        address from,
        address target,
        uint256 amount,
        bytes memory callPayload
    ) private {
        uint256 balanceBefore = weth.balanceOf(address(this));
        weth.approve(target, amount);

        (bool success, ) = target.call(callPayload);

        if (!success) {
            weth.transfer(from, amount);
            return;
        }

        uint256 remainingAfterCall = amount -
            (balanceBefore - weth.balanceOf(address(this)));

        // refund the sender with excess WETH
        weth.transfer(from, remainingAfterCall);
    }
```

## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

It is advisable to implement a restrictive approval permission. No users funds are at risk during the normal operations of the protocol. This suggestion is more of a design improvements.

Modify the approval to grant only the exact amount required for each transaction and then reset the value after its usage. This approach reduces the risk by limiting the target contract's access to funds.

```solidity
+ weth.approve(target, amount); // Grant only the necessary amount
+ (bool success, ) = target.call(callPayload);
+ weth.approve(target, 0); // Revoke the approval after the transaction
```


